(window.LH_SEARCH = window.LH_SEARCH || {})["ai"] = {"s":"ai","t":[["what-is-ai","What AI is and intelligent agents","1.1"],["search-problems","Problems as search","1.2"],["bfs-ucs","Breadth first and uniform cost search","2.1"],["dfs-ids","Depth first, iterative deepening and bidirectional search","2.2"],["greedy-astar","Greedy best first and A* search","3.1"],["heuristic-design","Designing and comparing heuristics","3.2"],["memory-bounded","Memory bounded heuristic search: IDA*, RBFS and SMA*","3.3"],["and-or-search","AND OR search and AO*","3.4"],["hill-climbing","Local search: hill climbing and simulated annealing","4.1"],["genetic-algorithms","Genetic algorithms","4.2"],["csp-basics","Constraint satisfaction problems and arc consistency","5.1"],["csp-backtracking","Backtracking search for CSPs","5.2"],["csp-local","Local search for CSPs and problem structure","5.3"],["minimax","Games and minimax","6.1"],["alpha-beta","Alpha beta pruning","6.2"],["expectimax","Games with chance: expectimax and Monte Carlo tree search","6.3"],["propositional-logic","Knowledge, logic and propositional logic","7.1"],["propositional-inference","Reasoning patterns: rules, resolution and chaining","7.2"],["first-order-logic","First order logic: syntax and semantics","7.3"],["fol-inference","Inference in first order logic: unification, chaining and resolution","7.4"],["situation-calculus","Situation calculus and deductive planning","8.1"],["strips-planning","STRIPS planning and sub goals","8.2"],["partial-order-planning","Partial order planning","8.3"],["probability-basics","Probability for AI agents","9.1"],["bayesian-networks","Bayesian networks and Markov networks","9.2"],["bn-inference","Inference in Bayesian networks","9.3"],["causality","Causality: seeing, doing and imagining","9.4"],["mdp","Markov decision processes","10.1"],["passive-rl","Passive reinforcement learning","10.2"],["active-rl","Active reinforcement learning","10.3"],["policy-search","Policy search and policy gradients","10.4"],["core-challenge","Challenge problems across Core AI","11.1"],["core-revision","Exam revision: from school to research universities","11.2"],["decision-problems","Decision problems and preferences","1.1"],["utility-functions","Utility functions","1.2"],["choosing-best-action","Choosing the best action","1.3"],["lotteries-cdf","Lotteries and distributions","2.1"],["expected-utility","Expected utility and compound lotteries","2.2"],["risk-attitudes","Risk attitudes and the St Petersburg paradox","2.3"],["backward-induction","Backward induction","3.1"],["discounting-consumption","Discounting and consumption over time","3.2"],["value-of-information","The value of information","3.3"],["normal-form-games","Static games and the normal form","4.1"],["modelling-games","Modelling games: Cournot, Bertrand and the streetlamp","4.2"],["solution-concepts-pareto","Solution concepts and Pareto optimality","4.3"],["dominance","Dominance and the dominant strategy equilibrium","5.1"],["iesds","Iterated elimination of dominated strategies","5.2"],["dominance-applications","Dominance at work: Cournot, auctions and voting","5.3"],["quiz1-solved","Quiz 1 solved: all four questions","6.1"],["exam-revision","Exam revision: from school to research universities","6.2"],["cheatsheet","Cheatsheet","📄"],["interview-questions","Interview Questions","🎤"]],"c":[[0,"","What AI is and intelligent agents","The four approaches to AI, rational agents, PEAS, environment types, the social machine and the five kinds of agent program. · artificial intelligence · machine learning · deep learning · generative AI · history of AI · rational agent · PEAS · Turing test · environment types · reflex agent · agent program Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 1: Intelligence, agents and problems›What AI is Step 1 · Topic 1 of 2 · School and college See how AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI fit inside each other and how AI grew, what intelligence and AI mean, the four approaches to AI, what a social machine needs, and how every AI system can be described as an agent that senses its environment and acts. About 45 min9 lessons4 animations6 formulas10 worked problems15 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The big picture: AI, machine learning and deep learning 2What intelligence and AI mean 3A social machine: the man machine symbiosis scene 4Agents, environments and PEAS 5Kinds of environment and kinds of agent 6The maths behind a rational agent 7How the rational agent view compares 8From intuition to research 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[0,"family","The big picture: AI, machine learning and deep learning","Source: Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, Deep Learning (2016), figure 1.4, for the nested circles, and Russell and Norvig, AIMA 4th edition, section 1.3, for the history. The uploaded slides do not have this diagram, so this lesson follows those textbooks. People often use the words AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI as if they mean the same thing. They do not. They fit inside each other like circles, one inside the next. The biggest circle is AI. Machine learning is a part of AI. Deep learning is a part of machine learning. Generative AI, such as ChatGPT, is a part of deep learning. What it is Artificial intelligence (AI) is any method that makes a machine act intelligently. Machine learning (ML) is the part of AI where the program learns its rules from data. Deep learning (DL) is the part of ML that uses neural networks with many layers. Generative AI is the part of DL that creates new text, pictures or sound. Why we need it Knowing which circle a system sits in tells you how it was built, how much data it needs, and how far you can trust and explain it. It also stops a common mistake: thinking that AI means only neural networks. Most of this course is AI that does not learn from data at all. How it works Each inner circle answers the question \"where do the rules come from?\" in a narrower way. In plain AI, people write the rules or the search method. In ML, the rules are learned from examples. In DL, even the features (what to look at in the data) are learned, by many layers of a network. In generative AI, the learned network produces new content. AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AIEach Step draws one circle inside the last. At the end, the example you pick is placed in the smallest circle it belongs to. Step 0 of 5 Example to place Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this A spam filter learns from marked emails, so it lands in machine learning, inside AI, but outside deep learning. A route finder with A* learns nothing from data. It stays in the outer AI ring, like most of this course. Face recognition with a deep network lands in deep learning. It is inside ML and AI too. A chatbot on a large language model lands in the smallest circle, generative AI. AI with rules and search Machine learning Deep learning Generative AI Who writes the rules People Learned from data Learned from data Learned from data Who picks the features People Mostly people The network learns them The network learns them Data needed Little or none Hundreds to thousands of examples Often millions of examples Huge amounts of text or images Easy to explain Yes, you can follow each rule Often (trees, linear models) Hard Hard Example A* route finder, chess search, expert system Spam filter, house price model Face recognition, speech to text ChatGPT, image generators In this course Steps 1 to 8 Parts of steps 9 and 10 Advanced courses Advanced courses Remember Goodfellow and colleagues draw one more circle between ML and DL: representation learning, methods that learn their own features, such as a shallow autoencoder. Deep learning is representation learning with many layers."],[0,"family","How AI grew","AI did not grow in a straight line. It began with rules and search in the 1950s, went through two \"AI winters\" when promises failed and funding was cut, moved to machine learning in the 1990s, and to deep learning after 2012 when big data and fast graphics chips (GPUs) arrived. Step through the timeline to see each milestone and which circle it belongs to. How AI grew, from the Turing test to ChatGPTEach Step adds one milestone. The colour shows which circle of the AI family it belongs to. Step 0 of 17 From the year1950 To the year2022 Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this The whole story. Watch the colours move from AI with rules (teal), to machine learning (purple), to deep learning (orange) and generative AI (pink). The early years, 1950 to 1979: rules, search and the first AI winter. The deep learning years, 2000 to 2022. Worked example: place three systems in the circles A sudoku solver that uses backtracking and arc consistency (chapters 5.1 and 5.2). Nobody gives it data. People wrote the method. So it is AI, but not ML. A loan approval model that learns a decision tree from 10,000 past loans. It learns its rules from data, but people chose the features (income, age, debt). So it is ML, but not DL. A program that reads X-ray images with a 50 layer convolutional network. The network learns its own features from pixels. So it is DL, and therefore also ML and AI. AI ⊃ ML ⊃ DL ⊃ generative AI: every inner circle is a special case of the outer one Lesson 2 of 9"],[0,"idea","What intelligence and AI mean","Source: slide Set 1 (Introduction) and the class of 18 January 2026. Agents and environments follow Russell and Norvig, chapter 2. Think of the people around you. A friend sees you carrying heavy bags, guesses you need help, and holds the door. Nobody told them to. They noticed, understood, and acted. That everyday skill is what we call intelligence: taking in what is around you, making sense of it, and choosing a sensible action. Artificial intelligence (AI) is the study and building of machines that do the same: sense their surroundings, reason about them, and act well. The slides start with a simple question: what makes humans different from other living things? We plan, we imagine, we explain, we help each other, and we learn from very few examples. What it is A field that builds computer programs that perceive, reason, learn and act so as to do well at a task. Why we need it Many tasks are too big, too fast or too boring for people: finding a route through a city, sorting spam, diagnosing faults, playing chess, steering a robot. AI lets a machine make those choices by itself. How it works We describe the task as an agent inside an environment. The agent gets percepts through sensors, and its agent program picks an action for its actuators. AI methods (search, logic, probability, learning) are different ways to write that program."],[0,"idea","The four approaches to AI","Russell and Norvig sort definitions of AI along two questions: should the machine copy humans or be rational (do the right thing)? And should we judge its thinking or its acting? Like a human Rationally (the right thing) Thinking Thinking humanly: model how people think (cognitive science, brain studies). Thinking rationally: follow correct rules of reasoning (logic, the \"laws of thought\"). Acting Acting humanly: behave so that people cannot tell it is a machine (the Turing test). Acting rationally: choose the action expected to do best (the rational agent approach, used in this course). Remember In class the four boxes were explained like this: thinking humanly is how we think, thinking rationally is making correct decisions in our head, acting humanly is doing what we think, and acting rationally is working it out so that it leads to a real, measurable result. The Turing test (1950): a person chats by text with a hidden machine and a hidden human. If the person cannot reliably tell which is which, the machine passes. Turing also said we should not try to build an adult mind directly, but a baby machine that can learn, and then teach it. The lecture repeated this idea: study babies, not adults. Lesson 3 of 9"],[0,"social","A social machine: the man machine symbiosis scene","The slides show a scene with a person and a helpful robot. To behave well in that scene the robot needs much more than a fast processor. It needs: Skill What it means in plain words Example in the scene Social etiquette Knowing polite and expected behaviour Waiting for the person to finish speaking Intuitive psychology Sensing that someone is in trouble or worried, and wanting to help Noticing the person is struggling and offering a hand Awareness of its own limits (proprioception) Knowing what its own body can and cannot do Not trying to lift something too heavy Common sense Everyday facts everyone knows Glass breaks if dropped Domain knowledge Expert facts about the task How a machine is repaired Intuitive physics A feel for how objects move and fall A ball rolls down a slope The slides then list the layers of behaviour such a machine shows, which come from Marvin Minsky's Society of Mind (1986): instinctive (built in reactions), learned (habits from experience), deliberative (thinking through options), reflective (thinking about its own thinking), self reflective (thinking about itself as a person with goals), and self conscious (caring about its values, such as \"scoring a good deed point\"). Real world scenario: a delivery robot in an office A delivery robot meets a person carrying a heavy box near a closed door. An instinctive layer stops it from bumping into them. A learned layer knows doors in this building open outward. A deliberative layer plans: put down my parcel, open the door, wait. A reflective layer notices \"last time I blocked the corridor, so stand to the side\". The lecture said today’s agentic AI is good at the learned, deliberative and reflective layers, while self-reflection and self-consciousness are still research. The slide draws these layers stacked one on top of the other, with innate urges and drives pushing up from below and values, censors, ideals and taboos pressing down from above. Step through it as a pyramid below. The base is the oldest and simplest kind of thinking, and each layer up looks further ahead. The questions slide asks what thoughts are, how we build a model of the world, and what makes curiosity and creativity work. These are open research questions. This course focuses on the parts we can already build well: search, reasoning, planning, probability and learning. Lesson 4 of 9"],[0,"agents","Agents, environments and PEAS","An agent is anything that senses its environment and acts on it. A person, a thermostat, a chess program and a self driving car are all agents. Agent functionExam favourite f : P* → A Maps every percept sequence (everything the agent has sensed so far, P*) to an action in the set A. It is the mathematical description of behaviour. Agent program agent = architecture + program The program runs on the physical machine (the architecture) and computes the agent function one percept at a time. Rational agentExam favourite choose a* = argmaxa E[performance | percepts so far, a] For every percept sequence, pick the action that is expected to give the best performance measure, given what the agent knows. Rational is not the same as all knowing. A rational agent does the best it can with the information it has. If it crosses the road after looking both ways and is hit by a falling plane, it was still rational. Rational agents also gather information (look before crossing) and learn from experience."],[0,"agents","PEAS: describing a task","Before building an agent, write down its Performance measure, Environment, Actuators and Sensors. Agent Performance Environment Actuators Sensors Self driving taxi Safe, fast, legal, comfortable, profit Roads, traffic, people, weather Steering, brake, accelerator, horn, display Cameras, radar, GPS, speedometer Medical diagnosis Healthy patient, low cost Patient, hospital, staff Questions, tests, treatments Symptoms, test results, answers Spam filter Spam caught, real mail kept Email stream, users Move to spam, deliver Words, sender, links Vacuum robot Clean squares over time, little energy Two squares A and B with dirt Left, Right, Suck, NoOp Location sensor, dirt sensor Lesson 5 of 9"],[0,"envtypes","Kinds of environment and kinds of agent","The environment decides how hard the job is. Ask six questions about it. Question Easy answer Hard answer Example of the hard case Can the agent see everything that matters? Fully observable Partially observable Poker: you cannot see the other cards Is the next state fixed by the action? Deterministic Stochastic (random) A dice game, a slippery floor Does each decision stand alone? Episodic Sequential Chess: a move now matters later Does the world change while the agent thinks? Static Dynamic Taxi driving Are states and times countable steps? Discrete Continuous Steering angle of a car How many agents? Single agent Multi agent (competitive or cooperative) Chess (competitive), football team (cooperative) Task Observable Deterministic Episodic Static Discrete Agents Crossword puzzle Fully Yes Sequential Static Discrete Single Chess with a clock Fully Yes Sequential Semi Discrete Multi Taxi driving Partially Stochastic Sequential Dynamic Continuous Multi Image classification Fully Yes Episodic Static Continuous Single"],[0,"envtypes","Four kinds of agent program, from simple to clever","From simple to clever Simple reflex agent: if this, then that. It looks only at the current percept. Fast, but it fails when it cannot see the whole state. Model based reflex agent: keeps an internal state (a model of the world it cannot see right now), updated from each percept. Goal based agent: knows what it wants to reach, and searches or plans a sequence of actions to get there. This is where search (chapters 1.2 to 4.2) comes in. Utility based agent: when many outcomes reach the goal, prefers the ones with higher utility (happiness). It handles trade offs and uncertainty. Learning agent: any of the above, plus a part that improves the program from feedback (a critic and a learning element). Reinforcement learning, chapter 10, is one way. Common mistake A table driven agent stores one action for every possible percept sequence. With 4 percepts and a lifetime of T steps it needs 4 + 4² + ... + 4T rows. For a taxi camera this number is larger than the atoms in the universe. Agent programs exist to produce good behaviour from a small program instead of a huge table. Lesson 6 of 9"],[0,"maths","The maths behind a rational agent","Two small formulas sit under the whole course. Performance over a lifetime U = ∑t=1T rt Add up the score rt earned at each time step t. In the vacuum world rt = number of clean squares (minus 1 per move if moving costs energy). Expected performance of an actionExam favourite E[U | a] = ∑s′ P(s′ | a) U(s′) Weight each possible result s′ by its probability. A rational agent picks the action with the largest value. Size of a table driven agentExam favourite rows = ∑t=1T |P|t = |P|(|P|T − 1) ⁄ (|P| − 1) |P| is the number of different percepts, T the lifetime. A geometric series, so it grows exponentially in T. Worked example: which action is rational? A delivery robot can take the lift or the stairs. The lift arrives in time with probability 0.7 and then scores 10. If it is late it scores 2. The stairs always arrive, but slowly, and score 7. E[lift] = 0.7 × 10 + 0.3 × 2 = 7 + 0.6 = 7.6 E[stairs] = 1 × 7 = 7 The rational choice is the lift, 7.6 against 7, even though the lift is sometimes late. Rational means best on average with what you know, not lucky every time. Worked example: vacuum score Both squares dirty, agent in A, simple reflex agent, 4 time steps, no move cost. Time 1: Suck in A. A is clean, B dirty. Score +1, total 1. Time 2: Right. Still 1 clean square. Total 2. Time 3: Suck in B. Both clean. +2, total 4. Time 4: Left. Both clean, +2. Total = 6 Lesson 7 of 9"],[0,"compare","How the rational agent view compares","Four ways to define AI Approach Strength Weakness Used today for Thinking humanly Explains human minds Hard to test, we do not fully know how people think Cognitive science, user models Thinking rationally Exact, provable logic Not everything is certain, and logic alone can be slow Theorem provers, verification Acting humanly Clear test (Turing test) Copies human mistakes too, says little about how Chatbots, games Acting rationally Measurable, general, works with uncertainty Needs a good performance measure Almost all modern AI systems Bottom line: acting rationally is the most useful definition for engineering, because we can measure it and improve it. That is why this course builds rational agents. Kinds of agent program Agent Memory Looks ahead? Good when Table driven Huge table No Never in practice, only as a thought experiment Simple reflex None No Fully observable, simple rules Model based Internal state No Partially observable worlds Goal based State + goal Yes, by search or planning Many steps are needed to reach a goal Utility based State + utility Yes, weighs trade offs Several goals, risk, uncertainty Learning Changes itself Depends on the base agent The world is unknown or changing Bottom line: move up the list only when the task needs it. A thermostat is a fine simple reflex agent. A taxi needs a learning, utility based agent. Lesson 8 of 9"],[0,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths An AI is a machine that notices what is going on and picks a good thing to do, like a helpful friend who sees you need help and opens the door. An agent maps percept sequences to actions. Describe the task with PEAS and the environment with its six properties. Pick the simplest agent program (reflex, model based, goal based, utility based, learning) that works. A rational agent maximises expected performance given its percept history and prior knowledge. Partial observability forces the agent to keep a belief state. Sequential, stochastic tasks lead to Markov decision processes (chapter 10.1). Bounded rationality (Simon) asks what is optimal when computing itself is costly. Open questions include how to specify the performance measure so that it matches what people really want (the value alignment problem), and how common sense and the reflective layers of the slides can be learned rather than hand written. Going deeper: why rationality is defined for each percept sequence Rationality depends on four things: the performance measure, the agent's prior knowledge, the actions it can take, and the percept sequence so far. Two agents with the same actions can both be rational yet act differently, because one knows more. This is why we say \"rational given what it knows\", and why gathering information can itself be the rational action. Going deeper: the research view: from Turing to large language models AI as a field began at the Dartmouth workshop in 1956. Early systems used logic and search (1956 to 1970s), then expert systems (1980s), then probability and machine learning (1990s and 2000s), then deep learning (2012 onward) and large language models (2020s). Each wave added a new way to write the agent program. The agent view of this chapter covers all of them. In the exam A rational agent is not one that is always right. It is one that chooses the best action given what it knows. Bad luck does not make a choice irrational. Lesson 9 of 9"],[0,"recap","Chapter summary","AI ⊃ machine learning ⊃ deep learning ⊃ generative AI. AI grew from rules and search (1950s), through two AI winters, to learning from data (1990s) and deep networks (2012 on). Intelligence means sensing, understanding and choosing a good action. AI builds machines that do this. Minsky’s six layers run from instinctive reactions at the base to self-conscious emotion at the top. Today’s agentic AI handles the learned, deliberative and reflective layers. Four approaches: thinking or acting, humanly or rationally. This course builds rational agents: act to get the best expected performance. An agent maps percept sequences to actions. Describe a task with PEAS and the environment by observability, determinism, episodes, change, discreteness and number of agents. Agent programs grow from simple reflex to model based, goal based, utility based and learning agents. A table driven agent is impossible in practice because it needs ∑ |P|t rows. A social machine needs etiquette, intuitive psychology, common sense and layers from instinctive to self conscious behaviour (Minsky). Next: chapter 1.2 turns a goal into a search problem, the first tool a goal based agent needs. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun the vacuum agent, count table sizes, and write your own reflex agent and table size function.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[1,"","Problems as search","States, actions, start and goal, nodes and the frontier, tree against graph search, and the four properties of search. · search problem · state space · successor function · node · frontier · graph search · water jugs · Romania Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 1: Intelligence, agents and problems›Problems as search Step 1 · Topic 2 of 2 · School and college Turn a goal into a search problem: states, actions, a start and a goal test. Learn what a node, the frontier and the explored set are, and the four properties used to judge every search algorithm. About 45 min8 lessons2 animations8 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why search, and what a search problem is 2The formalism: states, actions, start, goal 3Inputs, outputs and nodes of a search algorithm 4The selection rule and the four properties 5The maths behind search problems 6How problem formulation choices compare 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[1,"idea","Why search, and what a search problem is","Source: slide Set 2 (Searching Techniques), slides 1 to 24, and the class of 24 January 2026. You are on holiday in Edinburgh and your flight leaves from London tomorrow. You think: \"If I take the 6 am train, where will I be at 2 pm? Will I still reach the airport on time?\" This is hypothetical reasoning: you imagine what state you will be in after some actions, and look for a sequence of actions that brings you to a state you want. Search is the computer's way of doing exactly this. What it is A general method that tries out sequences of actions, on paper, to find one that turns the start state into a goal state. Why we need it Many problems have no special formula, but they can be written as states and actions. Search then solves them all with one method. It is the hidden engine inside route finders, puzzle solvers, game players and planners. How it works Write the problem as a state space with actions, a start and a goal test. Keep a list of paths still to explore (the frontier, also called OPEN). Repeatedly take a path out, check if it ends at a goal, and if not, extend it by every action."],[1,"idea","Why search works well, and where it stops","Why search Limits of search Game playing programs based on search beat world champions. Search does not tell you how to formulate the problem. Choosing the states and actions is up to you. Many problems have no special algorithm. Casting them as search is often the easiest way. Search only solves a problem once it is correctly formulated. Local search gives good approximations for huge optimisation problems. Without good knowledge (heuristics) it can be very slow. Problem specific heuristics let search use extra knowledge, and planning can be cast as search. Real world scenario: a teacher in class In class the professor described her own lecture as a search problem. The start state is \"I have just said something and five of you look confused\". The goal state is \"everybody understands\". Each transition is a new example or a new explanation. The heuristic is her sense of which example will help most, and a constraint is the time left in the class. Because students keep asking questions, the next state is never fully known in advance, so she keeps choosing again. That is a search through states, done live. Lesson 2 of 8"],[1,"formal","The formalism: states, actions, start, goal","To turn a problem into a search problem, write down four things. Part Meaning Romania trip Water jugs State space All configurations the problem can be in, kept as computer data The city you are in Pairs (gal3, gal4), gallons in each jug Actions (transitions) Moves from one state to another Drive to a neighbouring city Fill, empty, or pour one jug into the other Initial state and goal Where you start and the condition you want In Arad, goal: be in Bucharest Start (0, 0), goal e.g. (0, 2) or (*, 3) Heuristics Hints that guide the search Straight line distance to Bucharest How far each jug is from the target amount Abstraction means leaving out details that do not matter. For Romania we ignore the radio, the weather and where exactly on the road the car is. We keep only the city. A good abstraction keeps what you need to find a valid plan and nothing more. Observations from the slides about the jugs: from whole numbers you only ever reach whole numbers. Some states, for example (1, 2), can never be reached from (0, 0). Some actions do not change the state at all, for example emptying an empty jug. Of the 4 × 5 = 20 possible pairs, only 14 can be reached from (0, 0). Remember The goal can be a condition, not one state. \"(*, 3)\" means any state where the 4 gallon jug holds 3, whatever the 3 gallon jug holds. The goal test is a function that returns true or false. Lesson 3 of 8"],[1,"inout","Inputs, outputs and nodes of a search algorithm","Every search algorithm in this course gets the same inputs and returns the same kind of answer. Successor functionExam favourite S(x) = {⟨y, a⟩ : action a takes x to y} All states one action away from x, each tagged with the action used. Two different actions may reach the same state. Action cost C(x, a, y) ≥ ε > 0 The cost of going from x to y with action a. C = ∞ if a does not lead from x to y. Every cost is at least a small positive ε. Path costExam favourite g(n) = ∑i C(si, ai, si+1) Add the costs along the path from the start to node n. The output is a sequence of states (or actions) from the start to a state that passes the goal test. Some algorithms promise the cheapest path, some the shortest, some promise nothing."],[1,"inout","A node is a path","A node is a bookkeeping record for one path. It has four fields: n.STATE (the state it ends in), n.PARENT (the node it came from), n.ACTION (the action that made it) and n.PATH-COST, written g(n). Following PARENT links back to the start rebuilds the whole path. The search template (slide 18) function TREE-SEARCH(problem): frontier = { node(initial state) } loop: if frontier is empty: return failure n = take a node out of the frontier # the selection rule if GOAL-TEST(n.STATE): return the path of n add every child of n to the frontier function GRAPH-SEARCH(problem): same, but keep an explored set: add n.STATE to explored after taking n out, and add a child only if its state is not in the frontier or explored Common mistake Cycles can cause non termination. Arad → Sibiu → Arad → Sibiu ... is a valid path in tree search, so a search may never stop. Graph search fixes this by remembering explored states. Lesson 4 of 8"],[1,"props","The selection rule and the four properties","Every search algorithm in chapters 2 to 4 follows the template above. They differ in one thing only: which node they take out of the frontier next (the selection rule). This choice decides whether a solution is found, how much it costs, and the time and memory needed. Property Question it answers Completeness If a solution exists, will the algorithm always find one? Optimality Will it find the cheapest solution (when actions have costs)? Time complexity How many nodes does it generate before it finds a solution? Space complexity What is the largest number of nodes it must keep in memory? Branching factor and depth b = most children of any node, d = depth of the shallowest goal, m = largest depth These three numbers measure every algorithm. Nodes in a full treeExam favourite 1 + b + b² + ... + bd = (bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1) = O(bd) The number of nodes up to depth d. It grows exponentially with d. Worked example: how big is a search tree? Take b = 10 and d = 5. 1 + 10 + 100 + 1,000 + 10,000 + 100,000 = 111,111 nodes At 1 million nodes a second this takes about 0.1 seconds. With d = 10 it is about 1.1 × 10¹⁰ nodes, which takes about 3 hours. One more level multiplies the work by b."],[1,"props","Food for thought from the slides","Try to write each of these as a search problem with states, actions, start and goal: making a cup of tea, buying a book online, picking an example to explain a doubt in class, and a doctor's differential diagnosis. For tea, a state could be (water boiled?, tea in cup?, milk added?, sugar added?), and actions boil, add tea, add milk, add sugar, stir. Lesson 5 of 8"],[1,"maths","The maths behind search problems","Size of a state space |S| = product of the number of values of each part of the state Jugs: 4 values for gal3 (0 to 3) × 5 for gal4 (0 to 4) = 20 states, of which 14 are reachable from (0, 0). 8 puzzle reachable statesExam favourite 9! ⁄ 2 = 181,440 Nine tiles (with the blank) in 9! orders, but only half can be reached from a given start, because a move never changes the parity of the arrangement. Paths are much bigger than statesExam favourite tree nodes up to depth d ≈ bd while states may be few In a grid with 4 moves, a tree search to depth d makes 4d paths but only about 2d² distinct cells are within d steps. This is why graph search remembers explored states. Worked example: why repeated states matter in a grid Robot on an open grid, 4 moves: up, down, left, right. b = 4. Tree search to depth 10: 1 + 4 + ... + 4¹⁰ = (4¹¹ − 1) ⁄ 3 = 1,398,101 nodes. Cells within 10 steps (a diamond): 2 × 10² + 2 × 10 + 1 = 221. Graph search touches at most 221 states, about 6,000 times fewer Lesson 6 of 8"],[1,"compare","How problem formulation choices compare","Tree search against graph search Tree search Graph search Remembers explored states? No Yes (explored set) Can loop for ever on cycles? Yes No, on a finite state space Memory Only the frontier Frontier plus every explored state Best when The space is a true tree, or memory is very tight Many paths lead to the same state (maps, puzzles) Bottom line: use graph search when states repeat, which is almost always. Use tree search with a path check when memory is the problem (as in depth first search, chapter 2.2). Good and bad formulations of 8 queens Formulation States Comment Add one queen at a time anywhere 64 × 63 × ... × 57 ≈ 1.8 × 10¹⁴ sequences Very wasteful Add one queen per column, in a row not attacked 2,057 states Same problem, tiny space Complete state: 8 queens, one per column, move a queen in its column 8⁸ ≈ 16.8 million states Used by local search (chapter 4.1, 5.3) Bottom line: the same puzzle can be easy or hard depending on how you choose states and actions. Formulation is the most important step, and search cannot do it for you. Lesson 7 of 8"],[1,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Search is trying out moves in your head before you make them, like planning a train trip by checking which trains connect. A search problem has a state space, actions with costs, a start and a goal test. Nodes are paths with STATE, PARENT, ACTION and PATH-COST. Algorithms differ only in the selection rule, and are judged on completeness, optimality, time and space in terms of b, d and m. Graph search removes repeated states but needs memory for the explored set. The size of the reachable state space, not the tree, then bounds the work. Choosing the abstraction (what to keep in a state) can shrink a problem by many orders of magnitude. Automatically finding good abstractions and problem formulations is still open. Modern planners learn state representations and action models from data, and large language models are used to propose formulations that a classical search then solves exactly. Going deeper: why the 8 puzzle has exactly 9! ⁄ 2 reachable states Read the tiles in row order, ignoring the blank, and count inversions (pairs in the wrong order). A sideways move does not change this order. An up or down move jumps a tile over two others, which changes the number of inversions by 0 or ±2. So the parity of the inversion count never changes. Half of the 9! arrangements have each parity, and every arrangement with the right parity can be reached, so exactly 9! ⁄ 2 = 181,440 states are reachable. In the exam Exam questions often ask you to formulate a problem. Always list all four parts: states, actions (with costs), initial state, goal test. Say how big the state space is. Lesson 8 of 8"],[1,"recap","Chapter summary","Search is hypothetical reasoning: imagine sequences of actions and look for one that reaches the goal. A search problem has a state space, actions with costs C(x, a, y) ≥ ε > 0, an initial state and a goal test. Heuristics are optional hints. A node is a path: STATE, PARENT, ACTION, PATH-COST g(n). The frontier (OPEN) holds paths waiting to be extended. Tree search can loop on cycles. Graph search keeps an explored set and never expands a state twice. Algorithms differ only in the selection rule. Judge them by completeness, optimality, time and space, using b, d and m. A full tree to depth d has (bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1) nodes. Next: chapter 2.1 gives the first two selection rules: breadth first and uniform cost search. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterGenerate water jug successors, rebuild paths from nodes, and code your own successor function and path cost.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[2,"","Breadth first and uniform cost search","BFS with a FIFO queue, UCS with a priority queue on g(n), when each is optimal, their cost, and why UCS tests the goal late. · breadth first search · BFS · uniform cost search · UCS · Dijkstra · priority queue · optimality proof Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 2: Uninformed search›BFS and UCS Step 2 · Topic 1 of 2 · College Breadth first search explores level by level and finds the fewest actions. Uniform cost search explores in order of cost and finds the cheapest path. Learn both, their costs, and the three lemma proof that UCS is optimal. About 50 min8 lessons2 animations7 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Uninformed search and breadth first search 2Is BFS complete and optimal, and what does it cost 3Uniform cost search 4Why uniform cost search is optimal: the three lemmas 5The maths: geometric series and the cost bound 6BFS against UCS against Dijkstra 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[2,"idea","Uninformed search and breadth first search","Source: slide Set 2, slides 25 to 31, and the classes of 24 and 25 January 2026. Uninformed (or blind) search uses a fixed rule to choose the next node. The rule is the same for every problem and uses no knowledge about where the goal is. The five uninformed methods of the course are breadth first, uniform cost, depth first, depth limited and iterative deepening search. What it is Breadth first search (BFS) expands the shallowest node first. It looks at every path of length 1, then every path of length 2, and so on, level by level. Why we need it When every action costs the same, the shallowest goal is also the cheapest. BFS is guaranteed to find it, and it never gets lost down one long branch. How it works Keep the frontier as a first in, first out queue. Take the oldest path out, and add its children at the back. Because children always go to the back, all short paths are taken out before any longer one."],[2,"idea","BFS on the water jugs, as on slide 27","Start (0, 0), goal (*, 2). The frontier after each expansion (newly added paths at the end): Step OPEN 0 ⟨(0,0)⟩ 1 ⟨(0,0),(3,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4)⟩ 2 ⟨(0,0),(0,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(3,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,3)⟩ 3 ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(3,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,3)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4),(0,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4),(3,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4),(3,1)⟩ The slide shows tree search, so (0, 0) appears again as a child. Our code below uses graph search and skips states already seen. Graph search, one step at a timeOrange ring: in the frontier. Violet: being expanded now. Grey: explored. Green: the solution path. Step 0 of 8 Algorithm Problem Start Goal Depth limit (depth limited) or largest limit (iterative deepening)3 Jug A holds3 Jug B holds4 Goal: either jug holds exactly2 Roads, one per line: from to cost Heuristic h, one per line: place value Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this BFS from Arad to Bucharest. It finds Arad, Sibiu, Fagaras, Bucharest with cost 450, the path with the fewest roads, exactly as on the slides. Switch to uniform cost search. It keeps going after it first sees Bucharest (450) and finds the cheaper route through Rimnicu Vilcea and Pitesti, 418. Try A*. It reaches the same 418 but expands far fewer cities, because h pulls it toward Bucharest. Greedy best first on Romania: fast but it returns 450, not 418. The greedy trap from slide 58. Greedy runs to n3 because h(n3) = 10 looks close, and pays 110. A* pays 30. Depth first on the slide tree with goal M. Watch it dive down the left side first: A B D H I E J K C F L M. Iterative deepening on the same tree. Each iteration starts again from A with a bigger limit, and M is found at limit 3. The water jug puzzle with jugs of 3 and 4. Breadth first finds the shortest way to get exactly 2 litres. Python # Breadth first search on the water jug problem (jugs of 3 and 4 gallons).# Goal: either jug holds exactly 2 gallons. The frontier is a plain list used as a queue.def successors(state): a, b = state pour_ab = min(a, 4 - b) pour_ba = min(b, 3 - a)return [(3, b), (a, 4), (0, b), (a, 0), (a - pour_ab, b + pour_ab), (a + pour_ba, b - pour_ba)]def bfs(start): frontier = [[start]] # a list of paths, oldest first seen = [start]whilelen(frontier) > 0: path = frontier.pop(0) # take the OLDEST path (first in, first out)for child in successors(path[-1]):if child notin seen: new_path = path + [child]if2in child: # goal test when the child is generatedreturn new_path seen.append(child) frontier.append(new_path)returnNoneanswer = bfs((0, 0))print(\"path:\", answer)print(\"actions needed:\", len(answer) - 1) Output path: [(0, 0), (3, 0), (0, 3), (3, 3), (2, 4)] actions needed: 4 Breadth first search (graph version) function BFS(problem): node = start node if GOAL-TEST(node.STATE): return node frontier = FIFO queue with node reached = { start state } while frontier is not empty: node = POP the oldest node for each child of node: if child.STATE not in reached: if GOAL-TEST(child.STATE): return child # early goal test add child.STATE to reached push child at the back of frontier return failure Lesson 2 of 8"],[2,"bfsprops","Is BFS complete and optimal, and what does it cost","Complete: yes, if b is finite. The length of the path taken out of OPEN never goes down, because each expanded path is replaced by paths one step longer. So all paths of length d are examined before any of length d + 1, and a goal at depth d is found. Optimal: it finds the solution with the fewest actions. That is the cheapest only if every action costs the same. On Romania, BFS returns Arad, Sibiu, Fagaras, Bucharest (cost 140 + 99 + 211 = 450), but the cheapest route is Arad, Sibiu, Rimnicu Vilcea, Pitesti, Bucharest (140 + 80 + 97 + 101 = 418). Time of BFSExam favourite 1 + b + b² + ... + bd = O(bd) With the goal test when a node is generated. If you test only when a node is taken out, the last level is generated too and the time is O(bd+1). Space of BFSExam favourite O(bd−1) explored + O(bd) frontier = O(bd) Every node stays in memory. Memory, not time, is the real limit of BFS. Depth d Nodes (b = 10) Time at 1 million nodes a second Memory at 1 KB a node 2 110 0.11 ms 107 KB 4 11,110 11 ms 10.6 MB 6 10⁶ 1.1 s 1 GB 8 10⁸ 2 minutes 103 GB 10 10¹⁰ 3 hours 10 TB 12 10¹² 13 days 1 PB 14 10¹⁴ 3.5 years 99 PB Common mistake Two lessons from the table. Memory runs out long before time does. And no amount of faster hardware saves you: each extra level multiplies the cost by b. Worked example: goal test at generation against at expansion b = 2, goal at depth d = 3, and the goal is the last node of its level. Test at generation: we stop when the goal is generated, after 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 nodes at most. Test at expansion: every node of depth 3 is expanded before the goal is popped, so depth 4 is generated too: up to 15 + 16 = 31 nodes. early test: O(bd) late test: O(bd+1) Lesson 3 of 8"],[2,"ucs","Uniform cost search","Source: slide Set 2, slides 32 to 35. What it is Uniform cost search (UCS) always expands the path with the lowest path cost g(n). It is breadth first search ordered by cost instead of by depth. Why we need it Real actions have different costs. Roads have different lengths, flights have different prices. BFS ignores costs. UCS returns the cheapest path. How it works Keep the frontier as a priority queue ordered by g(n). Take out the cheapest path. Do the goal test only when a path is taken out, because a cheaper path to the goal may still be waiting. If a cheaper path to a state already in the frontier appears, replace the old one."],[2,"ucs","The Sibiu example of slide 34","Uniform cost search from Sibiu to Bucharest Expand Sibiu. Its children Rimnicu Vilcea (80) and Fagaras (99) go into the frontier. The cheapest is Rimnicu Vilcea (80). Expand it and add Pitesti with cost 80 + 97 = 177. The cheapest is now Fagaras (99). Expand it and add Bucharest with cost 99 + 211 = 310. A goal is in the frontier, but UCS does not stop. It takes out the cheapest, Pitesti (177), and finds a second path to Bucharest with cost 177 + 101 = 278. 278 is less than 310, so the old Bucharest path is discarded. Bucharest (278) is taken out next, so the answer is Sibiu, Rimnicu Vilcea, Pitesti, Bucharest, cost 278 From Arad, UCS takes cities out in this order of cost: Arad 0, Zerind 75, Timisoara 118, Sibiu 140, Oradea 146, Rimnicu Vilcea 220, Lugoj 229, Fagaras 239, Mehadia 299, Pitesti 317, Craiova 366, Drobeta 374 and finally Bucharest 418. It spreads out in all directions like a growing circle of equal cost, because it has no idea where Bucharest is. Complete and optimal if every action cost C(x, a, y) ≥ ε > 0 With zero cost actions UCS can loop for ever on a zero cost cycle. Time and space of UCSExam favourite O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) C* is the cost of the optimal solution and ε the smallest action cost. The search may go about C*⁄ε levels deep. When all costs are equal this is bd+1. Lesson 4 of 8"],[2,"proof","Why uniform cost search is optimal: the three lemmas","Source: slide Set 2, slides 36 to 38. Let c(n) be the cost of the path of node n. Assume every action costs at least ε > 0. Lemma 1: costs taken out never go down Claim: if n2 is expanded immediately after n1, then c(n1) ≤ c(n2). Case 1, n2 was already on OPEN when n1 was chosen. Then c(n1) ≤ c(n2), or else UCS would have chosen n2. Case 2, n2 was added when n1 was expanded. Then n2 extends n1 by one action, so c(n2) ≥ c(n1) + ε > c(n1). either way c(n1) ≤ c(n2) Lemma 2: every cheaper path is expanded first Claim: when n is expanded, every path with cost strictly less than c(n) has already been expanded. Take such a path nk = ⟨Start, s1, ..., sk⟩ with c(nk) < c(n), and its prefixes n0, n1, ..., nk. Let ni be the last prefix already expanded. Then ni+1 is on OPEN, and c(ni+1) ≤ c(nk) < c(n), since a prefix costs no more than the whole path. Then UCS would have expanded ni+1 instead of n, a contradiction. So the whole path nk was already expanded. Lemma 3: the first path to a state is the cheapest Claim: the first time UCS expands a node n ending at state S, it has found a cheapest path to S. By Lemma 2, all cheaper paths were already expanded, and none of them ended at S. By Lemma 1, every path expanded later costs at least c(n). so the first goal path taken out is optimal In the exam This is why UCS tests the goal when a path is taken OUT, not when it is generated. In the Sibiu example, Bucharest was generated with cost 310 before the 278 path existed. Testing at generation would have returned the wrong answer. Lesson 5 of 8"],[2,"maths","The maths: geometric series and the cost bound","Geometric seriesExam favourite ∑i=0d bi = (bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1) ≤ bd · b ⁄ (b − 1) The last level holds most of the nodes. For b = 10 it is about 90 percent of them. Fraction of nodes in the last level bd ⁄ ((bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1)) → (b − 1) ⁄ b For b = 2 half the nodes are in the last level. For b = 10, 90 percent. UCS depth bound depth ≤ ⌊C* ⁄ ε⌋ A path of more than C*⁄ε actions costs more than C*, so UCS never needs to expand it before the goal. Worked example: the UCS bound All road lengths are at least ε = 70 (the shortest Romania road is 70, Lugoj to Mehadia). The optimal cost is C* = 418. ⌊418 ⁄ 70⌋ = 5. A path of 6 roads costs at least 6 × 70 = 420 > 418, so UCS never expands a path with more than 5 roads. With b = 4 it generates at most about 41 + 5 = 4,096 nodes. the real search expands only 13 cities, so the bound is loose but safe Worked example: BFS memory b = 10, d = 8, and each node needs 1 KB. Nodes up to depth 8: (10⁹ − 1) ⁄ 9 ≈ 1.11 × 10⁸. memory ≈ 1.11 × 10⁸ KB ≈ 111 GB Lesson 6 of 8"],[2,"compare","BFS against UCS against Dijkstra","Breadth first against uniform cost Breadth first Uniform cost Frontier FIFO queue Priority queue on g(n) Finds Fewest actions Lowest total cost Goal test At generation (saves a level) At expansion (needed for optimality) Complete Yes, if b is finite Yes, if every cost ≥ ε > 0 Time and space O(bd) O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) Romania answer 450 (3 roads) 418 (4 roads) Bottom line: with equal costs the two find the same answer and BFS is a little cheaper. With different costs only UCS is optimal. Uniform cost search and Dijkstra's algorithm UCS Dijkstra Stops When the goal is taken out After the cheapest path to every node is known Graph given Implicitly, by a successor function Usually explicitly, all nodes known Frontier starts with Only the start Often every node at ∞ Bottom line: UCS is Dijkstra's algorithm with an early stop and lazy generation of the graph. That matters when the graph is huge or infinite, like a puzzle. Real world scenario: a delivery app A food delivery app finds the fastest route from the restaurant to you. Road segments take different times, so it cannot use BFS. A plain UCS from the restaurant spreads out in every direction. Real apps add a heuristic (chapter 3.1) to point the search toward you, and precompute shortcuts, so they answer in milliseconds on a country sized map. Lesson 7 of 8"],[2,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths BFS is like ripples in a pond: it checks everything one step away, then two steps away. UCS is the same but the ripples are measured in cost, like kilometres, not in steps. BFS uses a FIFO queue, is complete and optimal for equal costs, and costs O(bd) time and space. UCS uses a priority queue on g(n), tests the goal at expansion, and is complete and optimal when costs are at least ε, with cost O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋). The proof of UCS optimality rests on one fact: the g values taken out of the queue never decrease. The same argument proves Dijkstra correct, and A* with a consistent heuristic (chapter 3.1) is UCS on modified costs C'(x, y) = C(x, y) − h(x) + h(y). Bidirectional and frontier search variants cut memory by not storing the explored set. Research on very large graphs uses external memory BFS (on disk), parallel BFS on GPUs (the Graph500 benchmark), and bucket based priority queues (Dial's algorithm) when costs are small integers. Going deeper: why A* with a consistent h is a special case of UCS Define new costs C'(x, a, y) = C(x, a, y) − h(x) + h(y). Consistency h(x) ≤ C(x, a, y) + h(y) makes every C' ≥ 0. Along a path from s to n the new costs telescope: g'(n) = g(n) − h(s) + h(n) = f(n) − h(s). So ordering by g' is ordering by f = g + h, and UCS on C' expands exactly what A* expands on C. The three lemmas above then prove A* optimal for free. Lesson 8 of 8"],[2,"recap","Chapter summary","Uninformed search uses a fixed selection rule and no knowledge about the goal. BFS: FIFO queue, level by level. Complete for finite b. Finds the fewest actions, which is cheapest only when all costs are equal. Time and space O(bd). Memory is the real limit. Test the goal when a node is generated in BFS (saves a whole level), but when it is taken out in UCS. UCS: priority queue on g(n). Complete and optimal when every cost is at least ε > 0. Cost O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋). The proof: costs taken out never decrease (Lemma 1), so every cheaper path is expanded first (Lemma 2), so the first path to a state is the cheapest (Lemma 3). On Romania, BFS gives 450 with 3 roads and UCS gives 418 with 4 roads. Next: chapter 2.2 trades optimality for memory with depth first, depth limited, iterative deepening and bidirectional search. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun BFS on the water jugs and UCS on the Sibiu map, then write your own shortest path and cheapest cost functions.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[3,"","Depth first, iterative deepening and bidirectional search","DFS, DLS, IDS and bidirectional search, their memory and time, path checking, and the full comparison table. · depth first search · DFS · depth limited search · iterative deepening · IDS · bidirectional search · path checking Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 2: Uninformed search›DFS, DLS and IDS Step 2 · Topic 2 of 2 · College Depth first search saves memory by going deep first. Depth limited search stops endless dives, iterative deepening removes the need to guess the limit, and bidirectional search meets in the middle. About 50 min8 lessons2 animations4 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Depth first search 2Path checking and cycle checking 3Depth limited and iterative deepening search 4Is iterative deepening wasteful 5Bidirectional search 6All five uninformed methods side by side 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[3,"dfs","Depth first search","Source: slide Set 2, slides 40 to 44, and the class of 25 January 2026. What it is Depth first search (DFS) always expands the deepest node in the frontier. It goes down one branch as far as it can, and only when it hits a dead end does it go back and try the next branch. Why we need it BFS keeps whole levels in memory. DFS keeps only the current path and the untried brothers of its nodes, so it needs very little memory. It also matches how people solve mazes and puzzles: try a way, and back up when stuck. How it works Keep the frontier as a stack (last in, first out), or write it as a recursive function. Take out the newest path, and push its children on top. The nodes left on the stack are the backtrack points. On slide 43 DFS on the water jugs goes (0,0) → (3,0) → (0,0) → (3,0) → ... for ever in tree search, because Fill-3 then Empty-3 makes a cycle. The other children, (0,4), (3,4) and (0,3), wait on OPEN as backtrack points. This is why DFS needs a cycle check. Graph search, one step at a timeOrange ring: in the frontier. Violet: being expanded now. Grey: explored. Green: the solution path. Step 0 of 13 Algorithm Problem Start Goal Depth limit (depth limited) or largest limit (iterative deepening)3 Jug A holds3 Jug B holds4 Goal: either jug holds exactly2 Roads, one per line: from to cost Heuristic h, one per line: place value Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this BFS from Arad to Bucharest. It finds Arad, Sibiu, Fagaras, Bucharest with cost 450, the path with the fewest roads, exactly as on the slides. Switch to uniform cost search. It keeps going after it first sees Bucharest (450) and finds the cheaper route through Rimnicu Vilcea and Pitesti, 418. Try A*. It reaches the same 418 but expands far fewer cities, because h pulls it toward Bucharest. Greedy best first on Romania: fast but it returns 450, not 418. The greedy trap from slide 58. Greedy runs to n3 because h(n3) = 10 looks close, and pays 110. A* pays 30. Depth first on the slide tree with goal M. Watch it dive down the left side first: A B D H I E J K C F L M. Iterative deepening on the same tree. Each iteration starts again from A with a bigger limit, and M is found at limit 3. The water jug puzzle with jugs of 3 and 4. Breadth first finds the shortest way to get exactly 2 litres. Python # Depth first search on the slide tree: A has children B and C, B has D and E, and so on down to O.tree = {\"A\": [\"B\", \"C\"], \"B\": [\"D\", \"E\"], \"C\": [\"F\", \"G\"], \"D\": [\"H\", \"I\"], \"E\": [\"J\", \"K\"],\"F\": [\"L\", \"M\"], \"G\": [\"N\", \"O\"]}def dfs(node, goal, path): path = path + [node]print(\"visit\", node)if node == goal:return pathfor child in tree.get(node, []): # leaves have no entry, so they get [] found = dfs(child, goal, path)if found isnotNone:return foundreturnNone# dead end: go back up (backtrack)print(\"path:\", dfs(\"A\", \"M\", [])) Output visit A visit B visit D visit H visit I visit E visit J visit K visit C visit F visit L visit M path: ['A', 'C', 'F', 'M'] Property DFS Why Complete No on infinite or cyclic spaces (tree search). Yes on finite spaces with graph search or a path check. It can follow an infinite branch for ever. Optimal No It returns the first goal it meets, which may be deep and costly. Time O(bm) m is the largest depth. It may explore the whole tree, and m can be much bigger than d. Space O(b m) Only one path plus the untried children of each node on it. Remember Backtracking search, a version of DFS that generates one child at a time and undoes changes when it goes back, needs only O(m) memory. It is the basis of CSP solvers (chapter 5.2) and Prolog. Lesson 2 of 8"],[3,"cycles","Path checking and cycle checking","Source: slide Set 2, slide 52, food for thought III. DFS without memory can loop. There are two cheap fixes and one expensive one. Check What it does Memory What it misses Parent check Do not go straight back to the state you came from None Longer cycles like A → B → C → A Path check Do not add a child whose state is already on the current path O(m), the path is already stored Two different paths to the same state (redundant paths) Full cycle check (graph search) Do not add a child whose state was ever explored O(number of states) Nothing, but memory grows like BFS Worked example: path checking on the jugs DFS is at path (0,0), (3,0). The children of (3,0) are (3,4), (0,0) and (0,3). (0,0) is already on the path, so the path check drops it. DFS goes on to (3,4), whose children are (0,4) and (3,0). (3,0) is on the path, dropped. the cycle Fill-3, Empty-3 is gone and DFS keeps moving forward In the exam A path check makes DFS complete on any finite state space, while keeping memory at O(b m). It can still visit the same state many times along different paths, so the time may be much more than the number of states. Lesson 3 of 8"],[3,"dls","Depth limited and iterative deepening search","Source: slide Set 2, slides 45 to 51. What it is Depth limited search (DLS) is DFS that never goes deeper than a limit D. Iterative deepening search (IDS) runs DLS with D = 0, 1, 2, ... until it finds a goal. Why we need it BFS has memory problems and DFS can run down an endless path. DLS stops the endless path. IDS also removes the need to guess D, and gets the best of both: the low memory of DFS and the completeness and shortest answer of BFS. How it works In DLS, a node at depth D is treated as if it had no children. On the slides the root is depth 0, and no path longer than D + 1 states is put on OPEN. IDS repeats DLS with a bigger limit each time. It stops when a goal is found, or when a DLS finishes without cutting off any node, which proves that no solution exists. DLS result Meaning A solution A goal was found at depth ≤ D Failure No solution exists at all (nothing was cut off) Cutoff No solution within the limit, but deeper nodes were cut off, so one may exist deeper Remember IDS is the preferred uninformed method when the search space is large and the depth of the solution is not known (slide 49). Lesson 4 of 8"],[3,"idscost","Is iterative deepening wasteful","IDS regenerates the top levels again in every iteration. That sounds wasteful, but it is not, because almost all nodes are in the last level. Nodes generated by IDSExam favourite N(IDS) = (d + 1)·1 + d·b + (d − 1)·b² + ... + 1·bd = ∑i=0d (d + 1 − i) bi Level i is generated in every iteration from i to d, that is d + 1 − i times. Overhead of IDS over BFSExam favourite N(IDS) ⁄ N(BFS) ≈ b ⁄ (b − 1) For b = 10 that is 1.11, only 11 percent more work. For b = 2 it is 2 times. Cost of IDS time O(bd), space O(b d) Complete when b is finite, and optimal when all actions cost the same. Worked example: b = 10, d = 5 IDS: 6 × 1 + 5 × 10 + 4 × 100 + 3 × 1,000 + 2 × 10,000 + 1 × 100,000. = 6 + 50 + 400 + 3,000 + 20,000 + 100,000 = 123,456. BFS (tested at generation): 1 + 10 + 100 + 1,000 + 10,000 + 100,000 = 111,111. 123,456 ⁄ 111,111 ≈ 1.11 Going deeper: iterative lengthening search The cost version of IDS uses a limit on g(n) instead of depth: run a cost limited DFS, and raise the limit to the smallest cost that was cut off. It is optimal with costs, but with many different cost values it may need one iteration per distinct cost, which can be very slow. IDA* (chapter 3.3) uses the same idea with f = g + h, where it works much better. Lesson 5 of 8"],[3,"bidir","Bidirectional search","What it is Bidirectional search runs two searches at once: one forward from the start and one backward from the goal. It stops when the two frontiers meet. Why we need it Two searches of depth d ⁄ 2 are far smaller than one search of depth d, because the cost is exponential in depth. How it works Alternate one step of each search. Each time a node is generated, check whether its state is already in the other search. The backward search needs predecessors, so the actions must be reversible or easy to undo, and the goal must be one explicit state. Cost of bidirectional searchExam favourite 2 · bd⁄2 = O(bd⁄2) instead of O(bd) For b = 10 and d = 6 that is 2,000 nodes against 1,000,000. Worked example: b = 10, d = 8 One BFS: about 10⁸ = 100,000,000 nodes in the last level. Two BFS of depth 4: about 2 × 10⁴ = 20,000 nodes. 5,000 times less work Common mistake When the frontiers first meet, the joined path is not always the shortest. With uniform costs, finish the current level before stopping, then pick the best meeting point. Lesson 6 of 8"],[3,"compare","All five uninformed methods side by side","Comparing uninformed search (b branching factor, d depth of shallowest goal, m largest depth, l depth limit, C* optimal cost) Criterion BFS UCS DFS DLS IDS Bidirectional Complete Yes (finite b) Yes (costs ≥ ε) No No (only if d ≤ l) Yes (finite b) Yes (with BFS both ways) Optimal Equal costs only Yes No No Equal costs only Equal costs only Time O(bd) O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) O(bm) O(bl) O(bd) O(bd⁄2) Space O(bd) O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) O(b m) O(b l) O(b d) O(bd⁄2) Bottom line: IDS is the best default when costs are equal and the depth is unknown. UCS is the choice when costs differ. Bidirectional search wins when you can search backward from a single goal. Real world scenario: finding a friend of a friend A social network wants to show how you are linked to a stranger. Each person has about 300 friends. A forward BFS of depth 6 would touch 300⁶ ≈ 7 × 10¹⁴ people, more than exist. Searching 3 steps from each side touches about 2 × 300³ = 54 million, and real systems go further with indexes. This meet in the middle idea is how \"degrees of separation\" features work. Lesson 7 of 8"],[3,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths DFS is walking through a maze, keeping one hand on the wall: go as far as you can, then go back to the last turn you did not take. IDS does this with a rope that gets longer each time, so you never get lost too far away. DFS: stack, O(b m) space, not complete or optimal. DLS: DFS with a depth limit, returns solution, failure or cutoff. IDS: DLS with limits 0, 1, 2, ..., complete and optimal for equal costs, O(bd) time, O(b d) space, about b ⁄ (b − 1) times the work of BFS. Bidirectional: O(bd⁄2). IDS is asymptotically optimal among uninformed tree searches that use linear memory: any such method must look at the bd nodes of the last level in the worst case, and IDS adds only a constant factor. Choosing between path checking and full duplicate detection is a trade of memory against repeated work. Front to front bidirectional heuristic search, and the meet in the middle algorithm MM (Holte and others, 2016), guarantee that the two searches meet in the middle. Research on breadth first heuristic search and frontier search shows how to drop the explored set while still detecting duplicates, using the structure of the graph. Going deeper: why IDS is complete but DFS is not, in one line each DFS can pick an infinite branch first and never return. IDS bounds every iteration, so each iteration finishes, and the iteration with limit d finds a goal at depth d. Lesson 8 of 8"],[3,"recap","Chapter summary","DFS expands the deepest node (a stack or recursion). Space O(b m), time O(bm). Not optimal, and not complete on infinite or cyclic spaces. A path check drops children already on the current path. It makes DFS complete on finite spaces without extra memory. DLS stops at depth D and returns a solution, failure or cutoff. IDS runs DLS with D = 0, 1, 2, .... Complete, optimal for equal costs, O(bd) time and O(b d) space. It does about b ⁄ (b − 1) times the work of BFS (123,456 against 111,111 for b = 10, d = 5). Bidirectional search meets in the middle in O(bd⁄2), but needs reversible actions and one explicit goal. Next: chapter 3.1 adds knowledge about the goal with greedy best first and A* search. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterTrace DFS and iterative deepening on the slide tree, then write depth limited search and count the nodes IDS generates.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[4,"","Greedy best first and A* search","Greedy best first, A*, admissible and consistent heuristics, why A* is optimal, and weighted A*. · A* · A star · greedy best first · heuristic · admissible · consistent · weighted A* Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 3: Informed search›Greedy and A* Step 3 · Topic 1 of 4 · College Use a heuristic h(n), a guess of the cost still to go, to point the search at the goal. Greedy best first trusts h alone and can be fooled. A* adds the cost already paid and finds the cheapest path when h never overestimates. About 55 min9 lessons2 animations10 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Heuristics: knowing roughly where the goal is 2Greedy best first search 3A* search: f(n) = g(n) + h(n) 4Admissible and consistent heuristics 5Is A* optimal, and how fast is it 6The maths: weighted A* and the cost of a bad h 7Greedy against UCS against A* 8From intuition to research 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[4,"idea","Heuristics: knowing roughly where the goal is","Source: slide Set 2, slides 53 to 57, and the class of 31 January 2026. Uniform cost search expands the cheapest path so far, but it never asks how far that path still is from the goal. It spreads out in every direction. Often we know something about the \"merit\" of a node, for example the straight line distance from a city to Bucharest. A search that uses such knowledge is called informed or heuristic search. What it is A heuristic function h(n) is an estimate of the cost of the cheapest path from the state of node n to a goal. It must be non negative and h(n) = 0 at every goal. Why we need it It lets the search look ahead. If h(n1) < h(n2), n1 is probably closer to the goal, so the search can try it first and skip much useless work. How it works Design h from knowledge of the problem, often by solving a simpler version of it (chapter 3.2). Then use h to order the frontier: alone (greedy best first) or added to g (A*). A heuristic is a practical rule from experience that is \"good enough\" as an approximation, even if it is not perfect. The straight line distance to Bucharest, hSLD, is a classic example: Arad 366, Sibiu 253, Fagaras 176, Rimnicu Vilcea 193, Pitesti 100, Bucharest 0. In the exam Food for thought IV asks for necessary and sufficient conditions. For optimal A*: admissibility is sufficient for tree search, consistency is sufficient for graph search. Neither is necessary: a slightly overestimating h can still return the optimal path by luck, but then nothing is guaranteed. Lesson 2 of 9"],[4,"greedy","Greedy best first search","Source: slide Set 2, slides 58 and 59. What it is Greedy best first search always expands the node with the lowest h(n), the one that looks closest to the goal. Why we need it It often reaches a goal very quickly, expanding few nodes, because it heads straight for the goal. How it works Order the frontier by h(n) only. Ignore g(n), the cost already paid. On Romania, greedy goes Arad → Sibiu (253 is lower than Zerind 374 and Timisoara 329) → Fagaras (176) → Bucharest. It expands only nodes on its path, so the search cost is minimal, but the route costs 450, not 418. The trap of slide 58 From S there are two ways: S → n1 → n2 → Goal (10 + 10 + 10 = 30) and S → n3 → Goal (100 + 10 = 110). h(n1) = 20 and h(n3) = 10. Greedy takes n3 because it looks closer. From n3 the goal is one step away, so greedy returns S, n3, Goal with cost 110. greedy pays 110 while the best path costs 30, because it ignores the 100 it paid to reach n3 From Iasi to Fagaras greedy first goes to Neamt (closest by straight line), which is a dead end. In tree search Neamt puts Iasi back on the frontier, and the search loops for ever between them. Property Greedy best first Complete No in tree search (Iasi, Neamt loop). Yes in graph search on a finite space. Optimal No (450 against 418, 110 against 30) Time and space O(bm) in the worst case, but often far less with a good h Lesson 3 of 9"],[4,"astar","A* search: f(n) = g(n) + h(n)","Source: slide Set 2, slides 60 to 66. What it is A* expands the node with the lowest f(n) = g(n) + h(n): the cost paid so far plus the estimated cost still to go. Why we need it Greedy ignores the past (g) and UCS ignores the future (h). A* adds them, so f(n) estimates the cost of the cheapest solution through n. With a good h it is both optimal and much faster than UCS. How it works Keep the frontier as a priority queue on f. Take out the lowest f, test for the goal, and add the children with their f values. Replace a frontier entry if a cheaper g to the same state appears. Evaluation function of A*Exam favourite f(n) = g(n) + h(n) g(n) is the cost of the path of node n, h(n) the estimate from n to a goal. With h = 0 A* is UCS. With g ignored it is greedy. A* from Arad to Bucharest, as in the slide figures Expand Arad (f = 0 + 366 = 366). Add Sibiu 140 + 253 = 393, Timisoara 118 + 329 = 447, Zerind 75 + 374 = 449. Expand Sibiu (393). Add Rimnicu Vilcea 220 + 193 = 413, Fagaras 239 + 176 = 415, Oradea 291 + 380 = 671, and Arad again 280 + 366 = 646. Expand Rimnicu Vilcea (413). Add Pitesti 317 + 100 = 417, Craiova 366 + 160 = 526, Sibiu 300 + 253 = 553. Expand Fagaras (415). Add Bucharest 450 + 0 = 450 and Sibiu 338 + 253 = 591. A goal is on the frontier, but not with the lowest f. Expand Pitesti (417). Add Bucharest 418 + 0 = 418, Rimnicu Vilcea 607 and Craiova 615. take out Bucharest with f = 418: Arad, Sibiu, Rimnicu Vilcea, Pitesti, Bucharest, the optimal route A* takes out 6 cities. Uniform cost search takes out 13 for the same answer (chapter 2.1). Lesson 4 of 9"],[4,"conditions","Admissible and consistent heuristics","Source: slide Set 2, slides 67 to 69. AdmissibleExam favourite h(n) ≤ h*(n) for every n h*(n) is the true cost of an optimal path from n to a goal (∞ if there is none). An admissible h never overestimates: it is optimistic. Also h(goal) = 0. Consistent (monotone)Exam favourite h(n1) ≤ C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2) for every action a from n1 to n2 A triangle inequality: the estimate cannot drop by more than the cost of a step. It must hold for every action between n1 and n2. Consistency implies admissibility (∀ n1, n2, a) h(n1) ≤ C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2) ⇒ (∀ n) h(n) ≤ h*(n) Proof by induction on the number of steps to the goal (below). The other direction is false. Proof that consistent implies admissible Take an optimal path n = n0, n1, ..., nk = goal. Induction on k. k = 0: n is a goal, h(n) = 0 = h*(n). Step: by consistency h(n0) ≤ C(n0, n1) + h(n1). By induction h(n1) ≤ h*(n1). h(n0) ≤ C(n0, n1) + h*(n1) = h*(n0) Why consistency makes f never decrease along a path Let n2 be a child of n1 by action a. Then g(n2) = g(n1) + C(n1, a, n2). f(n2) = g(n1) + C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2). By consistency C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2) ≥ h(n1). f(n2) ≥ g(n1) + h(n1) = f(n1) Because f never decreases, A* with a consistent h expands nodes in rings of growing f, called contours. The first time it takes out a state, it has found the cheapest path to that state, just like UCS (Lemma 3 of chapter 2.1). So graph search never needs to reopen a closed state. Lesson 5 of 9"],[4,"optimal","Is A* optimal, and how fast is it","A* tree search with an admissible h is optimal Let the optimal cost be C*. Suppose a suboptimal goal G2 with g(G2) > C* is on the frontier. Some node n on an optimal path is also on the frontier (the start is, and its optimal child replaces it, and so on). Admissibility gives f(n) = g(n) + h(n) ≤ g(n) + h*(n) = C*. And f(G2) = g(G2) + 0 > C*. So f(n) < f(G2), and n is expanded before G2. every node on the optimal path is expanded before G2, so the first goal taken out costs C* In graph search, an admissible but inconsistent h can fail if closed states are never reopened. In the example below the search reaches B first by the expensive road (g = 4), closes it, and later ignores the cheaper road through A (g = 2). What A* expands surely every n with f(n) < C*, maybe some with f(n) = C*, never any with f(n) > C* With a consistent h. Optimally efficientExam favourite no other optimal algorithm using the same h expands fewer nodes (up to ties) Any algorithm that skips a node with f(n) < C* risks missing a cheaper solution. Effective branching factor N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + ... + (b*)d If A* generates N nodes for a solution at depth d, b* measures how well h focuses the search. b* close to 1 is ideal. Common mistake The time of A* is still exponential in general, and the memory is its real weak point: it keeps every generated node. That is why chapter 3.3 builds IDA*, RBFS and SMA*. Lesson 6 of 9"],[4,"maths","The maths: weighted A* and the cost of a bad h","Weighted A* f(n) = g(n) + w · h(n), w ≥ 1 w = 1 is A*. A larger w trusts h more, so the search is faster and greedier. Bounded suboptimalityExam favourite cost found ≤ w · C* With an admissible h, weighted A* is at most w times worse than the optimum. w = 1.5 means at most 50 percent more cost. Error and work if |h* − h| ≤ c for a constant c, the work is about bc, not bd Constant absolute error still costs exponential in c. Relative error ε = (h* − h) ⁄ h* gives about bε d. Worked example: weighted A* bound An optimal route costs C* = 418. We run weighted A* with w = 1.2 and an admissible h. The guarantee is cost ≤ 1.2 × 418. cost ≤ 501.6, so the 450 route via Fagaras is allowed, and is in fact what a greedy enough search returns Worked example: effective branching factor A* finds a solution at depth d = 2 after generating N = 6 nodes. Solve 6 + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)², so (b*)² + b* − 6 = 0. (b* + 3)(b* − 2) = 0. b* = 2 Lesson 7 of 9"],[4,"compare","Greedy against UCS against A*","Three ways to order the frontier UCS Greedy best first A* Order by g(n) h(n) g(n) + h(n) Looks at The past only The future only Both Optimal Yes No Yes, with admissible h (tree) or consistent h (graph) Complete Yes (costs ≥ ε) Only in graph search on finite spaces Yes (costs ≥ ε, finite b) Romania, Arad to Bucharest 418, 13 cities taken out 450, 4 cities 418, 6 cities Slide 58 trap 30 110 30 Bottom line: A* is the right default when you have an admissible heuristic. Greedy is useful only when any solution will do and speed matters most. Real world scenario: route planning in a game Units in a strategy game move across a grid with walls and forests. Game engines use A* with the Manhattan or octile distance as h, because it is admissible on grids and gives the shortest path. When thousands of units move at once, they switch to weighted A* (w around 1.5) or precomputed paths, trading a little path quality for a large saving in time. Lesson 8 of 9"],[4,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A* is like a hiker who adds the distance walked so far to a guess of the distance still to go, and always continues from the spot with the smallest total. If the guess is never too big, the hiker finds the shortest way. f = g + h. Admissible h ≤ h* makes A* tree search optimal. Consistent h(n1) ≤ C + h(n2) makes f non decreasing, so graph search is optimal without reopening. Consistent implies admissible. A* is optimally efficient but needs exponential memory. A* with a consistent h is UCS on reduced costs C − h(x) + h(y) ≥ 0. Weighted A* gives cost ≤ w C*. With inconsistent h, graph search must reopen closed states (or use pathmax), and the number of re expansions can be exponential in the worst case (Martelli 1977). Research directions include anytime A* variants (ARA*, which lowers w over time), real time search (LRTA*) for robots that must move before planning ends, multi agent path finding (CBS), and learned heuristics from neural networks that keep or relax admissibility guarantees. Going deeper: pathmax If an admissible h is inconsistent, you can make f non decreasing by using f(n2) = max(f(n1), g(n2) + h(n2)) for a child n2 of n1. This keeps admissibility, because the true cost through n2 is at least f(n1), which is a lower bound on any solution through n1. Lesson 9 of 9"],[4,"recap","Chapter summary","A heuristic h(n) estimates the cost from n to a goal. It is non negative and 0 at goals. Greedy best first orders by h only. Fast but not optimal (450 on Romania, 110 on the slide 58 trap) and can loop in tree search. A* orders by f = g + h. On Romania it takes out 6 cities and finds 418. Admissible: h ≤ h*. Enough for optimal tree search. Consistent: h(n1) ≤ C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2). Enough for optimal graph search, and it implies admissible. With consistent h, f never decreases along a path, and A* is optimally efficient. Its weak point is memory. Weighted A* with f = g + w h finds a cost at most w C*. Next: chapter 3.2 shows how to design good heuristics, compare them, and measure them with the effective branching factor. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun A* on Romania, see an inconsistent heuristic fail, then write A* and a consistency checker yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[5,"","Designing and comparing heuristics","8 puzzle heuristics, relaxed problems, dominance, max, pattern databases and the effective branching factor. · heuristic design · 8 puzzle · Manhattan distance · relaxed problem · dominance · pattern database · effective branching factor Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 3: Informed search›Heuristic design Step 3 · Topic 2 of 4 · College Where good heuristics come from, how to tell which of two heuristics is better, and how to measure one. The 8 puzzle, relaxed problems, dominance, pattern databases and the effective branching factor. About 45 min8 lessons1 animation8 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Two heuristics for the 8 puzzle 2Relaxed problems: where heuristics come from 3Dominance and combining heuristics 4Pattern databases and learned heuristics 5The maths: effective branching factor 6Comparing heuristics 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[5,"idea","Two heuristics for the 8 puzzle","Source: slide Set 2, slides 54 to 56 (food for thought IV), and AIMA 4th ed., section 3.6. The 8 puzzle has 8 numbered tiles and one blank on a 3 × 3 board. A move slides a tile into the blank. The average solution of a random puzzle is about 22 moves, and there are 9! ⁄ 2 = 181,440 reachable states. Two classic heuristics: h1: misplaced tiles h1(n) = number of tiles not in their goal square Every misplaced tile needs at least one move. h2: Manhattan distanceExam favourite h2(n) = ∑tiles (|row − goal row| + |column − goal column|) Every tile needs at least as many moves as its distance in rows plus columns. Two heuristics for the 8 puzzleh1 counts tiles in the wrong place. h2 adds how many rows and columns each tile is from its home. The blank does not count. Step 0 of 10 Start, row by row (0 is the blank) Goal, row by row Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this The textbook start state. h1 = 8 (all tiles misplaced) and h2 = 3 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 2 = 18. One move away from the goal. Both heuristics give 1, and the true cost is 1. Swap two tiles of the goal (1 and 2). The inversion count becomes odd, so this state can never reach the goal. Heuristics do not tell you that, the parity check does. Python # Two heuristics for the 8 puzzle. A state is a list of 9 numbers read row by row, 0 is the blank.start = [7, 2, 4,5, 0, 6,8, 3, 1]goal = [0, 1, 2,3, 4, 5,6, 7, 8]def misplaced(state): # h1: tiles not in their goal square count = 0for i inrange(9):if state[i] != 0and state[i] != goal[i]: count = count + 1return countdef manhattan(state): # h2: total up, down, left, right distance of every tile total = 0for i inrange(9): tile = state[i]if tile != 0: j = goal.index(tile) total = total + abs(i // 3 - j // 3) + abs(i % 3 - j % 3)return totalprint(\"h1 =\", misplaced(start))print(\"h2 =\", manhattan(start)) Output h1 = 8 h2 = 18 For the textbook start state, all 8 tiles are misplaced, so h1 = 8, and h2 = 3 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 2 = 18. The true solution needs 26 moves. Both heuristics are admissible, and h2 is closer to the truth. What it is A good heuristic is admissible (never too big) and as large as possible, so it is close to the true cost h*. Why we need it The closer h is to h*, the fewer nodes A* expands. With h = h* A* walks straight to the goal. With h = 0 it is uniform cost search. How it works Find h by solving an easier version of the problem exactly (a relaxed problem), or by storing exact costs of sub problems (pattern databases), and take the max of several admissible heuristics. Lesson 2 of 8"],[5,"relax","Relaxed problems: where heuristics come from","A relaxed problem has fewer rules than the real one. Every solution of the real problem is still a solution of the relaxed one, so the cost of the optimal relaxed solution can never be larger than the real optimal cost. That makes it an admissible heuristic. It is also consistent, because it is an exact cost in a real (relaxed) problem. Real rule: a tile can move from square A to square B if Remove this part Heuristic you get A is next to B and B is blank \"and B is blank\" h2: Manhattan distance A is next to B and B is blank \"A is next to B\" Gaschnig's heuristic: number of swaps with the blank A is next to B and B is blank both conditions h1: misplaced tiles (each tile jumps in one move) Problem Relaxation Heuristic Romania road trip You may fly in a straight line Straight line distance hSLD Robot on a grid with walls No walls Manhattan or octile distance Water jugs, get 2 litres Pour any amount you like 0 if a jug holds 2, else 1 Travelling salesperson The tour need not be a cycle Cost of a minimum spanning tree Remember The relaxed problem must be easy to solve, or computing h will cost more than the search saves. Manhattan distance is a sum of independent tile problems, which takes 9 small steps. Real world scenario: food for thought V from the slides Making a cup of tea: a relaxation lets you do every step at once, so h = number of steps not done yet (water not boiled, no tea, no milk, ...). It is admissible because every step must still be done at least once. For buying a book, h = number of checkout stages left. For a doctor, h might be the number of tests still needed to separate the remaining possible diseases. Lesson 3 of 8"],[5,"dominance","Dominance and combining heuristics","DominanceExam favourite h2 dominates h1 if h2(n) ≥ h1(n) for every n, and both are admissible Then A* with h2 expands no more nodes than A* with h1 (apart from ties). Why A* surely expands every n with f(n) < C*, that is h(n) < C* − g(n) A node with h2(n) < C* − g(n) also has h1(n) < C* − g(n). So every node expanded with h2 is also expanded with h1. CombiningExam favourite h(n) = max(h1(n), ..., hk(n)) The max of admissible heuristics is admissible, and it dominates each of them. The max of consistent heuristics is consistent. Worked example: Manhattan dominates misplaced A misplaced tile is at distance at least 1 from its goal square. So each misplaced tile adds 1 to h1 and at least 1 to h2. A tile in place adds 0 to both. So h2(n) ≥ h1(n) for every n. the start state: h2 = 18 ≥ h1 = 8"],[5,"dominance","How much it helps: our own experiment","We solved 100 random 8 puzzle states for each depth d with graph search A* and counted the nodes generated on average. Depth d BFS nodes A*(h1) nodes A*(h2) nodes b* BFS b* h1 b* h2 2 7 6 6 2.15 2.00 2.00 6 81 17 15 1.83 1.31 1.26 10 602 72 34 1.74 1.35 1.21 12 1,580 164 57 1.72 1.38 1.23 16 10,198 953 207 1.68 1.42 1.27 20 47,880 5,508 725 1.63 1.45 1.29 At depth 20, Manhattan distance generates about 66 times fewer nodes than BFS and 7.6 times fewer than misplaced tiles. On the textbook start (depth 26), A* generated 62,443 nodes with h1 and 6,172 with h2. Lesson 4 of 8"],[5,"pdb","Pattern databases and learned heuristics","What it is A pattern database (PDB) stores the exact cost to solve a sub problem, for example to put tiles 1 to 4 in place while the other tiles count as \"don't care\", for every possible position of those tiles. Why we need it Manhattan distance treats tiles as independent, so it misses how tiles block each other. A PDB captures those interactions exactly for a group of tiles, giving a much larger admissible h. How it works Before the search, run a backward BFS from the goal in the abstract space of the pattern tiles and store each cost in a table. During the search, look up the pattern of the current state. Take the max over several PDBs. Disjoint pattern databasesExam favourite h = PDB(tiles 1 to 4) + PDB(tiles 5 to 8) Adding two PDBs is admissible only if each counts only the moves of its own tiles. Then no move is counted twice. Size of a PDB for k pattern tiles plus the blank on 9 squares: 9! ⁄ (9 − k − 1)! entries For k = 4: 9 × 8 × 7 × 6 × 5 = 15,120 entries. Going deeper: learning heuristics from data A heuristic can be learned: solve many random instances, record features of each state (Manhattan distance, linear conflicts, number of tiles in the right row) and the true cost, and fit a model such as a linear regression h(n) = c1 x1(n) + c2 x2(n). A learned h is usually not admissible, so A* loses its guarantee, but it can find near optimal answers much faster. DeepCubeA (2019) learned a neural network heuristic for the Rubik's cube with reinforcement learning and solved it with weighted A*. Lesson 5 of 8"],[5,"maths","The maths: effective branching factor","Effective branching factor b*Exam favourite N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + ... + (b*)d N nodes generated for a solution at depth d. b* is the branching factor a uniform tree of depth d would need to hold N + 1 nodes. A good heuristic has b* close to 1. Worked example: N = 52, d = 5 We need 1 + b + b² + b³ + b⁴ + b⁵ = 53. b = 1.9: 1 + 1.9 + 3.61 + 6.86 + 13.03 + 24.76 = 51.2, a bit low. b = 1.92: 1 + 1.92 + 3.69 + 7.08 + 13.59 + 26.09 = 53.4, a bit high. b* ≈ 1.92 Worked example: what one heuristic is worth At d = 20, b* = 1.63 for BFS and 1.29 for A*(h2). 1.63²⁰ ≈ 17,500 and 1.29²⁰ ≈ 163. a smaller b* compounds over every level, which is why a good h wins by orders of magnitude Lesson 6 of 8"],[5,"compare","Comparing heuristics","Heuristics for the 8 puzzle Heuristic Admissible Value at the start state Cost to compute Nodes at d = 20 h = 0 (uniform cost) Yes 0 None about 48,000 (BFS) h1 misplaced tiles Yes 8 9 checks 5,508 h2 Manhattan Yes 18 9 small sums 725 max(h1, h2) Yes 18 (= h2, since h2 dominates) Both 725 Pattern database Yes At least as large as one group of tiles needs One table lookup, after building the table Much fewer h* (perfect) Yes 26 As hard as the problem 26 (straight to the goal) Bottom line: pick the largest admissible heuristic that is cheap to compute. Dominance tells you which is better without running anything. Common mistake Larger is better only while h stays admissible. 2 × h2 is larger, but it overestimates, so A* may then return a longer solution. Lesson 7 of 8"],[5,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A heuristic is a smart guess. For the sliding puzzle, count how far each tile is from home. The better the guess, the less you need to try. Get admissible heuristics from relaxed problems. If h2 ≥ h1 everywhere, A* with h2 expands no more nodes. Measure a heuristic with the effective branching factor b*. Combine heuristics with max. Pattern databases store exact abstract costs, disjoint PDBs can be added, and the 15 puzzle is solved optimally in milliseconds with them. The time to compute h must be weighed against the nodes saved. Research looks at heuristics from abstraction hierarchies, landmark heuristics in planning (chapter 8.2), cost partitioning to add many heuristics safely, and neural heuristics trained by self play (DeepCubeA), with work on keeping bounded suboptimality when h is learned. Lesson 8 of 8"],[5,"recap","Chapter summary","8 puzzle: h1 = misplaced tiles, h2 = Manhattan distance. The textbook start has h1 = 8, h2 = 18 and needs 26 moves. A relaxed problem removes rules. Its exact cost is an admissible and consistent heuristic (h1, h2, straight line distance). Dominance: if h2 ≥ h1 and both are admissible, A* with h2 expands no more nodes. The max of admissible heuristics is admissible. Effective branching factor b* solves N + 1 = 1 + b* + ... + (b*)d. At d = 20 we measured 1.63 for BFS, 1.45 for h1 and 1.29 for h2. Pattern databases store exact costs of sub problems. Disjoint ones can be added. Next: chapter 3.3 keeps A* optimal while cutting its memory, with IDA*, RBFS and SMA*. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCompute misplaced tiles and Manhattan distance, measure the effective branching factor, and write both functions yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[6,"","Memory bounded heuristic search: IDA*, RBFS and SMA*","IDA* with f cutoffs, RBFS with f-limits and backed up values, SMA*, and the memory against time trade. · IDA* · RBFS · SMA* · memory bounded search · iterative deepening A* · recursive best first search Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 3: Informed search›IDA*, RBFS, SMA* Step 3 · Topic 3 of 4 · College A* finds the cheapest path but keeps every node in memory. IDA*, RBFS and SMA* keep it optimal with linear or fixed memory, by repeating some work. Step through each one on the Romania map. About 55 min8 lessons2 animations4 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why A* runs out of memory 2IDA*: iterative deepening A* 3RBFS: recursive best first search 4SMA*: use all the memory you have 5The maths: memory and repeated work 6BFS against IDS against IDA* against RBFS 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[6,"idea","Why A* runs out of memory","Source: slide Set 2, slides 71 to 74, and the classes of 31 January and 1 February 2026. A* keeps every node it generates, in the frontier or the explored set. Its time is often fine, but its memory grows exponentially, and on hard problems it runs out of memory long before it runs out of time. Memory bounded methods keep the optimality of A* while using far less memory, at the price of repeating some work. Method Idea Memory IDA* Iterative deepening, but the cutoff is on f = g + h instead of depth O(b d), linear RBFS Best first search written as a recursion that remembers only the f of the best alternative O(b d), linear SMA* A* that uses all the memory it has, and drops the worst leaf when memory is full Whatever you give it In the exam The minor exam of this course was announced as a coding task, such as writing RBFS. The code in lesson 3 is short enough to learn by heart, and the notebook lets you practise it. Lesson 2 of 8"],[6,"ida","IDA*: iterative deepening A*","What it is IDA* runs a depth first search that stops whenever f = g + h is above a limit. If no goal is found, it raises the limit and starts again. Why we need it Iterative deepening saves memory, but on its own it ignores costs and h. Using f as the cutoff keeps the low memory and makes the search follow the A* contours, so it is optimal with an admissible h. How it works Start with limit = f(start) = h(start). In each iteration, remember the smallest f that went over the limit. That value is the next limit. Stop when a goal is taken out within the limit. IDA* from Arad to Bucharest Limit 366 = h(Arad). Every child of Arad is over: Sibiu 393, Timisoara 447, Zerind 449. Next limit: 393. Limit 393. Sibiu is expanded. Its children are over: Rimnicu Vilcea 413, Fagaras 415, Oradea 671. Next limit: 413. Limit 413. Rimnicu Vilcea is expanded. Pitesti 417 and Craiova 526 are over. Fagaras 415 is over too. Next limit: 415. Limit 415. Fagaras is expanded, Bucharest via Fagaras is 450, over. Pitesti 417 is still over. Next limit: 417. Limit 417. Pitesti is expanded, Bucharest via Pitesti is 418, over. Next limit: 418. limit 418: Bucharest is reached with f = 418, the optimal cost, after 6 iterations Iterations of IDA*Exam favourite one per distinct f value up to C* With integer costs and a good h that is few. With many different real valued costs, each iteration may add only one new node, and IDA* can take about N² time for N nodes A* would expand. Lesson 3 of 8"],[6,"rbfs","RBFS: recursive best first search","What it is RBFS is a recursive search that acts like best first search but stores only the current path and, for each node on it, the f value of its best alternative (the f-limit). Why we need it It keeps the best first order of A*, so it wastes less work than IDA*, while still using linear memory. How it works At a node, sort its children by f. Go into the best child with f-limit = min(the limit from above, f of the second best child). If the best child's f goes over the limit, unwind (backtrack) and replace the f of that node with the best f found below it (the backed up value). Then the alternative may become the best, and the search switches there. RBFS on Romania, as in the slide figure At Arad. Children: Sibiu 393, Timisoara 447, Zerind 449. Go to Sibiu with limit 447 (the best alternative, Timisoara). At Sibiu. Children: Rimnicu Vilcea 413, Fagaras 415, Oradea 671, Arad 646. Go to Rimnicu Vilcea with limit min(447, 415) = 415. At Rimnicu Vilcea. Best child Pitesti has f = 417 > 415. Unwind, and Rimnicu Vilcea gets the backed up value 417. At Sibiu again. Now Fagaras 415 is best. Go there with limit min(447, 417) = 417. At Fagaras. Best child Bucharest has f = 450 > 417. Unwind, and Fagaras gets 450. At Sibiu. Rimnicu Vilcea (417) is best again. Go there with limit min(447, 450) = 447, then Pitesti (417), then Bucharest (418). the path Arad, Sibiu, Rimnicu Vilcea, Pitesti, Bucharest with cost 418 RBFS (AIMA style) function RBFS(node, f_limit): if GOAL-TEST(node): return node, f(node) children = expand(node) if no children: return failure, ∞ for each child s: s.f = max(g(s) + h(s), node.f) loop: best = child with the lowest f if best.f > f_limit: return failure, best.f alt = second lowest f among the children result, best.f = RBFS(best, min(f_limit, alt)) if result is not failure: return result, best.f Lesson 4 of 8"],[6,"sma","SMA*: use all the memory you have","What it is SMA* (simplified memory bounded A*) behaves like A* until memory is full. Then, to add a new node, it drops the worst leaf, the one with the highest f (and the oldest among ties). Why we need it IDA* and RBFS use too little memory: they forget almost everything and must regenerate it. SMA* remembers as much as memory allows, so it repeats much less work. How it works When a leaf is dropped, its f value is backed up into its parent. The parent then knows how good the best path through the forgotten subtree was, and regenerates it only if every other path looks worse. Property SMA* Complete Yes, if the depth of the shallowest goal is less than the memory size (the path must fit) Optimal Yes, if an optimal solution fits in memory. Otherwise it returns the best solution that fits. Weak point When memory is very tight, it can thrash: switch back and forth between paths and regenerate them many times Remember All three methods are a trade between time and memory. In practice, SMA* style methods are the most robust when memory is limited but not tiny. Lesson 5 of 8"],[6,"maths","The maths: memory and repeated work","MemoryExam favourite A*: O(bd) IDA*, RBFS: O(b d) SMA*: M, the memory you give it b branching factor, d solution depth. IDA* number of iterations k = number of distinct f values in (h(start), C*] Romania: 366, 393, 413, 415, 417, 418 gives 6 iterations. Worst case of IDA* 1 + 2 + ... + N = N(N + 1) ⁄ 2 = O(N²) If each iteration adds just one new node to the N nodes A* expands. Worked example: memory of A* against IDA* b = 3, d = 20, 100 bytes a node. A* may store about 3²⁰ ≈ 3.5 × 10⁹ nodes, 350 GB. IDA* stores about b × d = 60 nodes, 6 KB. the price: re expanding the top of the tree once per iteration Worked example: IDA* worst case A* would expand N = 1,000 nodes, all with different f values. IDA* then needs 1,000 iterations, the k-th one expanding k nodes. 1,000 × 1,001 ⁄ 2 = 500,500 node expansions, about 500 times more than A* Lesson 6 of 8"],[6,"compare","BFS against IDS against IDA* against RBFS","Source: the question on slide 73. Memory bounded search compared BFS IDS A* IDA* RBFS SMA* Uses h No No Yes Yes Yes Yes Optimal Equal costs Equal costs Yes Yes (admissible h) Yes (admissible h) If the solution fits Memory O(bd) O(b d) O(bd) O(b d) O(b d) M Repeated work None Top levels per iteration None One iteration per f value Each time it switches back Only for dropped nodes Romania, Arad to Bucharest 450 450 418 418, 6 iterations 418, 2 switches 418 Bottom line: IDA* is simplest and works well with few distinct f values (unit costs, puzzles). RBFS wastes less when f values vary. SMA* is best when you have a fixed amount of memory to fill. Real world scenario: solving the 15 puzzle The 15 puzzle has about 10¹³ states. A* with Manhattan distance runs out of memory on hard instances, but IDA* with Manhattan distance (Korf, 1985) solves random instances optimally using a few kilobytes, because all costs are 1 and f takes few distinct values. Adding pattern databases makes it fast enough to solve the 24 puzzle too. Lesson 7 of 8"],[6,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths If your notebook is small, you cannot write down every road you have looked at. So you remember only the road you are on and the best other choice, and you come back to it when your road gets too long. IDA*: DFS with an f cutoff that grows to the smallest f over the last limit. RBFS: recursion with an f-limit equal to the best alternative, and backed up values when it unwinds. SMA*: A* that drops the worst leaf. All optimal with an admissible h. IDA* and RBFS use O(b d) memory. IDA* repeats work when f values are many and different, up to O(N²). RBFS also repeats work when it switches between paths. Both cannot detect repeated states without extra memory, so on graphs with many cycles they may explore exponentially many paths to the same state. Transposition tables fix this with a fixed memory budget. Modern variants include IDA* with transposition tables and pattern databases, frontier search that drops the closed list, breadth first heuristic search, and external memory A* that keeps the frontier on disk, which solved problems with more than 10¹² states. Lesson 8 of 8"],[6,"recap","Chapter summary","A* runs out of memory before time, because it stores every node. IDA*: DFS with an f cutoff. The next cutoff is the smallest f over the old one. Romania: 366, 393, 413, 415, 417, 418. Linear memory, optimal with admissible h. RBFS: go into the best child with f-limit = min(limit, best alternative). When the best f goes over the limit, unwind and back up that f. Linear memory, optimal. SMA*: A* until memory is full, then drop the worst leaf and back up its f to the parent. Optimal if the solution fits. The trade: less memory means more repeated work. IDA* can take O(N²) when every f is different. Next: chapter 3.4 searches when actions are non deterministic, with AND OR trees and AO*. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun IDA* and RBFS on Romania, then write IDA* and RBFS yourself on a small graph, the kind of task set in the minor exam.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[7,"","AND OR search and AO*","AND OR trees, conditional and cyclic plans, AND OR search on the erratic vacuum world, and AO* on the slide example. · AND OR search · AO* · conditional plan · contingency plan · erratic vacuum world · cyclic plan · non deterministic Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 3: Informed search›AND OR and AO* Step 3 · Topic 4 of 4 · College When an action can end in several states, a list of actions is not enough. AND OR trees describe the agent's choices and nature's outcomes, AND OR search builds a plan with if then branches, and AO* finds the cheapest one. About 50 min8 lessons3 animations5 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1When actions do not always do the same thing 2The erratic vacuum world and AND OR search 3Slippery worlds and cyclic plans 4AO*: best first search on AND OR graphs 5The maths: cost of a solution graph and complexity 6AND OR search against ordinary search 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[7,"idea","When actions do not always do the same thing","Source: slide Set 2, slide 88, and the classes of 1 and 7 February 2026. So far every action had exactly one result. In many real problems an action can end in several states, and the agent only learns which one after acting. A floor cleaning robot's suction may also clean the next square, or may drop dirt. A delivery may arrive or get lost. Such actions are non deterministic. What it is An AND OR search tree has two kinds of nodes. At an OR node the agent chooses one action (at least one child must work). At an AND node nature chooses the outcome, so every child must be solved. Why we need it With non deterministic actions a single sequence of actions is not enough. The agent needs a contingency plan (a conditional plan) that says what to do for each outcome: \"Suck, and if the other square is still dirty then go Right and Suck\". How it works Search the AND OR tree depth first. At an OR node, try actions until one gives a plan. At an AND node, find a plan for every outcome, and fail if any one fails. Stop a branch when it reaches a state already on its path. Real world scenario: getting a television, from the class You need a television. One way is to steal it. The other way is to earn money AND buy it. \"Get a TV\" is an OR node with two choices. \"Earn money and buy\" is an AND node: you must do both parts. An AND OR tree like this describes a problem broken into sub problems, also called problem reduction. The tree only describes the search space. You can search it depth first, breadth first or best first. OR node AND node Who chooses The agent Nature (the environment) Solved when At least one child is solved Every child is solved Cost The cheapest child (plus the edge) The sum over all children (plus the edges) Drawn as Separate branches Branches joined by an arc Lesson 2 of 8"],[7,"vacuum","The erratic vacuum world and AND OR search","The vacuum world has 8 states: the robot is in square A or B, and each square is dirty or clean. States 7 and 8 (both clean) are goals. In the erratic version, Suck on a dirty square cleans it and sometimes also cleans the other square, and Suck on a clean square sometimes drops dirt on it. AND-OR-SEARCH (AIMA figure 4.11) function OR-SEARCH(state, path): if GOAL-TEST(state): return the empty plan if state is on path: return failure for each action: plan = AND-SEARCH(RESULTS(state, action), [state] + path) if plan is not failure: return [action] + plan return failure function AND-SEARCH(states, path): for each s_i in states: plan_i = OR-SEARCH(s_i, path) if plan_i is failure: return failure return [if s_1 then plan_1 else if s_2 then plan_2 ... else plan_n] From state 1 the plan is [Suck, if State = 5 then [Right, Suck] else []]. If Suck cleaned both squares we are in state 7 and done. If it cleaned only A we are in state 5, so go Right and Suck. Remember Failing on a state already on the path does not miss solutions: if a plan exists from a repeated state, it also exists from its first occurrence. That keeps AND OR search complete on finite state spaces. Lesson 3 of 8"],[7,"cyclic","Slippery worlds and cyclic plans","In the slippery vacuum world, Right and Left sometimes fail and leave the robot where it is. Then every plan from state 5 can come back to state 5, which is on its path, so AND OR search finds no plan. But there is an obvious working plan: keep trying Right until it works. A cyclic planExam favourite [Suck, L1: Right, if State = 5 then L1 else Suck] L1 is a label. \"if State = 5 then L1\" means go back to the label and try Right again. In code it is a while loop: while State = 5: Right. When a cyclic plan is a solution every leaf is a goal, and a goal can be reached from every point of the plan Each try must succeed with some chance. If the floor is so slippery that Right never works, no plan helps. Common mistake A cyclic plan only works under a fairness assumption: an action that can succeed will succeed if you try it often enough. If the failure has a hidden cause (the wheel is broken), trying again is useless and the agent must learn the cause instead. Lesson 4 of 8"],[7,"aostar","AO*: best first search on AND OR graphs","Source: slide Set 2, slides 89 to 92, worked in the class of 7 February 2026. What it is AO* is the AND OR version of A*. It keeps a best partial solution graph, expands one of its unexpanded nodes, and revises the costs back up to the root. Why we need it AND OR search finds some plan. When the parts of a plan have costs, we want the cheapest solution graph, and AO* uses heuristic estimates to find it without expanding everything. How it works Cost of an OR choice: the cheapest connector. Cost of an AND connector: the sum of its edges and children. After each expansion, propagate the new cost to the ancestors, mark the best connector at each node, and mark a node solved when every child on its best connector is solved. Stop when the root is solved. The slide example (every edge costs 1) Expand A. Through B: 1 + h(B) = 1 + 5 = 6. Through C AND D: 1 + 2 + 1 + 4 = 8. The best is B (6). Expand B. B → E costs 1 + 7 = 8 and B → F costs 1 + 9 = 10. So h(B) becomes 8. Revise A. Through B: 1 + 8 = 9. Through C AND D: still 8. Now C AND D is better, so A switches. Expand C. C → G costs 1 + 3 = 4 and C → H AND I costs 1 + 0 + 1 + 0 = 2. h(C) = 2 and C is solved (H and I are goals). Expand D. D → J costs 1 + 0 = 1. h(D) = 1 and D is solved. revise A: through C AND D = 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 5. A is solved with cost 5: A → C + D, C → H + I, D → J Common mistake Look closely at the slide numbers: h(D) = 4 but D really costs only 1 (D → J). So this h overestimates and is not admissible. Here AO* still ends at the best answer 5, but try the second tip (h(H) = 6): AO* stops at 9 while the true best is 7. Like A*, AO* is only guaranteed optimal when h never overestimates. AO* (outline) G = graph with only the start node, marked unsolved loop until start is solved or its cost is ∞: follow the marked best connectors from the start and pick an unexpanded tip n expand n: add its successors, estimate each new node with h S = { n } while S is not empty: take a node m from S that has no descendant in S cost(m) = min over connectors of ∑ (edge cost + cost(child)) mark that connector. m is solved if all its children on it are solved if cost(m) changed or m became solved: add the parents of m to S Lesson 5 of 8"],[7,"maths","The maths: cost of a solution graph and complexity","Cost at an OR node cost(n) = minconnectors k cost(n, k) The agent picks the cheapest way. Cost of an AND connectorExam favourite cost(n, k) = ∑children c of k (C(n, c) + cost(c)) All parts must be done, so their costs add. Admissibility for AO* h(n) ≤ the true cost of the best solution graph below n With such an h, AO* returns an optimal solution graph (on graphs without cycles). Worked example: food for thought VII, the complexity of AO* Like A*, AO* may need to expand every node in the worst case, so time and memory are exponential in the depth: O(bd). Each revision walks up to the root through the ancestors, which costs O(d) per expansion on a tree, more on a graph with shared nodes. Data structures: a dictionary from node to its list of connectors, its cost, its marked connector and a solved flag, plus a parent list for each node to propagate costs upward. time and space O(bd), with an O(d) revision after each expansion on a tree Worked example: the size of a conditional plan Each AND node with k outcomes multiplies the number of branches by k. A plan of 3 actions, each with 2 possible outcomes, may need 2³ = 8 branches. conditional plans can be exponentially larger than plain action sequences Lesson 6 of 8"],[7,"compare","AND OR search against ordinary search","Plain search, AND OR search and AO* A* or BFS AND OR search (DFS) AO* Actions Deterministic Non deterministic, or problems split into parts Same as AND OR, with costs Solution A path (a list of actions) A tree: a conditional plan The cheapest solution graph Uses h A*: yes No Yes Handles loops Explored set Fails on a repeated state, cyclic plans need labels Classic AO* assumes no cycles (LAO* handles them) Example Romania route Erratic vacuum plan Slide graph, cost 5 Bottom line: use plain search when actions are certain, AND OR search when you need any plan that works for every outcome, and AO* when the parts have costs and you want the cheapest plan. Contingency planning against probabilistic planning AND OR (contingency) Markov decision process (chapter 10.1) Outcomes A set of possible states States with probabilities Goal Reach a goal in every outcome Maximise expected reward Result Conditional plan Policy for every state Bottom line: when you know the chances of each outcome, an MDP can trade a small risk for a big gain, which an AND OR plan cannot. Lesson 7 of 8"],[7,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Sometimes you cannot be sure what happens after you act. So you plan for every case: \"if it rains, take the bus, otherwise walk\". An AND OR tree writes down all those cases. OR nodes are the agent's choices, AND nodes are nature's outcomes. AND OR search returns a conditional plan, failing a branch that repeats a state on its path. Slippery worlds need cyclic plans. AO* finds the cheapest solution graph by expanding the best partial graph and revising costs upward. AO* is optimal with an admissible h on acyclic graphs. Plans can grow exponentially with the number of uncertain steps. For partially observable worlds the same search runs over belief states (sets of possible states), and sensing actions split a belief state into AND branches. LAO* extends AO* to cyclic graphs and is a bridge to MDP solvers. Fully observable non deterministic (FOND) planners such as PRP find strong cyclic plans for large planning benchmarks, and AND OR search is used in game solving and in proof search for theorem provers. Going deeper: belief states If the agent cannot see the state, it tracks the set of states it might be in, its belief state. With 8 vacuum states there are 2⁸ = 256 belief states. A sensorless agent can still solve the vacuum world: [Right, Suck, Left, Suck] cleans both squares from any start, moving the belief state from all 8 states to {7}. Lesson 8 of 8"],[7,"recap","Chapter summary","With non deterministic actions, the agent needs a contingency (conditional) plan, not a single sequence. In an AND OR tree, OR nodes are the agent's choices (one child must work) and AND nodes are nature's outcomes (every child must work). AND OR search is a depth first search that fails a branch when a state repeats on its path. Erratic vacuum from state 1: [Suck, if State = 5 then [Right, Suck] else []]. In a slippery world no plan without loops exists. A cyclic plan such as [Suck, L1: Right, if State = 5 then L1 else Suck] works if each try can succeed. AO*: expand the best partial solution graph, add costs at AND connectors, take the min at OR nodes, and revise upward. The slide example ends at 5 via C AND D. Next: chapter 4.1 drops paths entirely: local search keeps only the current state and improves it step by step. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun AND OR search on the erratic vacuum world and the AO* costs of the slide graph, then write cost propagation and a plan checker yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[8,"","Local search: hill climbing and simulated annealing","Hill climbing and its traps, sideways moves, restarts, beam search, and simulated annealing with cooling schedules. · local search · hill climbing · simulated annealing · cooling schedule · 8 queens · local beam search · random restart Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 4: Local and evolutionary search›Hill climbing and annealing Step 4 · Topic 1 of 2 · College When the space is too big to search and only the final state matters, keep one state and improve it. Hill climbing is fast but gets stuck. Restarts, beam search and simulated annealing help it escape. About 50 min8 lessons3 animations6 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Local search: forget the path, improve the state 2Hill climbing 3Ways out: sideways moves, randomness and restarts 4Simulated annealing 5The maths: acceptance, restarts and gradients 6Comparing local search methods 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[8,"idea","Local search: forget the path, improve the state","Source: slide Set 2, slides 77 to 80, and the class of 1 February 2026. The search methods so far explore paths systematically. They can handle spaces of up to roughly 10100 states (about 500 binary variables, a ballpark number). Some real problems are far bigger, for example 1030,000 states. And in many problems, like 8 queens or timetabling, only the final state matters, not the path to it. What it is Local search keeps one current state (or a few) and moves to a neighbouring state that is better, judged by an objective function. It does not store paths. Why we need it It uses very little memory, usually a constant amount, and it can find good solutions in huge or even continuous spaces where systematic search is hopeless. How it works Start from a guess (often random, maybe not even valid). Measure its quality. Make a small local change using a neighbourhood function. Repeat until a goal is found, time runs out, or the result is good enough. Picture the state space landscape: each state is a location, and its objective value is the height. Maximising means finding the highest peak (the global maximum). Minimising a cost means finding the lowest valley (the global minimum). The same ideas work for both by flipping the sign. Feature of the landscape What it is Why it is a problem Local maximum A peak higher than all its neighbours but lower than the global maximum Every move goes down, so a greedy climber stops Ridge A narrow line of peaks with slopes on both sides No single move goes up along the ridge, so the climber zig zags slowly Plateau: flat local maximum A flat area with no uphill exit No neighbour is better Plateau: shoulder A flat area from which you can still go up later The climber cannot see that, and stops unless it may move sideways Lesson 2 of 8"],[8,"hc","Hill climbing","What it is Steepest ascent hill climbing moves to the best neighbour, as long as it is better than the current state. It stops when no neighbour is better. Why we need it It is the simplest local search: fast, almost no memory, and often good enough. Each step uses only the current state. How it works Compute the objective of every neighbour. If the best one is higher, move there. Otherwise stop and return the current state, which is a peak, but maybe only a local one. Steepest ascent hill climbing function HILL-CLIMBING(problem): current = initial state loop: neighbour = the highest valued successor of current if VALUE(neighbour) <= VALUE(current): return current current = neighbour"],[8,"hc","8 queens","States: 8 queens, one per column. A move: move one queen within its column, so each state has 8 × 7 = 56 neighbours. The cost h is the number of attacking pairs, which we want to bring down to 0. Method on random 8 queens boards (our run, 2,000 boards) Solved Average steps when it works Average steps when stuck Steepest ascent hill climbing 14.8 percent 4.0 3.1 With up to 100 sideways moves 93.8 percent 19.3 60.9 Hill climbing is fast (only about 4 steps), but it gets stuck on 85 percent of the boards. The state space has 8⁸ ≈ 16.8 million states. Lesson 3 of 8"],[8,"variants","Ways out: sideways moves, randomness and restarts","Variant Idea Helps with Sideways moves Allow moves to an equal valued neighbour, up to a limit (to avoid looping on a flat top) Shoulders Stochastic hill climbing Pick a random uphill move, with more chance for steeper ones Some ridges, slower but sometimes better First choice hill climbing Generate random neighbours until one is better, take it States with thousands of neighbours Random restart hill climbing Run hill climbing again from new random starts, keep the best result Local maxima. Complete with probability approaching 1 Local beam search Keep k states. Generate all their successors, keep the best k overall Shares information between the k searches Stochastic beam search Keep k successors chosen at random, better ones more likely Stops all k states crowding onto one hill Random restartExam favourite expected restarts = 1 ⁄ p p is the chance one run succeeds. For 8 queens p ≈ 0.148, so about 7 runs: 6 failures (about 3 steps each) and 1 success (about 4 steps), roughly 22 steps in all. Remember Local beam search is not k independent restarts. If one state finds a great region, its children can fill all k places, so the search moves its effort to where the progress is. Lesson 4 of 8"],[8,"sa","Simulated annealing","Source: slide Set 2, slides 82 and 83. What it is Simulated annealing is hill climbing that sometimes accepts a worse move. The chance of accepting a worse move depends on how much worse it is and on a temperature T that slowly goes down. Why we need it A pure climber never goes down, so it gets stuck on the first peak. A random walk explores but never settles. Annealing starts like a random walk (exploration) and ends like a climber (exploitation), so it can escape local peaks early and settle on a good one later. How it works Pick a random neighbour. If it is better, move. If it is worse by ΔE < 0, move with probability eΔE⁄T. Lower T after each step by the cooling schedule. Acceptance probabilityExam favourite P(accept) = eΔE ⁄ T for ΔE < 0, and 1 for ΔE ≥ 0 High T: P near 1, almost any move is accepted. Low T: P near 0, only uphill moves. Why the slide table as T grows, ΔE ⁄ T → 0 and eΔE⁄T → e0 = 1 The slide lists ex for x = 1, 1⁄2, 1⁄4, ..., 1⁄1024: 2.718, 1.649, 1.284, ..., 1.001. As the exponent shrinks, the value approaches 1. Cooling schedule (slide 83) Formula Comment Geometric Tk+1 = α Tk, 0.8 ≤ α ≤ 1 The most common. α near 1 cools slowly and explores longer. Linear Tk = T0 − k ΔT Drops by the same amount each step. Logarithmic Tk = C ⁄ ln(k + 2) C = the highest energy barrier to cross. Very slow, but with it annealing finds the global optimum with probability approaching 1. Constant Tk = C This is the Metropolis algorithm, no cooling. Exponential Tk = T0 αk The closed form of geometric cooling. Remember Choose T0 so that most worse moves are accepted at the start, often from the average cost difference between neighbours. Too fast cooling turns annealing into plain hill climbing. Lesson 5 of 8"],[8,"maths","The maths: acceptance, restarts and gradients","Worked example: acceptance probability A worse move with ΔE = −2. At T = 10: e−2⁄10 = e−0.2 ≈ 0.819. Accepted about 82 percent of the time. At T = 1: e−2 ≈ 0.135. at T = 0.2: e−10 ≈ 0.0000454, almost never Worked example: geometric cooling T0 = 100, α = 0.9. After k steps T = 100 × 0.9k. We want T < 1: 0.9k < 0.01, so k > ln 0.01 ⁄ ln 0.9 = 4.605 ⁄ 0.1054 ≈ 43.7. 44 steps to cool from 100 to below 1 Gradient ascent for continuous statesExam favourite x ← x + η ∇f(x) In continuous spaces, move a small step η along the gradient. Too big η overshoots, too small is slow. Newton Raphson step x ← x − Hf(x)−1 ∇f(x) Uses the second derivatives (Hessian H) to choose the step. Very fast near an optimum. Probability of success with r restarts 1 − (1 − p)r p = 0.148, r = 10: 1 − 0.852¹⁰ ≈ 0.80. Lesson 6 of 8"],[8,"compare","Comparing local search methods","Source: slide Set 2, slide 87, food for thought VI. Hill climbing, simulated annealing and genetic algorithms (chapter 4.2) Hill climbing Simulated annealing Genetic algorithm Breadth of the search One state, narrow One state, wanders widely while hot A population, broad Tests worse solutions Never Yes, with probability eΔE⁄T Yes, weak individuals may still breed Avoids local optima No (only with restarts) Yes, if cooled slowly Often, through crossover and mutation Memory One state One state k states Speed Fastest per run Medium, depends on the schedule Slowest per generation Prefer it when The landscape is smooth or you can restart cheaply There are many local optima and one good answer is enough Good solutions are made of good parts that can be combined Bottom line: start with hill climbing and random restarts. Move to annealing when restarts keep landing on poor peaks, and to a genetic algorithm when solutions have reusable building blocks. Real world scenario: chip layout and timetabling Placing millions of logic cells on a chip so that wires are short is a huge optimisation problem. Simulated annealing was one of the first methods to do it well (Kirkpatrick and others, 1983), and annealing style placers are still used. University timetabling uses local search too: start with any timetable, then repeatedly move one lecture to a different room or time if it reduces clashes. Lesson 7 of 8"],[8,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Hill climbing is like walking up a hill in fog: you only feel the ground at your feet, so you always step uphill. You may end on a small hill. Annealing is like shaking a tray of marbles: shake hard at first, then gently, so the marbles settle into the deepest holes. Local search keeps one state and an objective. Hill climbing stops at local maxima, ridges and plateaux. Fixes: sideways moves, random restarts (1 ⁄ p runs), beam search. Annealing accepts worse moves with eΔE⁄T and cools T by a schedule. With logarithmic cooling Tk = C ⁄ ln(k + 2), annealing converges to a global optimum with probability 1, because it is a Markov chain whose stationary distribution concentrates on the optima as T → 0. The price is an impractically slow schedule, so real systems use geometric cooling and restarts. Research looks at parallel tempering (many temperatures that swap states), tabu search (forbid recent moves), large neighbourhood search that solves a sub problem exactly at each step, and quantum annealing hardware that uses tunnelling to cross energy barriers. Lesson 8 of 8"],[8,"recap","Chapter summary","Local search keeps one state, moves to better neighbours, and stores no path. It uses constant memory and works on huge spaces. Hill climbing takes the best neighbour until none is better. It stops at local maxima, ridges and plateaux. On 8 queens it solves about 14 percent of boards in about 4 steps. Sideways moves raise that to about 94 percent. Random restarts need about 1 ⁄ p runs. Local beam search keeps the best k states. Simulated annealing accepts a worse move with probability eΔE⁄T. High T explores, low T exploits. Cooling schedules: geometric α T, linear T0 − kΔT, logarithmic C ⁄ ln(k + 2), constant, exponential T0 αk. Next: chapter 4.2 searches with a whole population: genetic algorithms. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCount attacking queens, run hill climbing and simulated annealing on a small landscape, then write the 8 queens hill climber yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[9,"","Genetic algorithms","Population, fitness, selection, crossover and mutation, the slide x² example, and when a GA beats other local search. · genetic algorithm · crossover · mutation · roulette wheel · tournament selection · schema theorem · evolutionary search Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 4: Local and evolutionary search›Genetic algorithms Step 4 · Topic 2 of 2 · College Search with a whole population that evolves: fitter solutions get more children, children mix their parents, and small mutations keep variety. Follow the slide example that maximises x² one generation at a time. About 45 min8 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Search by evolution 2The slide example: maximise x² with 5 bits 3Selection, crossover and mutation in detail 4A GA for 8 queens 5The maths: selection pressure and schemata 6Comparing hill climbing, annealing and genetic algorithms 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[9,"idea","Search by evolution","Source: slide Set 2, slides 84 to 87, and the class of 1 February 2026. What it is A genetic algorithm (GA) keeps a population of candidate solutions, each written as a string (a chromosome) of genes. Better solutions are more likely to be chosen as parents, and children are made by mixing parents (crossover) and making small random changes (mutation). Why we need it It searches many regions at once, so it is less likely to get stuck than one hill climber. Crossover can join good parts found by different parents into one better solution. How it works Each generation: measure the fitness of every individual, select parents in proportion to fitness, cross them over at a random point, mutate a few genes, and replace the old population with the children. Repeat for many generations or until a good enough solution appears. Word Meaning In the x² example Gene One encoded decision value One bit Chromosome (individual) A full candidate solution, a string of genes 01101 Population The set of individuals in one generation 4 strings Fitness function How good an individual is (higher is better) f(x) = x², with x the number the bits encode Selection Choosing parents, fitter ones more often Roulette wheel Crossover Swap the tails of two parents after a cut point 0110|1 and 1100|0 give 01100 and 11001 Mutation A small random change Flip one bit Genetic algorithm function GENETIC-ALGORITHM(population, FITNESS): repeat: new_population = empty for i = 1 to size(population): x = RANDOM-SELECTION(population, FITNESS) y = RANDOM-SELECTION(population, FITNESS) child = REPRODUCE(x, y) # crossover at a random cut point with a small probability: child = MUTATE(child) add child to new_population population = new_population until some individual is fit enough, or enough time has passed return the best individual Lesson 2 of 8"],[9,"example","The slide example: maximise x² with 5 bits","Source: slide Set 2, slide 85. Find the 5 bit number x (0 to 31) that maximises f(x) = x². We know the answer is 11111 = 31, which lets us watch the GA head toward it. Start with four random strings. String x f(x) = x² P(pick) = f ⁄ ∑f Expected copies = f ⁄ average Actual copies 01101 13 169 0.14 0.58 1 11000 24 576 0.49 1.97 2 01000 8 64 0.05 (the slide rounds to 0.06) 0.22 0 10011 19 361 0.31 1.23 1 Total 1170 1.00 4.00 4 Average 292.5 (the slide rounds to 293) Maximum 576 Making the next generation Selection: the mating pool is 01101, 11000, 11000, 10011. The best string gets two copies, the worst none. Pair 1: 0110|1 and 1100|0, cut after bit 4. Children 01100 (x = 12, f = 144) and 11001 (x = 25, f = 625). Pair 2: 11|000 and 10|011, cut after bit 2. Children 11011 (x = 27, f = 729) and 10000 (x = 16, f = 256). New total 144 + 625 + 729 + 256 = 1754, average 438.5, maximum 729. in one generation the average rose from 292.5 to 438.5 and the best from 576 to 729 Common mistake The slide table lists x = 10 for the string 10000. In binary 10000 is 16, and 16² = 256 matches the f column, so x should read 16. Lesson 3 of 8"],[9,"operators","Selection, crossover and mutation in detail","Selection method How it works Strength Weakness Roulette wheel (fitness proportionate) P(i) = fi ⁄ ∑f. Spin a wheel with slices sized by fitness Simple, matches the theory One very fit individual can take over too early. Needs positive fitness Tournament Pick k individuals at random, the fittest of them wins Easy to tune with k, works with any fitness scale Large k is very greedy Rank Sort by fitness, pick by rank, not by raw value Not fooled by huge fitness gaps Ignores how much better the best is Elitism Copy the best few unchanged into the next generation Never loses the best found so far Can reduce diversity Operator (slide 84) Example Note One point crossover AB|CD and MN|OP give ABOP and MNCD The cut point is chosen at random Two point and uniform crossover Swap a middle segment, or each gene with chance 0.5 Mix more freely Flip mutation 01101 → 01001 For bit strings Switch (swap) mutation ABCD → ADCB Keeps a permutation valid, for example a tour Scramble mutation ABCD → ACBD (shuffle a segment) Bigger jump Common mistake For problems like the travelling salesperson, plain one point crossover makes invalid tours (a city twice). Use order crossover or swap mutation, which always give valid permutations. Lesson 4 of 8"],[9,"queens","A GA for 8 queens","AIMA writes an 8 queens state as 8 digits, the row of the queen in each column, for example 24748552. The fitness is the number of non attacking pairs, at most 28 for a solution. Selection in the AIMA example Four boards have fitness 24, 23, 20 and 11. The total is 78. Selection chances: 24 ⁄ 78 = 31 percent, 23 ⁄ 78 = 29 percent, 20 ⁄ 78 = 26 percent, 11 ⁄ 78 = 14 percent. Crossover joins the left columns of one parent with the right columns of the other. if each parent has its queens placed well on one side, the child can be good on both sides Fitness for 8 queens fitness = 28 − (number of attacking pairs) 28 is the number of pairs among 8 queens, 8 × 7 ⁄ 2. Search space for the digit string 88 ≈ 16.8 million The same as for hill climbing on 8 queens. Remember Crossover helps when nearby genes form meaningful blocks, like the left half of the board. If the encoding scatters related decisions, crossover mostly breaks good solutions, and a GA is no better than random restarts. Lesson 5 of 8"],[9,"maths","The maths: selection pressure and schemata","Roulette wheel probabilityExam favourite P(i) = fi ⁄ ∑j fj Expected copies of i in a population of n: n P(i) = fi ⁄ f̄. Schema theorem (Holland) E[m(H, t + 1)] ≥ m(H, t) · (f(H) ⁄ f̄) · [1 − pc δ(H) ⁄ (l − 1) − o(H) pm] m(H, t) individuals match schema H (like 1***1). f(H) is their average fitness, δ(H) the defining length, o(H) the order (fixed positions), l the string length, pc and pm the crossover and mutation rates. Chance a string escapes mutation (1 − pm)l With pm = 0.01 and l = 5: 0.99⁵ ≈ 0.951. Worked example: the schema 1**** in the slide example Strings matching 1****: 11000 (576) and 10011 (361). m = 2, average f(H) = (576 + 361) ⁄ 2 = 468.5. Population average f̄ = 292.5. δ(H) = 0 (one fixed position) and o(H) = 1, so crossover cannot break it. With no mutation the factor in brackets is 1. E[m] ≥ 2 × 468.5 ⁄ 292.5 ≈ 3.2 copies next generation (all 4 children start with 1 except 01100) Remember Short, low order schemata with above average fitness grow exponentially. These are the \"building blocks\" that crossover combines. Lesson 6 of 8"],[9,"compare","Comparing hill climbing, annealing and genetic algorithms","Source: slide Set 2, slide 86. The slide 86 comparison Feature Hill climbing Simulated annealing Genetic algorithm Inspiration Climbing a hill Annealing in metallurgy Natural selection and evolution Search space Local Broad (with controlled randomness) Very broad (population based) Moves to worse solutions No Yes (probabilistic) Yes (via mutation and crossover) Avoids local optima No Yes Yes Memory usage Low Medium High Parameter tuning needed Minimal High (temperature, cooling) High (population, mutation rate) Speed Fast Moderate Slower (population evolution) Complexity Simple Moderate High Best use case Small or simple problems Problems with many local optima Complex optimisation problems Bottom line: a GA is worth its cost when good solutions are built from parts that can be found separately and combined. Otherwise annealing or restarts are simpler. Real world scenario: designing an antenna NASA used an evolutionary algorithm to design the antenna of the ST5 spacecraft (2006). Each individual was a sequence of bending and branching instructions. The fitness came from a simulation of the radio pattern. The evolved shape looked like a bent paper clip that no engineer would draw, yet it met the mission needs, which is the classic strength of evolutionary search: it does not need intuition about what a good design looks like. Lesson 7 of 8"],[9,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A genetic algorithm copies nature: the fitter animals have more children, children mix their parents, and small random changes sometimes help. Over many generations the population gets better. Encode solutions as strings, choose parents by fitness (roulette, tournament, rank, elitism), cross over at a random point, mutate with a small probability. In the slide example the average fitness rose from 292.5 to 438.5 in one generation. The schema theorem explains why short, fit building blocks spread. Diversity matters: too much selection pressure leads to premature convergence on one local optimum. Encodings must keep children valid (order crossover for permutations). Research descendants include evolution strategies with self adapted mutation (CMA-ES), genetic programming that evolves programs as trees, neuroevolution of network weights and shapes (NEAT), and multi objective GAs (NSGA-II) that return a Pareto front of trade offs instead of one answer. Lesson 8 of 8"],[9,"recap","Chapter summary","A genetic algorithm evolves a population of chromosomes (strings of genes) using a fitness function. Each generation: selection by fitness, crossover at a cut point, mutation with a small probability. Roulette wheel: P(i) = fi ⁄ ∑f, expected copies fi ⁄ f̄. Slide example: 169, 576, 64, 361, total 1170, chances 0.14, 0.49, 0.06, 0.31. After one generation the children are 01100, 11001, 11011, 10000 with best fitness 729 (x = 27). Crossover helps when good solutions are built from blocks. GAs search broadly but use more memory and need more tuning than hill climbing or annealing. Next: chapter 5.1 turns to problems with explicit constraints: constraint satisfaction. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCompute the slide fitness table, spin a roulette wheel, then write crossover, mutation and one full generation yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[10,"","Constraint satisfaction problems and arc consistency","CSP formulation, constraint types, node and arc consistency, AC-3 and its three outcomes. · constraint satisfaction · CSP · AC-3 · arc consistency · map colouring · job scheduling · N queens Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 5: Constraint satisfaction›CSPs and AC-3 Step 5 · Topic 1 of 3 · College Some problems are best written as variables that need values under rules. Learn to formulate map colouring, N queens and job scheduling as CSPs, and to shrink domains with node and arc consistency and the AC-3 algorithm. About 50 min8 lessons2 animations6 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a constraint satisfaction problem is 2More CSPs: N queens and job scheduling 3Node and arc consistency 4The AC-3 algorithm 5Forward checking against arc consistency 6The maths: counting assignments and solutions 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[10,"idea","What a constraint satisfaction problem is","Source: slide Set 4, slides 1 to 4, and the class of 8 February 2026. In ordinary search a state is a black box: the algorithm can only ask \"is this the goal?\" and \"what are the successors?\". In a constraint satisfaction problem (CSP) the state has an inner structure, a set of variables with values, and the goal is described by rules, so the algorithm can look inside and reason. What it is A CSP has a set of variables X = {X1, ..., Xn}, a domain Di of allowed values for each variable, and a set of constraints C that say which combinations of values are allowed. A solution is a complete assignment that breaks no constraint. Why we need it Only the final assignment matters, not the path. Writing the problem as variables and constraints lets general methods prune huge parts of the space at once: one rule like \"WA and NT differ\" removes every assignment that breaks it. How it works Assign values one variable at a time, and after each choice use the constraints to remove values that can no longer work (constraint propagation). When a variable has no value left, go back (backtracking, chapter 5.2)."],[10,"idea","Example 1: colouring the map of Australia","Part Value Variables WA, NT, SA, Q, NSW, V, T Domains Di = {red, green, blue} Constraints Neighbouring regions differ: WA ≠ NT, WA ≠ SA, NT ≠ SA, NT ≠ Q, SA ≠ Q, SA ≠ NSW, SA ≠ V, Q ≠ NSW, NSW ≠ V A solution WA = red, NT = green, SA = blue, Q = red, NSW = green, V = red, T = green Python # Map colouring of Australia as a CSP. A full assignment is a solution if no two neighbours share a colour.neighbours = [(\"WA\", \"NT\"), (\"WA\", \"SA\"), (\"NT\", \"SA\"), (\"NT\", \"Q\"), (\"SA\", \"Q\"), (\"SA\", \"NSW\"), (\"SA\", \"V\"), (\"Q\", \"NSW\"), (\"NSW\", \"V\")]def broken(colour): bad = []for a, b in neighbours:if colour[a] == colour[b]: bad.append(a + \"-\" + b)return badgood = {\"WA\": \"red\", \"NT\": \"green\", \"SA\": \"blue\", \"Q\": \"red\", \"NSW\": \"green\", \"V\": \"red\", \"T\": \"green\"}poor = {\"WA\": \"red\", \"NT\": \"green\", \"SA\": \"blue\", \"Q\": \"green\", \"NSW\": \"red\", \"V\": \"red\", \"T\": \"red\"}print(\"good assignment breaks:\", broken(good))print(\"poor assignment breaks:\", broken(poor))print(\"number of full assignments:\", 3 ** 7) Output good assignment breaks: [] poor assignment breaks: ['NT-Q', 'NSW-V'] number of full assignments: 2187 The constraint graph has a node for each variable and an edge for each binary constraint. Tasmania (T) has no edges, so it is an independent sub problem: any colour works. Lesson 2 of 8"],[10,"examples","More CSPs: N queens and job scheduling","Source: slide Set 4, slides 3 and 4."],[10,"examples","N queens with Boolean variables (slide 3)","Part Formulation Variables Xij for 0 ≤ i, j < N, one for each square (row i, column j) Domains {0, 1}: 1 means a queen stands on the square Same row ∀ i, j, k: (Xij, Xik) ∈ {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)}, at most one queen Same column ∀ i, j, k: (Xij, Xkj) ∈ {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)} Diagonals (Xij, Xi+k, j+k) and (Xij, Xi+k, j−k) ∈ {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)} Count ∑i, j Xij = N, exactly N queens A smaller formulation uses one variable Qj per column with domain {0, ..., N − 1} (the row of its queen). Then \"one queen per column\" is built in, and only rows and diagonals need constraints: Qj ≠ Qk and |Qj − Qk| ≠ |j − k|."],[10,"examples","Job scheduling: assembling a car (slide 4)","Part Formulation Variables AxleF, AxleB, WheelRF, WheelLF, WheelRB, WheelLB, Nuts for each wheel, Cap for each wheel, Inspect: 15 tasks, each value is a start time Precedence T1 + d1 ≤ T2: task 1 must finish before task 2 starts. AxleF + 10 ≤ WheelRF, WheelRF + 1 ≤ NutsRF, NutsRF + 2 ≤ CapRF, ... Disjunctive The two axles share one tool: AxleF + 10 ≤ AxleB or AxleB + 10 ≤ AxleF Inspection last X + dX ≤ Inspect for every other task X, and Inspect takes 3 minutes Deadline 30 minutes Di = {1, 2, 3, ..., 27}, so even Inspect ends by minute 30 Kind of constraint Example Unary (one variable) SA ≠ green, B ≠ 3 Binary (two variables) WA ≠ NT, A < B Global (any number) Alldiff(all cells in a Sudoku row) Preference (soft) Prof. R prefers morning lectures. Breaking it costs, but is allowed (constrained optimisation) Lesson 3 of 8"],[10,"consistency","Node and arc consistency","Source: slide Set 4, slide 6. What it is Constraint propagation removes values from domains that cannot be part of any solution, before or during search. Node consistency handles unary constraints and arc consistency handles binary ones. Why we need it Every value removed now is a whole branch the search never has to try. Sometimes propagation alone solves the problem or proves it impossible, with no search at all. How it works A variable is node consistent if every value in its domain satisfies its unary constraints, so dom(B) = {1, 2, 3, 4} is not node consistent under B ≠ 3. An arc ⟨X, r(X, Y)⟩ is arc consistent if for each value x in dom(X) there is some y in dom(Y) with r(x, y) true. If not, delete the values of X that have no partner. Arc consistencyExam favourite ∀ x ∈ dom(X) ∃ y ∈ dom(Y): r(x, y) Every value of X has a supporting value of Y. A network is arc consistent when all its arcs are. The three outcomes (slide 6) a domain is empty ⇒ no solution. Every domain has one value ⇒ the unique solution. Some domains have several values ⇒ search is still needed Arc consistency alone does not always decide the problem. The constraint network of the slides has one node per variable, one node per constraint, and an edge between a variable and each constraint it appears in. Each binary constraint gives two arcs, one in each direction. Lesson 4 of 8"],[10,"ac3","The AC-3 algorithm","Source: slide Set 4, slide 7. AC-3 function AC-3(csp): queue = all arcs (X, Y) of the csp while queue is not empty: (X, Y) = remove the first arc if REVISE(X, Y): if dom(X) is empty: return false # no solution for each neighbour Z of X except Y: add (Z, X) to queue return true function REVISE(X, Y): revised = false for each x in dom(X): if no y in dom(Y) satisfies the constraint with x: delete x from dom(X) and set revised = true return revised AC-3 on the slide network A < B < C with domains {1, 2, 3} Arc (A, B): A = 3 has no larger B. Remove 3 from A. dom(A) = {1, 2}. Arc (B, A): B = 1 has no smaller A. Remove 1 from B. dom(B) = {2, 3}. Arc (B, C): B = 3 has no larger C. Remove 3. dom(B) = {2}. Add (A, B) to the queue again. Arc (C, B): C must be larger than 2, so C = 3. dom(C) = {3}. Arc (A, B) again: A must be less than 2, so A = 1. A = 1, B = 2, C = 3: every domain has one value, the unique solution, found with no search Cost of AC-3Exam favourite O(c d³) c binary constraints, domains of size at most d. Each arc can be added to the queue at most d times (once per value deleted from its head), and REVISE costs d² checks. Lesson 5 of 8"],[10,"fcvsac","Forward checking against arc consistency","Source: slide Set 4, slide 7. Two kinds of inference Forward checking Arc consistency (AC-3) What it checks Only arcs from the variable just assigned to its unassigned neighbours Every arc in the network, again and again until nothing changes Strength Weaker Stronger: it also catches conflicts between two unassigned variables Cost per step Cheap: O(d) per neighbour Heavier: up to O(c d³) Australia, after WA = red and Q = green NT = {blue}, SA = {blue}, and no failure is seen yet Checks NT against SA, finds both must be blue, empties a domain: failure detected at once Bottom line: forward checking is cheap and often enough. Running AC-3 after each choice (MAC, chapter 5.2) prunes more and is the usual choice for hard problems."],[10,"fcvsac","Stronger consistency","Level Meaning Node (1 consistency) Each value meets its unary constraints Arc (2 consistency) Each value of a variable has a partner in every neighbour Path (3 consistency) Each consistent pair (x, y) can be extended to any third variable z k consistency Any consistent assignment to k − 1 variables extends to any k-th variable Strong n consistency k consistent for every k ≤ n: then a solution can be found with no backtracking, but reaching it costs exponential time in general Common mistake Arc consistency cannot see that 2 colours are not enough for a triangle WA, NT, SA: each arc on its own is fine ({red, blue} against {red, blue}). Path consistency catches it. Lesson 6 of 8"],[10,"maths","The maths: counting assignments and solutions","Number of complete assignmentsExam favourite dn n variables, domain size d. Australia: 37 = 2,187. Naive search treeExam favourite n! · dn leaves If any unassigned variable can be chosen at each level: n d choices at the top, (n − 1) d next, and so on. Fixing the variable order (assignments commute) brings it back to dn. Solutions of the Australia map 3 × 2 × 3 = 18 Pick SA (3 ways). WA, NT, Q, NSW, V form a chain around SA and must alternate the other two colours (2 ways). T is free (3 ways). Worked example: why fixing the order matters Australia has n = 7 and d = 3. Naive tree: 7! × 3⁷ = 5,040 × 2,187 = 11,022,480 leaves. With a fixed order: 3⁷ = 2,187 leaves. assignments commute (WA = red then NT = green is the same as the reverse), so a CSP search needs only dn Worked example: Boolean N queens 4 queens with Xij: 16 Boolean variables. Complete assignments: 2¹⁶ = 65,536. With one variable per column (domain size 4): 4⁴ = 256. a good formulation shrinks the space 256 times Lesson 7 of 8"],[10,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A CSP is like a seating plan: everyone needs a seat (a value), and some people cannot sit next to each other (constraints). You fill seats one by one and cross out seats that would break a rule. A CSP is (X, D, C). Constraints can be unary, binary, global or soft. Node consistency fixes unary constraints, arc consistency makes every value have a partner. AC-3 runs in O(c d³) and ends with an empty domain (no solution), single values (unique solution) or a need to search. Any finite CSP can be turned into a binary CSP using auxiliary variables (dual graph). Global constraints have special propagators, for example Alldiff with matching gives stronger pruning than pairwise ≠. Strong k consistency removes the need to backtrack but costs exponential time in k. Modern solvers mix constraint propagation with SAT style clause learning (lazy clause generation), and constraint programming is used for industrial scheduling, rostering and chip verification. Research studies tractable classes by the structure of the constraint graph (tree width) and by the type of constraints (CSP dichotomy theorem, proved in 2017). Real world scenario: university exam timetabling Each exam is a variable, its domain is the set of time slots, and two exams that share a student must not be in the same slot (a binary ≠ constraint, like map colouring). Room capacity gives global constraints, and \"no student has two exams in one day\" gives more. Universities solve this every term with constraint solvers that combine propagation and search. Lesson 8 of 8"],[10,"recap","Chapter summary","A CSP is variables X, domains D and constraints C. A solution assigns every variable with no constraint broken. Only the final assignment matters. Examples: Australia map colouring (37 = 2,187 assignments, 18 solutions), N queens (Boolean Xij or one variable per column), and car assembly scheduling with precedence and disjunctive constraints. Constraints are unary, binary, global (Alldiff) or soft (preferences). Arc consistency: every value of X has a partner in Y. AC-3 repeats REVISE until no arc changes, in O(c d³). Outcomes: an empty domain means no solution, single values give the unique solution, otherwise search is needed. A < B < C with {1, 2, 3} gives A = 1, B = 2, C = 3. Forward checking only looks at the arcs from the variable just assigned. Arc consistency looks at all arcs and finds failures earlier. Next: chapter 5.2 searches for solutions with backtracking and the MRV, degree and LCV heuristics. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCheck map colourings, run AC-3 on the slide network, then write REVISE and AC-3 yourself and count the solutions of a small map.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[11,"","Backtracking search for CSPs","Backtracking, MRV, degree, LCV, forward checking, MAC, backjumping and learning. · backtracking search · MRV · degree heuristic · LCV · forward checking · MAC · backjumping Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 5: Constraint satisfaction›CSP backtracking Step 5 · Topic 2 of 3 · College Solve CSPs by assigning one variable at a time and going back as soon as a rule breaks. Choose the next variable with MRV and degree, the value with LCV, and prune ahead with forward checking and MAC. About 50 min8 lessons3 animations3 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Backtracking search 2Which variable next, which value first 3Forward checking and maintaining arc consistency 4Smarter backtracking: backjumping and learning 5The maths: branching and pruning 6Comparing CSP search strategies 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[11,"idea","Backtracking search","Source: slide Set 4, slide 5, and the class of 8 February 2026. What it is Backtracking search is depth first search that assigns one variable at a time and goes back as soon as a constraint is broken. Why we need it Arc consistency alone often leaves several values per variable. Plain search over complete assignments tries all dn of them. Backtracking checks constraints after each single choice, so a bad partial assignment is abandoned at once, together with every completion of it. How it works Pick an unassigned variable, try each value in its domain, and keep a value only if it fits with the values already chosen. Recurse. If no value works, undo the last choice and try that variable's next value. BACKTRACKING-SEARCH (AIMA figure 6.5) function BACKTRACK(assignment, csp): if assignment is complete: return assignment var = SELECT-UNASSIGNED-VARIABLE(csp, assignment) # MRV, degree for each value in ORDER-DOMAIN-VALUES(var, assignment): # LCV if value is consistent with assignment: add {var = value} to assignment inferences = INFERENCE(csp, var, value) # forward checking or MAC if inferences is not failure: add inferences to csp result = BACKTRACK(assignment, csp) if result is not failure: return result remove inferences from csp remove {var = value} from assignment return failure Backtracking only needs to consider one variable per level, because assignments commute: WA = red then NT = green is the same as NT = green then WA = red. That shrinks the tree from n! dn to dn leaves. Backtracking search colours the map of AustraliaNeighbours must get different colours. Small squares show the colours each region still has left. Step 0 of 21 Choose the next region by Try colours in order Inference after each choice Number of colours3 Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Plain backtracking in the fixed order. WA red, NT green and NSW red force Q to be blue, and then SA has no colour left. The search must back up and recolour NSW. Same order with forward checking. Each choice removes that colour from the neighbours, so a dead end is spotted as soon as a domain becomes empty. MRV with degree tie breaking and forward checking. SA goes first (degree 5) and the map is coloured with no backtracking at all. Only 2 colours. WA, NT and SA all touch each other, so it is impossible. Watch how quickly MAC proves it. Python # Plain backtracking on the map of Australia, fixed order and fixed colour order.order = [\"WA\", \"NT\", \"NSW\", \"Q\", \"SA\", \"V\", \"T\"]neighbours = {\"WA\": [\"NT\", \"SA\"], \"NT\": [\"WA\", \"SA\", \"Q\"], \"SA\": [\"WA\", \"NT\", \"Q\", \"NSW\", \"V\"],\"Q\": [\"NT\", \"SA\", \"NSW\"], \"NSW\": [\"Q\", \"SA\", \"V\"], \"V\": [\"SA\", \"NSW\"], \"T\": []}colours = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]tries = 0def backtrack(assignment):global triesiflen(assignment) == len(order):return assignment var = order[len(assignment)] # next variable in the fixed orderfor c in colours: tries = tries + 1 clash = Falsefor n in neighbours[var]:if assignment.get(n) == c: clash = Trueifnot clash: assignment[var] = c result = backtrack(assignment)if result isnotNone:return resultdel assignment[var] # undo and try the next colourprint(\"backtrack: undo\", var, \"=\", c)returnNoneprint(backtrack({}))print(\"colours tried:\", tries) Output backtrack: undo Q = blue backtrack: undo NSW = red {'WA': 'red', 'NT': 'green', 'NSW': 'green', 'Q': 'red', 'SA': 'blue', 'V': 'red', 'T': 'red'} colours tried: 17 In the fixed order WA, NT, NSW, Q, SA, V, T, the choices WA = red, NT = green, NSW = red force Q = blue, and then SA has no colour left. Backtracking undoes Q and then NSW, tries NSW = green, and succeeds. Lesson 2 of 8"],[11,"ordering","Which variable next, which value first","Source: slide Set 4, slide 5. Heuristic Rule Why it helps Slide example Minimum remaining values (MRV), also \"most constrained variable\" or \"fail first\" Choose the variable with the fewest legal values left If some variable has no values left, MRV picks it and the failure is found at once, before wasting time on others After WA and NT are coloured, SA has only one colour left, so colour SA next Degree heuristic Choose the variable involved in the most constraints on other unassigned variables Lowers the branching factor of future choices. Used to break ties of MRV, and at the very start when all domains are equal Choose SA first: it has degree 5 Least constraining value (LCV) Try first the value that rules out the fewest choices for neighbouring variables Leaves the most room for the rest, so a solution is found sooner After WA = red and NT = green, Q = blue would remove SA's last value, so LCV prefers Q = red In the exam Variable ordering is fail first: pick the hardest variable so a dead end shows up early. Value ordering is succeed first: pick the value most likely to work. When you want all solutions, or none exists, value ordering does not matter, because every value gets tried anyway. With MRV, degree tie breaking, LCV and forward checking, SA is chosen first and the map is coloured with no backtracking at all. Lesson 3 of 8"],[11,"inference","Forward checking and maintaining arc consistency","What it is Inference during search removes values from the domains of unassigned variables right after each choice. Why we need it Without inference, backtracking only notices a problem when it reaches the stuck variable, possibly many levels later. Inference notices it immediately, when a domain becomes empty. How it works Forward checking: after X = x, remove x (or any value in conflict with it) from the domain of each unassigned neighbour of X. MAC (maintaining arc consistency): after X = x, run AC-3 starting with the arcs (Y, X) for the unassigned neighbours Y, so the removals spread through the whole network. Forward checking on Australia (fixed order) WA = red. Remove red from NT and SA. NT = green. Remove green from SA and Q. SA = {blue}. NSW = red. Remove red from Q and V. Q = {blue}. Q = blue. Remove blue from SA. SA = { } is empty, so this branch fails at once, without trying SA. forward checking spots the dead end one level earlier than plain backtracking Common mistake Forward checking misses conflicts between two unassigned variables. After WA = red and Q = green, NT and SA both have only blue left and are neighbours. Forward checking does not see it. MAC does, because it also checks the arc NT, SA. Lesson 4 of 8"],[11,"smarter","Smarter backtracking: backjumping and learning","Plain backtracking goes back to the most recent variable (chronological backtracking), even if that variable had nothing to do with the failure. Example: why chronological backtracking wastes time Fixed order Q, NSW, V, T, SA, WA, NT. Assign Q = red, NSW = green, V = blue, T = red. SA neighbours Q, NSW and V, which use all three colours, so SA has no value. Chronological backtracking goes back to T and tries T = green, then T = blue. Neither helps: T is not connected to SA. backjumping goes straight back to V, the most recent variable in SA's conflict set {Q, NSW, V} Method Idea Backjumping Jump back to the most recent variable in the conflict set of the failed variable (the assigned variables that removed its values) Conflict directed backjumping When jumping back from X to Y, add X's conflict set (minus Y) to Y's, so the next jump from Y can also skip unrelated variables Constraint learning (no goods) Record the smallest set of assignments that caused the failure, for example {Q = red, NSW = green, V = blue}, and never try that combination again Symmetry breaking In map colouring, any solution with colours permuted is also a solution. Fix WA = red at the start to cut the work by 3! = 6 Remember Forward checking already finds every conflict that simple backjumping would jump over, so backjumping adds little to forward checking. Conflict directed backjumping and learning do add power, and they are the core of modern SAT solvers. Lesson 5 of 8"],[11,"maths","The maths: branching and pruning","Worst case of backtrackingExam favourite O(dn) Every partial assignment may be extended until the last variable. General CSPs are NP complete (3 colouring is), so no method avoids this in the worst case. Forward checking cost per assignment O(e d) at most, e = number of neighbours Each neighbour loses at most the conflicting values. Symmetry k colours ⇒ k! equivalent solutions Fixing the first colour divides the search by k, fixing an ordering of all colours by k!. Worked example: nodes visited in the fixed order Plain backtracking on Australia in the fixed order tries 17 colours in total (the code prints this count). With MRV, degree, LCV and forward checking, 7 choices are made and none is undone. The full space has 3⁷ = 2,187 complete assignments. good ordering and inference turn search into almost straight line decisions Worked example: LCV counts After WA = red and NT = green, Q can be red or blue. Q = red removes red from NSW (and SA has no red). Remaining values for SA and NSW: SA {blue}, NSW {green, blue}: total 3. Q = blue removes blue from SA and NSW. Remaining: SA { }, NSW {red, green}: total 2, and SA is empty. LCV prefers Q = red, which leaves more choices Lesson 6 of 8"],[11,"compare","Comparing CSP search strategies","Backtracking with different helpers on Australia, 3 colours Method Order Inference Backtracks Plain backtracking Fixed WA, NT, NSW, Q, SA, V, T None 2 With forward checking Fixed Forward checking 2, but each dead end is found one level earlier MRV with degree and LCV SA first Forward checking 0 2 colours with MAC SA first MAC Proves no solution after trying both colours for SA Bottom line: the variable order matters most. MRV with degree tie breaking plus forward checking is a strong default, and MAC pays off on hard or impossible problems. Backtracking for CSPs against plain search Search over states (chapter 2) CSP backtracking State Black box Partial assignment with visible structure Pruning Only by the goal test or h Constraints prune at every step, inference removes values ahead Heuristics Problem specific h General ones that work for any CSP: MRV, degree, LCV Bottom line: the structure of a CSP gives general purpose heuristics for free. That is the big win of formulating a problem as a CSP. Real world scenario: Sudoku A Sudoku is a CSP with 81 variables, domains {1, ..., 9}, and 27 Alldiff constraints (rows, columns, boxes). Newspaper puzzles are usually solved by arc consistency alone. Harder ones need search, and MRV picks the cell with the fewest candidates first, which is exactly what a human expert does when they look for a cell with only one or two possible digits. Lesson 7 of 8"],[11,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths To colour a map, start with the hardest region (the one touching the most others), pick the colour that blocks the fewest neighbours, and cross out colours as you go. If you get stuck, rub out the last choice and try again. Backtracking assigns one variable per level. MRV and degree choose the variable, LCV the value. Forward checking and MAC remove values ahead and catch dead ends early. Backjumping and learning avoid repeating the same mistake. CSPs are NP complete in general, so the worst case stays exponential, but heuristics change the average case enormously. Conflict directed backjumping and no good learning give the same kind of power as clause learning in SAT solvers. Random restarts with heavy tailed run times make solvers far more robust. State of the art constraint solvers (for example Google OR-Tools CP-SAT) combine propagation, clause learning, restarts and linear relaxations. Research studies phase transitions in random CSPs (hard instances sit at a critical ratio of constraints to variables) and learned branching heuristics. Lesson 8 of 8"],[11,"recap","Chapter summary","Backtracking is DFS that assigns one variable at a time and undoes a choice as soon as a constraint breaks. Commutativity means one variable per level, dn leaves. MRV (fail first): fewest legal values. Degree: most constraints on unassigned variables (SA first). LCV: the value that rules out the fewest neighbour values (Q = red, not blue). Forward checking removes conflicting values from neighbours after each choice. MAC runs AC-3 and also catches conflicts between unassigned variables. Australia in the fixed order needs 2 backtracks. MRV with degree, LCV and forward checking needs 0. Backjumping, conflict directed backjumping and no good learning avoid going back to variables that did not cause the failure. Next: chapter 5.3 solves CSPs with local search (min-conflicts) and by using the shape of the constraint graph. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun plain backtracking and MRV with forward checking on Australia, then write backtracking and the MRV choice yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[12,"","Local search for CSPs and problem structure","Min-conflicts local search, tabu and weighting, tree CSPs, cutset conditioning and tree decomposition. · min-conflicts · local search CSP · tree structured CSP · cutset conditioning · tree decomposition · tabu search Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 5: Constraint satisfaction›Min-conflicts and structure Step 5 · Topic 3 of 3 · College Repair a complete assignment instead of building one: min-conflicts solves huge N queens problems in a few steps. Then use the shape of the constraint graph: trees are solved in linear time, and cutsets turn near trees into trees. About 45 min8 lessons3 animations8 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Local search for CSPs: min-conflicts 2Plateaux, tabu search and constraint weighting 3Using the shape of the problem: independent parts and trees 4Cutset conditioning and tree decomposition 5The maths of problem structure 6Backtracking against min-conflicts against structure 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[12,"idea","Local search for CSPs: min-conflicts","Source: slide Set 2, slides 78 and 79, and slide Set 4. What it is Min-conflicts starts with a complete assignment, maybe breaking many constraints, and repeatedly picks a variable that is in conflict and gives it the value that breaks the fewest constraints. Why we need it Backtracking builds a solution one variable at a time and can get lost deep in a huge tree. For many large CSPs, solutions are spread densely all over the space, so starting from a full guess and repairing it is much faster. How it works Assign every variable at random. Loop: if no constraint is broken, stop. Otherwise choose a conflicted variable at random, count the conflicts for each of its values, and move it to a value with the fewest conflicts (ties at random). MIN-CONFLICTS (AIMA figure 6.8) function MIN-CONFLICTS(csp, max_steps): current = a complete assignment (random, or greedy) for i = 1 to max_steps: if current is a solution: return current var = a randomly chosen conflicted variable value = the value v of var that minimises CONFLICTS(var, v, current) set var = value in current return failure Min-conflicts local search for N queensOne queen per column. Each step picks a queen that is attacked and moves it, within its column, to the row with the fewest attacks. Step 0 of 8 Board size N6 Random seed for the start48 Most moves allowed30 Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Six queens. A random full placement is fixed in a handful of moves. The numbers in the chosen column are the attacks a queen would face in each row. Six queens with seed 9. Here it wanders for a while through placements with one attacking pair before it finds a solution. Plateaus slow min-conflicts down. Ten queens. Min-conflicts still needs only a few moves, which is why it solves even a million queens quickly. Python # Min-conflicts for 4 queens. board[c] is the row of the queen in column c.import randomrandom.seed(2)n = 4board = [random.randrange(n) for c inrange(n)] # a random complete assignmentdef conflicts(col, row): count = 0for c inrange(n):if c != col and (board[c] == row or abs(board[c] - row) == abs(c - col)): count = count + 1return countprint(\"start:\", board)for step inrange(10): attacked = [c for c inrange(n) if conflicts(c, board[c]) > 0]iflen(attacked) == 0:print(\"solved:\", board)break col = random.choice(attacked) # a variable in conflict, picked at random counts = [conflicts(col, r) for r inrange(n)] best = min(counts) board[col] = random.choice([r for r inrange(n) if counts[r] == best])print(\"column\", col, \"attacks per row\", counts, \"-> move to row\", board[col]) Output start: [0, 0, 0, 2] column 1 attacks per row [3, 2, 1, 0] -> move to row 3 column 2 attacks per row [1, 1, 3, 2] -> move to row 0 column 0 attacks per row [1, 0, 3, 1] -> move to row 1 solved: [1, 3, 0, 2] AIMA reports that min-conflicts solves even the million queens problem in about 50 steps on average after the initial assignment, and that a min-conflicts style method cut the time to schedule a week of Hubble Space Telescope observations from three weeks to around 10 minutes. Lesson 2 of 8"],[12,"landscape","Plateaux, tabu search and constraint weighting","Min-conflicts is hill climbing (chapter 4.1) with the number of broken constraints as the cost. It has the same traps: plateaux where every move keeps the same number of conflicts, and local minima. Fix Idea Sideways moves Allow moves to a value with the same number of conflicts, so the search can cross a plateau Tabu search Keep a short list of recently visited states (or recently changed variables) and do not go back to them, so the search does not bounce between the same few states Constraint weighting Give every constraint a weight, starting at 1. After each step, add 1 to the weight of every broken constraint. Minimise the total weight. Hard constraints that keep breaking become expensive, which changes the landscape and pushes the search out of local minima Random restarts and random walk Start again, or sometimes make a random move instead of the best one Online repair When the problem changes (a flight is cancelled), start min-conflicts from the old solution: only a few variables need to change Common mistake Local search cannot prove that a CSP has no solution. It just fails to find one. Backtracking with propagation can prove it, for example that 2 colours are not enough for Australia. Lesson 3 of 8"],[12,"structure","Using the shape of the problem: independent parts and trees","Independent sub problemsExam favourite connected components solved separately: O(dc · n ⁄ c) instead of O(dn) Tasmania is not connected to the mainland, so colour it on its own. If n = 80 splits into 4 parts of 20 with d = 2: 4 × 2²⁰ ≈ 4 million against 2⁸⁰ ≈ 10²⁴. Tree structured CSPExam favourite solvable in O(n d²) If the constraint graph is a tree (no loops), there is no need to backtrack at all. The tree algorithm Pick any variable as the root and list the variables so that each parent comes before its children (a topological order). Backward pass: for j from n down to 2, make the arc (parent of Xj, Xj) arc consistent. This is called directional arc consistency. Forward pass: for j from 1 to n, give Xj any value consistent with its parent's value. each arc is revised once at cost d², so the total is O(n d²), and the forward pass never gets stuck A chain A < B < C < D is a tree. After arc consistency each variable has two values left, and a forward pass in order A, B, C, D always finds a solution, for example A = 1, B = 2, C = 3, D = 4. Lesson 4 of 8"],[12,"cutset","Cutset conditioning and tree decomposition","What it is Cutset conditioning turns a graph with loops into a tree by assigning a few variables first. Tree decomposition groups variables into overlapping clusters that form a tree. Why we need it Most real constraint graphs are not trees, but many are close to trees. Both methods use that closeness to get far below dn. How it works Cutset: choose a set S of variables whose removal leaves a tree (a cycle cutset). For every consistent assignment of S, remove the values in the rest that clash with it, and solve the remaining tree in O((n − c) d²). Tree decomposition: solve each cluster as a mega variable whose values are the cluster's solutions, and join the clusters with the tree algorithm. Cutset conditioningExam favourite O(dc · (n − c) d²) c is the size of the cutset. Australia: removing SA leaves a tree (a chain plus T), so c = 1. Tree decomposition O(n dw+1) w is the tree width: the size of the largest cluster minus 1. CSPs with small tree width are easy. Worked example: Australia with the cutset {SA} Assign SA one of 3 colours. Remove that colour from WA, NT, Q, NSW and V. They now form the chain WA, NT, Q, NSW, V, plus T on its own. Solve that tree in O((n − 1) d²) = 6 × 9 = 54 checks at most. total work at most 3 × 54 = 162 checks, against 3⁷ = 2,187 assignments Lesson 5 of 8"],[12,"maths","The maths of problem structure","Backtracking worst case O(dn) For any CSP. Tree CSP O(n d²) Linear in n. Cutset O(dc (n − c) d²) Exponential only in the cutset size c. Tree decomposition O(n dw+1) Exponential only in the tree width w. Worked example: how much a tree saves n = 20 variables, d = 3 values, the graph is a tree. Backtracking worst case: 3²⁰ ≈ 3.5 × 10⁹. Tree algorithm: 20 × 3² = 180 checks. about 19 million times less work Worked example: a cutset A graph of n = 30 variables with d = 2 becomes a tree after removing c = 3 variables. dc = 2³ = 8 assignments of the cutset. Each leaves a tree of 27 variables: 27 × 2² = 108 checks. 8 × 108 = 864 checks, against 2³⁰ ≈ 1.07 × 10⁹ Lesson 6 of 8"],[12,"compare","Backtracking against min-conflicts against structure","Three ways to solve a CSP Backtracking with propagation Min-conflicts (local search) Structure based (tree, cutset) Starts from An empty assignment A complete random assignment The shape of the constraint graph Complete (finds a solution if one exists) Yes No Yes Can prove no solution Yes No Yes Memory O(n) for the path O(n), one assignment O(n d) for the domains Best on Tight problems with few solutions Large problems with many solutions spread out (N queens, scheduling) Graphs that are trees or nearly trees N queens with a million queens Very slow About 50 steps (AIMA) Not a tree Bottom line: use min-conflicts when solutions are plentiful and the problem is big or keeps changing. Use backtracking with MRV and MAC when solutions are rare or you need a proof. Check the graph shape first: a tree is solved in linear time. Real world scenario: airline crew rescheduling When a storm cancels flights, an airline must reassign crews quickly. Starting from scratch would move almost everyone. Min-conflicts style repair starts from yesterday's schedule, which is almost right, and changes only the crews involved in broken constraints. The new schedule is close to the old one, which crews and passengers prefer. Lesson 7 of 8"],[12,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Min-conflicts is fixing a messy seating plan: put everyone somewhere, then keep moving one unhappy person to the seat where the fewest neighbours bother them, until everyone is happy. Min-conflicts: random full assignment, then move a conflicted variable to its least conflicting value. Very fast on N queens, but incomplete. Tree CSPs: O(n d²) with a backward arc consistency pass and a forward assignment pass. Cutset conditioning: O(dc(n − c)d²). Tree decomposition: O(n dw+1). Min-conflicts works well when the ratio of constraints to variables is low, because solutions are dense. Near the phase transition, where random problems switch from mostly solvable to mostly unsolvable, all methods slow down. Finding a minimum cycle cutset or an optimal tree decomposition is itself NP hard, so heuristics are used. Modern local search solvers for SAT and CSP (WalkSAT, constraint weighting methods such as breakout) are competitive on random instances, and hybrid solvers run local search and systematic search together, sharing learned clauses. Tree width also explains why Bayesian network inference (chapter 9.3) is easy on some graphs and hard on others. Lesson 8 of 8"],[12,"recap","Chapter summary","Min-conflicts: start from a complete assignment, pick a conflicted variable at random, move it to the value with the fewest conflicts. AIMA reports about 50 steps for a million queens. It is hill climbing on the number of broken constraints: helped by sideways moves, tabu lists, constraint weighting and restarts. It cannot prove that no solution exists. Independent sub problems are solved separately. Tree structured CSPs take O(n d²): directional arc consistency backward, then assign forward with no backtracking. Cutset conditioning: assign a cycle cutset of size c, then solve the tree, O(dc(n − c)d²). Australia has the cutset {SA}. Tree decomposition: O(n dw+1) for tree width w. Next: chapter 6.1 moves to games, where an opponent works against you: minimax. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun min-conflicts on 4 queens and the tree algorithm on a small map, then write min-conflicts for N queens and the tree solver yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[13,"","Games and minimax","Game formalism, minimax values, the slide examples, the cost of minimax and evaluation functions. · minimax · adversarial search · game tree · zero sum · evaluation function · tic-tac-toe · horizon effect Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 6: Adversarial search›Minimax Step 6 · Topic 1 of 3 · College When an opponent moves too, plan for the reply. Write a game as a search problem, back values up the game tree with minimax, and see why real programs cut off the search and estimate. About 45 min8 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Games as search problems 2The minimax algorithm 3The tic-tac-toe example of the slides 4Properties, and what to do when the tree is too big 5The maths: zero sum games and evaluation 6Minimax against other ways to choose a move 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[13,"idea","Games as search problems","Source: slide Set 3, slides 1 and 2, and the class of 8 February 2026. In a game, other agents act too, and they want something different. Each agent tries to raise its own utility or cut its loss, and one agent's move changes what the others can get. Uncertainty comes from not knowing what the opponent will do, which is called strategic uncertainty. Searching in such a setting is called adversarial search. Part (slide 2) Meaning Tic-tac-toe S0 The initial state The empty board PLAYER(s) Whose turn it is in state s X and O take turns ACTIONS(s) The legal moves in s The empty squares RESULT(s, a) The transition model: the state after move a The board with the new mark TERMINAL-TEST(s) True when the game is over Three in a row, or a full board UTILITY(s, p) The final payoff for player p in a terminal state s +1 win, −1 loss, 0 draw Deterministic Chance Perfect information Chess, Go, tic-tac-toe, checkers Backgammon, Monopoly Imperfect information Battleship, Kriegspiel Poker, bridge, Scrabble This chapter covers deterministic, two player, turn taking, zero sum games with perfect information: what one player wins, the other loses, so one number, the utility for MAX, describes both. Lesson 2 of 8"],[13,"minimax","The minimax algorithm","Source: slide Set 3, slides 3 and 4. What it is Minimax computes, for every node of the game tree, the best utility MAX can guarantee against an opponent who plays perfectly. MAX nodes take the largest child value and MIN nodes take the smallest. Why we need it You cannot pick a move by looking only at your own options: the opponent answers. Minimax assumes the worst sensible answer, so the move it picks is safe against any opponent. How it works Generate the game tree down to terminal states. Give each terminal its utility. Back the values up: max at MAX nodes, min at MIN nodes. The root value is what MAX can guarantee, and the move leading to it is the optimal move. Minimax valueExam favourite MINIMAX(s) = UTILITY(s) if s is terminal, maxa MINIMAX(RESULT(s, a)) if MAX moves, mina MINIMAX(RESULT(s, a)) if MIN moves A recursive definition, computed depth first. The slide example (three levels, 27 leaves) C nodes are MAX nodes over 3 leaves each: C1 = 5, C2 = 11, C3 = 8, C4 = 4, C5 = 12, C6 = 2, C7 = 3, C8 = 11, C9 = 5. B nodes are MIN nodes: B1 = min(5, 11, 8) = 5 (from C1), B2 = min(4, 12, 2) = 2 (from C6), B3 = min(3, 11, 5) = 3 (from C7). A is a MAX node: A = max(5, 2, 3) = 5. MAX plays towards B1 and is guaranteed at least 5 MINIMAX-DECISION function MINIMAX-DECISION(state): return the action a in ACTIONS(state) with the largest MIN-VALUE(RESULT(state, a)) function MAX-VALUE(state): if TERMINAL-TEST(state): return UTILITY(state) v = −∞ for each a in ACTIONS(state): v = max(v, MIN-VALUE(RESULT(state, a))) return v function MIN-VALUE(state): if TERMINAL-TEST(state): return UTILITY(state) v = +∞ for each a in ACTIONS(state): v = min(v, MAX-VALUE(RESULT(state, a))) return v Lesson 3 of 8"],[13,"ttt","The tic-tac-toe example of the slides","Source: slide Set 3, slide 5. The board is O X O on top, X _ X in the middle and _ O _ at the bottom. O is MAX and moves now. Three squares are free: the centre (square 4), bottom left (6) and bottom right (8). Minimax on this position (the slide numbers the boards 1 to 14) Board 2: O takes the centre. X can answer in either corner (boards 5 and 6), but O then takes the last square and wins with a diagonal (boards 11 and 12). Value +1, whatever X does: X's choice is irrelevant. Board 3: O takes bottom left. X takes the centre and completes the middle row (board 7). Value −1. Board 4: O takes bottom right. X takes the centre and wins the same way (board 9). Value −1. Level 1 is X's decision, a MIN level. Level 0 is O's decision, a MAX level. O chooses board 2 (the centre), the only move that wins Remember Boards 8, 10, 13 and 14 show what happens if X does not take the centre: O wins. Minimax ignores those lines because a perfect X would not play them. Lesson 4 of 8"],[13,"props","Properties, and what to do when the tree is too big","Source: slide Set 3, slide 6, food for thought. Property Minimax Complete Yes, if the tree is finite Optimal Yes, against an optimal opponent. Against a weak one it is safe but may miss chances Time O(bm): it visits the whole tree to depth m Space O(b m) when it explores depth first and generates all moves at once, O(m) when it generates one move at a time Data structures A recursion stack (or explicit stack) of states, the list of moves at each level, and the best value and move so far For chess, b ≈ 35 and a game lasts m ≈ 100 plies (moves by one player), so the tree has about 35100 ≈ 10154 nodes. Real programs stop early and estimate. Depth limited minimaxExam favourite replace TERMINAL-TEST by CUTOFF-TEST(s, d) and UTILITY by EVAL(s) Search to a fixed depth d, then use a heuristic evaluation of the position. Weighted linear evaluation EVAL(s) = w1 f1(s) + w2 f2(s) + ... + wn fn(s) Chess material: pawn 1, knight 3, bishop 3, rook 5, queen 9, counted for MAX minus for MIN. Problem with cutting off Fix Non quiet positions: the evaluation changes wildly in the next move (a capture is about to happen) Quiescence search: keep searching captures until the position is quiet Horizon effect: a delaying move pushes an unavoidable loss beyond the search depth, so it looks avoided Singular extensions: search a clearly best move one level deeper Too many moves to search Forward pruning: skip moves that look bad (beam search on moves). Risky but used in practice Lesson 5 of 8"],[13,"maths","The maths: zero sum games and evaluation","Zero sumExam favourite UTILITY(s, MAX) + UTILITY(s, MIN) = constant Chess: 1 + 0, 0 + 1 or ½ + ½. So MIN minimising MAX's utility is the same as maximising its own. Multiplayer games each node holds a vector (u1, ..., uk), and the player to move picks the child with the best uown With three players, alliances can appear and break, which plain minimax cannot express. Size of a game tree bm Tic-tac-toe has at most 9! = 362,880 move sequences (fewer, because games end early), and only 5,478 legal positions. Worked example: a material evaluation White has 8 pawns, 2 knights, 1 bishop, 2 rooks and 1 queen. Black has 7 pawns, 1 knight, 2 bishops, 2 rooks and 1 queen. White: 8 + 2 × 3 + 3 + 2 × 5 + 9 = 36. Black: 7 + 3 + 2 × 3 + 2 × 5 + 9 = 35. EVAL = 36 − 35 = +1 for White (a pawn up) Worked example: tree size A game with b = 10 and depth m = 6. Leaves: 10⁶ = 1,000,000. At 1 million leaves a second, about 1 second. At depth 8, 100 seconds. every two extra plies cost 100 times more for b = 10 Lesson 6 of 8"],[13,"compare","Minimax against other ways to choose a move","Choosing a move in a game Greedy (best immediate result) Minimax Expectimax (chapter 6.3) Assumes about the opponent Nothing, ignores replies Plays perfectly against you Plays at random with known chances Safe against a perfect opponent No Yes No Exploits a weak opponent Sometimes No, it is cautious Yes, if the chances are right Cost O(b) O(bm) O(bm) Slide tic-tac-toe Can pick a losing corner Picks the centre, wins Picks the centre too Bottom line: minimax is the right tool for two player zero sum games of skill. Alpha beta pruning (chapter 6.2) gives the same answer much faster. Real world scenario: food for thought: real uses of minimax Chess and checkers engines (Chinook solved checkers in 2007: perfect play is a draw). Security games, where a defender places guards against an attacker who adapts, use the same max min reasoning. Robust planning treats nature as an opponent: \"choose the plan whose worst case is best\", for example when a power grid operator plans for the worst line failure. The utility function must capture what matters: in chess material, king safety and mobility, in security the value of each target. Lesson 7 of 8"],[13,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Minimax is thinking \"if I do this, what is the worst my opponent can do to me?\", for every move, and choosing the move whose worst case is best. A game has S0, PLAYER, ACTIONS, RESULT, TERMINAL-TEST and UTILITY. Minimax backs up max at MAX nodes and min at MIN nodes. It is complete and optimal against a perfect opponent, O(bm) time and O(b m) space. Real programs cut off at a depth and use an evaluation function. The minimax value of a zero sum game equals the value from von Neumann's minimax theorem for mixed strategies when moves are simultaneous. For turn taking perfect information games, backward induction (Zermelo, 1913) shows a pure strategy optimum exists. The horizon effect and quiescence show why a static evaluation is fragile. Modern engines replace hand tuned evaluation with neural networks (Stockfish NNUE) or replace minimax by Monte Carlo tree search guided by networks (AlphaZero). Game theory research extends this to imperfect information (counterfactual regret minimisation in poker) and to many players. Lesson 8 of 8"],[13,"recap","Chapter summary","A game is a search problem with S0, PLAYER, ACTIONS, RESULT, TERMINAL-TEST and UTILITY. Two player zero sum games with perfect information are the classic case. Minimax: MAX nodes take the max of their children, MIN nodes the min, terminals give their utility. The root value is what MAX can guarantee. Slide example: C = 5, 11, 8, 4, 12, 2, 3, 11, 5, then B1 = 5, B2 = 2, B3 = 3, and A = 5. Slide tic-tac-toe: O takes the centre and wins. Complete for finite trees, optimal against a perfect opponent, O(bm) time and O(b m) space. Chess has about 10154 nodes. Cut off at a depth with an evaluation function, and watch for quiet positions and the horizon effect. Next: chapter 6.2 prunes the minimax tree with alpha and beta and gets the same answer much faster. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun minimax on the 27 leaf example and on the slide tic-tac-toe position, then write minimax for nested trees and for tic-tac-toe yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[14,"","Alpha beta pruning","Alpha and beta, the prune rule, the slide example, move ordering and the cost of alpha beta. · alpha beta pruning · alpha · beta · prune · move ordering · Knuth Moore · Deep Blue · effective branching factor Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 6: Adversarial search›Alpha beta Step 6 · Topic 2 of 3 · College Get the minimax answer while reading far fewer leaves. Learn what α and β mean, follow the slide example prune by prune, and see why move ordering lets the same time search twice as deep. About 45 min9 lessons2 animations5 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why we can skip parts of the tree 2The worked example of the slides 3The algorithm 4Move ordering decides how much is pruned 5The maths: how much faster, and how much deeper 6Food for thought from the slides 7Alpha beta against other game search methods 8From intuition to research 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[14,"idea","Why we can skip parts of the tree","Source: slide Set 3, slide 7, and the class of 8 February 2026. What it is Alpha beta pruning is minimax with two extra numbers, α and β, that let it skip branches which cannot change the final decision. It always returns the same root value as minimax. Why we need it Minimax looks at every leaf, and the tree grows as bm. Often one look at a branch already shows that a player will never let the game go there. Reading the rest of that branch is wasted work. How it works Search depth first, as minimax does. Carry α (the best value MAX can already guarantee on the path, a lower bound) and β (the best value MIN can already guarantee, an upper bound) down to the children. Send node values back up and update α at MAX nodes and β at MIN nodes. As soon as α ≥ β at a node, stop looking at its remaining children. Value (slide 7) Meaning Starts at Changed at α The highest value MAX can guarantee so far on the path to this node. A lower bound −∞ MAX nodes: α = max(α, value) β The lowest value MIN can guarantee so far on the path to this node. An upper bound +∞ MIN nodes: β = min(β, value) Direction (slide 7) What moves Parent to child The current α and β values Child to parent The node value, which then updates α (at a MAX parent) or β (at a MIN parent) The rule to prune: if at any point α ≥ β, the current branch cannot affect the final decision, because the opponent will avoid this path for a better one. So the branch is pruned and the search moves to the next branch. Common mistake The slide text says \"the initial value of alpha is +∞\" in the line about beta. That is a typing slip: it is β that starts at +∞, and α starts at −∞. Lesson 2 of 9"],[14,"example","The worked example of the slides","Source: slide Set 3, slides 8 and 9. A is MAX. Its children B and C are MIN. Their children D, E (under B) and F, G (under C) are MAX. The leaves are 2, 3 (under D), 5, 9 (under E), 0, 1 (under F) and 7, 5 (under G). In the animation the same nodes are called B1, B2 and C1 to C4. Step by step Start at A with α = −∞ and β = +∞. Pass them down to B and then to D. D is MAX. It reads 2 (α = 2), then 3 (α = 3). D = 3. Send 3 up to B. B is MIN: β = min(+∞, 3) = 3. Pass α = −∞ and β = 3 down to E. E is MAX. It reads 5, so α = 5. Now α = 5 ≥ β = 3. MIN at B already has 3 and will never let play reach E, whatever the 9 is. Prune the 9. E = 5 (at least 5). B = min(3, 5) = 3. Send 3 up to A: α = max(−∞, 3) = 3. Pass α = 3 and β = +∞ down to C, then to F. F reads 0, then 1. F = 1. C is MIN: β = min(+∞, 1) = 1. Now α = 3 ≥ β = 1. MAX at A already has 3 and C can give at most 1. Prune the whole of G (7 and 5). A = max(3, 1) = 3, the same as minimax, after reading only 5 of the 8 leaves In the exam A pruned node gets a bound, not an exact value: E is \"at least 5\" and C is \"at most 1\". These bounds are enough to choose the move, which is all we need at the root. Lesson 3 of 9"],[14,"algo","The algorithm","ALPHA-BETA-SEARCH function ALPHA-BETA-SEARCH(state): v = MAX-VALUE(state, −∞, +∞) return the action in ACTIONS(state) whose child has value v function MAX-VALUE(state, α, β): if TERMINAL-TEST(state): return UTILITY(state) v = −∞ for each a in ACTIONS(state): v = max(v, MIN-VALUE(RESULT(state, a), α, β)) if v ≥ β: return v (prune: MIN above will not allow this) α = max(α, v) return v function MIN-VALUE(state, α, β): if TERMINAL-TEST(state): return UTILITY(state) v = +∞ for each a in ACTIONS(state): v = min(v, MAX-VALUE(RESULT(state, a), α, β)) if v ≤ α: return v (prune: MAX above will not allow this) β = min(β, v) return v The test \"v ≥ β\" at a MAX node is the same as \"α ≥ β\" after updating α, so the slide rule and this code agree. Our Python code uses the slide form. Property Alpha beta Same answer as minimax Yes, always, at the root. Pruning never changes the decision Complete and optimal Same as minimax: complete for finite trees, optimal against a perfect opponent Time, best order O(bm/2) Time, random order About O(b3m/4) for moderate b Time, worst order O(bm), no pruning at all Space O(b m), like depth first minimax. Only the current path and its α, β values are stored Data structures The recursion stack. Each frame holds a state, its list of moves, v, α and β. Real programs add a transposition table (a hash map of positions already searched) Lesson 4 of 9"],[14,"ordering","Move ordering decides how much is pruned","Source: the class of 8 February 2026 (\"arrangement of nodes also matter\") and Knuth and Moore, 1975. Alpha beta prunes most when each node looks at its best child first. Then α and β get tight early and many later children fail the test at once. If the worst child always comes first, nothing is pruned. Tree Leaves Read by alpha beta Pruned Slide example, slide order (b = 2, depth 3) 8 5 3 Same values, worst order 8 8 0 27 leaf slide tree, slide order (b = 3, depth 3) 27 11 16 Same 27 values, worst order 27 27 0 Both slide trees are already in the best possible order: no order can read fewer leaves. We checked this by running alpha beta on every tree, and it matches the formula below. Fewest leaves alpha beta can read (Knuth and Moore)Exam favourite b⌈d/2⌉ + b⌊d/2⌋ − 1 For a uniform tree with branching b and depth d, with perfect ordering. b = 2, d = 3: 4 + 2 − 1 = 5. b = 3, d = 3: 9 + 3 − 1 = 11. How real programs get a good order Idea Iterative deepening Search depth 1, 2, 3 and so on. The best move found at depth d − 1 is tried first at depth d Killer moves A move that caused a prune at the same depth elsewhere is tried early, since it is likely good again Captures first In chess, try captures and threats before quiet moves Transposition table Store each searched position with its value and best move, so a position reached by a different move order is not searched twice Lesson 5 of 9"],[14,"maths","The maths: how much faster, and how much deeper","Best order costExam favourite O(bm/2) = O((√b)m) The effective branching factor drops from b to √b. Chess: √35 ≈ 5.9. Depth for the same work bd = (√b)2d In the time minimax searches depth d, alpha beta with good ordering searches depth 2d. Random order cost about O(b3m/4) Still a big saving: the depth grows by about a third. Negamax form max(a, b) = −min(−a, −b) So one function can serve both players: value = max over children of −value(child), with the window (−β, −α). Worked example: chess Say a computer can look at 10⁹ positions per move. With b = 35, minimax reaches depth d where 35d = 10⁹. d = 9 ⁄ log₁₀ 35 = 9 ⁄ 1.544 ≈ 5.8, so about 5 to 6 plies. Alpha beta with near perfect ordering: (√35)d = 10⁹ gives d ≈ 11.7. about twice as deep: roughly 11 plies instead of 5 or 6 Worked example: the best case formula for b = 10, d = 4 b⌈d/2⌉ = 10² = 100. b⌊d/2⌋ = 10² = 100. 100 + 100 − 1 = 199 leaves. 199 instead of 10⁴ = 10,000 leaves, about 50 times fewer Going deeper: Why the best case is b⌈d/2⌉ + b⌊d/2⌋ − 1 To prove that the root value is v, MAX must show \"I can get at least v\" and \"I cannot get more than v\". For the first, MAX needs only one good move at each of its levels, but all of MIN's replies. For the second, MIN needs one good reply at each of its levels, but all of MAX's moves. So one proof tree branches only at MIN levels (about b⌊d/2⌋ leaves), the other only at MAX levels (about b⌈d/2⌉ leaves). They share one leaf, the principal variation, which gives the −1. With perfect ordering alpha beta reads exactly these leaves and no more. Lesson 6 of 9"],[14,"food","Food for thought from the slides","Source: slide Set 3, slide 10. Question (slide 10) Answer Time and space complexity Time O(bm/2) with best ordering, about O(b3m/4) with random ordering, O(bm) in the worst case. Space O(b m) Data structures A recursion stack with α and β per frame, a move list per level, and in practice a hash table of positions (transposition table) and a small table of killer moves per depth Real life uses Chess, checkers, Othello and Connect Four engines. Adversarial planning such as security patrols, where the attacker answers the defender. Puzzle game AI in mobile apps Utility functions Chess: material, king safety, mobility, pawn structure, weighted and added. Connect Four: number of open lines of 2 and 3. Security: value of each target minus the cost of guarding it Real world scenario: IBM Deep Blue, 1997 Deep Blue beat the world chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997. It used alpha beta search on special chess chips that could examine about 200 million positions a second, with a hand written evaluation of about 8,000 features. Today Stockfish still uses alpha beta (in the form called principal variation search), now with a neural network evaluation called NNUE. Alpha beta is the reason chess programs became strong long before deep learning. Remember In class we noted that when the order of nodes is poor, or when you add your own thresholds to prune more, you may cut away a good move. Plain alpha beta never does that: its pruning is exact. Only extra rules such as forward pruning or chance thresholds can lose the best move. Lesson 7 of 9"],[14,"compare","Alpha beta against other game search methods","Searching a game tree Minimax Alpha beta Depth limited alpha beta Monte Carlo tree search (6.3) Same answer as full minimax Yes Yes Only as good as EVAL Approaches it with many rollouts Leaves read All bm bm/2 to bm, depends on order bd/2 to bd for depth d Chosen by statistics, not by depth Needs an evaluation function No (full tree) No (full tree) Yes No, uses random playouts Good for Tiny games Small to medium games Chess, checkers Go, games with b in the hundreds Handles chance No No No Yes, naturally Bottom line: alpha beta is free speed: same answer as minimax, often twice the depth. Use it for any two player zero sum game with a good evaluation function, and switch to Monte Carlo tree search when b is huge or no good evaluation exists. Improvement What it adds Aspiration windows Start with a narrow window [v − δ, v + δ] around the last value instead of [−∞, +∞]. More prunes, research if the result falls outside Principal variation search (NegaScout) Search the first move with the full window, the rest with a zero width window to prove they are worse. Research only when the proof fails Null move pruning Let the opponent move twice. If MAX is still above β, the real position is surely good enough, so prune Transposition tables Reuse values of positions already seen through another move order Lesson 8 of 9"],[14,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths If one reply already shows that a move is worse than a move you have, you do not need to check the other replies to that move. Alpha beta is minimax with this common sense built in. α is the best MAX can force so far, β the best MIN can force so far. They pass down, values pass up, and a branch is pruned when α ≥ β. The root value equals minimax. Time is O(bm/2) with best ordering, about O(b3m/4) with random ordering, and O(bm) in the worst case. Knuth and Moore (1975) proved the minimal tree has b⌈d/2⌉ + b⌊d/2⌋ − 1 leaves and that alpha beta reads exactly it with perfect ordering. Pearl (1982) showed alpha beta is asymptotically optimal among game tree search algorithms with random leaf values. Iterative deepening, killer moves and transposition tables get close to the best order in practice. Research went on with best first methods such as SSS* and MTD(f), which can read fewer leaves than alpha beta by using null windows and memory. Modern engines combine alpha beta with learned evaluation (NNUE) and learned move ordering, and AlphaZero showed a different path with Monte Carlo tree search and deep networks. Lesson 9 of 9"],[14,"recap","Chapter summary","α is the best value MAX can guarantee so far (starts at −∞), β the best value MIN can guarantee so far (starts at +∞). α and β pass from parent to child. Node values pass from child to parent and update α at MAX nodes and β at MIN nodes. When α ≥ β, the rest of the branch cannot change the decision, so it is pruned. The root value is always the same as minimax. Slide example: leaves 2 3 5 9 0 1 7 5. The 9 under E and the whole subtree G are pruned, and A = 3 after reading 5 of 8 leaves. Good move ordering is key: best case O(bm/2), so the same time searches about twice as deep. Worst case O(bm). Next: chapter 6.3 adds dice and other chance events with expectimax, and Monte Carlo tree search for very large games. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun alpha beta on the slide example, compare the slide order with the worst order, then write alpha beta and a leaf counter yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[15,"","Games with chance: expectimax and Monte Carlo tree search","Expectimax, expectiminimax, their problems, and Monte Carlo tree search with UCB1. · expectimax · expectiminimax · chance node · expected value · Monte Carlo tree search · MCTS · UCB1 · backgammon · AlphaGo Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 6: Adversarial search›Expectimax and MCTS Step 6 · Topic 3 of 3 · College When dice or random events decide part of the game, plan for the average result. Back up expected values with expectimax and expectiminimax, see the problems the slides ask about, and choose moves from random games with Monte Carlo tree search. About 50 min9 lessons3 animations7 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1When dice decide part of the game 2The expectimax example of the slides 3Expectiminimax: chance and an opponent 4What problems do you see here? 5Monte Carlo tree search 6The maths: expected value, UCB1 and cost 7Which game search to use 8From intuition to research 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[15,"idea","When dice decide part of the game","Source: slide Set 3, slide 11, and the class of 8 February 2026. Minimax and alpha beta assume the opponent always picks the move that is worst for us. Many games are not like that. In backgammon, Ludo or snakes and ladders, a dice roll decides which moves are possible. In real life the \"opponent\" is often not clever at all: the weather, traffic, or a customer who acts at random with some known pattern. In class we said: the world does not always play optimally against you. What it is Expectimax is a game tree search where some nodes are chance nodes. A chance node does not choose. Its value is the average of its children, each weighted by its probability: the expected value. Why we need it If the next event is random, planning for the worst case (minimax) is too careful, and ignoring the randomness is wrong. The expected value tells you what you will get on average if you play the same move many times. How it works Build the tree with MAX nodes for your moves, chance nodes for random events, and (in expectiminimax) MIN nodes for the opponent. Leaves give utilities. Back the values up: max at MAX, min at MIN, and probability times value, added up, at chance nodes. Expectimax value (slide 11)Exam favourite V(s) = maxs′ ∈ successors(s) V(s′) for agent controlled states. V(s) = ∑s′ ∈ successors(s) p(s′ | s) V(s′) for chance states. V(s) = known for terminal states The same recursion as minimax, with the MIN rule replaced by an expectation. Expectimax (slide 11) def value(state): if the state is a terminal state: return the state's utility if the agent is MAX: return max-value(state) if the agent is EXP: return exp-value(state) def max-value(state): initialize v = −∞ for each successor of state: v = max(v, value(successor)) return v def exp-value(state): initialize v = 0 for each successor of state: p = probability(successor) v += p × value(successor) return v Lesson 2 of 9"],[15,"example","The expectimax example of the slides","Source: slide Set 3, slide 11. MAX has three moves. Each leads to a chance node with three outcomes, each with probability 1/3. The outcome values are 3, 12, 9 for the first move, 2, 4, 6 for the second, and 15, 6, 0 for the third. Back up the values First chance node: 1/3 × 3 + 1/3 × 12 + 1/3 × 9 = 24 ⁄ 3 = 8. Second chance node: 1/3 × 2 + 1/3 × 4 + 1/3 × 6 = 12 ⁄ 3 = 4. Third chance node: 1/3 × 15 + 1/3 × 6 + 1/3 × 0 = 21 ⁄ 3 = 7. MAX = max(8, 4, 7) = 8, so MAX takes the first move Remember If the same tree had MIN nodes instead of chance nodes, minimax would give min values 3, 2 and 0, and MAX would still pick the first move, but with value 3. The third move looks worst to minimax (0) and second best to expectimax (7). The two methods can choose different moves when the risk differs. Lesson 3 of 9"],[15,"emm","Expectiminimax: chance and an opponent","Source: slide Set 3, slide 12, and the class of 8 February 2026. In backgammon both players roll dice. So the tree has four kinds of layers in turn: MAX, CHANCE (my roll), MIN, CHANCE (the opponent's roll). This is expectiminimax. ExpectiminimaxExam favourite EMM(s) = UTILITY(s) if terminal, maxa EMM(RESULT(s, a)) if MAX, mina EMM(RESULT(s, a)) if MIN, ∑r P(r) EMM(RESULT(s, r)) if CHANCE r is a chance outcome, such as a dice roll. The middle branch of slide 12 The chance node has three outcomes with probabilities 0.5, 0.4 and 0.1. Each leads to a MIN node. MIN node 1 over 5, 4, 2 takes 2. MIN node 2 over 1, 7, 2 takes 1. MIN node 3 over 3, 4, 5 takes 3. Chance node: 0.5 × 2 + 0.4 × 1 + 0.1 × 3 = 1 + 0.4 + 0.3. = 1.7 The slide labels the bottom row CHANCE too. That row stands for the next chance layer of the game. Here we read its circles as the values that the search below returns. Lesson 4 of 9"],[15,"issues","What problems do you see here?","Source: slide Set 3, slides 11 and 12 ask these questions. The answers come from the class of 8 February 2026 and AIMA section 5.5. Problem Why it hurts Solution strategies We must know the probabilities In class: a coin tossed 1,000 times is never exactly 50:50, a coin may be biased, and probabilities from experience are skewed towards what we have seen Estimate from data, update the estimates as you play, or test how much the decision changes when the probabilities change a little The tree is much bigger Time O(bm nm), where n is the number of chance outcomes. Backgammon has 21 distinct rolls of two dice and about 20 moves per roll Depth limit with an evaluation function, or sample a few rolls instead of all (Monte Carlo) The size of the values matters Minimax only cares about the order of the leaves. Expectimax averages, so stretching some values can change the choice (see below) The evaluation must be a positive linear function of the true expected payoff, for example the chance of winning Pruning is harder One unseen child of a chance node can change the average by any amount If the values are bounded, say between 0 and 10, a chance node gets an upper bound after a few children, and alpha beta style pruning works again The model of the opponent matters Expectimax against a clever opponent can walk into a trap. Minimax against a random opponent is too careful Pick the model that fits: MIN nodes for an adversary, chance nodes for random behaviour Why the size of the values matters (AIMA, chance 0.9 and 0.1) Move 1 leads to values 2 (chance 0.9) and 3 (chance 0.1). Move 2 leads to values 1 (0.9) and 4 (0.1). Move 1: 0.9 × 2 + 0.1 × 3 = 2.1. Move 2: 0.9 × 1 + 0.1 × 4 = 1.3. Choose move 1. Now change the values to 20, 30, 1, 400. The order of the four values is the same. Move 1: 0.9 × 20 + 0.1 × 30 = 21. Move 2: 0.9 × 1 + 0.1 × 400 = 40.9. Now choose move 2. the order is kept, but the choice flips: expectimax needs values on the right scale Lesson 5 of 9"],[15,"mcts","Monte Carlo tree search","Source: the class of 8 February 2026 (\"certain advanced algorithms also come like Monte Carlo simulation\") and Kocsis and Szepesvari, 2006. What it is Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS) chooses a move by playing many quick random games (rollouts) and keeping a win record for each move. It grows a search tree only where the results look promising. Why we need it For Go, b is about 250, and no one could write a good evaluation function by hand. Alpha beta cannot go deep enough. Random games need no evaluation function: the result of a finished game is the evaluation. How it works Repeat four steps. Selection: from the root, pick children by UCB1 until you reach a node with untried moves. Expansion: add one new child. Simulation: play random moves from it to the end. Back up: add the result to the win and visit counts of every node on the path. Finally play the root move with the most visits. UCB1 (upper confidence bound)Exam favourite UCB1(i) = wi ⁄ ni + C √(ln N ⁄ ni) wi wins in ni visits of move i, N visits of the parent, C balances the two parts. The first part is exploitation (how good the move looks), the second is exploration (how unsure we still are). C = √2 ≈ 1.4 is the usual start. With seed 2 the search gives move 2 (the truly best move, 60% wins) 9 of the 20 visits. Change the seed: with seed 1 it ends on move 1 after 20 rounds. Twenty random games are few. Real programs play thousands per move. Remember MCTS also handles chance naturally: a dice roll in a rollout is just one more random step. That is one reason it replaced expectimax in many games with dice and cards. Lesson 6 of 9"],[15,"maths","The maths: expected value, UCB1 and cost","Expected valueExam favourite E[X] = ∑x P(x) × x The average result over many plays. The probabilities of the outcomes of one chance node add up to 1. Linearity E[a X + b] = a E[X] + b for a > 0 This is why a positive linear change of all values keeps the expectimax choice, while other changes may not. Cost of expectiminimax O(bm nm) b moves and n chance outcomes per level, m levels of each. Where UCB1 comes from P(true mean > sample mean + ε) ≤ e−2 n ε² (Hoeffding) Choose ε so the chance of being wrong falls like 1 ⁄ N⁴: ε = √(2 ln N ⁄ n). That is UCB1 with C = √2. Worked example: dice in backgammon Two dice give 36 ordered results. A double such as 3 and 3 happens 1 way in 36. A mixed roll such as 3 and 5 happens 2 ways (3 then 5, or 5 then 3), so 2 in 36 = 1 in 18. Distinct rolls: 6 doubles + 15 mixed pairs = 21. n = 21 chance outcomes: 6 with p = 1/36 and 15 with p = 1/18 Worked example: one UCB1 value Move i: 3 wins in 5 visits. The root has N = 10 visits. C = 1.4. Exploitation: 3 ⁄ 5 = 0.6. Exploration: 1.4 × √(ln 10 ⁄ 5) = 1.4 × √(2.303 ⁄ 5) = 1.4 × √0.4605 = 1.4 × 0.679 = 0.950. UCB1 = 0.6 + 0.950 ≈ 1.55 Going deeper: Pruning chance nodes when values are bounded Suppose every leaf lies between 0 and 10, and a chance node has three children with probability 1/3 each. After reading the first child, say 2, the largest the node can still become is (2 + 10 + 10) ⁄ 3 ≈ 7.33. If MAX above already has a move worth 8, this chance node can never win, so its other children can be skipped. This idea is called *-minimax (star minimax), due to Ballard (1983). It needs known bounds on the values. Lesson 7 of 9"],[15,"compare","Which game search to use","Game search methods Minimax and alpha beta Expectimax Expectiminimax MCTS Other side modelled as A perfect opponent Random events with known chances An opponent plus random events Whatever the rollout policy does Value at a non MAX node min Weighted average min or weighted average Win rate from rollouts Needs an evaluation function Yes, at the depth limit Yes, on the right scale Yes, on the right scale No Pruning Alpha beta, exact Only with bounded values Only with bounded values Not needed: it grows only good parts Typical games Chess, checkers, tic-tac-toe Single player games with randomness, Pac-Man ghosts that move at random Backgammon Go, many board and card games, planning Bottom line: use minimax with alpha beta for a clever opponent and no chance, expectimax when the other side is random, expectiminimax when both happen, and MCTS when b is huge or no good evaluation function exists. Real world scenario: TD-Gammon and AlphaGo TD-Gammon (Tesauro, 1992) played backgammon with a shallow expectiminimax search and a neural network evaluation learned by playing against itself, and reached the level of top human players. AlphaGo (2016) beat Lee Sedol at Go using MCTS guided by deep networks for move choice and position value. AlphaZero (2017) learned chess, shogi and Go from self play alone, with the same MCTS. Lesson 8 of 9"],[15,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths When luck is involved, think about the average result of a move, not only the worst one. If a move gives 3, 12 or 9 with equal chance, on average it gives 8. Expectimax adds chance nodes whose value is ∑ p × value. Expectiminimax mixes MAX, MIN and chance layers. Cost O(bm nm). Values must be on the right scale. MCTS replaces exact backup by random rollouts and UCB1 selection. UCB1 comes from the multi armed bandit problem: its regret grows only like log N (Auer, Cesa-Bianchi and Fischer, 2002). UCT applies UCB1 at every tree node and converges to the minimax value as the number of rollouts grows (Kocsis and Szepesvari, 2006). Expectimax is the same backup as the Bellman equation of a Markov decision process, which chapter 10 builds on. AlphaZero uses PUCT, which adds a prior from a policy network to the exploration term, and replaces random rollouts by a value network. MuZero learns the game model itself. For hidden information (poker), counterfactual regret minimisation and its deep variants beat top professionals (Libratus, Pluribus). Lesson 9 of 9"],[15,"recap","Chapter summary","A chance node does not choose. Its value is ∑ p(s′ | s) V(s′), the expected value of its children. Slide 11: chance nodes over 3 12 9, 2 4 6 and 15 6 0 with 1/3 each give 8, 4 and 7, so MAX picks 8. Expectiminimax has MAX, CHANCE and MIN layers. Slide 12: 0.5 × 2 + 0.4 × 1 + 0.1 × 3 = 1.7. Problems: the probabilities must be known, the tree grows as bm nm, the scale of values matters, and pruning needs bounded values. MCTS chooses moves from random rollouts with UCB1 = w ⁄ n + C √(ln N ⁄ n), and needs no evaluation function. Next: step 7 turns from search to logic: how an agent can store facts and reason with them. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun expectimax on the slide 11 tree and expectiminimax on the slide 12 branch, see how scaling flips a choice, run UCB1, then write expectimax, expectiminimax and the UCB1 move picker yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[16,"","Knowledge, logic and propositional logic","Knowledge based agents, entailment, propositional logic and model checking. · propositional logic · knowledge base · entailment · model · wumpus world · truth table · TT-ENTAILS · satisfiability · validity Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 7: Logic and knowledge›Propositional logic Step 7 · Topic 1 of 4 · College Build an agent that knows facts and works out new ones. Meet the wumpus world, learn what models and entailment mean, write sentences in propositional logic, and check entailment with a truth table. About 50 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Knowledge based agents 2The wumpus world 3Logic: syntax, semantics, models and entailment 4Propositional logic 5A knowledge base for the wumpus world, and model checking 6Validity, satisfiability and equivalence 7The maths: counting models 8Logic against other ways to store knowledge 9From intuition to research 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[16,"kba","Knowledge based agents","Source: Russell and Norvig, AIMA 4th ed., section 7.1. The uploaded slides stop at adversarial search, so this step follows the textbook. What it is A knowledge based agent keeps a knowledge base (KB): a set of sentences in a formal language that say what the agent believes about the world. It adds new sentences with TELL and asks questions with ASK. The answer to ASK must follow from what was told. Why we need it Search agents know only a fixed list of states and actions. They cannot use general facts such as \"a breeze means a pit next door\" in new situations. A KB lets an agent combine general rules with what it sees and work out facts it never saw directly. How it works Each time step: TELL the KB what the agent perceives, ASK the KB which action is best, TELL the KB that this action was taken, then act. The reasoning happens inside ASK, by an inference procedure. KB-AGENT function KB-AGENT(percept) returns an action persistent: KB, a knowledge base. t, a counter for time, at first 0 TELL(KB, MAKE-PERCEPT-SENTENCE(percept, t)) action = ASK(KB, MAKE-ACTION-QUERY(t)) TELL(KB, MAKE-ACTION-SENTENCE(action, t)) t = t + 1 return action This is the declarative way to build an agent: tell it what it needs to know and let it reason. The procedural way writes the behaviour straight into code. Real systems mix both. Lesson 2 of 10"],[16,"wumpus","The wumpus world","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 7.2. A cave of 4 × 4 rooms. The agent starts in [1,1] facing right. One room holds the wumpus, a beast that eats anyone who enters its room. Some rooms hold bottomless pits. One room holds gold. The agent has one arrow. PEAS part Wumpus world Performance +1000 for climbing out with the gold, −1000 for falling into a pit or being eaten, −1 for each action, −10 for using the arrow Environment 4 × 4 grid of rooms, agent starts in [1,1]. The wumpus and the gold are placed at random in other rooms, and each other room has a pit with probability 0.2 Actuators Forward, TurnLeft, TurnRight, Grab, Shoot, Climb Sensors Stench (wumpus next door), Breeze (pit next door), Glitter (gold here), Bump (walked into a wall), Scream (the wumpus died) Reasoning in the first moves In [1,1] the percept is [None, None, None, None, None]: no stench and no breeze. So [1,2] and [2,1] are safe. The agent moves to [2,1] and feels a Breeze. So there is a pit in [2,2] or [3,1], or both. It goes back and moves to [1,2]. It smells a Stench but feels no Breeze. No breeze in [1,2] means no pit in [2,2]. So the pit is in [3,1]. The stench in [1,2], with no stench in [2,1], puts the wumpus in [1,3]. the agent has worked out facts it never saw directly: this is logical inference Remember Room names are [column, row]. [2,1] is one step right of the start, [1,2] is one step up. Lesson 3 of 10"],[16,"logic","Logic: syntax, semantics, models and entailment","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 7.3. Idea Meaning Example Syntax Which strings are well formed sentences x + y = 4 is a sentence, x4y+ = is not Semantics When a sentence is true. A sentence is true or false in each possible world x + y = 4 is true in the world where x = 2, y = 2 Model A possible world, fixed exactly. In propositional logic: a true or false value for every symbol P = T, Q = F, R = T M(α) The set of all models in which α is true M(P ∨ Q) has 3 of the 4 models of P, Q Entailment KB ⊨ α α is true in every model where the KB is true {x = 0} ⊨ x y = 0 EntailmentExam favourite KB ⊨ α if and only if M(KB) ⊆ M(α) The more a KB says, the fewer models it has. If all of them make α true, α follows. Property of an inference procedure i Meaning KB ⊢i α Procedure i derives α from the KB Sound (truth preserving) It derives only entailed sentences: if KB ⊢i α then KB ⊨ α Complete It can derive every entailed sentence: if KB ⊨ α then KB ⊢i α Grounding The link between the sentences and the real world. It comes from the sensors and from learning, not from logic Lesson 4 of 10"],[16,"pl","Propositional logic","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 7.4. The simplest logic. Atomic sentences are single symbols such as P, Q or P1,2 (\"there is a pit in [1,2]\"). True and False are also sentences. Complex sentences are built with five connectives. Connective Name Read as True when ¬P negation not P P is false P ∧ Q conjunction P and Q both are true P ∨ Q disjunction P or Q at least one is true P ⇒ Q implication if P then Q P is false, or Q is true. False only for P = T, Q = F P ⇔ Q biconditional P if and only if Q both have the same value Precedence, from strongest to weakest: ¬, ∧, ∨, ⇒, ⇔. So ¬A ∧ B ∨ C ⇒ D means ((¬A ∧ B) ∨ C) ⇒ D. Common mistake P ⇒ Q is true whenever P is false. \"If 5 is even, then Sam is smart\" is true in propositional logic, whatever Sam is like. Implication does not mean that P causes Q. Lesson 5 of 10"],[16,"wkb","A knowledge base for the wumpus world, and model checking","Source: AIMA 4th ed., sections 7.4.3 and 7.4.4. We ran the check ourselves and got the same numbers as the textbook. Symbols: Px,y is true if there is a pit in [x,y], Bx,y if the agent feels a breeze in [x,y]. After the first two moves the KB holds: Name Sentence Meaning R1 ¬P1,1 no pit in the start room R2 B1,1 ⇔ (P1,2 ∨ P2,1) a room is breezy if and only if a neighbour has a pit R3 B2,1 ⇔ (P1,1 ∨ P2,2 ∨ P3,1) the same rule for [2,1] R4 ¬B1,1 percept: no breeze in [1,1] R5 B2,1 percept: breeze in [2,1] Model checking There are 7 symbols: B1,1, B2,1, P1,1, P1,2, P2,1, P2,2, P3,1. So 2⁷ = 128 models. Only 3 of them make R1 to R5 all true: pits in [2,2] and [3,1], a pit in [2,2] only, or a pit in [3,1] only. P1,2 is false in all 3, so KB ⊨ ¬P1,2: [1,2] is safe. P2,2 is true in 2 and false in 1, so the KB entails neither P2,2 nor ¬P2,2. The agent cannot tell yet. KB ⊨ ¬P1,2, KB ⊨ P2,2 ∨ P3,1, but not KB ⊨ P2,2 TT-ENTAILS (truth table enumeration) function TT-ENTAILS(KB, α): symbols = the symbols in KB and α return TT-CHECK-ALL(KB, α, symbols, { }) function TT-CHECK-ALL(KB, α, symbols, model): if symbols is empty: if PL-TRUE(KB, model): return PL-TRUE(α, model) else: return true (a model where KB is false does not matter) P = FIRST(symbols), rest = REST(symbols) return TT-CHECK-ALL(KB, α, rest, model ∪ {P = true}) and TT-CHECK-ALL(KB, α, rest, model ∪ {P = false}) TT-ENTAILS is sound and complete, but it takes O(2n) time for n symbols, and O(n) space because it explores the models depth first. Lesson 6 of 10"],[16,"valid","Validity, satisfiability and equivalence","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 7.5.1. Term Meaning Example Valid (a tautology) True in every model P ∨ ¬P Satisfiable True in at least one model P ∧ Q Unsatisfiable True in no model P ∧ ¬P Equivalent, α ≡ β True in exactly the same models. Same as α ⊨ β and β ⊨ α P ⇒ Q ≡ ¬P ∨ Q Deduction theoremExam favourite KB ⊨ α if and only if (KB ⇒ α) is valid Entailment can be checked as validity. Proof by contradiction (refutation)Exam favourite KB ⊨ α if and only if (KB ∧ ¬α) is unsatisfiable Assume α is false and show this is impossible. Resolution (chapter 7.2) works this way. Equivalence Name α ∧ β ≡ β ∧ α, α ∨ β ≡ β ∨ α commutativity (α ∧ β) ∧ γ ≡ α ∧ (β ∧ γ), and the same for ∨ associativity ¬(¬α) ≡ α double negation α ⇒ β ≡ ¬β ⇒ ¬α contraposition α ⇒ β ≡ ¬α ∨ β implication elimination α ⇔ β ≡ (α ⇒ β) ∧ (β ⇒ α) biconditional elimination ¬(α ∧ β) ≡ ¬α ∨ ¬β, ¬(α ∨ β) ≡ ¬α ∧ ¬β De Morgan α ∧ (β ∨ γ) ≡ (α ∧ β) ∨ (α ∧ γ), α ∨ (β ∧ γ) ≡ (α ∨ β) ∧ (α ∨ γ) distributivity Deciding if a sentence is satisfiable (the SAT problem) was the first problem proved NP-complete (Cook, 1971). So we do not expect any method to beat 2n on every input, yet modern SAT solvers handle millions of symbols in practice. Lesson 7 of 10"],[16,"maths","The maths: counting models","Number of modelsExam favourite 2n for n symbols 3 symbols: 8 models. 7 symbols: 128. 30 symbols: over 10⁹. Number of different sentences up to equivalence 22n Each sentence is fixed by the set of models where it is true, and there are 22n such sets. For n = 2 there are 16 different truth tables. Entailment as sets KB ⊨ α ⟺ M(KB) ⊆ M(α) ⟺ M(KB) ∩ M(¬α) = ∅ The last form is proof by contradiction. Worked example: how many models make P ∨ Q true, with symbols P, Q, R? 2³ = 8 models in total. P ∨ Q is false only when P = F and Q = F. R can then be T or F: 2 models. 8 − 2 = 6. |M(P ∨ Q)| = 6 out of 8 Worked example: is (P ⇒ Q) ⇒ (¬Q ⇒ ¬P) valid? ¬Q ⇒ ¬P is equivalent to P ⇒ Q (contraposition). So the sentence is (P ⇒ Q) ⇒ (P ⇒ Q), which has the form α ⇒ α. α ⇒ α ≡ ¬α ∨ α, true in every model. valid Lesson 8 of 10"],[16,"compare","Logic against other ways to store knowledge","Ways to represent what an agent knows Atomic states (search) Propositional logic First order logic (7.3) Probability (step 9) A state is one name, no inside parts true or false values for fixed facts objects, relations and functions a distribution over possible worlds Can say \"pits cause breezes in every room\" in one sentence No No, one sentence per room Yes Yes, with a rule per variable Handles uncertainty No Only by listing cases with ∨ Only by listing cases Yes, with numbers Inference Search Model checking, resolution, chaining Unification, chaining, resolution Bayes rule, Bayesian networks Decidable Yes Yes (but NP-complete) Only semidecidable Yes for finite models Bottom line: propositional logic is the simplest language with a clear meaning and sound, complete inference. Its weakness is that it cannot talk about many objects at once, which first order logic fixes. Real world scenario: Where propositional reasoning is used Hardware verification: chip makers check that a circuit design meets its specification by turning the question into a SAT problem. Software package managers (such as the ones for Linux) decide which versions of packages to install with SAT solvers. Planning systems such as SATPlan turn \"is there a plan of length T\" into one big propositional sentence. Lesson 9 of 10"],[16,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A knowledge base is a list of facts and rules the agent believes. If something must be true in every world that fits these facts, the agent can be sure of it, even without seeing it. Propositional logic has symbols and five connectives. KB ⊨ α means M(KB) ⊆ M(α). Truth table model checking is sound and complete but costs 2n. Validity, satisfiability and the deduction theorem connect entailment to SAT. SAT is NP-complete (Cook 1971, Levin 1973). Random 3-SAT shows a sharp phase change near 4.26 clauses per symbol, where problems are hardest. Propositional logic is compact: the same set of models can need exponentially long sentences in one normal form and short ones in another. Research looks at proof complexity (how long the shortest proof of an unsatisfiable formula must be in a given proof system), knowledge compilation into forms such as d-DNNF that answer many queries fast, and neurosymbolic systems that combine logic with learned models. Lesson 10 of 10"],[16,"recap","Chapter summary","A knowledge based agent TELLs its KB what it perceives and ASKs it what to do. The answers come from inference. In the wumpus world, the agent works out where pits and the wumpus are from breezes and stenches. A model fixes every fact. KB ⊨ α means α is true in every model of the KB: M(KB) ⊆ M(α). Sound procedures derive only entailed sentences, complete ones derive all of them. Propositional logic: symbols, ¬ ∧ ∨ ⇒ ⇔. P ⇒ Q is false only when P is true and Q is false. Model checking with a truth table is sound and complete but costs 2n. The wumpus KB R1 to R5 has 3 models out of 128 and entails ¬P1,2. Next: chapter 7.2 proves things without listing every model: inference rules, resolution and chaining. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterPrint the truth table of the connectives and check entailment by listing models, then write a sentence evaluator, a model counter and TT-ENTAILS for the wumpus knowledge base.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[17,"","Reasoning patterns: rules, resolution and chaining","Inference rules, resolution, CNF, forward and backward chaining, DPLL and WalkSAT. · inference rules · modus ponens · resolution · CNF · Horn clause · forward chaining · backward chaining · DPLL · WalkSAT Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 7: Logic and knowledge›Propositional inference Step 7 · Topic 2 of 4 · College Prove things without listing every model. Use inference rules, turn sentences into clauses and resolve them, chain through if then rules forwards and backwards, and see how SAT solvers like DPLL decide huge problems. About 55 min8 lessons2 animations6 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Inference rules and proofs 2Resolution and conjunctive normal form 3Horn clauses, forward and backward chaining 4Fast satisfiability: DPLL and WalkSAT 5The maths: how many clauses, and how fast 6Which inference method to use 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[17,"rules","Inference rules and proofs","Source: Russell and Norvig, AIMA 4th ed., section 7.5. What it is An inference rule takes sentences of a certain shape and lets you write a new sentence. A proof is a chain of rule uses that ends with the sentence you want. Why we need it Model checking looks at all 2n models. A proof can ignore every symbol that does not matter, so it can be far shorter when the KB is large. How it works Start from the KB. Apply a sound rule to sentences you already have and add the result. Stop when the goal appears. This is a search problem: states are sets of sentences, actions are rule uses. Rule From Conclude Modus Ponens α ⇒ β and α β And-Elimination α ∧ β α (and β) Biconditional elimination α ⇔ β (α ⇒ β) ∧ (β ⇒ α) Contraposition α ⇒ β ¬β ⇒ ¬α De Morgan ¬(α ∨ β) ¬α ∧ ¬β Unit resolution α ∨ β and ¬β α Proof that there is no pit in [1,2] or [2,1] (from R2 and R4 of chapter 7.1) R2: B1,1 ⇔ (P1,2 ∨ P2,1). Biconditional elimination gives R6: (B1,1 ⇒ (P1,2 ∨ P2,1)) ∧ ((P1,2 ∨ P2,1) ⇒ B1,1). And-Elimination on R6 gives R7: (P1,2 ∨ P2,1) ⇒ B1,1. Contraposition gives R8: ¬B1,1 ⇒ ¬(P1,2 ∨ P2,1). Modus Ponens with R4 (¬B1,1) gives R9: ¬(P1,2 ∨ P2,1). De Morgan gives R10: ¬P1,2 ∧ ¬P2,1. neither [1,2] nor [2,1] has a pit, proved in 5 steps without listing 128 models Remember Logic is monotonic: adding sentences to a KB never removes something it already entails. If KB ⊨ α then KB ∧ β ⊨ α. So a rule can be used whenever its premises are in the KB, whatever else is there. Lesson 2 of 8"],[17,"resolution","Resolution and conjunctive normal form","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 7.5.2. The resolution ruleExam favourite from ℓ1 ∨ ... ∨ ℓk and m1 ∨ ... ∨ mn, where ℓi and mj are opposite literals, conclude the clause of all the other literals Example: from P ∨ Q and ¬Q ∨ R, conclude P ∨ R. Copies of the same literal are kept once (factoring). Why it is sound if ℓi is true then mj is false, so the rest of the second clause must be true. If ℓi is false, the rest of the first clause must be true Either way, the joined rest is true. Resolution works on clauses: ORs of literals. A sentence that is an AND of clauses is in conjunctive normal form (CNF). Every sentence can be turned into an equivalent CNF sentence. Converting R2: B1,1 ⇔ (P1,2 ∨ P2,1) to CNF Remove ⇔: (B1,1 ⇒ (P1,2 ∨ P2,1)) ∧ ((P1,2 ∨ P2,1) ⇒ B1,1). Remove ⇒ (α ⇒ β becomes ¬α ∨ β): (¬B1,1 ∨ P1,2 ∨ P2,1) ∧ (¬(P1,2 ∨ P2,1) ∨ B1,1). Move ¬ inwards with De Morgan: (¬B1,1 ∨ P1,2 ∨ P2,1) ∧ ((¬P1,2 ∧ ¬P2,1) ∨ B1,1). Distribute ∨ over ∧: (¬B1,1 ∨ P1,2 ∨ P2,1) ∧ (¬P1,2 ∨ B1,1) ∧ (¬P2,1 ∨ B1,1). three clauses PL-RESOLUTION function PL-RESOLUTION(KB, α) returns true or false clauses = the clauses of the CNF form of KB ∧ ¬α new = { } loop: for each pair of clauses Ci, Cj in clauses: resolvents = PL-RESOLVE(Ci, Cj) if resolvents contains the empty clause: return true new = new ∪ resolvents if new ⊆ clauses: return false (nothing new: no contradiction) clauses = clauses ∪ new In the exam The ground resolution theorem: if a set of clauses is unsatisfiable, resolution will reach the empty clause. So resolution is a complete refutation procedure for propositional logic. It cannot list every entailed sentence directly, but it can always confirm or refute a given one. Lesson 3 of 8"],[17,"horn","Horn clauses, forward and backward chaining","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 7.5.4. Clause type At most / exactly Example As an implication Definite clause exactly one positive literal ¬L ∨ ¬M ∨ P L ∧ M ⇒ P Fact one positive literal and nothing else A A Horn clause at most one positive literal ¬A ∨ ¬B A ∧ B ⇒ False (a goal clause) What it is Forward chaining starts with the known facts, fires every rule whose premises are all known, and adds its conclusion, until the query appears or nothing new comes. Backward chaining starts with the query and looks for rules that conclude it, then tries to prove their premises the same way. Why we need it Most rule based systems use rules of the form \"if these hold then that holds\". For these definite clauses, chaining decides entailment in time linear in the size of the KB, far faster than general methods. How it works Forward chaining keeps a count of unknown premises for each rule and an agenda of new facts. Backward chaining is a depth first AND OR search: OR over the rules for a goal, AND over the premises of a rule, with a check for goals already on the path. Forward chaining Backward chaining Driven by Data (the facts) The goal Good for Watching a stream of new facts, such as sensors Answering one question, such as \"what should I do now?\" Wasted work May derive many facts unrelated to the query Only touches relevant rules Cost O(size of KB), each rule counted down at most once per premise Often much less than the KB size Sound and complete for definite clauses Yes Yes, with the loop check Lesson 4 of 8"],[17,"sat","Fast satisfiability: DPLL and WalkSAT","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 7.6. What it is DPLL is a depth first search over truth values for a CNF formula, with three tricks that cut the search. WalkSAT is a local search that flips one symbol at a time. Why we need it Proving KB ⊨ α is the same as showing KB ∧ ¬α is unsatisfiable. A good SAT solver therefore gives a good prover, and SAT solvers also find plans, schedules and circuit errors. How it works DPLL assigns symbols one at a time and simplifies. Early termination: stop a branch as soon as a clause is false, or accept once every clause is true. Pure symbol: a symbol with the same sign in every remaining clause can be set to make them true. Unit clause: a clause with one literal left forces its value. WalkSAT picks a false clause and flips one of its symbols, either the one that makes the most clauses true or, with probability p, a random one. Method Complete Typical use Truth table (TT-ENTAILS) Yes Teaching, tiny problems Resolution Yes (refutation complete) Theorem provers, the basis of first order provers Forward or backward chaining Yes, for definite clauses only Rule based systems, Prolog DPLL and its modern form CDCL Yes Industrial SAT solving with millions of clauses WalkSAT No: it can find a model, but it cannot prove there is none Large satisfiable problems Going deeper: Modern SAT solvers Modern solvers use conflict driven clause learning (CDCL): when a branch fails, they work out which choices caused the conflict, add a new clause that rules out that combination, and jump back several levels at once. With watched literals for fast unit propagation, restarts and smart variable order, they solve industrial problems with millions of variables. Lesson 5 of 8"],[17,"maths","The maths: how many clauses, and how fast","Possible clauses over n symbolsExam favourite 3n Each symbol is positive, negative or absent in a clause. So resolution always stops: there are at most 3n distinct clauses to add. Clauses from distributing (a ∧ b) ∨ (c ∧ d) gives (a ∨ c) ∧ (a ∨ d) ∧ (b ∨ c) ∧ (b ∨ d) An OR of k ANDs with 2 parts each gives 2k clauses: CNF can blow up. The Tseitin trick adds new symbols to keep it linear. Forward chaining cost O(n) for a KB of total size n Each premise of each rule is counted down at most once. Random 3-SAT hardness m ⁄ n ≈ 4.26 With m clauses over n symbols, problems are hardest near this ratio. Below it almost all are satisfiable, above it almost none. Worked example: resolve (A ∨ B ∨ ¬C) with (¬B ∨ D) The opposite pair is B and ¬B. Keep the rest of the first clause: A ∨ ¬C. Keep the rest of the second: D. A ∨ ¬C ∨ D Worked example: why resolving on two pairs at once is wrong Clauses: (P ∨ Q) and (¬P ∨ ¬Q). Wrong: cancel both pairs and get the empty clause, claiming a contradiction. But P = T, Q = F makes both clauses true, so they are satisfiable. resolve on one pair only: P ∨ ¬P or Q ∨ ¬Q, both always true and useless Lesson 6 of 8"],[17,"compare","Which inference method to use","Inference in propositional logic Model checking Inference rules by hand Resolution Chaining DPLL and WalkSAT Works on Any sentences Any sentences CNF Definite clauses only CNF Sound and complete Yes Depends on the rules chosen Refutation complete Yes, for definite clauses DPLL yes, WalkSAT not for proving Time 2n always Search over proofs Can be exponential Linear Exponential worst case, fast in practice Easy for people to follow Yes, but long Yes Fairly Yes No Bottom line: use chaining whenever the knowledge fits definite clauses, a SAT solver (DPLL or CDCL) for general problems, and resolution when you need a proof that can be checked and lifted to first order logic. Real world scenario: Rule based systems Business rule engines (such as Drools) and the expert systems of the 1980s run forward chaining over large sets of if then rules. The Rete algorithm (Forgy, 1982) speeds this up by remembering partial matches, so a new fact only touches the rules it can affect. Prolog runs backward chaining. Lesson 7 of 8"],[17,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Instead of checking every possible world, follow safe rules step by step, like \"if A gives B, and A is true, then B is true\", until you reach what you want. Sound rules: Modus Ponens, And-Elimination, equivalences, resolution. Resolution on CNF is refutation complete. Definite clauses allow linear time forward and backward chaining. DPLL adds early termination, pure symbols and unit clauses, and WalkSAT searches locally. Resolution proofs can need exponential length: Haken (1985) showed the pigeonhole formulas need exponentially long resolution proofs. That is a limit of the proof system, not just of a search strategy. CDCL solvers are, in a precise sense, as strong as general resolution (Pipatsrisawat and Darwiche, 2011). Research topics: proof systems stronger than resolution (cutting planes, extended resolution), certified SAT solving where the solver outputs a checkable proof of unsatisfiability, and learning heuristics for SAT with neural networks. Lesson 8 of 8"],[17,"recap","Chapter summary","Inference rules such as Modus Ponens and And-Elimination give proofs that skip irrelevant symbols. Logic is monotonic. Resolution: from ℓ ∨ α and ¬ℓ ∨ β, get α ∨ β. Turn sentences into CNF in four steps: remove ⇔, remove ⇒, move ¬ in, distribute ∨ over ∧. Proof by resolution adds ¬α and searches for the empty clause. It is refutation complete. Definite clauses allow forward chaining (from facts) and backward chaining (from the goal) in linear time. DPLL (early termination, pure symbols, unit clauses) and WalkSAT solve SAT. Modern CDCL solvers handle millions of clauses. Next: chapter 7.3 moves to first order logic, which can talk about objects and relations, so one sentence covers every room. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun resolution, forward chaining and DPLL on small examples, then write the resolution rule, forward chaining and a DPLL solver yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[18,"","First order logic: syntax and semantics","Objects, relations, functions, quantifiers, models and how to write knowledge in FOL. · first order logic · predicate logic · quantifier · forall · exists · model · interpretation · kinship · knowledge engineering Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 7: Logic and knowledge›First order logic Step 7 · Topic 3 of 4 · College Say things about all objects at once. Learn the parts of first order logic, what a model and an interpretation are, how ∀ and ∃ work and where they trick people, and how to write the kinship and wumpus worlds in it. About 50 min9 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why we need more than propositional logic 2Syntax: terms, atoms and sentences 3Semantics: models and interpretations 4Quantifiers and their traps 5Using first order logic: kinship and the wumpus world 6The maths: counting interpretations 7First order logic against its neighbours 8From intuition to research 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[18,"why","Why we need more than propositional logic","Source: Russell and Norvig, AIMA 4th ed., section 8.1. The uploaded slides do not cover logic, so this step follows the textbook. What it is First order logic (FOL, also called predicate logic) talks about objects, the relations between them and functions that map objects to objects. Quantifiers let one sentence speak about all objects, or about some object. Why we need it Propositional logic has no way to say \"a room is breezy if and only if a neighbouring room has a pit\" once. It needs a separate sentence for every room: 16 rooms, 16 sentences, and a new set for every cave size. Natural language and mathematics talk about objects and their relations all the time. How it works Replace fixed symbols like P1,2 by atoms such as Pit([1,2]) built from a predicate and terms, and add ∀ (for all) and ∃ (there exists). One sentence, ∀s Breezy(s) ⇔ ∃r Adjacent(r, s) ∧ Pit(r), now covers every room of every cave. Language (AIMA figure 8.1) What exists in the world (ontological commitment) What an agent believes about facts (epistemological commitment) Propositional logic facts true, false, unknown First order logic facts, objects, relations true, false, unknown Temporal logic facts, objects, relations, times true, false, unknown Probability theory facts a degree of belief between 0 and 1 Fuzzy logic facts with a degree of truth between 0 and 1 a known interval value Lesson 2 of 9"],[18,"syntax","Syntax: terms, atoms and sentences","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 8.2. Element What it names Examples Constant symbol one object Richard, John, M1, 2 Predicate symbol a relation (true or false for its arguments) Brother(·, ·), King(·), Adjacent(·, ·) Function symbol a function: objects in, one object out LeftLeg(·), Mother(·), + Variable any object, to be fixed by a quantifier x, y, s Term a constant, a variable, or a function applied to terms John, x, LeftLeg(John), Mother(Mother(x)) Atomic sentence a predicate applied to terms, or term = term Brother(Richard, John), Father(x) = John Complex sentence atoms joined by ¬ ∧ ∨ ⇒ ⇔ King(Richard) ∨ King(John) Quantified sentence ∀x S or ∃x S ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x) A term names an object. A sentence is true or false. LeftLeg(John) is a term (a leg), King(John) is a sentence. Remember In this course and in the animations, names that start with a lower case letter are variables, and names that start with a capital letter are constants, predicates or functions. Prolog uses the opposite convention. Lesson 3 of 9"],[18,"semantics","Semantics: models and interpretations","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 8.2.1 and figure 8.2. A model of first order logic has a domain, the set of objects, and an interpretation that says which object each constant names, which tuples of objects each predicate holds for, and what each function returns. The textbook model (figure 8.2) Domain: Richard the Lionheart, King John, the left legs of each, and a crown. Five objects. Relations: the brother relation holds for (Richard, John) and (John, Richard). OnHead holds for (crown, John). Person holds for Richard and John. King holds for John only. Crown holds for the crown. Function: LeftLeg maps Richard to Richard's left leg and John to John's left leg. Brother(Richard, John) is true here, King(Richard) is false. an atom is true when the objects named by its terms are in the relation named by its predicate The animation below uses this model without the two legs, to keep the picture small. Common mistake First order logic has infinitely many models: any domain size from 1 upwards, and every interpretation of every symbol. So entailment cannot be checked by listing models, as we did with truth tables. Lesson 4 of 9"],[18,"quant","Quantifiers and their traps","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 8.2.6. Sentence Read as True when ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x) all kings are persons for every object, if it is a king then it is a person ∃x Crown(x) ∧ OnHead(x, John) some crown is on John's head at least one object is a crown and on John's head ∀x ∃y Loves(x, y) everybody loves somebody for each x there is a y (maybe different for each x) ∃y ∀x Loves(x, y) there is someone whom everybody loves one y works for all x Rule of thumb Why ∀ goes with ⇒ ∀x King(x) ∧ Person(x) says everything is a king and a person, which is far too strong ∃ goes with ∧ ∃x Crown(x) ⇒ OnHead(x, John) is true as soon as one object is not a crown, so it says almost nothing Order matters for mixed quantifiers ∀x ∃y and ∃y ∀x mean different things The same kind can be swapped ∀x ∀y ≡ ∀y ∀x, ∃x ∃y ≡ ∃y ∃x De Morgan for quantifiersExam favourite ∀x ¬P ≡ ¬∃x P, ¬∀x P ≡ ∃x ¬P, ∀x P ≡ ¬∃x ¬P, ∃x P ≡ ¬∀x ¬P \"Nobody likes parsnips\" = \"everybody dislikes parsnips\". ∀ as a big AND, ∃ as a big OR ∀x P(x) ≡ P(o1) ∧ P(o2) ∧ ..., ∃x P(x) ≡ P(o1) ∨ P(o2) ∨ ... Over a finite domain with objects o1, o2, ... Equality: t1 = t2 is true when both terms name the same object. \"Richard has at least two brothers\" is ∃x, y Brother(x, Richard) ∧ Brother(y, Richard) ∧ ¬(x = y). Without ¬(x = y), x and y could be the same brother. Going deeper: Database semantics Databases and Prolog assume three extra things: unique names (different constants name different objects), the closed world assumption (an atom not known to be true is false), and domain closure (there are no objects other than those named). Under these rules \"Richard has two brothers, John and Geoffrey\" is simply Brother(John, Richard) ∧ Brother(Geoffrey, Richard). In full first order logic you must add ¬(John = Geoffrey) and say that no one else is a brother. Lesson 5 of 9"],[18,"use","Using first order logic: kinship and the wumpus world","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 8.3. Kinship axiom Meaning ∀m, c Mother(c) = m ⇔ Female(m) ∧ Parent(m, c) a mother is a female parent ∀w, h Husband(h, w) ⇔ Male(h) ∧ Spouse(h, w) a husband is a male spouse ∀p, c Parent(p, c) ⇔ Child(c, p) parent and child are inverse relations ∀g, c Grandparent(g, c) ⇔ ∃p Parent(g, p) ∧ Parent(p, c) a grandparent is a parent of a parent ∀x, y Sibling(x, y) ⇔ x ≠ y ∧ ∃p Parent(p, x) ∧ Parent(p, y) siblings share a parent and are different people Wumpus world in FOL Meaning ∀x, y, a, b Adjacent([x, y], [a, b]) ⇔ (x = a ∧ (y = b − 1 ∨ y = b + 1)) ∨ (y = b ∧ (x = a − 1 ∨ x = a + 1)) which rooms are neighbours, for any cave size ∀s Breezy(s) ⇔ ∃r Adjacent(r, s) ∧ Pit(r) one sentence for every room ∀t, s At(Agent, s, t) ∧ Breeze(t) ⇒ Breezy(s) what a percept at time t tells about room s The knowledge engineering process (AIMA 8.4) Identify the questions the KB must answer. Assemble the relevant knowledge, often by talking to experts. Decide on a vocabulary of predicates, functions and constants (an ontology). Encode general knowledge about the domain as axioms. Encode the specific problem instance as facts. Pose queries to an inference procedure and get answers. debug the KB: a wrong answer means a missing or wrong axiom Lesson 6 of 9"],[18,"maths","The maths: counting interpretations","Interpretations over a fixed domain of d objectsExam favourite dc × (2d)u × (2d²)b c constants (each names one of d objects), u unary predicates (each is any subset of the d objects), b binary predicates (any subset of the d² pairs). Functions add more. A k-ary relation 2dk possible extensions There are dk tuples, each in or out. Grounding ∀ ∀x P(x) becomes d ground sentences Over a finite domain, FOL can be turned into propositional logic. Functions make the set of ground terms infinite. Worked example: 2 constants, 1 binary predicate, d = 2 objects Constants: 2² = 4 ways to map 2 constants to 2 objects. Binary predicate: d² = 4 pairs, so 2⁴ = 16 possible relations. 4 × 16 = 64. 64 interpretations for d = 2, and more for every larger d Worked example: ∀x ∃y Loves(x, y) over d = 3 people Grounded, it is (L(a, a) ∨ L(a, b) ∨ L(a, c)) ∧ (L(b, a) ∨ ...) ∧ (L(c, a) ∨ ...). That is 3 clauses of 3 literals each. Each person needs at least one person they love. 3 × 3 = 9 ground atoms involved Lesson 7 of 9"],[18,"compare","First order logic against its neighbours","Choosing a logic Propositional First order Higher order Description logics Talks about fixed facts objects, relations, functions also relations of relations classes and properties Can quantify over nothing objects relations and functions too objects, in limited patterns Entailment decidable Yes Semidecidable: a yes answer is found if true, but a search may never end if false No complete proof method exists Yes, for most variants Compactness Low: one sentence per case High Highest High for taxonomies Used in SAT, hardware checking Theorem provers, Prolog, databases Proof assistants such as Coq and Lean Web ontologies (OWL), medical terms (SNOMED) Bottom line: first order logic is the usual choice: rich enough for most knowledge, with sound and complete proof methods (chapter 7.4), at the price that a proof search for a false query may not stop. Real world scenario: SQL is first order logic A relational database query is a first order formula. SELECT name FROM person p WHERE EXISTS (SELECT * FROM parent q WHERE q.child = p.name) asks for all x with ∃y Parent(y, x). Codd built the relational model on this link in 1970, and database query languages still follow it. Lesson 8 of 9"],[18,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Instead of a separate yes or no fact for every room, say things about all rooms at once: \"every room next to a pit is breezy\". First order logic gives you words for things (objects), how they relate, and \"all\" and \"some\". Terms name objects, atoms are predicates on terms, sentences combine atoms with connectives, ∀ and ∃. A model has a domain and an interpretation. Use ∀ with ⇒ and ∃ with ∧. Quantifier order matters. Equality and database semantics change what a list of facts means. Gödel's completeness theorem (1929): every entailed sentence has a proof, so entailment is semidecidable. Church and Turing (1936): entailment is not decidable. Löwenheim and Skolem: a set of sentences with an infinite model has models of every infinite size, so FOL cannot pin down the natural numbers exactly. Research uses decidable fragments of FOL (the two variable fragment, guarded fragments, description logics), SMT solvers that combine SAT with theories such as arithmetic, and the encoding of commonsense knowledge (Cyc, knowledge graphs) in first order style. Lesson 9 of 9"],[18,"recap","Chapter summary","First order logic talks about objects, relations and functions, and quantifies over objects with ∀ and ∃. Terms (constants, variables, functions of terms) name objects. Atoms apply predicates to terms. Sentences combine atoms. A model has a domain and an interpretation. There are infinitely many models, so we cannot list them. Use ∀ with ⇒ and ∃ with ∧. ∀x ∃y differs from ∃y ∀x. ¬∀x P ≡ ∃x ¬P. Knowledge engineering: questions, knowledge, vocabulary, general axioms, instance facts, queries, debugging. Next: chapter 7.4 shows how to reason in first order logic: unification, chaining and resolution. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCheck quantified sentences in a small model and derive kinship relations, then write quantifier checks, kinship rules and an interpretation counter yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[19,"","Inference in first order logic: unification, chaining and resolution","Instantiation, unification, GMP, forward and backward chaining, and resolution in FOL. · unification · generalised modus ponens · forward chaining · backward chaining · Prolog · resolution · Skolemisation · crime example · Datalog Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 7: Logic and knowledge›FOL inference Step 7 · Topic 4 of 4 · College Reason with rules that have variables. Reduce first order logic to propositional logic and see why that blows up, then lift inference with unification, prove that Colonel West is a criminal by forward chaining, backward chaining and resolution, and meet Prolog. About 60 min9 lessons3 animations6 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1From first order inference to propositional inference 2Unification and lifting 3Forward chaining: the crime example 4Backward chaining and Prolog 5Resolution in first order logic 6The maths: instances, ground terms and proof size 7Which first order inference method to use 8From intuition to research 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[19,"prop","From first order inference to propositional inference","Source: Russell and Norvig, AIMA 4th ed., section 9.1. The uploaded slides do not cover logic, so this step follows the textbook. Rule What it does Example Universal Instantiation (UI) From ∀v α, infer α with v replaced by any ground term g From ∀x King(x) ∧ Greedy(x) ⇒ Evil(x), infer King(John) ∧ Greedy(John) ⇒ Evil(John) Existential Instantiation (EI) From ∃v α, infer α with v replaced by a brand new constant k (a Skolem constant) From ∃x Crown(x) ∧ OnHead(x, John), infer Crown(C1) ∧ OnHead(C1, John) UI can be used many times, with every ground term. EI is used once, and then the ∃ sentence is dropped. Using both on every quantified sentence, a first order KB becomes a propositional KB: this is propositionalisation. Why that is not enough With k constants, a rule with v variables becomes kv ground rules. Most of them are useless. With a function symbol such as Father(·), there are infinitely many ground terms: John, Father(John), Father(Father(John)), and so on. Herbrand's theorem saves completeness: if α is entailed, it is entailed by a finite part of the ground KB. So try terms of depth 0, then depth 1, then depth 2, until a proof is found. But if α is not entailed, this can go on for ever. first order entailment is semidecidable: a yes is always found, a no may never come Lesson 2 of 9"],[19,"unify","Unification and lifting","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 9.2. What it is Unification finds a substitution θ that makes two atoms look the same. Generalised Modus Ponens uses it to apply a rule to facts directly, without making every ground copy of the rule first. This is called lifting an inference rule to first order logic. Why we need it Propositionalisation makes kv copies of each rule. Unification makes only the substitutions that the facts at hand need, so most useless work disappears. How it works Compare the two atoms from the outside in. Same predicate and argument count: unify the arguments pair by pair. A variable meets a term: bind it, unless the variable occurs inside the term (the occurs check). Two different constants, or two different functions: fail. Keep the bindings in θ and apply them as you go. Generalised Modus Ponens (GMP)Exam favourite from p1′, ..., pn′ and (p1 ∧ ... ∧ pn ⇒ q), infer SUBST(θ, q), where SUBST(θ, pi′) = SUBST(θ, pi) for every i King(John), Greedy(y) and King(x) ∧ Greedy(x) ⇒ Evil(x) with θ = {x/John, y/John} give Evil(John). UNIFY UNIFY(p, q) = θ where SUBST(θ, p) = SUBST(θ, q) θ is written as a set of bindings variable/term. p q θ = UNIFY(p, q) Knows(John, x) Knows(John, Jane) {x/Jane} Knows(John, x) Knows(y, Bill) {x/Bill, y/John} Knows(John, x) Knows(y, Mother(y)) {y/John, x/Mother(John)} Knows(John, x) Knows(x, Elizabeth) fail: x cannot be John and Elizabeth Knows(John, x) Knows(x17, Elizabeth) {x17/John, x/Elizabeth}, after standardising apart Standardising apart: rename the variables of one sentence so that the two sentences share none. The x in \"John knows x\" and the x in \"x knows Elizabeth\" are different variables that happen to share a name. Most general unifier (MGU): Knows(John, x) and Knows(y, z) unify with {y/John, x/z} or with {y/John, x/John, z/John}. The first is more general, since it commits to less. Up to renaming of variables, every unifiable pair has a single MGU. Common mistake The occurs check (does the variable appear inside the term?) makes unification cost quadratic in the size of the terms. Prolog leaves it out for speed, so Prolog can unify x with F(x) and build an endless term. Lesson 3 of 9"],[19,"fc","Forward chaining: the crime example","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 9.3. \"The law says that it is a crime for an American to sell weapons to hostile nations. The country Nono, an enemy of America, has some missiles, and all of its missiles were sold to it by Colonel West, who is American. Prove that West is a criminal.\" Sentence in definite clauses Meaning American(x) ∧ Weapon(y) ∧ Sells(x, y, z) ∧ Hostile(z) ⇒ Criminal(x) the law Owns(Nono, M1), Missile(M1) Nono has a missile, M1 (EI on \"Nono has some missiles\") Missile(x) ∧ Owns(Nono, x) ⇒ Sells(West, x, Nono) all of Nono's missiles were sold by West Missile(x) ⇒ Weapon(x) missiles are weapons Enemy(x, America) ⇒ Hostile(x) an enemy of America counts as hostile American(West), Enemy(Nono, America) facts Forward chaining Round 1: Missile(x) ⇒ Weapon(x) with {x/M1} gives Weapon(M1). Round 1: Missile(x) ∧ Owns(Nono, x) ⇒ Sells(West, x, Nono) with {x/M1} gives Sells(West, M1, Nono). Round 1: Enemy(x, America) ⇒ Hostile(x) with {x/Nono} gives Hostile(Nono). Round 2: the law with {x/West, y/M1, z/Nono} gives Criminal(West). proved in 2 rounds, and a fixed point: nothing more can be added Fact about first order forward chaining Detail Sound and complete for first order definite clauses Every entailed atom is eventually derived Datalog Definite clauses with no function symbols. Forward chaining then stops after polynomially many steps Pattern matching is hard Finding all θ that match a rule's premises to the facts is like solving a CSP, and is NP-hard in general. Order the premises, most constrained first Incremental forward chaining In round t, only try rules with a premise that matches a fact new in round t − 1 Rete Store partial matches in a network so new facts update them cheaply (used in rule engines) Lesson 4 of 9"],[19,"bc","Backward chaining and Prolog","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 9.4. Backward chaining starts with the goal Criminal(West). It finds a rule whose head unifies with the goal, then makes each premise a new goal, carrying the bindings found so far from left to right. It is a depth first AND OR search over proofs. Prolog feature What it means Syntax criminal(X) :- american(X), weapon(Y), sells(X, Y, Z), hostile(Z). Upper case letters are variables Search Depth first, rules in the order written, premises left to right Negation as failure not P (written with a special operator in Prolog) succeeds if P cannot be proved. This is the closed world assumption, not real negation No occurs check Faster unification, but unsound in rare cases Incomplete A left recursive rule such as path(X, Z) :- path(X, Y), link(Y, Z) can loop for ever. Writing link first fixes it Tabling Remembering solved subgoals (memoisation) avoids repeated work and many loops Remember Backward chaining is the reason Prolog programs can be read as logic and run as programs. But the order of rules and premises changes what happens, which pure logic does not care about. Lesson 5 of 9"],[19,"res","Resolution in first order logic","Source: AIMA 4th ed., section 9.5. Conversion to CNF: \"everyone who loves all animals is loved by someone\" Start: ∀x [∀y Animal(y) ⇒ Loves(x, y)] ⇒ [∃y Loves(y, x)]. Remove ⇒: ∀x ¬[∀y ¬Animal(y) ∨ Loves(x, y)] ∨ [∃y Loves(y, x)]. Move ¬ inwards (¬∀ becomes ∃¬): ∀x [∃y Animal(y) ∧ ¬Loves(x, y)] ∨ [∃y Loves(y, x)]. Standardise variables: ∀x [∃y Animal(y) ∧ ¬Loves(x, y)] ∨ [∃z Loves(z, x)]. Skolemise: each ∃ variable becomes a Skolem function of the ∀ variables around it: y becomes F(x), z becomes G(x). ∀x [Animal(F(x)) ∧ ¬Loves(x, F(x))] ∨ Loves(G(x), x). Drop ∀ and distribute ∨ over ∧: [Animal(F(x)) ∨ Loves(G(x), x)] ∧ [¬Loves(x, F(x)) ∨ Loves(G(x), x)] Binary resolutionExam favourite from ℓ1 ∨ ... ∨ ℓk and m1 ∨ ... ∨ mn with UNIFY(ℓi, ¬mj) = θ, infer SUBST(θ, all other literals) Example: [Animal(F(x)) ∨ Loves(G(x), x)] and [¬Loves(u, v) ∨ ¬Kills(u, v)] with θ = {u/G(x), v/x} give Animal(F(x)) ∨ ¬Kills(G(x), x). Resolution proof that West is a criminal (add ¬Criminal(West)) ¬Criminal(West) with the law gives ¬American(West) ∨ ¬Weapon(y) ∨ ¬Sells(West, y, z) ∨ ¬Hostile(z). With American(West): ¬Weapon(y) ∨ ¬Sells(West, y, z) ∨ ¬Hostile(z). With ¬Missile(x) ∨ Weapon(x): ¬Missile(y) ∨ ¬Sells(West, y, z) ∨ ¬Hostile(z). With Missile(M1): ¬Sells(West, M1, z) ∨ ¬Hostile(z). With the Sells rule: ¬Missile(M1) ∨ ¬Owns(Nono, M1) ∨ ¬Hostile(Nono). With Missile(M1), then Owns(Nono, M1): ¬Hostile(Nono). With the Hostile rule: ¬Enemy(Nono, America). With Enemy(Nono, America): the empty clause. 9 resolution steps, and they follow exactly the steps of backward chaining Fact about first order resolution Detail Refutation complete (Robinson, 1965) If KB ⊨ α, resolution with factoring derives { } from KB ∧ ¬α Factoring Merge two literals of one clause that unify, for example P(x) ∨ P(A) becomes P(A) Equality Needs extra rules: demodulation (rewrite with x = y) and paramodulation Strategies Unit preference (resolve with short clauses), set of support (always use a clause from ¬α or derived from it), input resolution, subsumption (delete clauses implied by shorter ones) Used in Theorem provers such as Vampire and E, and software verification Lesson 6 of 9"],[19,"maths","The maths: instances, ground terms and proof size","Ground instances of one ruleExam favourite kv k constants, v variables. The crime law has v = 3 and the KB has k = 4 constants (West, M1, Nono, America): 64 copies, of which forward chaining needs 1. Ground terms with one unary function F and one constant A, up to depth d d + 1 A, F(A), F(F(A)), and so on. Ground terms with c constants and one binary function, depth at most 1 c + c² Each argument is a constant. Depth 2 grows much faster. Worked example: unify P(x, F(y)) with P(G(z), F(A)) Same predicate P with 2 arguments: unify pair by pair. x with G(z): x is a variable, so bind x/G(z). F(y) with F(A): same function, so unify y with A: bind y/A. θ = {x/G(z), y/A}, and both become P(G(z), F(A)) Worked example: why P(x, x) does not unify with P(A, B) First pair: x with A, bind x/A. Second pair: x with B. x is already bound to A, so compare A with B. A and B are different constants. fail Lesson 7 of 9"],[19,"compare","Which first order inference method to use","Inference in first order logic Propositionalisation Forward chaining Backward chaining (Prolog) Resolution Works on Any FOL KB Definite clauses Definite clauses Any FOL KB in CNF Complete Yes, with growing term depth Yes No with plain depth first search (loops), yes with tabling Yes, refutation complete Main cost kv copies of every rule Matching premises to facts Repeated subgoals Many useless resolvents Best for Small finite domains Databases, rule engines, Datalog Answering queries, logic programming Mathematics, software verification Bottom line: use forward chaining when facts stream in and many conclusions are wanted, backward chaining for one query at a time, and resolution when the knowledge is not in definite clause form. Real world scenario: Proving theorems with computers In 1996 the resolution style prover EQP proved the Robbins conjecture, an open question in algebra for over 60 years. Amazon uses automated reasoning to check access policies in its cloud, and chip companies prove properties of floating point units with first order and higher order provers. Lesson 8 of 9"],[19,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A rule with variables is a pattern. Unification is the matching step: it finds which values for the variables make the rule fit the facts you have. UI and EI reduce FOL to propositional logic but blow up. Unification and GMP lift inference instead. Forward chaining, backward chaining (Prolog) and resolution (with CNF and Skolemisation) are the three main methods. First order entailment is semidecidable. Unification can be done in linear time (Paterson and Wegman, 1978). Herbrand's theorem underlies propositionalisation. Resolution with a fair strategy is refutation complete. Datalog with stratified negation is the basis of deductive databases. Research continues on superposition calculi for equality, SMT solving that mixes theories, higher order unification (undecidable in general), and machine learned premise selection for large libraries of mathematics such as the Lean mathlib. Lesson 9 of 9"],[19,"recap","Chapter summary","Universal and existential instantiation turn FOL into propositional logic, but rules get kv copies and functions make it infinite. FOL entailment is semidecidable. Unification finds the most general θ that makes two atoms equal, with the occurs check. Standardise variables apart first. Generalised Modus Ponens applies a rule through unification. Forward chaining proves Criminal(West) in 2 rounds. Backward chaining (Prolog) works from the goal, depth first and left to right. It is fast but can loop. Resolution needs CNF with Skolem functions, and is refutation complete. The crime proof takes 9 resolution steps. Next: step 8 uses logic to plan: situation calculus, STRIPS and partial order planning. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun unification on the textbook pairs and forward chaining on the crime example, then write subst, unify and a forward chainer yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[20,"","Situation calculus and deductive planning","Situations, fluents, action axioms, the frame problem, successor state axioms and deductive planning. · situation calculus · fluent · frame problem · successor state axiom · deductive planning · Green · Reiter · possibility axiom Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 8: Planning›Situation calculus Step 8 · Topic 1 of 3 · College Describe a changing world in logic and plan by proving theorems. Learn situations, fluents and action axioms, see the frame problem and how successor state axioms solve it, and extract a plan from a proof. About 45 min8 lessons1 animation4 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What planning is 2Situation calculus 3The frame problem and successor state axioms 4Deductive planning: a plan is a proof 5The maths: counting axioms and situations 6Situation calculus against other ways to describe actions 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[20,"plan","What planning is","Source: Russell and Norvig, AIMA 3rd ed., chapter 10 and 4th ed., chapter 11. The uploaded slides do not cover planning, so this step follows the textbook and the papers it cites. What it is Planning is finding a sequence of actions that takes an agent from a starting state to a state where its goals hold. Classical planning assumes the world is fully observable, deterministic, static and known. Why we need it A search agent (step 2) needs a hand written successor function and a heuristic for each new problem. A planning agent reads a general description of its actions in logic, so the same planner works in every domain, and it can use the structure of the description (which facts an action changes) to search much faster. How it works Describe states with logical facts, and actions with what they need (preconditions) and what they change (effects). Then search for a plan, either by logical deduction (this chapter), by searching through states with STRIPS actions (chapter 8.2), or by refining a partial plan (chapter 8.3). Planning language States Actions Main use Situation calculus (McCarthy, 1963) situations: terms such as Result(Go(B), S0) axioms in first order logic Deductive planning, theory of action STRIPS (Fikes and Nilsson, 1971) sets of ground facts precondition, add list, delete list Fast state space planners PDDL (1998) like STRIPS, with types and more action schemas with parameters The standard language of planning competitions Lesson 2 of 8"],[20,"sitcalc","Situation calculus","Source: McCarthy and Hayes, 1969, and AIMA 3rd ed., section 10.4.2. What it is Situation calculus is a way to describe change in first order logic. A situation is a snapshot of the world after a sequence of actions. The function Result(a, s) names the situation after doing action a in situation s. Why we need it Plain first order logic has no notion of time: a fact is just true or false. To plan, we must say what is true before and after each action, and reason about sequences of actions. How it works Add one extra argument, the situation, to every relation that can change. At(Robot, A, S0) says the robot is in room A at the start. Write axioms that say when an action is possible and how it changes these relations. Part Meaning Example Initial situation S0 the start At(Robot, A, S0), At(Gold, B, S0) Result(a, s) the situation after action a in s S1 = Result(Go(B), S0) Fluent a relation or function whose value changes, with the situation as last argument Holding(Gold, s) Atemporal (eternal) predicate true or false in every situation Gold(G1), Adjacent(A, B) Possibility axiom when an action can be done Poss(Grab, s) ⇔ ∃x At(Gold, x, s) ∧ At(Robot, x, s) ∧ ¬Holding(Gold, s) Effect axiom what an action changes Poss(Grab, s) ⇒ Holding(Gold, Result(Grab, s)) Lesson 3 of 8"],[20,"frame","The frame problem and successor state axioms","Source: McCarthy and Hayes, 1969 (the frame problem), and Reiter, 1991 (successor state axioms). Effect axioms say what changes. They say nothing about what stays the same. After Go(B), is the gold still in room B? Nothing in the effect axioms of Go says so, so the logic cannot conclude it. This is the frame problem. Version of the problem What goes wrong Representational frame problem Writing a frame axiom such as At(Gold, x, s) ∧ ¬Holding(Gold, s) ⇒ At(Gold, x, Result(Go(y), s)) for every pair of action and fluent: A × F axioms Inferential frame problem Carrying every unchanged fact forward one action at a time costs time for every fact at every step Qualification problem Listing every condition for an action to succeed is endless: the robot can grab the gold unless it is glued down, too heavy, ... Ramification problem Indirect effects: when the robot moves while holding the gold, the gold moves too Successor state axiom (one per fluent F)Exam favourite Poss(a, s) ⇒ [F(Result(a, s)) ⇔ (a made F true) ∨ (F(s) ∧ a did not make F false)] A fluent is true after an action if the action made it true, or if it was already true and the action did not make it false. The successor state axioms of the robot world Holding(Gold, Result(a, s)) ⇔ a = Grab ∨ (Holding(Gold, s) ∧ a ≠ Release). At(Robot, x, Result(a, s)) ⇔ a = Go(x) ∨ (At(Robot, x, s) ∧ ¬∃y a = Go(y)). At(Gold, x, Result(a, s)) ⇔ (Holding(Gold, s) ∧ a = Go(x)) ∨ (At(Gold, x, s) ∧ ¬(Holding(Gold, s) ∧ ∃y a = Go(y))). This also handles the ramification: the held gold moves with the robot. Unique action axioms say different action names are different: Grab ≠ Release, Go(x) ≠ Grab, and Go(x) = Go(y) only if x = y. F successor state axioms replace A × F frame axioms Lesson 4 of 8"],[20,"deductive","Deductive planning: a plan is a proof","Source: Green, 1969, \"Application of theorem proving to problem solving\". What it is In deductive planning the goal is a query to a theorem prover: is there a situation s in which the goal holds? A constructive proof must name such an s, and that name is the plan. Why we need it It uses one general reasoner for everything: the same prover answers questions and makes plans. Every plan comes with a proof that it works. How it works Ask ∃s At(Gold, A, s) ∧ ¬Holding(Gold, s). A resolution prover with answer extraction (it keeps track of what the variable s was bound to) returns s = Result(Release, Result(Go(A), Result(Grab, Result(Go(B), S0)))). Read the term from the inside out to get the plan: Go(B), Grab, Go(A), Release. Remember The search above is not a resolution prover. It looks through the same situation terms breadth first, which is what a fair prover ends up doing for this small problem, and it finds the same answer term. Strength of deductive planning Weakness Very expressive: quantifiers, functions, partial knowledge General theorem provers are slow and have no planning heuristics A proof that the plan works No guarantee of a short plan Clear meaning (semantics) The frame problem must be handled with care, and the search space of proofs is huge These weaknesses led Fikes and Nilsson to STRIPS in 1971: keep the idea of preconditions and effects, but drop full logic and search over states directly. That is chapter 8.2. Lesson 5 of 8"],[20,"maths","The maths: counting axioms and situations","Frame axioms neededExam favourite A × F One for each action and fluent pair that the action does not change. 20 actions and 50 fluents: up to 1,000. Successor state axioms neededExam favourite F One per fluent, however many actions there are. 50 fluents: 50 axioms. Situations up to depth d 1 + b + b² + ... + bd b actions possible in each situation. Situations are terms, so two different action sequences are always two different situations, even when the world looks the same. Worked example: 4 actions, depth 3 Depth 0: 1 situation (S0). Depth 1: 4. Depth 2: 16. Depth 3: 64. 1 + 4 + 16 + 64 = 85. 85 situation terms, but only 6 different world states in the robot world Worked example: is Release possible in S2 = Result(Grab, Result(Go(B), S0))? Poss(Release, s) ⇔ Holding(Gold, s). Holding(Gold, Result(Grab, S1)) ⇔ Grab = Grab ∨ ... which is true. So Holding(Gold, S2) holds. Release is possible in S2 Lesson 6 of 8"],[20,"compare","Situation calculus against other ways to describe actions","Representing actions Situation calculus STRIPS and PDDL Event calculus Logic Full first order Ground facts, no quantifiers in states First order, time points Frame problem Solved by successor state axioms Solved by the STRIPS assumption: facts not in the delete list stay Solved by circumscription over events Actions at the same time Hard No (sequential plans) Yes, events happen at time points Planner speed Slow (theorem proving) Fast (heuristic search) Slow Used for Theory, GOLOG robot programs, reasoning about what is known Practical planners, competitions Narratives, commonsense reasoning Bottom line: situation calculus gives the clearest meaning to actions and change. STRIPS keeps the useful part, preconditions and effects, and gives up expressiveness for speed. Real world scenario: Shakey the robot Shakey (SRI, 1966 to 1972) was the first mobile robot that reasoned about its own actions. Its first planner, QA3, used Green's deductive planning. It was too slow, so the team built STRIPS, and also the A* search algorithm (Hart, Nilsson and Raphael, 1968) to plan its routes. Lesson 7 of 8"],[20,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Keep a diary of the world: what is true at the start, and after each action. A rule for each fact says how one action changes it, and everything else stays as it was. Situations S0, Result(a, s). Fluents take a situation argument. Possibility and effect axioms. The frame problem and Reiter's successor state axioms. Green's deductive planning extracts a plan from a proof of ∃s Goal(s). Reiter's basic action theories make regression possible: to check if a goal holds after a plan, rewrite it step by step into a question about S0 only. GOLOG (1997) builds a programming language on top of situation calculus, and knowledge fluents describe what the agent knows after sensing. Research continues on epistemic planning (planning about other agents' knowledge), verification of GOLOG agent programs, and on mixing logic based action theories with probabilistic and learned models. Lesson 8 of 8"],[20,"recap","Chapter summary","Classical planning finds action sequences in a fully observable, deterministic, static and known world. Situation calculus names situations with S0 and Result(a, s). Fluents take the situation as an extra argument. Possibility and effect axioms describe actions. The frame problem: effect axioms do not say what stays the same. Successor state axioms fix it with one axiom per fluent instead of A × F frame axioms. Deductive planning (Green, 1969) proves ∃s Goal(s). The binding of s, read from the inside out, is the plan. Theorem proving is slow for planning, which led to STRIPS. Next: chapter 8.2 describes actions with STRIPS and searches through states, and shows how sub goals can interact. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun successor state axioms on a plan and find the plan term by search, then write the axioms, a plan checker and a planner yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[21,"","STRIPS planning and sub goals","STRIPS actions, forward and backward search, planning heuristics, sub goals and the Sussman anomaly. · STRIPS · PDDL · blocks world · progression · regression · planning heuristic · sub goal · Sussman anomaly · linear planning Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 8: Planning›STRIPS and sub goals Step 8 · Topic 2 of 3 · College Describe actions by what they need, add and delete, and search for plans forwards and backwards. Build planning heuristics by relaxing the problem, and see with the Sussman anomaly why solving sub goals one at a time can fail. About 50 min8 lessons2 animations5 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1STRIPS: actions with preconditions, add lists and delete lists 2Searching forwards and backwards 3Heuristics for planning 4Sub goals and the Sussman anomaly 5The maths: how big planning problems are 6Ways to plan with STRIPS actions 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[21,"strips","STRIPS: actions with preconditions, add lists and delete lists","Source: Fikes and Nilsson, 1971, and Russell and Norvig, AIMA 3rd ed., section 10.1 (4th ed., section 11.1). What it is STRIPS describes a state as a set of ground facts, such as On(C, A) and Clear(B). An action schema lists its preconditions (facts that must hold), its add list (facts it makes true) and its delete list (facts it makes false). Why we need it Situation calculus needs a theorem prover and frame axioms. STRIPS is a small language where the frame problem disappears and states can be searched directly, which made fast planners possible. How it works An action can run in state s when every precondition is in s. The new state is (s − delete list) ∪ add list. Any fact not mentioned stays as it was: this is the STRIPS assumption. Facts not listed in a state are false (closed world). Applying an actionExam favourite RESULT(s, a) = (s − DEL(a)) ∪ ADD(a), if PRE(a) ⊆ s Delete first, then add. Domain Action schema (PDDL style) Blocks world Move(b, x, y): PRE On(b, x) ∧ Clear(b) ∧ Clear(y). ADD On(b, y) ∧ Clear(x). DEL On(b, x) ∧ Clear(y) Blocks world MoveToTable(b, x): PRE On(b, x) ∧ Clear(b). ADD On(b, Table) ∧ Clear(x). DEL On(b, x) Air cargo Fly(p, from, to): PRE At(p, from) ∧ Plane(p) ∧ Airport(from) ∧ Airport(to). ADD At(p, to). DEL At(p, from) Air cargo Load(c, p, a): PRE At(c, a) ∧ At(p, a) ∧ Cargo(c) ∧ Plane(p) ∧ Airport(a). ADD In(c, p). DEL At(c, a) Spare tyre PutOn(t, Axle): PRE Tire(t) ∧ At(t, Ground) ∧ ¬At(Flat, Axle). ADD At(t, Axle). DEL At(t, Ground) Schemas have variables. A ground action replaces every variable with a constant: Move(C, A, Table) is not allowed (y must be a block), but MoveToTable(C, A) is. Remember PDDL (Planning Domain Definition Language, 1998) is the modern form of STRIPS. It adds types, negative preconditions, equality and more, and is used in the International Planning Competition. Lesson 2 of 8"],[21,"search","Searching forwards and backwards","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 10.2. Forward (progression) search Backward (regression) search Starts from the initial state the goal A step apply an action whose preconditions hold regress the goal through a relevant action Branching every applicable action, often many irrelevant ones only actions that achieve part of the goal Search nodes complete states sets of goal facts (partial descriptions) Heuristics easy to compute from a full state harder, since nodes are sets of states RegressionExam favourite g′ = (g − ADD(a)) ∪ PRE(a) a is relevant if it adds some fact of g, and consistent if it deletes none. Common mistake Regressing On(A, B) ∧ On(B, C) through Move(B, Table, C) gives a goal with both On(A, B) and Clear(B), which no state can satisfy. Backward planners need extra rules (state constraints) to throw such goals away early. Lesson 3 of 8"],[21,"heur","Heuristics for planning","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 10.2.3, and Hoffmann and Nebel, 2001 (the FF planner). What it is Planning heuristics are made automatically from the action schemas by relaxing the problem: removing some of its rules so that it becomes easy to solve. The cost of the relaxed plan estimates the real cost. Why we need it Without a heuristic, forward search tries every applicable action. With a good one, planners like FF and Fast Downward solve problems with thousands of actions. How it works Ignore preconditions: every action can always run, so count how many actions are needed to make each goal fact true. Ignore delete lists: actions never undo anything, so facts only grow, and a relaxed plan can be found fast. The length of that relaxed plan is the heuristic. Relaxation Heuristic Admissible Ignore preconditions number of unsatisfied goal facts (when each action adds one goal fact), or a set cover Yes in its exact form, the set cover version is hard to compute exactly Ignore delete lists h+, the cost of the best relaxed plan Yes, but finding h+ is NP-hard Ignore delete lists, greedy relaxed plan hFF (FF planner) No, but very informative Sub goal independence sum (or max) of the costs of the goals solved one at a time Max is admissible, sum is not Heuristics for the Sussman anomaly (true cost 3) Unsatisfied goals: On(A, B) and On(B, C). Ignore preconditions: h = 2. Ignore delete lists: MoveToTable(C, A) gives Clear(A). Move(A, Table, B) gives On(A, B). Clear(B) is never deleted, so Move(B, Table, C) still runs and gives On(B, C). Relaxed plan length: 3. h = 2 with ignore preconditions, h+ = 3 with ignore delete lists, true cost 3 Lesson 4 of 8"],[21,"subgoal","Sub goals and the Sussman anomaly","Source: Sussman, 1975, and AIMA 3rd ed., section 10.4.4. What it is A conjunctive goal such as On(A, B) ∧ On(B, C) has sub goals. A linear (non interleaved) planner solves the sub goals one at a time, in some order, and puts the plans one after the other. The original STRIPS planner worked this way with a goal stack. Why we need it Splitting a goal into parts makes each part easy. If the parts do not interact (they are independent), this is fast and gives a good plan. How it works Pick a sub goal, plan for it from the current state, run the plan, then move to the next sub goal. If a later plan undoes an earlier sub goal (clobbers it), go back and achieve it again. The Sussman anomaly: start C on A, A and B on the table. Goal On(A, B) ∧ On(B, C) Order 1, On(A, B) first: move C to the table, put A on B. Now B is under A. To get On(B, C), A must come off B, which undoes On(A, B). Order 2, On(B, C) first: put B on C. Now A is under C, which is under B. To get On(A, B), C must come off A, so B must come off C first, which undoes On(B, C). Either order undoes the first sub goal. A linear planner without going back gives a longer plan than needed (5 or 7 actions here). The best plan interleaves the two sub goals: MoveToTable(C, A) (for On(A, B)), Move(B, Table, C) (for On(B, C)), Move(A, Table, B) (for On(A, B)). sub goals interact, so plans for them must be interleaved: 3 actions Interleaving can be done by searching over partial plans instead of states, where steps for different sub goals can be ordered freely. That is the partial order planner of chapter 8.3. Lesson 5 of 8"],[21,"maths","The maths: how big planning problems are","Ground actions in the blocks world with n blocksExam favourite n(n − 1) MoveToTable + n(n − 1) Move from the table + n(n − 1)(n − 2) Move between blocks n = 3: 6 + 6 + 6 = 18. n = 4: 12 + 12 + 24 = 48. States of the blocks world 1, 3, 13, 73, 501, ... for n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 The ways to arrange n named blocks into stacks. It grows faster than n!. Complexity PlanSAT (is there any plan?) is PSPACE-complete for STRIPS Bylander, 1994. Bounded PlanSAT (is there a plan of length ≤ k?) is also PSPACE-complete in general, and NP-complete for many restricted forms. Worked example: regress On(A, B) through Move(A, Table, B) ADD = {On(A, B)}, so the action is relevant. DEL = {On(A, Table), Clear(B)} does not touch the goal, so it is consistent. g − ADD = { }. Add PRE = {On(A, Table), Clear(A), Clear(B)}. g′ = On(A, Table) ∧ Clear(A) ∧ Clear(B) Lesson 6 of 8"],[21,"compare","Ways to plan with STRIPS actions","Planning algorithms Forward search + heuristic Backward search Linear sub goal planning Partial order planning (8.3) GraphPlan and SATPlan Searches over states goal sets sub goals, one at a time partial plans a planning graph, or a SAT formula Handles interacting sub goals Yes Yes No (gives long plans or fails) Yes Yes Plan form sequence sequence sequence partial order: flexible sequence of parallel steps Strength Fastest today (FF, Fast Downward) Few relevant actions Simple Least commitment, easy to explain Good for plans with parallel actions Weakness Many irrelevant actions without a heuristic Goal sets can be impossible Incomplete for interacting goals Slower than heuristic state search Large graphs or formulas Bottom line: heuristic forward search is the usual choice today. Partial order planning still matters when a flexible plan is needed, for example when people or several robots carry out the steps. Real world scenario: Planning in practice NASA's Remote Agent planned and controlled the Deep Space 1 spacecraft in 1999. The Mars rovers use planning tools to build each day's list of actions. Logistics companies, video games (the F.E.A.R. game used STRIPS style goal planning for its enemies) and cloud systems that set up servers all use STRIPS style planners. Lesson 7 of 8"],[21,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Each action is a card: \"you need these things\", \"you get these things\", \"you lose these things\". A planner chains cards from where you are to what you want. STRIPS states are sets of facts. RESULT(s, a) = (s − DEL) ∪ ADD. Forward search uses full states, backward search regresses goals through relevant actions. Heuristics come from relaxations. Linear sub goal planning fails on the Sussman anomaly. Planning with STRIPS is PSPACE-complete. The planning graph (Blum and Furst, 1995) gives admissible heuristics with mutual exclusion links. Landmarks (facts every plan must make true) and abstraction heuristics such as pattern databases power modern optimal planners. Current research: learning heuristics and generalised policies with neural networks, planning with large language models checked by a classical planner, and temporal and numeric planning (PDDL 2.1) for real time and resources. Lesson 8 of 8"],[21,"recap","Chapter summary","STRIPS: states are sets of ground facts, actions have preconditions, an add list and a delete list. Facts not mentioned stay the same. Forward search applies actions to states. Backward search regresses goals: g′ = (g − ADD) ∪ PRE through relevant, consistent actions. Heuristics come from relaxed problems: ignore preconditions, ignore delete lists (h+, hFF), sub goal independence. Solving sub goals one at a time fails when they interact. The Sussman anomaly needs the steps for On(A, B) and On(B, C) to be interleaved. STRIPS planning is PSPACE-complete, yet heuristic planners solve large real problems. Next: chapter 8.3 plans with partial orders: add steps only as needed, link them, and order them only when they clash. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun forward search on the Sussman anomaly and regress a goal through actions, then write action application, regression and a blocks world planner yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[22,"","Partial order planning","Partial order plans, causal links, threats, promotion and demotion, and the POP algorithm. · partial order planning · POP · causal link · threat · promotion · demotion · least commitment · linearization · SNLP · UCPOP Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 8: Planning›Partial order planning Step 8 · Topic 3 of 3 · College Build plans that order steps only when they must. Learn causal links, threats, promotion and demotion, and watch the partial order planner solve socks and shoes, the spare tyre and the Sussman anomaly. About 50 min7 lessons3 animations8 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Partial plans: steps, orderings and causal links 2Threats, promotion and demotion 3The POP algorithm 4The maths: counting orders and checking threats 5POP compared with other planners 6From intuition to research 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[22,"partial","Partial plans: steps, orderings and causal links","Source: Russell and Norvig, AIMA 3rd ed., section 10.4.4, and AIMA 2nd ed., section 11.3, which gives the full algorithm used here. Sacerdoti (1975) and Tate (1977) built the first planners of this kind. What it is A partial order plan is a set of steps with only the orderings that are really needed. It has four parts: the steps, ordering constraints (A ≺ B means A comes before B), causal links (A gives fact p to B, written A →p→ B), and open preconditions (facts some step needs that no link supplies yet). Why we need it State space planners fix the full order of every action, even when the order does not matter. Fixing an order too early can force the planner to undo work later. Leaving steps unordered until a clash appears is called least commitment, and it handles sub goals that interact, such as the Sussman anomaly. How it works Start with two dummy steps. Start has the initial state as its effects. Finish has the goal as its preconditions. Then repeat: pick an open precondition, add or reuse a step that achieves it, record a causal link and an ordering, and protect every link from steps that could undo it. Part Meaning Socks and shoes example Steps actions in the plan, plus Start and Finish RightSock, RightShoe, LeftSock, LeftShoe Ordering A ≺ B A must run before B RightSock ≺ RightShoe Causal link A →p→ B A makes p true for B, and nothing may undo p between them RightSock →RightSockOn→ RightShoe Open precondition a precondition with no causal link yet at the start: RightShoeOn and LeftShoeOn of Finish Partial order planning: add steps, link them, fix threatsA partial plan has steps, ordering constraints (A before B) and causal links (A gives fact p to B). The planner picks an open precondition, finds a step that achieves it, and protects every causal link from steps that would delete its fact. Step 0 of 7 Problem Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Socks and shoes. The final plan only says each sock goes before its shoe. Left and right are not ordered: any of the 6 orders that keep sock before shoe works. The spare tyre. LeaveOvernight would delete facts that causal links need, so it is never added. Free(Axle) stands for the textbook precondition ¬At(Flat, Axle). The Sussman anomaly. Partial order planning handles the interacting sub goals by ordering steps around threats, and finds the 3 step plan. The finished plan says only that each sock goes on before its shoe. Any total order that keeps those two rules is a valid plan. Such a total order is called a linearization of the partial plan. Python # A partial order plan keeps only the orderings it needs. Count the total orders (linearizations) it allows.steps = [\"RightSock\", \"RightShoe\", \"LeftSock\", \"LeftShoe\"]before = [(\"RightSock\", \"RightShoe\"), (\"LeftSock\", \"LeftShoe\")] # (a, b) means a must come before bdef linearize(done, left):ifnot left:return [done] out = []for s in left: ready = Truefor a, b in before:if b == s and a in left: # a must come first but has not been placed ready = Falseif ready: rest = [x for x in left if x != s] out = out + linearize(done + [s], rest)return outorders = linearize([], steps)print(\"total orders allowed:\", len(orders))for o in orders:print(\" \", \", \".join(o)) Output total orders allowed: 6 RightSock, RightShoe, LeftSock, LeftShoe RightSock, LeftSock, RightShoe, LeftShoe RightSock, LeftSock, LeftShoe, RightShoe LeftSock, RightSock, RightShoe, LeftShoe LeftSock, RightSock, LeftShoe, RightShoe LeftSock, LeftShoe, RightSock, RightShoe Remember A partial plan is consistent when its orderings have no cycle (A ≺ B and B ≺ A cannot both hold) and no causal link is threatened. A consistent plan with no open preconditions is a solution: every linearization of it reaches the goal. Lesson 2 of 7"],[22,"threats","Threats, promotion and demotion","Source: AIMA 2nd ed., section 11.3, and McAllester and Rosenblitt, 1991 (SNLP). What it is A step C threatens the causal link A →p→ B when C deletes p and the orderings allow C to run after A and before B. If C ran there, B would no longer have p. Why we need it Causal links are the promises a partial plan makes. A threat can break a promise, so the plan would fail in some of its linearizations. How it works Fix a threat by adding one ordering. Demotion puts C before A (C ≺ A). Promotion puts C after B (B ≺ C). Keep the fix only if the orderings still have no cycle. If neither works, the planner goes back and tries another choice. ThreatExam favourite C deletes p, and neither C ≺ A nor B ≺ C follows from the orderings C could sit between A and B. Demotion add C ≺ A C runs before p is made. Promotion add B ≺ C C runs after p is used. Worked example from the Sussman anomaly animation Link: Start →Clear(C)→ MoveToTable(C, A). MoveToTable(C, A) needs C to be clear. New step Move(B, Table, C) deletes Clear(C), and nothing yet orders it. Demotion would put Move(B, Table, C) before Start, which is not allowed: Start is always first. Promotion puts it after MoveToTable(C, A). The orderings stay free of cycles. MoveToTable(C, A) ≺ Move(B, Table, C) Remember Some textbooks add a third fix for lifted plans with variables: separation, a constraint such as x ≠ C that stops the threatening step from deleting this exact fact. Lesson 3 of 7"],[22,"algorithm","The POP algorithm","Source: AIMA 2nd ed., section 11.3, Penberthy and Weld, 1992 (UCPOP), and Weld, 1994, An Introduction to Least Commitment Planning. POP (partial order planner) plan = steps {Start, Finish}, orderings {Start ≺ Finish}, links { } agenda = every precondition of Finish loop: if the agenda is empty: return plan pick an open precondition p of step B from the agenda choose a step A that adds p (an old step, or a new one from the schemas) if A is new: add A, add Start ≺ A ≺ Finish, put the preconditions of A on the agenda add the causal link A →p→ B and the ordering A ≺ B for every threat C to any link A →q→ B: choose demotion (C ≺ A) or promotion (B ≺ C) if it keeps the orderings free of cycles if neither works: fail and go back to the last choice There are two kinds of choices. Picking which open precondition to work on next does not need going back: every one must be solved in the end. Choosing the achieving step and the threat fix does need going back, so POP is a search over partial plans. Why POP solves the Sussman anomaly It adds Move(A, Table, B) for On(A, B) and MoveToTable(C, A) to clear A, with no order against the B steps yet. It adds Move(B, Table, C) for On(B, C). This step deletes Clear(C), which MoveToTable(C, A) needs, so it is promoted after MoveToTable(C, A). Move(A, Table, B) deletes Clear(B), which Move(B, Table, C) needs, so it is promoted after Move(B, Table, C). plan: MoveToTable(C, A), Move(B, Table, C), Move(A, Table, B), 3 actions Remember POP is sound (every plan it returns works) and complete (it finds a plan if one exists) when the step and threat choices are searched fully, for example breadth first or with iterative deepening on the number of steps. The animation uses iterative deepening, so it reports the step limit it needed. Lesson 4 of 7"],[22,"maths","The maths: counting orders and checking threats","Linearizations of independent chainsExam favourite (n₁ + n₂ + ... + n_k)! ÷ (n₁! n₂! ... n_k!) k chains of steps, chain i has n_i steps in a fixed order, and the chains do not constrain each other. This is the multinomial coefficient. Two chains of length 2 4! ÷ (2! × 2!) = 24 ÷ 4 = 6 The socks and shoes plan allows 6 total orders. Steps with no orderings at all n! Every order is allowed. Causal links in a finished plan number of links = total number of preconditions of all steps (including Finish) Each precondition gets exactly one link. Cost of one threat check links × steps pairs, each needing a reachability test in the ordering graph Reachability is a graph search, about O(steps + orderings) each time. Worked example: count the causal links in the finished Sussman plan Finish needs On(A, B) and On(B, C): 2 preconditions. Move(A, Table, B) needs On(A, Table), Clear(A), Clear(B): 3. MoveToTable(C, A) needs On(C, A), Clear(C): 2. Move(B, Table, C) needs On(B, Table), Clear(B), Clear(C): 3. 2 + 3 + 2 + 3 = 10 causal links Worked example: socks, shoes and gloves Sock then shoe on the right is a chain of 2 steps. Sock then shoe on the left is another chain of 2. The two gloves need no order, so each glove is a chain of 1 step. Chains have lengths 2, 2, 1, 1. Total steps: 6. 6! = 720. Divide by 2! × 2! × 1! × 1! = 4. 720 ÷ 4 = 180 total orders Lesson 5 of 7"],[22,"compare","POP compared with other planners","Partial order planning and its neighbours Partial order planning Forward state search with heuristics Linear sub goal planning GraphPlan Hierarchical task networks (HTN) Searches over partial plans world states one sub goal at a time a layered planning graph ways to break big tasks into smaller ones Plan form partial order, flexible total order total order layers of parallel actions partial or total order Interacting sub goals handled by threat fixes handled by search often fails or gives long plans handled by mutex links handled by the method author Speed today slower on large problems fastest general method (FF, Fast Downward) fast but incomplete good on small parallel problems very fast with good methods Best use flexible plans to explain or to run in parallel large benchmark problems teaching, independent goals parallel plans, heuristics real systems with expert knowledge Bottom line: POP lost the speed race in the late 1990s, but RePOP (Nguyen and Kambhampati, 2001) showed that state space heuristics make it competitive again. Its plans are easy to explain and to run with several agents. Real world scenario: Where partial order plans are used NASA spacecraft and rover planners such as the Remote Agent on Deep Space 1 (1999) used plans with flexible timing, a direct descendant of partial order planning. Plans that people carry out, such as maintenance procedures and project schedules, keep only the orderings that matter so that work can run in parallel. Multi robot systems use partial orders so that each robot can move as soon as its own steps are ready. Lesson 6 of 7"],[22,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Getting dressed: you must put a sock on before its shoe, but you can do the left or right foot first. A partial order plan writes down only the \"before\" rules that matter. A partial plan has steps, orderings, causal links and open preconditions. POP closes open preconditions with causal links and fixes threats by demotion or promotion. A plan is a solution when it has no open preconditions, no threats and no ordering cycles. Chapman (1987) gave the Modal Truth Criterion for his planner TWEAK, an exact test of when a fact must hold at a point of a partial plan, and showed that such tests become intractable for richer action languages. SNLP (McAllester and Rosenblitt, 1991) made the search systematic, so no partial plan is visited twice. UCPOP (Penberthy and Weld, 1992) added variables, conditional effects and universal quantifiers. Research: temporal planners with simple temporal networks keep flexible start times. Plan repair and plan explanation reuse causal links. Large language models are now tested on producing partial order plans, with a classical planner or validator such as VAL used to check them. Lesson 7 of 7"],[22,"recap","Chapter summary","A partial order plan has steps, ordering constraints, causal links and open preconditions. Start gives the initial state and Finish needs the goal. POP picks an open precondition, adds or reuses a step that achieves it, and records a causal link and an ordering. A step threatens a causal link A →p→ B if it deletes p and could run between A and B. Fix it by demotion (before A) or promotion (after B). Least commitment: steps stay unordered until a threat forces an order. This solves the Sussman anomaly with 3 actions. A finished plan stands for all its linearizations. Independent chains of lengths n₁, ..., n_k give (Σ n_i)! ÷ Π n_i! orders. Next: step 9 moves from plans to beliefs: probability, Bayesian networks and reasoning under uncertainty. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCount the linearizations of a partial plan and run a small partial order planner on the spare tyre problem, then write a cycle check, a threat fixer and an order counter yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[23,"","Probability for AI agents","Probability axioms, joint distributions, Bayes’ rule, independence and naive Bayes. · probability · joint distribution · marginalisation · conditional probability · Bayes' rule · base rate · independence · conditional independence · naive Bayes Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 9: Probability and Bayesian networks›Probability basics Step 9 · Topic 1 of 4 · College Measure belief with numbers. Answer questions from a full joint table, turn causes into diagnoses with Bayes’ rule, and use conditional independence to build a naive Bayes classifier. About 50 min8 lessons1 animation19 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why agents need probability 2The full joint distribution and inference by enumeration 3Bayes' rule 4Independence, conditional independence and naive Bayes 5The maths: rules every probability step uses 6Probability compared with other ways to handle uncertainty 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[23,"why","Why agents need probability","Source: Russell and Norvig, AIMA 3rd ed., sections 13.1 and 13.2 (4th ed., sections 12.1 and 12.2). The uploaded slides and transcripts do not cover this step, so it follows the textbook. What it is Probability gives a number between 0 and 1 to a sentence, saying how strongly the agent believes it. 1 means sure it is true, 0 means sure it is false. Everything in between is a degree of belief. Why we need it Logic needs facts that are surely true or false. Real agents rarely have that. A dentist cannot list every cause of a toothache (laziness), does not know all of medicine (theoretical ignorance), and has not run every test on this patient (practical ignorance). Logic rules like \"Toothache ⇒ Cavity\" are simply false for many patients. How it works Give each possible world a probability. The probability of a sentence is the sum over the worlds where it is true. New evidence changes beliefs by conditioning: throw away the worlds the evidence rules out and rescale the rest. Kolmogorov axiomsExam favourite 0 ≤ P(ω) ≤ 1 for every world ω, and the sum of P(ω) over all worlds is 1 Everything else follows from these. Probability of a sentence P(φ) = Σ P(ω) over worlds ω where φ is true A sentence is a set of worlds. Inclusion and exclusion P(a ∨ b) = P(a) + P(b) − P(a ∧ b) Do not count the shared worlds twice. Conditional probabilityExam favourite P(a | b) = P(a ∧ b) ÷ P(b), when P(b) > 0 Belief in a after learning only b. Remember Why these rules and no others: de Finetti (1931) showed that an agent whose beliefs break the axioms can be offered a set of bets it accepts and always loses (a Dutch book). So a rational agent that bets on its beliefs must follow the axioms. Common mistake P(a | b) is not \"if b then a with probability p\". It is belief in a when b is all the agent knows. If more evidence c arrives, the agent must use P(a | b ∧ c). Lesson 2 of 8"],[23,"jointdist","The full joint distribution and inference by enumeration","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 13.3, figure 13.3 (the dentist example). What it is A random variable has a set of possible values, such as Cavity ∈ {true, false}. The full joint distribution P(Toothache, Catch, Cavity) gives the probability of every combination of values. Why we need it From the full joint we can answer every question about these variables: a marginal such as P(cavity), or a conditional such as P(cavity | toothache). How it works Marginalise (sum out) the variables we do not care about. Condition by keeping only the rows that match the evidence. Normalise by dividing by the total that is left, written with α = 1 ÷ P(evidence). Marginalisation P(Y) = Σ over z of P(Y, z) Sum out the other variables Z. Inference by enumerationExam favourite P(X | e) = α P(X, e) = α Σ over y of P(X, e, y) X query, e evidence, y hidden variables. Size of the full joint 2ⁿ entries for n true or false variables (2ⁿ − 1 free numbers) This is why we need Bayesian networks. Worked example: P(cavity ∨ toothache) The worlds where cavity or toothache is true: all four cavity rows, plus the two no cavity rows with a toothache. Cavity rows: 0.108 + 0.012 + 0.072 + 0.008 = 0.2. No cavity, toothache rows: 0.016 + 0.064 = 0.08. P(cavity ∨ toothache) = 0.2 + 0.08 = 0.28 Lesson 3 of 8"],[23,"bayesrule","Bayes' rule","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 13.5. Thomas Bayes (1763) and Pierre Simon Laplace (1812). What it is Bayes' rule turns a probability around: from P(effect | cause), which is easy to measure, to P(cause | effect), which we need for diagnosis. Why we need it A doctor knows how often a disease causes a symptom. That number stays the same in an epidemic. The chance that a symptom means the disease changes with how common the disease is, and Bayes’ rule takes care of that. How it works Multiply the likelihood P(e | c) by the prior P(c), then divide by P(e). Find P(e) by adding the same product over every possible cause (total probability). Bayes' ruleExam favourite P(c | e) = P(e | c) P(c) ÷ P(e) posterior = likelihood × prior ÷ evidence. Total probability P(e) = P(e | c) P(c) + P(e | ¬c) P(¬c) The bottom line of Bayes’ rule. Odds form posterior odds = prior odds × likelihood ratio, where the likelihood ratio is P(e | c) ÷ P(e | ¬c) Easy to update one test at a time. Worked example: the medical test (base rate) Prior: 1 percent of people have the disease, P(d) = 0.01. The test finds 90 percent of real cases, P(+ | d) = 0.9, and wrongly says positive for 9 percent of healthy people, P(+ | ¬d) = 0.09. P(+) = 0.9 × 0.01 + 0.09 × 0.99 = 0.009 + 0.0891 = 0.0981. P(d | +) = 0.009 ÷ 0.0981. P(d | +) ≈ 0.0917, only about 9 percent Common mistake The base rate fallacy: most people guess about 90 percent for the test above. Healthy people are so many that their few false positives outnumber the true positives. Lesson 4 of 8"],[23,"indep","Independence, conditional independence and naive Bayes","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., sections 13.4 and 13.5.2, and the naive Bayes text classifier in section 13.5.3 (4th ed., 12.6). What it is Two variables are independent when learning one tells nothing about the other: P(a | b) = P(a). They are conditionally independent given C when, once C is known, one tells nothing more about the other: P(a | b, c) = P(a | c). Why we need it Independence splits a huge joint table into small pieces. Absolute independence is rare, but conditional independence is common: Toothache and Catch both depend on Cavity, but given Cavity they no longer depend on each other. How it works Write the joint as a product of small tables. With a cause and n effects that are conditionally independent given the cause, the joint needs 2n + 1 numbers instead of 2ⁿ⁺¹ − 1. This is the naive Bayes model. Independence P(a ∧ b) = P(a) P(b) Same as P(a | b) = P(a). Conditional independenceExam favourite P(a ∧ b | c) = P(a | c) P(b | c) Written a ⫫ b | c. Naive Bayes P(Cause, e₁, ..., eₙ) = P(Cause) × Π P(eᵢ | Cause) Then P(Cause | e) = α P(Cause) Π P(eᵢ | Cause). Laplace (add one) smoothing P(w | c) = (count(w, c) + 1) ÷ (total words in c + vocabulary size) A word never seen with class c no longer gives probability 0. Worked example: check Toothache ⫫ Catch | Cavity in the dentist table P(toothache ∧ catch | cavity) = 0.108 ÷ 0.2 = 0.54. P(toothache | cavity) = (0.108 + 0.012) ÷ 0.2 = 0.6. P(catch | cavity) = (0.108 + 0.072) ÷ 0.2 = 0.9. 0.6 × 0.9 = 0.54, so they are conditionally independent given Cavity Remember Naive Bayes is called naive because the effects are rarely truly independent given the class. It still classifies well in practice (spam filters, document topics), because the class with the highest score is often right even when the probabilities themselves are off. Lesson 5 of 8"],[23,"maths","The maths: rules every probability step uses","Product rule P(a ∧ b) = P(a | b) P(b) = P(b | a) P(a) Bayes’ rule comes from these two forms. Chain ruleExam favourite P(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π over i of P(xᵢ | x₁, ..., xᵢ₋₁) Always true. Bayesian networks shorten each condition. Bayes with background evidence P(c | e, b) = P(e | c, b) P(c | b) ÷ P(e | b) Keep b on every term. Expected value E[X] = Σ x P(X = x) Used again for decisions and for reinforcement learning. Variance Var[X] = E[X²] − (E[X])² How spread out X is. Worked example: Monty Hall with Bayes’ rule The car is behind door 1, 2 or 3, each with prior 1/3. You pick door 1. The host, who knows where the car is, opens door 3 and shows a goat. Likelihood of \"host opens 3\": if the car is behind 1, he picks 2 or 3 at random, so 1/2. If behind 2, he must open 3, so 1. If behind 3, he cannot open it, so 0. P(host opens 3) = 1/2 × 1/3 + 1 × 1/3 + 0 × 1/3 = 1/2. P(car behind 2 | host opens 3) = (1 × 1/3) ÷ 1/2 = 2/3. switching wins with probability 2/3, staying with 1/3 Going deeper: Where probabilities come from The frequentist view says a probability is a long run frequency. The Bayesian (subjective) view says it is a degree of belief that must obey the axioms, updated with Bayes’ rule. The principle of indifference gives equal probability to symmetric outcomes. AI systems mostly take the Bayesian view, while learning the numbers from data (chapter on learning in AIMA chapter 20). Lesson 6 of 8"],[23,"compare","Probability compared with other ways to handle uncertainty","Ways to reason under uncertainty Probability theory Logic with default rules Certainty factors (MYCIN) Fuzzy logic Dempster Shafer theory What a number means degree of belief that a fact is true no numbers, rules hold \"by default\" change in belief from one rule degree to which a vague fact holds (\"tall\") belief and plausibility, an interval Combines evidence by Bayes' rule withdrawing conclusions fixed formulas per rule min and max Dempster's rule Sound for chains of reasoning Yes Partly No (can double count evidence) It answers a different question (vagueness) Yes, but costly Used today Everywhere: medicine, robotics, speech, spam Knowledge bases Historic Control systems Sensor fusion niches Bottom line: probability is the standard because it is the only system that rational betting forces (Dutch book) and because Bayesian networks make it efficient. Real world scenario: A spam filter that learns Paul Graham’s 2002 essay \"A Plan for Spam\" made naive Bayes spam filters popular. Each word gets P(word | spam) and P(word | ham) from mail the user has already sorted. A new message is spam when α P(spam) Π P(wordᵢ | spam) is larger than the same product for ham. Smoothing keeps a word seen only in ham from making spam impossible. Lesson 7 of 8"],[23,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Probability is a number for how sure you are. When you learn something new, cross out the cases that cannot happen and share the belief among the cases left. Axioms, the full joint, marginalising and conditioning, Bayes’ rule with total probability, independence and conditional independence, naive Bayes with smoothing. Exchangeability (de Finetti), conjugate priors (Beta for a coin, Dirichlet for word counts), maximum likelihood versus maximum a posteriori estimates, and why the full joint is exponential: the motivation for graphical models. Research: probabilistic programming languages (Stan, Pyro) that compile models into inference code, calibration of neural network probabilities, and imprecise probability for agents that cannot pin down a single number. Lesson 8 of 8"],[23,"recap","Chapter summary","Probability measures degree of belief. The Kolmogorov axioms, forced by the Dutch book argument, fix its rules. The full joint distribution answers every query: marginalise hidden variables, keep worlds that match the evidence, normalise with α. Bayes' rule P(c | e) = P(e | c) P(c) ÷ P(e) turns causal knowledge into diagnosis. Watch the base rate. Independence and conditional independence break the joint into small factors. Naive Bayes needs 2n + 1 numbers for a cause with n effects. The full joint has 2ⁿ entries, too many for real problems. Bayesian networks, next, fix this. Next: chapter 9.2 draws conditional independence as a graph: Bayesian networks and Markov networks. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterAnswer queries from the dentist joint table, apply Bayes’ rule to a medical test and train a naive Bayes spam filter, then write marginals, conditionals and Bayes’ rule yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[24,"","Bayesian networks and Markov networks","Directed and undirected graphical models: CPTs, d-separation, Markov blankets, factors and Z. · Bayesian network · CPT · d-separation · explaining away · Markov blanket · noisy OR · Markov network · partition function · directed model · undirected model Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 9: Probability and Bayesian networks›Bayesian networks Step 9 · Topic 2 of 4 · College Draw uncertain knowledge as a graph. Directed Bayesian networks store small tables P(node | parents), d-separation reads independence from the arrows, and undirected Markov networks score worlds with factors and a partition function. About 55 min8 lessons2 animations14 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions4 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Bayesian networks: a graph plus small tables 2Reading independence from the graph: d-separation 3Compact tables: deterministic nodes, noisy OR and continuous variables 4Undirected models: Markov networks 5The maths: counting parameters and checking independence 6Graphical models compared 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[24,"bn","Bayesian networks: a graph plus small tables","Source: Pearl (1988), Probabilistic Reasoning in Intelligent Systems, and AIMA 3rd ed., sections 14.1 and 14.2 (4th ed., 13.1 and 13.2). The burglary network is AIMA figure 14.2. What it is A Bayesian network is a directed graph with no cycles. Each node is a random variable. An arrow X → Y means X directly influences Y. Each node has a conditional probability table (CPT) giving P(node | its parents). Why we need it The full joint has 2ⁿ entries. Most variables only depend directly on a few others. The network stores only those local tables, so a model of hundreds of variables fits in a few thousand numbers, and experts can read and check it. How it works The joint probability of any world is the product of one entry from each table: P(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)). This is the chain rule, with each condition cut down to the parents. Network semanticsExam favourite P(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π over i of P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)) One table entry per variable. Size of a CPT a true or false node with k true or false parents needs 2ᵏ numbers One number per row, since P(false) = 1 − P(true). Size of the network Σ over nodes of 2^(number of parents) n nodes with at most k parents: at most n 2ᵏ, against 2ⁿ − 1. Node Parents Table (P of true) Burglary none 0.001 Earthquake none 0.002 Alarm Burglary, Earthquake b e: 0.95, b ¬e: 0.94, ¬b e: 0.29, ¬b ¬e: 0.001 JohnCalls Alarm a: 0.90, ¬a: 0.05 MaryCalls Alarm a: 0.70, ¬a: 0.01 Python # The burglary network of AIMA figure 14.2. Each table gives P(variable = true | its parents).parents = {\"B\": [], \"E\": [], \"A\": [\"B\", \"E\"], \"J\": [\"A\"], \"M\": [\"A\"]}table = {\"B\": {(): 0.001}, \"E\": {(): 0.002},\"A\": {(True, True): 0.95, (True, False): 0.94, (False, True): 0.29, (False, False): 0.001},\"J\": {(True,): 0.90, (False,): 0.05}, \"M\": {(True,): 0.70, (False,): 0.01}}def prob(var, value, world): key = tuple(world[p] for p in parents[var]) p = table[var][key]return p if value else1 - pdef joint(world):# chain rule shortened by the network: multiply P(x | parents(x)) for every variable result = 1for var in [\"B\", \"E\", \"A\", \"J\", \"M\"]: result = result * prob(var, world[var], world)return resultw = {\"B\": False, \"E\": False, \"A\": True, \"J\": True, \"M\": True}print(\"P(j, m, a, not b, not e) =\", round(joint(w), 6))count = 0for var in parents: count = count + 2 ** len(parents[var])print(\"numbers in the network:\", count, \" numbers in the full joint:\", 2 ** 5 - 1) Output P(j, m, a, not b, not e) = 0.000628 numbers in the network: 10 numbers in the full joint: 31 Worked example: P(j, m, a, ¬b, ¬e) Take one entry from each table: P(j | a) = 0.90, P(m | a) = 0.70, P(a | ¬b, ¬e) = 0.001. P(¬b) = 0.999, P(¬e) = 0.998. Multiply: 0.90 × 0.70 × 0.001 × 0.999 × 0.998. ≈ 0.000628 Remember Build a network in causal order: causes first, then effects. The diagnostic order (MaryCalls, JohnCalls, Alarm, Burglary, Earthquake) is still correct but needs more arrows and harder numbers, such as P(Earthquake | Burglary, Alarm). Lesson 2 of 8"],[24,"dsepl","Reading independence from the graph: d-separation","Source: Pearl (1988), Geiger, Verma and Pearl (1990), and AIMA 3rd ed., section 14.2.2. What it is The graph says which variables are conditionally independent. Each node is independent of its non descendants given its parents. More generally, X and Y are independent given Z when Z d-separates them: every path between them is blocked. Why we need it With d-separation we can see from the drawing alone which evidence matters for a query. Inference programs use it to skip whole parts of the network. How it works Check each path three nodes at a time. A chain A → M → B or a fork A ← M → B is blocked when M is observed. A collider A → M ← B is blocked unless M or one of its descendants is observed. Pattern Example Blocked when Plain meaning Chain A → M → B Burglary → Alarm → JohnCalls M is observed once you know the alarm rang, the burglary tells nothing more about John Fork A ← M → B JohnCalls ← Alarm → MaryCalls M is observed both calls depend only on the alarm Collider A → M ← B Burglary → Alarm ← Earthquake M and all its descendants are not observed two causes are independent until we see their common effect (explaining away) Markov blanketExam favourite parents, children, and the children’s other parents Given its blanket, a node is independent of every other node. Remember Explaining away: if the alarm rang, a burglary is likely. If we then learn an earthquake happened, the burglary becomes less likely, because the earthquake already explains the alarm. The two causes were independent before we saw the alarm. Lesson 3 of 8"],[24,"cpts","Compact tables: deterministic nodes, noisy OR and continuous variables","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 14.3, and Pearl (1988) for noisy OR. What it is Some CPTs follow a simple rule, so they need far fewer than 2ᵏ numbers. A noisy OR node has a separate chance qᵢ that cause i fails to produce the effect, and the failures are independent. Why we need it A disease with 10 possible causes needs 1024 rows in a full CPT. Noisy OR needs 10 numbers, which an expert can actually give. How it works The effect is false only if every present cause fails: P(¬effect | present causes) = product of their qᵢ. Deterministic nodes are fixed functions of their parents. Continuous nodes use formulas, such as a linear Gaussian whose mean is a linear function of the parents. Noisy ORExam favourite P(¬x | parents) = Π over true parents j of q_j q_j is the chance that cause j alone fails. Linear Gaussian P(x | h) = N(x | mean a h + b, variance σ²) A continuous child of a continuous parent h. Worked example: fever with q_cold = 0.6, q_flu = 0.2, q_malaria = 0.1 (AIMA) Cold and flu present, no malaria. P(¬fever) = 0.6 × 0.2 = 0.12. P(fever | cold, flu, ¬malaria) = 1 − 0.12 = 0.88 Lesson 4 of 8"],[24,"undirected","Undirected models: Markov networks","Source: Kindermann and Snell (1980), Koller and Friedman (2009), Probabilistic Graphical Models, chapter 4 (the misconception example), and AIMA 4th ed., section 13.2. What it is A Markov network (Markov random field) is an undirected graph. Each clique (a set of nodes all joined to each other) has a factor φ, a table of non negative numbers that scores how well the values fit together. Why we need it Some relations have no direction: neighbouring pixels in an image tend to share a colour, friends tend to share opinions. Forcing arrows on them gives the wrong independencies. Undirected models state them directly. How it works Multiply the factors of a world to get its score. Divide by the partition function Z, the sum of the scores of all worlds, to get a probability. Two sets of nodes are independent given S when every path between them passes through S. Gibbs distributionExam favourite P(x) = (1 ÷ Z) Π over cliques c of φ_c(x_c) Factors need not add up to 1. Partition function Z = Σ over all worlds x of Π φ_c(x_c) A sum with 2ⁿ terms: computing it is the hard part. Hammersley Clifford theorem a positive distribution has the independencies of the graph exactly when it factorises over the cliques Links the graph to the formula. Log linear (energy) form P(x) = (1 ÷ Z) exp(Σ wᵢ fᵢ(x)) Used by conditional random fields and Boltzmann machines. Bayesian network (directed) Markov network (undirected) Parts CPTs P(X | parents) factors φ on cliques Normalised each CPT row adds up to 1, so the product is a probability only after dividing by Z Independence test d-separation plain graph separation Can express explaining away (colliders) cycles of mutual influence, such as A - B - C - D - A Learning the numbers counting, one table at a time needs Z, so iterative methods Remember To turn a Bayesian network into a Markov network, moralise it: join the parents of each child (they \"marry\"), then drop the arrow heads. The result may lose some independencies, such as Burglary ⫫ Earthquake. Lesson 5 of 8"],[24,"maths","The maths: counting parameters and checking independence","Parameters with at most k parentsExam favourite n 2ᵏ, against 2ⁿ − 1 for the full joint n = 30, k = 5: 960 against about 1.07 × 10⁹. Local Markov property X ⫫ NonDescendants(X) | Parents(X) Equivalent to the product formula for a Bayesian network. Markov blanket property P(X | everything else) = P(X | MB(X)) ∝ P(X | parents) × Π over children Y of P(y | parents(Y)) Used by Gibbs sampling in chapter 9.3. Moral ancestral graph test X ⫫ Y | Z exactly when X and Y are not joined in the moralised graph of the ancestors of X, Y and Z, after removing Z Lauritzen and others, 1990. This is the algorithm in the code above. Worked example: P(Cloudy, ¬Sprinkler, Rain, WetGrass) in the sprinkler network P(Cloudy) = 0.5. P(¬Sprinkler | Cloudy) = 1 − 0.1 = 0.9. P(Rain | Cloudy) = 0.8. P(WetGrass | ¬Sprinkler, Rain) = 0.9. 0.5 × 0.9 × 0.8 × 0.9 = 0.324 Worked example: Z for the chain A - B - C where each factor gives 4 to equal values and 1 to different values Worlds 000 and 111 score 4 × 4 = 16 each: 32. Worlds with one disagreement (001, 011, 100, 110) score 4 × 1 = 4 each: 16. Worlds 010 and 101 disagree twice and score 1 each: 2. Z = 32 + 16 + 2 = 50 Lesson 6 of 8"],[24,"compare","Graphical models compared","Choosing a model for uncertain knowledge Full joint table Naive Bayes Bayesian network Markov network Factor graph Structure none one cause, independent effects directed graph, no cycles undirected graph variables joined to factors Size 2ⁿ − 1 2n + 1 Σ 2^(parents) Σ clique table sizes Σ factor table sizes Strength answers anything tiny and fast causal, readable, easy to learn from counts symmetric relations, images, text labels one form for both directed and undirected models, used by message passing Weakness exponential size ignores links between effects cannot show some symmetric dependencies Z is hard, numbers are hard to read less intuitive for experts Bottom line: use a Bayesian network when causes and effects are clear, a Markov network for symmetric relations, and a factor graph inside inference code. Real world scenario: Bayesian networks in real use Microsoft’s Windows printer troubleshooter (1990s) used a Bayesian network to pick the next question to ask. The Pathfinder system (Heckerman, 1991) diagnosed lymph node diseases at expert level. The CTBT monitoring system NET-VISA (Arora, Russell and Sudderth, 2013), which detects nuclear tests from seismic signals, is a large probabilistic model of this family. Image segmentation and part of speech tagging used Markov random fields and conditional random fields (Lafferty, McCallum and Pereira, 2001). Lesson 7 of 8"],[24,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Draw arrows from causes to effects, and for each effect write how likely it is for each mix of its causes. The drawing tells you what can affect what. A Bayesian network is a DAG with CPTs, and the joint is the product of the CPT entries. d-separation (chain, fork, collider) reads independence from the graph. Markov networks use clique factors and Z. I-maps and perfect maps: some distributions have no perfect directed map and some no perfect undirected map. Markov equivalence: graphs with the same skeleton and the same colliders (v-structures) encode the same independencies, so data alone cannot always fix arrow directions. Research: learning the structure of a network from data (score based search, the PC algorithm), deep generative models seen as graphical models (variational autoencoders), and relational models (Markov logic networks) that mix first order logic with Markov networks. Lesson 8 of 8"],[24,"recap","Chapter summary","A Bayesian network is a directed graph with no cycles plus a CPT per node. P(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)). It needs Σ 2^(parents) numbers instead of 2ⁿ − 1. The burglary network needs 10 instead of 31. d-separation: chains and forks are blocked by observing the middle, colliders are opened by observing the middle or a descendant. Noisy OR, deterministic and linear Gaussian nodes make tables even smaller. Markov networks use clique factors: P(x) = (1 ÷ Z) Π φ_c(x_c). Z is a sum over all worlds, which is what makes them hard. Next: chapter 9.3 computes answers from these models: exact variable elimination and approximate sampling. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCompute a joint entry of the burglary network, test d-separation with the moral ancestral graph and compute Z of a Markov network, then write joint probability, parameter counting, Markov blankets and d-separation yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[25,"","Inference in Bayesian networks","Enumeration, variable elimination, complexity, rejection sampling, likelihood weighting and Gibbs sampling. · inference · enumeration · variable elimination · factor · treewidth · polytree · rejection sampling · likelihood weighting · Gibbs sampling · MCMC Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 9: Probability and Bayesian networks›Inference Step 9 · Topic 3 of 4 · College Get answers out of a Bayesian network. Sum exactly with enumeration and variable elimination, see why exact inference can be hard, and estimate with rejection sampling, likelihood weighting and Gibbs sampling. About 55 min7 lessons2 animations14 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Exact inference by enumeration 2Variable elimination 3Approximate inference by sampling 4The maths: why the samplers give the right answer 5Inference methods compared 6From intuition to research 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[25,"enumeration","Exact inference by enumeration","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 14.4.1 (4th ed., 13.3.1), figures 14.8 and 14.9. What it is Inference means computing P(query | evidence) from the network. Enumeration writes the answer as a sum of products of CPT entries, summing over every value of every hidden variable. Why we need it It is the simplest correct method and the reference that every faster method must agree with. How it works Go through the variables in topological order. For a query or evidence variable, multiply in its table entry. For a hidden variable, try both values, recurse, and add. Do this for each query value, then normalise. EnumerationExam favourite P(B | j, m) = α Σ_e Σ_a P(B) P(e) P(a | B, e) P(j | a) P(m | a) Hidden variables E and A are summed out. Moving factors outside α P(B) Σ_e P(e) Σ_a P(a | B, e) P(j | a) P(m | a) P(B) does not depend on e or a, so it moves out of both sums. Cost time O(2ⁿ), memory O(n) Depth first over the hidden variables. Python # Inference by enumeration on the burglary network (AIMA figure 14.9): P(Burglary | j, m).parents = {\"B\": [], \"E\": [], \"A\": [\"B\", \"E\"], \"J\": [\"A\"], \"M\": [\"A\"]}table = {\"B\": {(): 0.001}, \"E\": {(): 0.002},\"A\": {(True, True): 0.95, (True, False): 0.94, (False, True): 0.29, (False, False): 0.001},\"J\": {(True,): 0.90, (False,): 0.05}, \"M\": {(True,): 0.70, (False,): 0.01}}order = [\"B\", \"E\", \"A\", \"J\", \"M\"]def prob(var, value, world): p = table[var][tuple(world[q] for q in parents[var])]return p if value else1 - pdef enum_all(i, world):if i == len(order):return1.0 var = order[i]if var in world: # evidence or query: one valuereturn prob(var, world[var], world) * enum_all(i + 1, world) total = 0# hidden: sum over both valuesfor v in [True, False]: world[var] = v total = total + prob(var, v, world) * enum_all(i + 1, world)del world[var]return totalyes = enum_all(0, {\"B\": True, \"J\": True, \"M\": True})no = enum_all(0, {\"B\": False, \"J\": True, \"M\": True})print(\"unnormalised:\", round(yes, 7), round(no, 7))print(\"P(Burglary | j, m) =\", round(yes / (yes + no), 3)) Output unnormalised: 0.0005922 0.0014919 P(Burglary | j, m) = 0.284 Worked example: P(b, j, m) for the burglary network P(b) = 0.001. For each e and a, multiply P(e) P(a | b, e) P(j | a) P(m | a). e true: 0.002 × (0.95 × 0.9 × 0.7 + 0.05 × 0.05 × 0.01) = 0.002 × 0.598525. e false: 0.998 × (0.94 × 0.9 × 0.7 + 0.06 × 0.05 × 0.01) = 0.998 × 0.59223. Sum: 0.00119705 + 0.59104554 = 0.59224259. Times P(b): 0.00059224. P(¬b, j, m) ≈ 0.0014919 in the same way, so P(b | j, m) = 0.00059224 ÷ 0.00208414 ≈ 0.284 Common mistake Enumeration repeats work: P(j | a) P(m | a) is computed again for each value of e. Variable elimination stores such parts once. Lesson 2 of 7"],[25,"ve","Variable elimination","Source: Zhang and Poole (1994), Dechter (1999), and AIMA 3rd ed., section 14.4.2. What it is Variable elimination does the sums of enumeration from the inside out and keeps the results as tables called factors, so nothing is computed twice. Why we need it It turns many repeated products into a few table operations. On a polytree (a network with at most one path between any two nodes) it runs in time linear in the size of the network. How it works Make one factor per CPT with the evidence plugged in. For each hidden variable in some order: multiply all factors that mention it (a pointwise product) and sum the variable out. At the end multiply what is left and normalise. Pointwise product f(X, Y) × g(Y, Z) = h(X, Y, Z), with h(x, y, z) = f(x, y) g(y, z) The result covers the union of the variables. Summing outExam favourite h(X, Z) = Σ_y f(X, y, Z) Add the entries for each value of the removed variable. Cost exponential in the size of the largest factor made, which depends on the order The best possible largest factor size, minus one, is the treewidth of the graph. Remember Irrelevant variables: every variable that is not an ancestor of the query or the evidence can be removed before starting. Summing it out only gives factors full of 1s, as the second tip of the animation shows. Going deeper: Complexity of exact inference On polytrees, variable elimination and Pearl’s message passing are linear. In general, exact inference in Bayesian networks is #P-hard (Cooper, 1990), because it can count the solutions of a SAT formula. Finding the best elimination order is NP-hard, so programs use greedy rules such as min fill or min degree. The junction tree algorithm (Lauritzen and Spiegelhalter, 1988) groups variables into clusters so that all marginals come from one pass of messages. Lesson 3 of 7"],[25,"sampling","Approximate inference by sampling","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 14.5 (4th ed., 13.4). Likelihood weighting: Fung and Chang (1989), Shachter and Peot (1989). Gibbs sampling: Geman and Geman (1984). What it is Sampling methods draw many random worlds from the network and count. The fraction of samples where the query holds estimates its probability. Why we need it Exact inference can take exponential time on big networks with many loops. Sampling gives an answer of any wanted accuracy, and more samples give a better answer. How it works Prior sampling sets each variable from its CPT, parents first. Rejection sampling throws away samples that disagree with the evidence. Likelihood weighting fixes the evidence and weights each sample by how likely that evidence was. Gibbs sampling walks from world to world, resampling one hidden variable at a time from its Markov blanket. Rejection sampling P(X | e) ≈ N(X, e) ÷ N(e) Consistent, but wastes the fraction 1 − P(e) of samples. Likelihood weightExam favourite w = Π over evidence variables Eᵢ of P(eᵢ | parents(Eᵢ)) Estimate: sum of weights with X true ÷ sum of all weights. Gibbs step P(x | mb(X)) = α P(x | parents(X)) Π over children Y of P(y | parents(Y)) Only the Markov blanket is needed. Error of an estimate standard error ≈ √(p (1 − p) ÷ N) Four times the samples halves the error. Worked example: one likelihood weighted sample for P(Rain | Sprinkler = true, WetGrass = true) Cloudy is sampled from P(Cloudy) = 0.5: say true. Sprinkler is evidence (true): weight × P(s | c) = 0.1. Rain is sampled from P(Rain | c) = 0.8: say true. WetGrass is evidence (true): weight × P(w | s, r) = 0.99. w = 0.1 × 0.99 = 0.099, the textbook example Common mistake Likelihood weighting suffers when evidence comes late in the order (at the leaves): the samples ignore it while choosing the earlier variables, so most of them get tiny weights. Lesson 4 of 7"],[25,"maths","The maths: why the samplers give the right answer","Prior sampling is consistent S(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)) = P(x₁, ..., xₙ) Each world is drawn with exactly its probability. Likelihood weighting is consistentExam favourite S_WS(z, e) w(z, e) = Π P(zᵢ | parents) × Π P(eᵢ | parents) = P(z, e) The sampling distribution times the weight is the true joint. Gibbs sampling the posterior P(x | e) is the stationary distribution of the Markov chain Detailed balance: π(x) q(x → x′) = π(x′) q(x′ → x). Variable elimination size eliminating X makes a factor over all variables that share a factor with X Bad orders build large factors. Worked example: a Gibbs step for Cloudy, with Sprinkler = true and Rain = false The Markov blanket of Cloudy is its two children, Sprinkler and Rain. Cloudy has no parents, and its children have no other parents. Cloudy true: P(c) P(s | c) P(¬r | c) = 0.5 × 0.1 × 0.2 = 0.01. Cloudy false: 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.8 = 0.2. P(Cloudy | s, ¬r) = 0.01 ÷ 0.21 ≈ 0.0476 Lesson 5 of 7"],[25,"compare","Inference methods compared","Inference in graphical models Enumeration Variable elimination Junction tree Rejection sampling Likelihood weighting Gibbs sampling (MCMC) Loopy belief propagation Exact Yes Yes Yes No (converges) No (converges) No (converges) No (may not converge) Cost O(2ⁿ) time, O(n) memory exponential in the largest factor exponential in the largest cluster grows as 1 ÷ P(e) cheap per sample cheap per step cheap per pass Good for tiny networks, checking one query, small treewidth all marginals at once likely evidence evidence near the roots large networks, any evidence large networks with loops, error correcting codes Weak when more than about 25 variables large treewidth large treewidth rare evidence evidence at the leaves near deterministic tables (slow mixing) strong loops Bottom line: use variable elimination or the junction tree when the treewidth is small, and Gibbs sampling or other MCMC methods when it is not. Real world scenario: Inference at work Turbo codes and LDPC codes in mobile phones and Wi-Fi are decoded with belief propagation on a loopy graph (McEliece, MacKay and Cheng, 1998). Stan and PyMC run Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, a modern MCMC method, for statistics in medicine and science. Bayesian network tools such as Hugin and GeNIe use the junction tree for exact answers in medical and fault diagnosis. Lesson 6 of 7"],[25,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Exact inference adds up every way the world could be. Sampling plays the world many times at random and counts what happened. Enumeration sums products over hidden variables. Variable elimination stores factors and sums out one variable at a time. Rejection sampling, likelihood weighting and Gibbs sampling estimate the answer, with error about √(p(1 − p) ÷ N). Exact inference is #P-hard, and even approximating it within a fixed error is NP-hard in general (Dagum and Luby, 1993). Treewidth decides exact cost. Variational methods turn inference into optimisation of a simpler distribution by minimising a KL divergence. Research: amortised inference, where a neural network learns to output posteriors fast (as in variational autoencoders), sequential Monte Carlo (particle filters) for changing worlds, and knowledge compilation into arithmetic circuits for fast exact queries. Lesson 7 of 7"],[25,"recap","Chapter summary","Enumeration: P(X | e) = α Σ over hidden y of Π CPT entries. O(2ⁿ) time, O(n) memory. P(b | j, m) ≈ 0.284. Variable elimination multiplies factors and sums out hidden variables one at a time. Its cost depends on the largest factor, set by the order and the treewidth. Exact inference is linear on polytrees and #P-hard in general. Rejection sampling wastes samples that disagree with rare evidence. Likelihood weighting fixes evidence and weights samples by its likelihood. Gibbs sampling resamples one variable from its Markov blanket and converges to the posterior. Next: chapter 9.4 asks a different question: what happens if we act on the world, not just watch it. That is causality. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun enumeration on the burglary network and likelihood weighting on the sprinkler network, then write enumeration, rejection sampling and likelihood weighting yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[26,"","Causality: seeing, doing and imagining","Causal networks, the do operator, back door adjustment, Simpson’s paradox and counterfactuals. · causality · do operator · intervention · confounder · back door criterion · adjustment formula · Simpson's paradox · counterfactual · structural causal model · front door Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 9: Probability and Bayesian networks›Causality Step 9 · Topic 4 of 4 · College Tell correlation from cause. Use the do operator and graph surgery for actions, the back door adjustment to undo confounding (and explain Simpson’s paradox), and structural causal models for counterfactual questions. About 50 min8 lessons1 animation9 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Seeing, doing and imagining: the ladder of causation 2The do operator and graph surgery 3Confounders, the back door criterion and Simpson's paradox 4Counterfactuals with structural causal models 5The maths: adjustment, front door and effects 6Ways to learn what causes what 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[26,"ladder","Seeing, doing and imagining: the ladder of causation","Source: Pearl (2009), Causality, 2nd ed., Pearl and Mackenzie (2018), The Book of Why, and AIMA 4th ed., section 13.5 (causal networks). The uploaded slides and transcripts do not cover this topic. What it is A causal network is a Bayesian network whose arrows mean \"directly causes\". It answers three kinds of question: seeing (what does the evidence tell me), doing (what happens if I act), and imagining (what would have happened if things had been different). Why we need it Probability alone mixes up correlation and cause. Ice cream sales and drownings rise together because hot weather causes both. Banning ice cream would not save swimmers. An agent that acts on the world needs to know what its actions cause, not only what goes with what. How it works Seeing uses conditioning: P(y | x). Doing uses the do operator: P(y | do(x)) means we set X to x by our own hand, which cuts every arrow into X. Imagining uses a structural causal model to replay one case with a change. Rung Question Written as Example 1. Association (seeing) What if I see x? P(y | x) Patients who took the drug recovered more often. 2. Intervention (doing) What if I do x? P(y | do(x)) Would recovery rise if we gave everyone the drug? 3. Counterfactual (imagining) What if I had done x instead? P(y_x | x′, y′) This patient took the drug and recovered. Would they have recovered without it? Common mistake A plain Bayesian network can be read in a non causal order and still give the right P(y | x). It can only answer rung 2 questions when its arrows are causal. Lesson 2 of 8"],[26,"dooper","The do operator and graph surgery","Source: Pearl (2009), sections 1.3 and 3.2, and Spirtes, Glymour and Scheines (1993), the manipulation theorem. What it is do(X = x) is an action that sets X to x from outside the system. In the graph it removes every arrow into X and fixes X. The rest of the network stays the same. Why we need it When we observe the sprinkler on, it also tells us the day is probably not cloudy, so rain is less likely. When we switch the sprinkler on ourselves, the weather is untouched. Conditioning cannot tell these apart, and the do operator can. How it works Use the truncated product: the same product of CPTs as the network, but leave out the table of X and plug in X = x everywhere. Truncated factorisationExam favourite P(v | do(x)) = Π over i with Vᵢ not X of P(vᵢ | parents(Vᵢ)), with X = x The table of X is removed. Root causes if X has no parents, P(y | do(x)) = P(y | x) Nothing to cut, so seeing equals doing. Average causal effect ACE = P(y | do(x)) − P(y | do(¬x)) The quantity a randomised trial estimates. Worked example: seeing versus doing for the sprinkler Seeing: P(Rain | Sprinkler = true) = Σ_c P(c) P(s | c) P(r | c) ÷ Σ_c P(c) P(s | c) = (0.5 × 0.1 × 0.8 + 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.2) ÷ (0.05 + 0.25). That is 0.09 ÷ 0.3 = 0.3. The sprinkler being on suggests a sunny day. Doing: cut Cloudy → Sprinkler. Rain is untouched: P(Rain | do(s)) = Σ_c P(c) P(r | c) = 0.5 × 0.8 + 0.5 × 0.2. P(Rain | do(Sprinkler = true)) = 0.5, not 0.3 Remember A randomised controlled trial (Fisher, 1935) performs do(X) in the real world: a coin, not the patient or the doctor, decides the treatment, so every arrow into the treatment is cut. Lesson 3 of 8"],[26,"backdoor","Confounders, the back door criterion and Simpson's paradox","Source: Pearl (1993, 2009), section 3.3, Simpson (1951), and Charig and others (1986) for the kidney stone data. What it is A confounder is a common cause of the treatment and the outcome. It opens a back door path T ← Z → R, so the observed link between T and R mixes the real effect with the effect of Z. Why we need it We often cannot run an experiment. The back door criterion says when we can still compute P(r | do(t)) from observational data, and how. How it works A set Z satisfies the back door criterion for (T, R) when no node in Z is a descendant of T, and Z blocks every path between T and R that starts with an arrow into T. Then adjust: average P(r | t, z) over the population share P(z). Back door adjustmentExam favourite P(r | do(t)) = Σ_z P(r | t, z) P(z) Weight each group by its share of everyone, not its share among those treated. Compare with seeing P(r | t) = Σ_z P(r | t, z) P(z | t) The only difference is P(z | t) in place of P(z). Role of Z Graph Adjust for Z? Confounder T ← Z → R Yes: it closes the back door Mediator T → Z → R No: it would remove part of the effect you want Collider T → Z ← R No: conditioning on it creates a false link (Berkson’s paradox) Common mistake Simpson’s paradox is not a fault in arithmetic. Which answer is right, the pooled one or the one inside groups, depends on the causal graph. With stone size as a confounder, the group answer (A is better) is right. If Z were a mediator, the pooled answer would be right. Lesson 4 of 8"],[26,"counterfactual","Counterfactuals with structural causal models","Source: Pearl (2009), chapter 7, and Balke and Pearl (1994). What it is A structural causal model (SCM) writes each variable as a function of its parents and a hidden noise term U: Y = f(X, U). A counterfactual asks what Y would have been in this very case if X had been different. Why we need it Questions about blame, fairness and regret are counterfactual: \"would she have been hired if she were a man\", \"would the patient have lived without the drug\". Rung 2 answers are about populations. Rung 3 answers are about one case. How it works Three steps. Abduction: use what happened to work out the noise U for this case. Action: replace the equation of X by X = x′. Prediction: compute Y from the changed model with the same U. Worked example: Y = 2X + U We saw X = 1 and Y = 3. Abduction: 3 = 2 × 1 + U, so U = 1 for this case. Action: set X = 0. Prediction: Y = 2 × 0 + 1. Y would have been 1 Going deeper: Identification and the do calculus Pearl’s three rules of the do calculus (1995) turn expressions with do into expressions without it whenever that is possible. The calculus is complete: if the rules cannot remove every do, no method can compute the effect from observational data alone (Shpitser and Pearl, 2006, Huang and Valtorta, 2006). The front door adjustment P(y | do(x)) = Σ_m P(m | x) Σ_x′ P(y | m, x′) P(x′) handles a hidden confounder when the effect passes through an observed mediator M, as in smoking → tar → cancer. Lesson 5 of 8"],[26,"maths","The maths: adjustment, front door and effects","Back doorExam favourite P(y | do(x)) = Σ_z P(y | x, z) P(z) Z blocks every back door path and has no descendants of X. Front door P(y | do(x)) = Σ_m P(m | x) Σ_x′ P(y | m, x′) P(x′) M carries all of the effect and has no hidden common cause with X. Inverse probability weighting P(y | do(x)) = Σ over units with X = x of 1 ÷ (N P(x | z)) Same answer as back door adjustment, used with many variables in Z. Average causal effect ACE = P(y | do(x)) − P(y | do(¬x)) Kidney stones: 0.833 − 0.779 ≈ 0.054. Worked example: a front door problem P(x) = 0.5. P(m | x) = 0.9, P(m | ¬x) = 0.1. P(y | m, x) = 0.6, P(y | m, ¬x) = 0.4, P(y | ¬m, x) = 0.3, P(y | ¬m, ¬x) = 0.1. For m: Σ_x′ P(y | m, x′) P(x′) = 0.6 × 0.5 + 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.5. For ¬m: 0.3 × 0.5 + 0.1 × 0.5 = 0.2. Weight by P(m | x): 0.9 × 0.5 + 0.1 × 0.2 = 0.45 + 0.02. P(y | do(x)) = 0.47 Lesson 6 of 8"],[26,"compare","Ways to learn what causes what","Getting causal answers Randomised trial Back door adjustment Front door adjustment Instrumental variables Causal discovery from data Needs the power to assign treatment all confounders measured a mediator that carries the whole effect a variable that moves T but affects R only through T assumptions such as faithfulness Gives P(r | do(t)) directly P(r | do(t)) from observations P(r | do(t)) even with a hidden confounder the effect under linear or monotone models a set of possible graphs Weak when costly, slow or unethical a confounder is missed the mediator has other causes shared with T the instrument is weak data are few or variables hidden Example drug trials kidney stones smoking, tar and cancer distance to a hospital as an instrument the PC algorithm (Spirtes and Glymour, 1991) Bottom line: run an experiment when you can. When you cannot, draw the causal graph first, then pick the formula the graph allows. Real world scenario: Causal reasoning in practice Medical guidelines rest on randomised trials because they cut every arrow into the treatment. Online companies run A/B tests, which are small randomised trials, before changing a web page. Economists won the 2021 Nobel prize (Card, Angrist and Imbens) for natural experiments and instrumental variables. In AI, causal models help with fairness (would the decision change if only the protected attribute changed) and with robustness, since causal links stay the same when the data distribution shifts. Lesson 7 of 8"],[26,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Seeing two things together does not mean one causes the other. To find out, change one thing yourself and watch what happens to the other. A causal network adds meaning to the arrows. do(x) cuts the arrows into X. The truncated product, the back door adjustment and Simpson’s paradox follow from this. The do calculus is complete for identification. Counterfactuals need structural equations, not just CPTs. Markov equivalent graphs differ in causal meaning, so data alone cannot always fix the arrows. Research: causal representation learning (finding causal variables in raw data such as images), causal reinforcement learning, invariant risk minimisation, and testing whether large language models can reason about interventions and counterfactuals. Lesson 8 of 8"],[26,"recap","Chapter summary","Three rungs: seeing P(y | x), doing P(y | do(x)), imagining counterfactuals. do(x) cuts the arrows into X. The truncated product drops the table of X. Seeing and doing agree for root causes. Back door adjustment: P(r | do(t)) = Σ_z P(r | t, z) P(z) when Z blocks every back door path and is not caused by T. Simpson’s paradox: kidney stone treatment A looks worse overall (78 against 83 percent) but is better in each group, and better under do (83.3 against 77.9 percent). Counterfactuals: abduction, action, prediction on a structural causal model. Next: step 10 moves from beliefs to decisions over time: Markov decision processes and reinforcement learning. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCompare seeing and doing in the sprinkler network and adjust the kidney stone data, then write the observed rate, the back door adjustment and a do query with graph surgery yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[27,"","Markov decision processes","MDPs, the Bellman equation, value iteration, policy iteration and Q-values. · MDP · policy · discount · Bellman equation · value iteration · policy iteration · Q-value · contraction · POMDP Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 10: Reinforcement learning›MDPs Step 10 · Topic 1 of 4 · College Plan when actions have chance outcomes. Describe the problem as states, actions, transitions and rewards, then find the best policy with the Bellman equation, value iteration, policy iteration and Q-values. About 55 min7 lessons2 animations14 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Markov decision processes 2The Bellman equation and value iteration 3Policy iteration and Q-values 4The maths: bounds and counts 5MDP solvers compared 6From intuition to research 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[27,"mdp","","Source: Bellman (1957), Howard (1960), and AIMA 3rd ed., sections 17.1 to 17.3 (4th ed., 16.1 and 16.2). The 4 × 3 world is AIMA figure 17.1. The racing car example is from Berkeley CS188. The uploaded slides and transcripts do not cover this step. What it is A Markov decision process (MDP) is a problem where an agent acts again and again in a world with chance. It has states, actions, a transition model P(s′ | s, a), a reward for each state (or each move), and usually a discount γ. The Markov property says the next state depends only on the current state and action, not on the past. Why we need it Search assumed every action has one sure result. Real robots slip, markets move, opponents surprise. A plan as a fixed list of actions breaks the first time something unexpected happens. How it works Instead of a plan, find a policy π(s): the action to take in every state. The best policy π* gets the highest expected total discounted reward from every state. It is found with the Bellman equation. Part 4 × 3 world Racing car States 11 squares cool, warm, overheated (end) Actions Up, Down, Left, Right slow, fast Transitions intended move 0.8, each side 0.1 cool + fast: cool or warm (0.5 each), warm + fast: overheated Rewards R = −0.04 per step, +1 and −1 at the end squares slow +1, fast +2, overheating −10 Discount γ = 1 γ = 0.9 Utility of a reward sequenceExam favourite U = R₀ + γ R₁ + γ² R₂ + ... = Σ γᵗ Rₜ γ between 0 and 1. With γ < 1 the sum is at most R_max ÷ (1 − γ). Utility of a state under π U^π(s) = E[Σ γᵗ R(Sₜ) | S₀ = s, actions from π] Expected discounted reward when starting in s. Optimal policy π*(s) = argmax over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U(s′) One step look ahead with the true utilities. Remember Why discount: a reward now is worth more than the same reward later, an agent may stop at any time, and with γ < 1 infinite sums stay finite. γ close to 1 means a patient agent. Lesson 2 of 7"],[27,"bellmanl","The Bellman equation and value iteration","Source: Bellman (1957), and AIMA 3rd ed., section 17.2. What it is The Bellman equation says the utility of a state is its reward plus the discounted expected utility of the next state, if the agent then acts optimally. Value iteration solves it by repeating the equation as an update until the values stop changing. Why we need it There is one equation per state, but the max makes them non linear, so they cannot be solved like ordinary linear equations. Repeating the update always works and is simple. How it works Start with U(s) = 0. In each sweep, set every U(s) to R(s) + γ max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U(s′), using the old values. Stop when the largest change is small. Then read the policy from the values. Bellman equationExam favourite U(s) = R(s) + γ max over a of Σ over s′ of P(s′ | s, a) U(s′) One equation per state. Bellman update U_(i+1)(s) ← R(s) + γ max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U_i(s′) Value iteration repeats this. Contraction ‖U_(i+1) − U‖ ≤ γ ‖U_i − U‖ Each sweep shrinks the error by at least γ (in the max norm), so value iteration always converges for γ < 1. Stopping rule if ‖U_(i+1) − U_i‖ < ε (1 − γ) ÷ γ then ‖U_(i+1) − U‖ < ε A safe test for \"close enough\". Worked example: the first sweep for square (3,3) of the 4 × 3 world All utilities start at 0, except the end squares: +1 and −1. Action Right: 0.8 × U(4,3) + 0.1 × U(3,3) + 0.1 × U(3,2) = 0.8 × 1 + 0 + 0 = 0.8. No other action does better. U₁(3,3) = −0.04 + 1 × 0.8 = 0.76 Lesson 3 of 7"],[27,"pil","Policy iteration and Q-values","Source: Howard (1960), Puterman and Shin (1978) for modified policy iteration, and AIMA 3rd ed., section 17.3. What it is Policy iteration works with policies, not values. It alternates two steps: policy evaluation (find U^π for the current policy) and policy improvement (change the action in each state to the best one under those values). A Q-value Q(s, a) is the expected utility of taking action a in state s and acting optimally afterwards. Why we need it Often the policy becomes optimal long before the values stop changing. Policy iteration stops as soon as the policy stops changing, usually after very few rounds. Q-values let an agent pick the best action without a transition model, which is the key to Q-learning in chapter 10.3. How it works Evaluation: with the action in each state fixed, the Bellman equations lose the max and become linear, so solve them exactly (or with a few sweeps, as in modified policy iteration). Improvement: π(s) ← argmax over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U(s′). Stop when nothing changes. Policy evaluationExam favourite U^π(s) = R(s) + γ Σ P(s′ | s, π(s)) U^π(s′) n linear equations, O(n³) to solve exactly. Q-value Q(s, a) = Σ P(s′ | s, a) [R(s, a, s′) + γ max over a′ of Q(s′, a′)] U(s) = max over a of Q(s, a). Termination there are |A|^|S| policies and each round is at least as good as the last So policy iteration ends after a finite number of rounds. Worked example: evaluate the policy (cool: fast, warm: slow) of the racing car with γ = 0.9 V(cool) = 0.5 (2 + 0.9 V(cool)) + 0.5 (2 + 0.9 V(warm)) = 2 + 0.45 V(cool) + 0.45 V(warm). V(warm) = 0.5 (1 + 0.9 V(cool)) + 0.5 (1 + 0.9 V(warm)) = 1 + 0.45 V(cool) + 0.45 V(warm). Subtract: V(cool) − V(warm) = 1. Put V(cool) = V(warm) + 1 into the second line: V(warm) = 1.45 + 0.9 V(warm). V(warm) = 14.5, V(cool) = 15.5 Going deeper: Partially observable MDPs When the agent cannot see the state, it keeps a belief state b(s), a probability for each state, and updates it with Bayes’s rule after each action and percept. A POMDP is an MDP over belief states, which are continuous, so it is much harder: finding optimal POMDP policies is PSPACE-hard even for finite horizons (Papadimitriou and Tsitsiklis, 1987). Point based value iteration and online search such as POMCP make large POMDPs practical. Lesson 4 of 7"],[27,"maths","The maths: bounds and counts","Bound on utilitiesExam favourite |U(s)| ≤ R_max ÷ (1 − γ) Geometric series: R_max (1 + γ + γ² + ...). Sweeps needed N = ⌈log(2 R_max ÷ (ε (1 − γ))) ÷ log(1 ÷ γ)⌉ Enough sweeps for error below ε, starting from 0. It grows fast as γ nears 1. Policy loss if ‖U_i − U‖ < ε then ‖U^(π_i) − U‖ < 2 ε γ ÷ (1 − γ) The greedy policy is good even before the values have fully converged. Number of policies |A|^|S| 4 × 3 world: 4⁹ = 262,144 policies for the 9 squares where a choice matters. Worked example: two sweeps of value iteration on the racing car (γ = 0.9) Sweep 1 (from 0): cool: slow gives 1, fast gives 2, so 2. warm: slow gives 1, fast gives −10, so 1. Sweep 2, cool, fast: 0.5 (2 + 0.9 × 2) + 0.5 (2 + 0.9 × 1) = 1.9 + 1.45 = 3.35. Slow: 1 + 0.9 × 2 = 2.8. So 3.35. Sweep 2, warm, slow: 0.5 (1 + 1.8) + 0.5 (1 + 0.9) = 1.4 + 0.95 = 2.35. Fast: −10. So 2.35. V₂(cool) = 3.35, V₂(warm) = 2.35 Lesson 5 of 7"],[27,"compare","MDP solvers compared","Solving a known MDP Value iteration Policy iteration Modified policy iteration Linear programming Expectimax search (6.3) Works on utilities policies policies, with a few sweeps for evaluation utilities as LP variables a tree from the current state Cost per round O(|S|² |A|) O(|S|³) for exact evaluation plus O(|S|² |A|) O(k |S|²) plus O(|S|² |A|) polynomial overall exponential in the depth Rounds many when γ is near 1 few few one LP one search per move Best for simple and general small state spaces the usual choice in practice theory: MDPs are solvable in polynomial time huge state spaces, online decisions Bottom line: value iteration is the easiest to write, and policy iteration usually needs fewer rounds. All of them need the full model, which reinforcement learning (next chapters) does without. Real world scenario: MDPs in the real world MDPs plan maintenance (when to repair a machine as it wears), stock control (how much to order each week), medical treatment plans (when to change a drug), and robot navigation with wheel slip. Airline seat pricing and the energy grid also use dynamic programming over MDP models. Lesson 6 of 7"],[27,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A policy is a rule book: in this situation, do this. The value of a situation is how much reward you expect from it on average if you follow the best rule book. MDP = states, actions, P(s′ | s, a), R, γ. Bellman equation, value iteration (contraction by γ), policy iteration (evaluate, improve), Q-values. Bounds: utilities at most R_max ÷ (1 − γ), stopping rule ε (1 − γ) ÷ γ, policy loss 2εγ ÷ (1 − γ). MDPs are P-complete (Papadimitriou and Tsitsiklis, 1987), POMDPs are much harder. Research: approximate dynamic programming with function approximation, factored MDPs that exploit structure, safe and constrained MDPs, and large scale planning with learned models (as in MuZero, Schrittwieser and others, 2020). Lesson 7 of 7"],[27,"recap","Chapter summary","An MDP has states, actions, P(s′ | s, a), rewards and a discount γ. A solution is a policy π(s). Bellman equation: U(s) = R(s) + γ max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U(s′). Value iteration repeats the Bellman update. It converges because each sweep shrinks the error by γ. In the 4 × 3 world U(1,1) = 0.705. Policy iteration alternates exact evaluation (linear equations) and greedy improvement, and stops after a few rounds. Q(s, a) = Σ P(s′ | s, a) [R + γ max Q(s′, a′)] gives the best action without a model, the bridge to reinforcement learning. Next: chapter 10.2 learns utilities when the agent does not know P or R: passive reinforcement learning. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun value iteration and policy iteration on the racing car MDP, then write discounted returns, a Bellman update and value iteration with policy extraction yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[28,"","Passive reinforcement learning","Direct utility estimation, adaptive dynamic programming and temporal difference learning for a fixed policy. · passive reinforcement learning · direct utility estimation · adaptive dynamic programming · temporal difference · TD error · learning rate · TD lambda · Monte Carlo Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 10: Reinforcement learning›Passive RL Step 10 · Topic 2 of 4 · College Learn how good a fixed policy is from experience alone. Average rewards to go, learn a model and solve it, or update after every step with temporal difference learning. About 45 min7 lessons1 animation9 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Passive learning: judge a fixed policy without a model 2Temporal difference learning 3Adaptive dynamic programming 4The maths: bias, variance and fixed points 5Passive learners compared 6From intuition to research 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[28,"passive","Passive learning: judge a fixed policy without a model","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., sections 21.1 and 21.2 (4th ed., 22.1 and 22.2), Sutton and Barto (2018), Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction, chapters 5 and 6. The uploaded slides and transcripts do not cover this step. What it is Reinforcement learning (RL) learns to act well from rewards alone, without being told the transition model P or the reward function R. In passive RL the policy π is fixed, and the agent only learns how good it is: the utilities U^π(s). Why we need it In most real problems nobody can write down P and R: a robot does not know how its wheels slip on a new floor, a game program does not know how its opponents play. The agent must learn from trials. How it works Run trials from a start state to an end state, following π, and record the states and rewards seen. Then use them in one of three ways: average what followed each state (direct estimation), learn P and R and solve the Bellman equations (adaptive dynamic programming), or nudge each U(s) towards what happened next (temporal difference learning). Method What it learns Update Speed Direct utility estimation U(s) as an average average of all rewards to go seen from s slow: ignores links between states Adaptive dynamic programming (ADP) P̂(s′ | s, π(s)) and R(s) solve U(s) = R(s) + γ Σ P̂ U(s′) after each step fastest per trial, costly per step Temporal difference (TD) U(s) directly U(s) ← U(s) + α (R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s)) cheap per step, a bit slower than ADP Python # The three trials of AIMA section 21.2 in the 4 x 3 world. Each step costs -0.04, the end square gives +1 or -1.trials = [[\"11\", \"12\", \"13\", \"12\", \"13\", \"23\", \"33\", \"43\"], [\"11\", \"12\", \"13\", \"23\", \"33\", \"32\", \"33\", \"43\"], [\"11\", \"21\", \"31\", \"32\", \"42\"]]end = {\"43\": 1.0, \"42\": -1.0}# Direct utility estimation: average the reward to go of every visit.total, count = {}, {}for t in trials:for i inrange(len(t) - 1): to_go = -0.04 * (len(t) - 1 - i) + end[t[-1]] s = t[i] total[s] = total.get(s, 0) + to_go count[s] = count.get(s, 0) + 1print(\"direct estimate of U(1,1):\", round(total[\"11\"] / count[\"11\"], 4))# Adaptive dynamic programming learns the model first: count where each square led.moves = {}for t in trials:for i inrange(len(t) - 1): moves.setdefault(t[i], []).append(t[i + 1])for s in [\"13\", \"33\"]: nxt = moves[s]print(\"from\", s, \":\", {n: str(nxt.count(n)) + \"/\" + str(len(nxt)) for n in sorted(set(nxt))}) Output direct estimate of U(1,1): 0.0933 from 13 : {'12': '1/3', '23': '2/3'} from 33 : {'32': '1/3', '43': '2/3'} Worked example: direct estimation of U(1,1) from the three textbook trials Trial 1 visits (1,1) once, then 6 more squares before reaching +1: reward to go = 7 × (−0.04) + 1 = 0.72. Trial 2 also gives 0.72. Trial 3 goes (1,1), (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) and ends at −1: 4 × (−0.04) − 1 = −1.16. U(1,1) ≈ (0.72 + 0.72 − 1.16) ÷ 3 ≈ 0.0933, far from the true 0.705 after only 3 trials Lesson 2 of 7"],[28,"tdl","Temporal difference learning","Source: Sutton (1988), Learning to predict by the methods of temporal differences, and AIMA 3rd ed., section 21.2.3. What it is TD learning updates the guess U(s) after every single step, using the guess for the next state: the target is R(s) + γ U(s′). The difference between the target and the old guess is the TD error. Why we need it It needs no model and no waiting until the end of a trial. Because the target uses U(s′), information about rewards flows backwards from state to state, much faster than averaging whole trials. How it works After the move s → s′, compute δ = R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s), then set U(s) ← U(s) + α δ. With a learning rate α that shrinks the right way, U converges to U^π. TD(0) updateExam favourite U(s) ← U(s) + α (R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s)) α is the learning rate. TD error δ = R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s) Positive: things went better than expected. Learning rate schedule Σ αₙ = ∞ and Σ αₙ² < ∞, for example αₙ = 1 ÷ n or 60 ÷ (59 + n) Robbins and Monro (1951). Then TD converges. TD(λ) U(x) ← U(x) + α δ e(x), with traces e(x) that decay by γ λ each step λ = 0 is TD(0), λ = 1 is like averaging whole trials. Worked example: one TD update U(s) = 0.5, the agent moves to s′ with U(s′) = 0.8, R(s) = −0.04, γ = 1, α = 0.1. TD error δ = −0.04 + 0.8 − 0.5 = 0.26. U(s) ← 0.5 + 0.1 × 0.26. U(s) = 0.526 Remember TD learning from experience is the same idea as the Bellman update, but it uses the one next state that actually happened instead of an average over all next states. Over many steps, states are reached in proportion to P(s′ | s, a), so the averages come out right. Lesson 3 of 7"],[28,"adp","Adaptive dynamic programming","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 21.2.2, and Moore and Atkeson (1993) for prioritised sweeping. What it is An ADP agent learns the transition model from the counts of what happened, then solves the Bellman equations of the learned MDP for the fixed policy. Why we need it It uses every bit of the data: each observed move updates the model and, through the equations, all utilities at once. It learns from the fewest trials. How it works Keep counts N(s, a, s′). Set P̂(s′ | s, a) = N(s, a, s′) ÷ N(s, a). After each step, run policy evaluation on (P̂, R). Prioritised sweeping updates first the states whose values are most likely to change. Learned modelExam favourite P̂(s′ | s, a) = N(s, a, s′) ÷ N(s, a) Maximum likelihood from counts. Worked example: the model from the three trials (1,3) was left 3 times with the action Right: twice to (2,3), once to (1,2). P̂((2,3) | (1,3), Right) = 2 ÷ 3. (3,3) was left 3 times with Right: twice to (4,3), once to (3,2). P̂((4,3) | (3,3), Right) = 2 ÷ 3, close to the true 0.8 only after more trials Lesson 4 of 7"],[28,"maths","The maths: bias, variance and fixed points","Monte Carlo target Gₜ = Rₜ + γ Rₜ₊₁ + γ² Rₜ₊₂ + ... Unbiased, but high variance: it depends on every later random step. TD targetExam favourite Rₜ + γ U(Sₜ₊₁) Biased while U is wrong, but low variance: it depends on one step. Eligibility trace e(s) ← γ λ e(s), then e(Sₜ) ← e(Sₜ) + 1 Credit fades by γλ per step. Batch TD answer the values of the maximum likelihood Markov model of the data Batch Monte Carlo gives the averages of the observed returns instead. Worked example: batch TD versus Monte Carlo (Sutton and Barto, example 6.4) Eight episodes: once A, 0, B, 0. Six times B, 1. Once B, 0. (γ = 1) Both methods: U(B) = 6 ÷ 8 = 0.75. Monte Carlo: the only episode through A returned 0, so U(A) = 0. Batch TD: A always went to B with reward 0, so U(A) = 0 + U(B). U(A) = 0.75 by TD, 0 by Monte Carlo. TD uses the Markov structure. Lesson 5 of 7"],[28,"compare","Passive learners compared","Learning U^π from trials Direct estimation (Monte Carlo) ADP TD(0) TD(λ) Needs a model No learns one No No Work per step tiny solve a system of equations one update update all traced states Data needed most least medium medium, often less than TD(0) Uses the Markov links No Yes, fully Yes, one step Yes, mixed Famous use Monte Carlo evaluation model based RL TD-Gammon (Tesauro, 1995) TD-Gammon also used TD(λ) Bottom line: TD learning is the usual choice: almost as data efficient as ADP, and as cheap as direct estimation. Real world scenario: TD-Gammon Gerald Tesauro’s TD-Gammon (1992 to 1995) learned backgammon by playing about 1.5 million games against itself, with a neural network trained by TD(λ) to predict the chance of winning. It reached the level of the best human players and changed how experts play some opening moves. Lesson 6 of 7"],[28,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths You learn how good a place is by going there many times. TD learning updates your opinion of a place as soon as you see where it led, not only at the end of the trip. Passive RL: fixed π, learn U^π. Direct estimation averages rewards to go. ADP learns P̂ and solves the equations. TD(0): U(s) ← U(s) + α(R + γU(s′) − U(s)). Robbins Monro conditions for convergence. TD as stochastic approximation of the Bellman operator. Bias and variance of TD and Monte Carlo targets. TD(λ) and n step returns as a family between them. Research: off policy evaluation (estimating a new policy from old logs), TD with neural networks and the deadly triad (function approximation, bootstrapping and off policy data can diverge), and distributional RL that learns the whole return distribution. Lesson 7 of 7"],[28,"recap","Chapter summary","Passive RL: the policy is fixed, the agent learns U^π(s) from trials without knowing P or R. Direct estimation averages rewards to go. Three textbook trials give U(1,1) ≈ 0.093. ADP learns P̂(s′ | s, a) = N(s, a, s′) ÷ N(s, a) and solves the Bellman equations. Most data efficient. TD(0): U(s) ← U(s) + α (R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s)). It converges with a learning rate that satisfies Robbins Monro. TD targets have lower variance than Monte Carlo returns but are biased while U is wrong. Next: chapter 10.3 lets the agent choose its own actions: active RL, exploration and Q-learning. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterEstimate utilities from the textbook trials and run TD(0) on the random walk, then write direct estimation, the TD update and a full TD learner yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[29,"","Active reinforcement learning","Exploration, Q-learning, SARSA, function approximation and deep Q-networks. · active reinforcement learning · exploration · epsilon greedy · UCB · Q-learning · SARSA · on policy · off policy · function approximation · DQN Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 10: Reinforcement learning›Active RL Step 10 · Topic 3 of 4 · College Let the agent choose its own actions. Balance exploration and exploitation, learn action values with Q-learning and SARSA, see why they differ on a cliff, and scale up with function approximation. About 50 min8 lessons1 animation13 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Active learning: explore or exploit 2Q-learning 3SARSA, on policy and off policy 4Function approximation and deep Q-networks 5The maths: the Bellman equation for Q and convergence 6Active learners compared 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[29,"explore","Active learning: explore or exploit","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 21.3.1 (4th ed., 22.3), Sutton and Barto (2018), chapter 2, and Auer, Cesa-Bianchi and Fischer (2002) for UCB1. The uploaded slides and transcripts do not cover this step. What it is In active RL the agent picks its own actions. It must balance exploitation (take the action that looks best now) and exploration (try other actions to learn whether they are better). Why we need it A purely greedy agent locks onto the first policy that looks good and never finds out that another route was better. A purely random agent learns a lot but never uses it. Both lose reward. How it works Explore more at first and less later. ε-greedy takes a random action with probability ε. An exploration function makes rarely tried actions look attractive. UCB adds a bonus that shrinks as an action is tried more often. ε-greedy with probability ε a random action, otherwise argmax over a of Q(s, a) Simple and widely used. Exploration function (AIMA)Exam favourite f(u, n) = R⁺ if n < N_e, else u Optimistic about actions tried fewer than N_e times. UCB1 pick argmax over i of x̄ᵢ + √(2 ln n ÷ nᵢ) Mean so far plus a bonus for uncertainty. Its regret grows only like log n. GLIE try every action infinitely often, and become greedy in the limit A condition under which the learned policy becomes optimal. Python # The exploration function of AIMA section 21.3.1: be optimistic about actions tried fewer than Ne times.R_PLUS, NE = 2.0, 5def f(u, n):returnR_PLUSif n < NEelse u# Two actions in one state: the agent has tried \"safe\" 20 times and \"new\" only twice.estimate = {\"safe\": 0.6, \"new\": 0.1}tries = {\"safe\": 20, \"new\": 2}for a in estimate:print(a, \" estimate\", estimate[a], \" tries\", tries[a], \" value used\", f(estimate[a], tries[a]))print(\"chosen:\", max(estimate, key=lambda a: f(estimate[a], tries[a]))) Output safe estimate 0.6 tries 20 value used 0.6 new estimate 0.1 tries 2 value used 2.0 chosen: new Remember The same trade off appears in the expectimax chapter (6.3) in UCT, which uses UCB1 inside Monte Carlo tree search to pick which move to try next. Lesson 2 of 8"],[29,"ql","Q-learning","Source: Watkins (1989), Watkins and Dayan (1992), and AIMA 3rd ed., section 21.3.2. What it is Q-learning learns Q(s, a), the value of each action in each state, straight from experience. The agent acts, sees the reward and the next state, and moves Q(s, a) towards r + γ max over a′ of Q(s′, a′). Why we need it With Q the agent can pick the best action by looking up a table: no transition model is needed, neither for learning nor for choosing actions. That makes it model free all the way. How it works After each step (s, a, r, s′): Q(s, a) ← Q(s, a) + α (r + γ max over a′ of Q(s′, a′) − Q(s, a)). Act with ε-greedy or an exploration function. With every pair tried infinitely often and a Robbins Monro learning rate, Q converges to Q*. Q-learning updateExam favourite Q(s, a) ← Q(s, a) + α [r + γ max over a′ of Q(s′, a′) − Q(s, a)] Off policy: the target uses the best next action. SARSA update Q(s, a) ← Q(s, a) + α [r + γ Q(s′, a′) − Q(s, a)] On policy: a′ is the action the agent really takes next. Greedy policy π(s) = argmax over a of Q(s, a) No model needed. Worked example: one Q-learning update Q(s, a) = 0.2. The agent gets r = 0 and lands in s′, where the best Q value is 0.8. γ = 0.9, α = 0.5. Target: 0 + 0.9 × 0.8 = 0.72. Error: 0.72 − 0.2 = 0.52. Q(s, a) ← 0.2 + 0.5 × 0.52 = 0.46 Lesson 3 of 8"],[29,"sarsal","SARSA, on policy and off policy","Source: Rummery and Niranjan (1994), and Sutton and Barto (2018), section 6.5 and example 6.6 (cliff walking). What it is SARSA is named after the five things in its update: State, Action, Reward, next State, next Action. It learns the value of the policy it actually follows, exploration included. Q-learning learns the value of the greedy policy, whatever it does while exploring. Why we need it When exploration is costly, the difference matters. On a cliff edge, an exploring agent sometimes slips. SARSA learns to keep a safe distance. Q-learning learns the shortest path along the edge, which is optimal only for an agent that never explores. How it works Both use the same loop. SARSA chooses a′ first and uses Q(s′, a′) in the target. Q-learning uses max over a′ of Q(s′, a′) and may then take a different a′. Q-learning SARSA Learns Q* of the greedy policy (off policy) Q of the ε-greedy policy it follows (on policy) Cliff walking path shortest, along the edge (13 steps) safer, one or two rows up Reward while learning lower: it falls more while exploring higher Can learn from any data, even another agent’s its own current policy only Lesson 4 of 8"],[29,"approx","Function approximation and deep Q-networks","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 21.4, Mnih and others (2015), Human level control through deep reinforcement learning, Nature, and Tesauro (1995). What it is A table with one entry per state is impossible for chess (about 10⁴⁰ positions) or for screen images. Function approximation replaces the table with a formula Q̂_θ(s, a) that has a few parameters θ, such as a weighted sum of features or a neural network. Why we need it The formula generalises: what is learned in one state carries over to similar states never seen before. A few thousand parameters can stand for billions of states. How it works Use the same TD target, and move θ a little in the direction that reduces the error: θ ← θ + α [r + γ max over a′ of Q̂(s′, a′) − Q̂(s, a)] ∇_θ Q̂(s, a). DQN does this with a deep network, plus a replay buffer of past steps and a slowly updated target network for stability. Linear approximation Û_θ(s) = θ₀ + θ₁ f₁(s) + ... + θₙ fₙ(s) f are features, such as the x and y of a square. Gradient TD updateExam favourite θᵢ ← θᵢ + α [target − Q̂(s, a)] ∂Q̂ ÷ ∂θᵢ For a linear Q̂ the derivative is just the feature value. Worked example: a linear utility for the 4 × 3 world Û(x, y) = θ₀ + θ₁ x + θ₂ y with θ = (0.5, 0.2, 0.1). At (1,1): Û = 0.5 + 0.2 + 0.1 = 0.8. The observed reward to go is 0.705 (the true value), so the error is 0.705 − 0.8 = −0.095. With α = 0.1: θ₀ ← 0.5 + 0.1 × (−0.095) × 1 = 0.4905. θ₁ and θ₂ also fall by 0.0095, since x = y = 1 Common mistake With function approximation, Q-learning can diverge: off policy data, bootstrapping and approximation together (the deadly triad). DQN’s replay buffer and target network were added to keep training stable. Lesson 5 of 8"],[29,"maths","The maths: the Bellman equation for Q and convergence","Bellman optimality for QExam favourite Q*(s, a) = Σ P(s′ | s, a) [R(s, a, s′) + γ max over a′ of Q*(s′, a′)] Q-learning is a sampled version of this. ε-greedy action chances best action: 1 − ε + ε ÷ |A|, each other action: ε ÷ |A| The random choice can also pick the best action. Convergence of Q-learning every (s, a) tried infinitely often, Σ αₜ = ∞, Σ αₜ² < ∞ Watkins and Dayan, 1992, for tables. UCB1 regret expected regret after n pulls is O(log n) Lai and Robbins (1985) showed log n is the best possible order. Worked example: the optimal Q values of the corridor 0 1 2 3 (goal 3 gives +1, γ = 0.9) Q*(2, R) = 1: one step to the goal. Q*(1, R) = 0 + 0.9 × 1 = 0.9. Q*(0, R) = 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81. Q*(1, L) = 0 + 0.9 × max Q*(0, ·) = 0.9 × 0.81. Q*(1, L) = 0.729 Lesson 6 of 8"],[29,"compare","Active learners compared","Learning to act Greedy ADP ADP + exploration function Q-learning SARSA DQN Model learned learned none none none Exploration none, gets stuck optimistic values ε-greedy or similar ε-greedy or similar ε-greedy Learns a policy that may be poor near optimal policy Q* (off policy) Q of its own policy (on policy) Q* with a neural network Data use best best one update per step one update per step replays old steps many times Weak when always big state spaces (must solve the model) huge state spaces without approximation same as Q-learning needs millions of frames, can be unstable Bottom line: model based learning uses data best, model free learning is simpler and scales with function approximation. Modern systems such as AlphaZero and MuZero mix both. Real world scenario: Deep reinforcement learning milestones DQN (2015) learned 49 Atari games from screen pixels and the score, and played many of them as well as a professional human tester. AlphaGo (Silver and others, 2016) beat Lee Sedol at Go, combining policy and value networks with Monte Carlo tree search. RL from human feedback (Christiano and others, 2017, and later chat models) trains language models with a reward learned from people’s preferences. Lesson 7 of 8"],[29,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths To learn which choices are good, sometimes try something new. Keep a score for each choice in each situation and update it after every try. Exploration (ε-greedy, exploration functions, UCB). Q-learning: Q ← Q + α(r + γ max Q′ − Q), off policy. SARSA: on policy. Function approximation with gradient TD. GLIE conditions, Watkins and Dayan convergence for tabular Q-learning, regret bounds for bandits, maximisation bias in Q-learning and double Q-learning (van Hasselt, 2010), the deadly triad. Research: sample efficient exploration (count based bonuses, curiosity), offline RL from fixed datasets, safe RL with constraints, and RL for reasoning in large language models. Lesson 8 of 8"],[29,"recap","Chapter summary","Active RL: the agent chooses actions and must balance exploration and exploitation. Greedy agents get stuck. ε-greedy, exploration functions f(u, n) and UCB1 make the agent try uncertain actions. Q-learning: Q(s, a) ← Q(s, a) + α [r + γ max Q(s′, a′) − Q(s, a)]. Off policy, model free, converges to Q*. SARSA uses the next action actually taken. On the cliff it learns a safer path than Q-learning. Function approximation and deep networks (DQN) scale Q-learning to huge state spaces, with care for stability. Next: chapter 10.4 skips values and searches directly for a good policy: policy gradient methods. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun Q-learning in a corridor and try the exploration function, then write the Q update, ε-greedy action choice and a full Q-learner yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[30,"","Policy search and policy gradients","Policy gradients, REINFORCE, baselines, actor critic and learning from experts. · policy search · policy gradient · REINFORCE · baseline · softmax policy · actor critic · advantage · PPO · inverse reinforcement learning · imitation learning Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 10: Reinforcement learning›Policy search Step 10 · Topic 4 of 4 · College Skip learning values and tune the policy itself. Use softmax policies, the policy gradient theorem and REINFORCE with a baseline, then speed up with actor critic methods, and learn from experts with imitation and inverse RL. About 45 min8 lessons1 animation14 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Policy search: tune the policy directly 2The policy gradient and REINFORCE 3Actor critic and other refinements 4Learning from an expert: imitation and inverse RL 5The maths: deriving the policy gradient 6Value based and policy based methods compared 7From intuition to research 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[30,"search","Policy search: tune the policy directly","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 21.5 (4th ed., 22.5), Williams (1992), and Sutton and Barto (2018), chapter 13. The uploaded slides and transcripts do not cover this step. What it is Policy search writes the policy as a formula π_θ with parameters θ and changes θ to raise the policy value ρ(θ), the expected total reward of following π_θ. It never needs to learn exact values for every state. Why we need it Sometimes the best policy is simple even when the values are complicated: \"steer towards the centre of the road\" is easier than predicting the exact future reward of every position. Policy search also works with continuous actions, such as how hard to brake, where taking a max over actions is hard. How it works Use a stochastic policy, such as a softmax over action preferences, so that ρ(θ) changes smoothly with θ. Estimate the gradient of ρ from trials and climb it: θ ← θ + α ∇ρ(θ). Policy valueExam favourite ρ(θ) = E[Σ γᵗ Rₜ | actions from π_θ] What policy search maximises. Softmax policy π_θ(a | s) = e^(θ · f(s, a)) ÷ Σ over b of e^(θ · f(s, b)) Every action keeps some chance, so the policy explores. Finite difference gradient ∂ρ ÷ ∂θᵢ ≈ (ρ(θ + h eᵢ) − ρ(θ)) ÷ h Works for any policy, but needs many trials per parameter and is noisy. Python # A softmax policy over two actions and the gradient of log pi, which REINFORCE needs.import maththeta = [0.0, 1.0] # one preference per actiondef policy(theta): e = [math.exp(t) for t in theta] total = sum(e)return [x / total for x in e]pi = policy(theta)print(\"pi =\", [round(p, 3) for p in pi])for a in [0, 1]: grad = [(1if k == a else0) - pi[k] for k inrange(2)] # d log pi(a) / d theta_kprint(\"action\", a, \"gradient of log pi:\", [round(g, 3) for g in grad]) Output pi = [0.269, 0.731] action 0 gradient of log pi: [0.731, -0.731] action 1 gradient of log pi: [-0.269, 0.269] Common mistake With a greedy policy (always the best action under some score) ρ(θ) jumps when the best action changes, so its gradient is zero almost everywhere. That is why policy gradient methods use stochastic policies. Lesson 2 of 8"],[30,"reinforcel","The policy gradient and REINFORCE","Source: Williams (1992), Simple statistical gradient following algorithms for connectionist reinforcement learning, and Sutton, McAllester, Singh and Mansour (2000), the policy gradient theorem. What it is The policy gradient theorem gives ∇ρ as an average we can sample: E[∇ log π_θ(a | s) × (return after taking a)]. REINFORCE uses one trial at a time as a sample of this average. Why we need it It needs no model and no derivative of the world, only the derivative of our own policy, which we wrote down. Actions followed by high returns become more likely, actions followed by low returns less likely. How it works Run a trial. For each step t, with return Gₜ from then on, update θ ← θ + α (Gₜ − b) ∇ log π_θ(aₜ | sₜ). The baseline b, for example the average return so far, does not change the expected update but makes it much less noisy. Policy gradient theoremExam favourite ∇ρ(θ) = E[∇_θ log π_θ(a | s) Q^π(s, a)] Only the gradient of the policy appears. REINFORCE update θ ← θ + α (Gₜ − b) ∇_θ log π_θ(aₜ | sₜ) b is a baseline that does not depend on the action. Softmax gradient ∂ log π(a) ÷ ∂θ_k = 1[k = a] − π(k) For a softmax over preferences θ. Why a baseline is safe E[∇ log π(a)] = Σ π(a) ∇ log π(a) = ∇ Σ π(a) = ∇ 1 = 0 So subtracting b changes no expected value. Worked example: one REINFORCE step on two actions θ = (0, 0), so π = (0.5, 0.5). The agent picks action 1 and gets r = 1. α = 0.4. Gradient of log π(1): (0 − 0.5, 1 − 0.5) = (−0.5, 0.5). No baseline: θ ← (0, 0) + 0.4 × 1 × (−0.5, 0.5) = (−0.2, 0.2). With baseline b = 0.5 the step is half as big: θ = (−0.1, 0.1) Lesson 3 of 8"],[30,"ac","Actor critic and other refinements","Source: Barto, Sutton and Anderson (1983), Konda and Tsitsiklis (2000), Ng and Jordan (2000) for PEGASUS, and Schulman and others (2017) for PPO. What it is An actor critic method keeps two learners: the actor is the policy π_θ, the critic learns a value function V_w. The critic’s TD error tells the actor whether an action turned out better or worse than expected. Why we need it REINFORCE waits for the end of a trial and its returns are noisy. The critic gives a lower variance signal after every step, so learning is faster and works for tasks that never end. How it works After each step: δ = r + γ V_w(s′) − V_w(s). Critic: w ← w + β δ ∇V_w(s). Actor: θ ← θ + α δ ∇ log π_θ(a | s). Correlated sampling (PEGASUS) reuses the same random numbers when comparing two policies, which removes much of the noise from finite difference estimates. Actor criticExam favourite δ = r + γ V(s′) − V(s), θ ← θ + α δ ∇ log π_θ(a | s) δ estimates the advantage of the action taken. Advantage A(s, a) = Q(s, a) − V(s) How much better a is than the policy’s average action. PPO clipped objective E[min(r_t(θ) Â, clip(r_t(θ), 1 − ε, 1 + ε) Â)], with r_t = π_θ ÷ π_old Keeps each policy update small and safe. Worked example: a TD error as the actor’s signal The critic says V(s) = 2.5. The agent acts, gets r = 1 and lands in s′ with V(s′) = 2. γ = 0.9. δ = 1 + 0.9 × 2 − 2.5 = 1 + 1.8 − 2.5. δ = 0.3 is positive: the action did better than expected. the actor raises the chance of that action, by α × 0.3 × ∇ log π Lesson 4 of 8"],[30,"apprentice","Learning from an expert: imitation and inverse RL","Source: AIMA 3rd ed., section 21.6, Ng and Russell (2000) for inverse reinforcement learning, and Abbeel and Ng (2004) for apprenticeship learning. What it is Instead of a reward, the agent may watch an expert. Imitation learning (behaviour cloning) copies the expert’s actions with supervised learning. Inverse reinforcement learning works out a reward function that explains the expert’s behaviour, then plans with it. Why we need it Writing a good reward by hand is hard: a reward for \"drive well\" is much harder to write than a few hours of a good driver’s data. A learned reward also carries over to new situations the expert never met. How it works Behaviour cloning fits π(a | s) to the expert’s pairs. Inverse RL searches for a reward under which the expert’s policy is optimal (or nearly), often matching expected feature counts, and then solves the MDP for that reward. Real world scenario: Helicopter aerobatics Abbeel, Coates, Quigley and Ng (2007) used apprenticeship learning and policy search to fly a model helicopter through stunts such as flips, rolls and tic tocs, learned from a few demonstrations by an expert pilot. Policy search with PEGASUS had earlier learned to hover the same helicopter (Ng and others, 2004). Lesson 5 of 8"],[30,"maths","The maths: deriving the policy gradient","Log derivative trickExam favourite ∇ π(a) = π(a) ∇ log π(a) Turns a gradient of a sum into an expectation. One step (bandit) case ∇ρ = ∇ Σ π(a) R(a) = Σ π(a) ∇ log π(a) R(a) = E[∇ log π(a) R(a)] Sample a, multiply the gradient of log π(a) by its reward. Exact bandit gradient for a softmax ∂ρ ÷ ∂θ_k = π(k) (μ_k − ρ) An action with above average reward gets pushed up. Variance with a baseline Var[(G − b) ∇ log π] is smallest near b = E[G] The usual choice is an estimate of V(s). Worked example: the exact gradient for two arms with means 0.3 and 0.7, at π = (0.5, 0.5) ρ = 0.5 × 0.3 + 0.5 × 0.7 = 0.5. ∂ρ ÷ ∂θ₁ = π(1) (μ₁ − ρ) = 0.5 × (0.7 − 0.5). That is 0.5 × 0.2 = 0.1. For arm 0: 0.5 × (0.3 − 0.5) = −0.1. ∇ρ = (−0.1, 0.1): push towards the better arm Lesson 6 of 8"],[30,"compare","Value based and policy based methods compared","Ways to learn a policy Q-learning (value based) REINFORCE Actor critic Finite differences or evolution Imitation and inverse RL Learns Q(s, a), acts greedily π_θ directly π_θ and V_w π_θ by trying perturbed θ π from an expert, or a reward Actions discrete (needs a max) discrete or continuous discrete or continuous any any Variance low high medium high, unless correlated sampling low (supervised) Data efficiency good (off policy, replay) poor (on policy) better poor good with an expert Known for DQN simple, unbiased A3C, PPO, SAC PEGASUS, evolution strategies (Salimans and others, 2017) self driving data, helicopters Bottom line: use value based methods for discrete actions and plenty of data, policy gradients and actor critic methods for continuous actions or stochastic policies, and imitation when an expert is available. Lesson 7 of 8"],[30,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Keep a dial for each choice. After you try a choice, turn its dial up if things went better than usual and down if worse. π_θ as a softmax, ρ(θ) as the expected return, REINFORCE θ += α (G − b) ∇ log π(a), the baseline, actor critic with the TD error. The policy gradient theorem, compatible function approximation, natural policy gradients and trust regions (TRPO, PPO), entropy regularisation, and why on policy data limits sample efficiency. Research: RL from human feedback for language models (reward models plus PPO or direct preference optimisation), offline policy optimisation, and learning policies for robots in simulation that transfer to the real world. Lesson 8 of 8"],[30,"recap","Chapter summary","Policy search tunes θ of a policy π_θ to raise ρ(θ), the expected return. It handles continuous actions and simple policies well. Use stochastic (softmax) policies so ρ(θ) is smooth. ∂ log π(a) ÷ ∂θ_k = 1[k = a] − π(k). REINFORCE: θ ← θ + α (G − b) ∇ log π(a | s). A baseline cuts noise without changing the expected update. Actor critic uses the critic’s TD error δ in place of G − b, for faster, lower variance learning. Imitation learning and inverse RL learn from an expert when a reward is hard to write. Next: step 11 brings every Core AI topic together in hard challenge problems and a revision guide. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterCompute softmax gradients and run REINFORCE on a two armed bandit, then write the softmax policy, its log gradient and a full REINFORCE learner yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[31,"","Challenge problems across Core AI","Hard exam style problems across every Core AI step, with proofs and a method guide. · challenge problems · hard questions · proofs · A* optimality · alpha beta · resolution · Sussman anomaly · Bayes rule · value iteration · Q-learning Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 11: Challenge and revision›Challenge problems Step 11 · Topic 1 of 2 · All levels Twenty one hard problems in the style of top university exams, one set for each step of Core AI, then the proofs examiners ask for and a guide for choosing a method. About 75 min12 lessons7 animations13 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How to attack a hard Core AI problem 2Challenge set 1: search and heuristics 3Challenge set 2: constraint satisfaction and local search 4Challenge set 3: adversarial search 5Challenge set 4: logic and inference 6Challenge set 5: planning 7Challenge set 6: probability and Bayesian networks 8Challenge set 7: MDPs and reinforcement learning 9Proofs examiners ask for 10Choosing the right method 11From intuition to research 12Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 12"],[31,"how","How to attack a hard Core AI problem","Source: Original challenge problems written for this course in the style of exams at top universities (MIT 6.034, Berkeley CS188, Stanford CS221, CMU 15-381, IIT and IISc AI courses, and the GATE Data Science and AI paper). None is copied from a real past paper. They draw on AIMA 3rd ed. chapters 3 to 21, the uploaded slides on search, CSPs and games, and every earlier chapter of this subject. Every number was checked by running the code on this page. What it is A challenge problem mixes two or more ideas, hides a trap, or asks you to prove why a method works. This chapter has one challenge set for each step of Core AI, then proofs, then a guide for choosing a method. Why we need it Easy questions test whether you remember a formula. Hard questions test whether you know when the formula holds. Top exams almost always ask about the edge: an inconsistent heuristic, a collider that becomes open, a discount that breaks the bound. How it works Use the same five moves every time: name the model (search, CSP, game, logic, plan, Bayes net or MDP), write the formula, check its conditions, work the numbers one step at a time, and say what the answer means. Move Ask yourself Example 1. Name the model What are the states, actions, variables or rules? A route finder is a search problem with a step cost. 2. Write the formula Which equation decides the answer? f(n) = g(n) + h(n) for A*. 3. Check the conditions Does the theorem’s condition hold here? Is h consistent, or only admissible? 4. Work it step by step Can a reader follow each line? Write the frontier after every expansion. 5. Say what it means Is the answer sensible? A probability must lie between 0 and 1. Remember Each challenge below names the chapter that teaches the idea, so you can go back if a step is not clear. Lesson 2 of 12"],[31,"search","Challenge set 1: search and heuristics",""],[31,"search","Challenge 1. An admissible heuristic that still gives a wrong answer","Edges: S to A costs 4, S to B costs 1, B to A costs 1, A to G costs 4. The heuristic is h(S) = 0, h(A) = 0, h(B) = 5, h(G) = 0. Is h admissible? Is it consistent? What does A* graph search return if it never reopens a closed node? (Chapters 3.1 and 3.2.) Model answer True costs to G: from A it is 4, from B it is 1 + 4 = 5, from S it is 1 + 1 + 4 = 6. Every h is at most the true cost, so h is admissible. Consistency needs h(B) ≤ c(B, A) + h(A) = 1 + 0 = 1. But h(B) = 5, so h is not consistent. Start: frontier S (f = 0). Expand S: A gets g = 4, f = 4. B gets g = 1, f = 1 + 5 = 6. A has the smaller f, so A is expanded first with g = 4 and closed. G gets g = 8, f = 8. Next B (f = 6). It finds A again with g = 2, but A is closed, so this better path is thrown away. G is popped with cost 8, but the best path S, B, A, G costs 6 In the exam The fix: use a consistent heuristic, or let graph search reopen a closed node when a cheaper path to it turns up. Tree search with an admissible heuristic is always optimal, because it never closes anything."],[31,"search","Challenge 2. How much extra work does iterative deepening do?","Branching factor b = 10, solution depth d = 5. Count the nodes generated by BFS and by IDS. BFS generates b + b² + … + b⁵ = 10 + 100 + 1,000 + 10,000 + 100,000 = 111,110. IDS generates level i once for every limit from i to d, that is (d + 1 − i) times. IDS: 5 × 10 + 4 × 100 + 3 × 1,000 + 2 × 10,000 + 1 × 100,000 = 123,450. IDS does only about 11 percent more work, and needs only O(bd) memory"],[31,"search","Challenge 3. Effective branching factor","A* generates N = 52 nodes and finds a solution at depth d = 5. Find the effective branching factor b*. b* is the branching factor of a uniform tree of depth d with N + 1 nodes: N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + … + (b*)⁵. So 53 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + (b*)³ + (b*)⁴ + (b*)⁵. Try b* = 2: the sum is 63, too big. Try 1.9: it is about 51.5, a little small. Halving the interval a few times gives b* ≈ 1.92. b* ≈ 1.92, the value given in AIMA section 3.6.1 Lesson 3 of 12"],[31,"csp","Challenge set 2: constraint satisfaction and local search",""],[31,"csp","Challenge 4. AC-3 alone solves this CSP","Variables X, Y and Z each have the domain 1, 2, 3, with constraints X < Y and Y < Z. Run AC-3. (Chapter 5.1.) Model answer Arc X to Y: X = 3 has no larger Y, so remove it. X becomes 1, 2. Arc Y to X: Y = 1 has no smaller X, so remove it. Y becomes 2, 3. Arc Y to Z: Y = 3 has no larger Z, so remove it. Y becomes 2. Arc Z to Y: Z must be above 2, so Z becomes 3. Y changed, so the arc X to Y goes back on the queue. X = 2 has no larger Y now, so X becomes 1. X = 1, Y = 2, Z = 3, found with no search at all"],[31,"csp","Challenge 5. Why a tree shaped CSP is easy","A CSP has n = 10 variables, each with d = 3 values, and its constraint graph is a tree. Bound the work. Pick a root and order the variables so every parent comes before its children. Go backwards, making each arc from parent to child consistent. Each of the n − 1 arcs needs at most d² checks. Then assign values from the root down. Arc consistency means a good value is always left, so there is no backtracking. (n − 1) d² = 9 × 9 = 81 checks, against dⁿ = 59,049 full assignments"],[31,"csp","Challenge 6. Simulated annealing accepts a worse move","The current state has value 10. A random neighbour has value 8, so ΔE = −2 when we maximise. The temperature is T = 1. What is the chance of moving? A worse move is taken with probability e^(ΔE ÷ T). e^(−2 ÷ 1) = e^(−2). ≈ 0.135. At T = 10 it would be e^(−0.2) ≈ 0.819 Lesson 4 of 12"],[31,"games","Challenge set 3: adversarial search",""],[31,"games","Challenge 7. Which leaves does alpha beta skip?","A MAX root has three MIN children with leaves 3 12 8, 2 4 6 and 14 5 2, read left to right. Find the minimax value and the leaves alpha beta never looks at. (Chapters 6.1 and 6.2.) Model answer First MIN node: min(3, 12, 8) = 3. Now α = 3 at the root. Second MIN node: its first leaf is 2. Its value can only be 2 or less, and MAX already has 3. So the leaves 4 and 6 are pruned. Third MIN node: 14, then 5, then 2. None of 14 or 5 is at most 3, so all three are read. Its value is 2. root = max(3, ≤ 2, 2) = 3, with 7 of the 9 leaves read"],[31,"games","Challenge 8. The best case for alpha beta","With perfect move ordering, how many leaves does alpha beta read for b = 3 and depth d = 4? Knuth and Moore (1975): the best case reads b^⌈d ÷ 2⌉ + b^⌊d ÷ 2⌋ − 1 leaves. 3² + 3² − 1. 17 leaves out of 3⁴ = 81. Search can go about twice as deep in the same time"],[31,"games","Challenge 9. Expectimax is not safe under a change of scale","A chance node picks between leaves 0 and 4 with equal chance. Another branch gives 3 for sure. Replace every leaf x by x². Can MAX change its move? Before: the chance node is 0.5 × 0 + 0.5 × 4 = 2 and the sure branch is 3, so MAX picks the sure 3. After: the chance node is 0.5 × 0 + 0.5 × 16 = 8 and the sure branch is 9. MAX still picks the sure branch. Now try leaves 0 and 6 against a sure 4. Before squaring: 3 against 4, so MAX picks the sure branch. After squaring: 18 against 16, so MAX picks the gamble. minimax only needs the order of leaf values, expectimax needs the values themselves Lesson 5 of 12"],[31,"logic","Challenge set 4: logic and inference",""],[31,"logic","Challenge 10. A resolution proof","KB: P ⇒ Q, Q ⇒ R, P. Prove R by resolution. (Chapter 7.2.) Model answer Turn the KB into clauses: ¬P ∨ Q, ¬Q ∨ R, P. Add the negated goal ¬R. Resolve ¬R with ¬Q ∨ R to get ¬Q. Resolve ¬Q with ¬P ∨ Q to get ¬P. Resolve ¬P with P to get the empty clause. KB ∧ ¬R is unsatisfiable, so KB ⊨ R"],[31,"logic","Challenge 11. Count the models","How many models over P, Q and R make (P ∨ Q) ∧ (¬P ∨ R) true? If P is true, the first clause holds and the second needs R true. Q is free: 2 models. If P is false, the second clause holds and the first needs Q true. R is free: 2 models. 2 + 2 = 4 models out of 8"],[31,"logic","Challenge 12. Unification and the occurs check","Unify Knows(John, x) with Knows(y, Mother(y)). Then try x with F(x). First arguments: John and y, so bind y to John. Second arguments: x and Mother(y), which is now Mother(John). Bind x to Mother(John). The most general unifier is y/John, x/Mother(John). x with F(x) fails: x would have to contain itself, which the occurs check forbids In the exam Forward chaining is complete for definite clauses only. Resolution is refutation complete for any clauses. A favourite exam trap gives a KB with a disjunction such as A ∨ B and asks for forward chaining. Lesson 6 of 12"],[31,"planning","Challenge set 5: planning",""],[31,"planning","Challenge 13. The Sussman anomaly","C is on A, and A and B are on the table. The goal is On(A, B) ∧ On(B, C). Why does solving one sub-goal at a time fail? (Chapters 8.2 and 8.3.) Model answer Try On(A, B) first: move C to the table, put A on B. Now B is under A, and to get On(B, C) we must take A off again. Try On(B, C) first: put B on C. Now A is buried under C and B, and to get On(A, B) we must undo On(B, C). Either order undoes the sub-goal it reached first. The sub-goals interact. the shortest plan interleaves them: C to table, B on C, A on B"],[31,"planning","Challenge 14. Regress a goal through an action","Goal: At(B) ∧ Have(Milk). Action Go(A, B): precondition At(A), add At(B), delete At(A). Regress the goal. The action is relevant because it adds At(B), part of the goal. It deletes nothing in the goal. Regression removes what the action adds and adds its preconditions: (goal minus add list) plus preconditions. the new goal is At(A) ∧ Have(Milk)"],[31,"planning","Challenge 15. Resolve a threat","A partial order plan has a causal link from Buy to Cook that protects Have(Milk). Another step Drink deletes Have(Milk). Fix it. Drink is a threat: if it runs between Buy and Cook, Cook loses its precondition. Demotion puts Drink before Buy. Promotion puts Drink after Cook. pick whichever ordering keeps the plan consistent, and backtrack if neither does Lesson 7 of 12"],[31,"bayes","Challenge set 6: probability and Bayesian networks",""],[31,"bayes","Challenge 16. A positive test for a rare disease","A disease affects 1 percent of people. The test is positive for 90 percent of the ill and for 10 percent of the healthy. P(ill | positive)? P(pos) = 0.9 × 0.01 + 0.1 × 0.99 = 0.009 + 0.099 = 0.108. Bayes rule: P(ill | pos) = 0.009 ÷ 0.108. ≈ 0.083. Most positives are healthy people, because healthy people are so many more"],[31,"bayes","Challenge 17. Parameters saved by a Bayesian network","The burglary network has Boolean nodes B, E, A (parents B and E), J and M (parent A each). Count the numbers needed. B and E need 1 each. A needs 4, one for each pair of parent values. J and M need 2 each. 1 + 1 + 4 + 2 + 2 = 10. 10 numbers, against 2⁵ − 1 = 31 for the full joint table"],[31,"bayes","Challenge 18. When does a collider open?","In the same network, are B and E independent? Are they independent given J? With no evidence, the only path B, A, E has a collider at A with nothing observed below it. It is blocked, so B and E are independent. J is a child of A. Observing a descendant of a collider opens it. B and E become dependent given J: explaining away In the exam The answer P(B | j, m) ≈ 0.284 from chapter 9.3 is worth knowing by heart. Exams often give the burglary tables and ask for it, or for P(J | b) by summing out A. Lesson 8 of 12"],[31,"rl","Challenge set 7: MDPs and reinforcement learning",""],[31,"rl","Challenge 19. Watch value iteration shrink its error","State s1 always pays 2 and stays. In s0, \"stay\" pays 1 and stays, \"go\" pays 0 and moves to s1. γ = 0.9. (Chapter 10.1.) Model answer Exact values: V(s1) = 2 ÷ (1 − 0.9) = 20. Staying in s0 forever is worth 1 ÷ 0.1 = 10. Going is worth 0 + 0.9 × 20 = 18. So V(s0) = 18 and the best action in s0 is \"go\". Starting from 0, the biggest change in each sweep is 2, 1.8, 1.62, and so on: it shrinks by γ every time. that is the contraction property: ‖BU − BU′‖ ≤ γ ‖U − U′‖"],[31,"rl","Challenge 20. How many sweeps are enough?","With R_max = 1 and γ = 0.9, how many sweeps make the error at most ε = 0.1, using the bound N = ⌈log(2 R_max ÷ (ε (1 − γ))) ÷ log(1 ÷ γ)⌉ from AIMA? 2 R_max ÷ (ε (1 − γ)) = 2 ÷ (0.1 × 0.1) = 200. log 200 ÷ log(1 ÷ 0.9) ≈ 5.298 ÷ 0.1054 ≈ 50.3. N = 51 sweeps"],[31,"rl","Challenge 21. One Q-learning step","Q(s, a) = 2, α = 0.5, γ = 0.9. The agent gets r = 1 and the best Q in s′ is 3. Update Q(s, a). Target = r + γ max Q(s′, a′) = 1 + 0.9 × 3 = 3.7. Error = 3.7 − 2 = 1.7. Q(s, a) ← 2 + 0.5 × 1.7 = 2.85 Lesson 9 of 12"],[31,"rl","Proofs examiners ask for","At the top universities, about a third of the marks are for short proofs. Each model answer below names the assumption it uses, which is where marks are lost."],[31,"rl","Proof 1. A* graph search with a consistent heuristic is optimal","Model answer If n′ is a successor of n, then g(n′) = g(n) + c(n, n′), so f(n′) = g(n) + c(n, n′) + h(n′) ≥ g(n) + h(n) = f(n) by consistency. So f never goes down along a path. Suppose A* expands a node n with a path that is not the cheapest. Some node m on the cheapest path to n is on the frontier, with f(m) ≤ f(n) on that path. Since f does not decrease, m would be expanded before n. Repeating the argument, the cheapest path reaches n first. So every node is first expanded with its cheapest g. The goal is too, and h(goal) = 0 gives f = the optimal cost."],[31,"rl","Proof 2. Alpha beta returns the minimax value","Model answer At a MIN node with value v ≤ α, MAX above already has a choice worth α, so MAX will never choose this node. Its exact value cannot change the root. At a MAX node with v ≥ β, MIN above has a choice worth β and will avoid it. The same reasoning applies. Only these branches are cut, so every node on the principal line keeps its exact minimax value."],[31,"rl","Proof 3. The Bellman update is a contraction","Model answer Write (BU)(s) = R(s) + γ max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U(s′). For any two functions f and g, |max f − max g| ≤ max |f − g|. So |(BU)(s) − (BU′)(s)| ≤ γ max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) |U(s′) − U′(s′)| ≤ γ ‖U − U′‖. With γ < 1 the error shrinks each sweep, so there is one fixed point and value iteration reaches it from any start (Banach fixed point theorem)."],[31,"rl","Proof 4. Resolution is sound","Model answer Take clauses A ∨ ℓ and B ∨ ¬ℓ, and a model that makes both true. If ℓ is true in it, then ¬ℓ is false, so B must be true. If ℓ is false, A must be true. Either way A ∨ B is true. So every model of the parents is a model of the resolvent."],[31,"rl","Proof 5. Tree shaped CSPs need no backtracking","Model answer Order the variables so each one comes after its parent, then make every parent to child arc consistent, last to first. Assign the root any value left in its domain. For each child, arc consistency guarantees a value that agrees with the parent’s value. Each variable has only one parent, so no other constraint can rule that value out. So the forward pass never fails, in O(n d²) time. Lesson 10 of 12"],[31,"choose","Choosing the right method","Which Core AI method fits the problem Problem looks like Use Why Watch out for Find a path, all step costs equal BFS or IDS Optimal for equal costs, IDS uses little memory BFS memory grows as bᵈ Find the cheapest path, a good estimate known A* (IDA* or SMA* if memory is short) Optimal with a consistent h, focused search Inconsistent h in graph search Actions may fail or have several outcomes AND-OR search, AO* Returns a conditional plan Cycles need loops in the plan Only the final state matters Hill climbing, annealing, genetic algorithms Little memory, works on huge spaces Local maxima and plateaus Assign values under constraints Backtracking with MRV, forward checking, AC-3, or min-conflicts Prunes whole sets of assignments Variable order changes the time a lot An opponent moves against you Minimax with alpha beta, MCTS for huge trees Assumes the opponent plays well Evaluation function quality Chance moves in a game Expectimax, expectiminimax Uses averages over chance Needs real values, not only their order Facts and rules, need a proof Forward or backward chaining, resolution Sound and complete for their logic First order logic is only semi decidable Reach a goal by a sequence of actions STRIPS with forward or backward search, partial order planning Factored states, reuse of heuristics Sub-goal interaction Uncertain facts Bayesian networks, exact or sampling inference Compact, answers any query Exact inference is NP hard in general Cause and effect, an intervention Causal graphs and do-calculus Separates seeing from doing Hidden confounders Decide over time, model known Value or policy iteration Exact optimal policy Large state spaces Decide over time, model unknown TD learning, Q-learning, SARSA, policy gradients Learn from experience Exploration and slow learning Bottom line: name the model first. The model decides the family of methods, and the conditions of each theorem decide which member to trust. Lesson 11 of 12"],[31,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Every Core AI method answers one question: what should I do next? Search tries paths, logic proves facts, Bayes nets weigh chances, and RL learns from rewards. Run each method by hand on a small example: A* with a table of f values, AC-3 on three variables, alpha beta with α and β written at every node, enumeration on the burglary net, and a value iteration sweep. Know when each theorem holds: consistency for A* graph search, definite clauses for chaining, d-separation for independence, γ < 1 for contraction, and the conditions for Q-learning to converge (every pair visited forever, step sizes with Σ α = ∞ and Σ α² < ∞). Research problems join the steps: learned heuristics for search (AlphaZero joins MCTS and deep networks), neuro symbolic reasoning, planning with learned models (MuZero), causal reinforcement learning, and large language models used as planners and checked by classical planners or verifiers. Lesson 12 of 12"],[31,"recap","Chapter summary","Use five moves on any problem: name the model, write the formula, check its conditions, work step by step, and say what the answer means. Search: an admissible but inconsistent h can fool A* graph search. IDS costs only about 11 percent more than BFS for b = 10. CSPs and games: AC-3 can solve a CSP alone, tree CSPs take O(n d²), alpha beta reads b^⌈d ÷ 2⌉ + b^⌊d ÷ 2⌋ − 1 leaves at best. Logic and planning: refute KB ∧ ¬α by resolution, use the occurs check, interleave interacting sub-goals, and fix threats by promotion or demotion. Uncertainty and RL: mind the base rate, open colliders by observing a descendant, and use the γ contraction to bound value iteration. Next: the last chapter is a revision guide by exam tier, with a formula sheet and a mock quiz. ipynb Practice notebook for this chapterRun the inconsistent heuristic trap, alpha beta with a leaf counter, AC-3 and value iteration, then write the IDS node count, a consistency checker and alpha beta yourself.Open it in Jupyter, VS Code or Google Colab. It needs only plain Python 3, no extra packages. Each task has a starter cell, a check cell that tells you which tests pass, and a full solution further down in case you get stuck. Download notebook"],[32,"","Exam revision: from school to research universities","Core AI exam questions from school level to research universities, with worked answers, a formula sheet and a mock quiz. · exam · revision · formula sheet · mock quiz · numericals · proofs · IIT · NPTEL · GATE · MIT · Berkeley Brainy BytesArtificial Intelligence learning path Artificial Intelligence›Core AI›Step 11: Challenge and revision›Exam revision Step 11 · Topic 2 of 2 · All levels Practise the question shapes of every level with worked answers: agents, search orders and simple chances, algorithms run by hand, quick checks for GATE and NPTEL papers, and proofs with their conditions. About 60 min6 lessons3 animations3 formulas12 worked problems12 quiz questionsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How Core AI is examined 2Tier 1: school and first year 3Tier 2: university semester and IIT course exams 4Tier 3: GATE and NPTEL style papers 5Tier 4: proofs, derivations and critique 6Formula sheet and last minute checks Lesson 1 of 6"],[32,"map","How Core AI is examined","Source: Original practice questions written in the style of each exam tier. None is copied from a real past paper. The topics follow AIMA 3rd ed. chapters 3 to 21, the uploaded slides on search, CSPs and games, and the AI part of the GATE Data Science and AI syllabus (search, logic, and reasoning under uncertainty with conditional independence, variable elimination and sampling). Every number was checked with Python. This chapter is for the week before an exam. It does not teach new material. It shows the shapes questions take at each level, with worked answers, and points back to the chapter where each idea is taught. Tier Typical format What earns the marks Where to practise School and first year Short answers, small trees, simple chances A clear definition with an example, careful counting Lesson 2 University semester and IIT course exams Run an algorithm by hand on a small graph, net or MDP The frontier or table after every step, the answer stated with its meaning Lesson 3 GATE and NPTEL style papers Multiple choice, multiple select and numerical answer type Speed, quick checks that rule out options, exact rounding Lesson 4 MIT, Berkeley, Stanford, CMU and IISc style Proofs, derivations and critique of a method Conditions named, a complete argument, when it breaks Lesson 5"],[32,"map","The fifteen ideas examined most often","PEAS and the types of environment (chapter 1.1). The five parts of a search problem and the four ways to judge a search (chapter 1.2). BFS, UCS, DFS and IDS: completeness, optimality, time and memory (chapters 2.1 and 2.2). A*: admissible and consistent heuristics, dominance, relaxed problems (chapters 3.1 and 3.2). Hill climbing, simulated annealing and genetic algorithms (chapters 4.1 and 4.2). CSP formulation, AC-3, backtracking with MRV, degree and LCV, forward checking (chapters 5.1 and 5.2). Minimax, alpha beta and its best case, expectimax (chapters 6.1 to 6.3). Entailment, truth tables, CNF and resolution (chapters 7.1 and 7.2). Translating English to first order logic, unification, forward and backward chaining (chapters 7.3 and 7.4). STRIPS actions, forward and backward planning, partial order planning and threats (chapters 8.1 to 8.3). Bayes rule, marginalising and normalising (chapter 9.1). Bayesian network structure, parameter counts and d-separation (chapter 9.2). Inference by enumeration, variable elimination and sampling (chapter 9.3). MDPs, the Bellman equation, value and policy iteration (chapter 10.1). TD learning, Q-learning, SARSA and exploration (chapters 10.2 to 10.4). Lesson 2 of 6"],[32,"school","Tier 1: school and first year","Questions at this level check that you can describe an agent, follow a search order and work out a simple chance. Rows of a truth tableExam favourite 2ⁿ n symbols, each true or false. Nodes at depth dExam favourite bᵈ Each node has b children. Chance of an event P(A) = favourable cases ÷ all equally likely cases Only when every case is equally likely. Question 1. Give the PEAS description of a robot vacuum cleaner. Performance measure: how clean the floor is, the time taken and the power used. Environment: the rooms, the dirt, the furniture and the people. Actuators: wheels, brushes and the suction motor. Sensors: a dirt sensor, bump sensors and a camera. The environment is partially observable, stochastic and dynamic (chapter 1.1). Question 2. A tree has root A with children B and C. B has children D and E. C has child F. List the order in which BFS and DFS visit the nodes (left child first). BFS goes level by level: A, then B and C, then D, E and F. DFS goes as deep as it can first: A, B, D, then back up to E, then C and F. BFS: A B C D E F. DFS: A B D E C F. Question 3. Two fair dice are thrown. What is the chance the total is 7? There are 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely pairs. The pairs that give 7 are (1, 6), (2, 5), (3, 4), (4, 3), (5, 2) and (6, 1). That is 6 pairs. P = 6 ÷ 36 = 1 ÷ 6 ≈ 0.167 Question 4. MAX moves first. Move 1 lets MIN choose 3 or 5. Move 2 lets MIN choose 2 or 9. Which move should MAX play? MIN picks the smaller value: move 1 is worth min(3, 5) = 3, move 2 is worth min(2, 9) = 2. MAX picks the larger: max(3, 2) = 3. MAX plays move 1. The 9 looks tempting, but MIN would never allow it. Question 5. How many rows does a truth table for P, Q, R and S need? Each symbol can be true or false. 2 × 2 × 2 × 2. 2⁴ = 16 rows Lesson 3 of 6"],[32,"school","Tier 2: university semester and IIT course exams","Here the examiner gives a small graph, tree, net or MDP and asks you to run the algorithm by hand. Write the frontier or the table after every step."],[32,"school","Question 6. UCS and A* on one graph","Edges: S to A costs 1, S to B costs 5, A to B costs 2, A to G costs 6, B to G costs 1. Heuristic: h(S) = 3, h(A) = 3, h(B) = 1, h(G) = 0. Run UCS and A* and give the path and its cost. Model answer UCS frontier (g values): S 0. Expand S: A 1, B 5. Expand A: B 3 (a cheaper way in), G 7. Expand B (3): G 4. UCS takes G out with cost 4. Path S, A, B, G. A* frontier (f = g + h): S 3. Expand S: A 4, B 6. Expand A: B 3 + 1 = 4, G 7. Expand B: G 4 + 0 = 4. A* also finds S, A, B, G with cost 4. Here h is consistent, so A* graph search is safe. both return S, A, B, G with cost 4"],[32,"school","Question 7. Alpha beta on the tree of the slides","Leaves 2 3 5 9 0 1 7 5 under MAX, MIN, MAX. Find the root value and the pruned parts. The first MAX node is max(2, 3) = 3, so the MIN node above it has β = 3. The next MAX node reads 5, which is at least β = 3. MIN will never let play reach it, so the 9 is pruned. The left MIN node is 3, so the root has α = 3. On the right, the first MAX node is max(0, 1) = 1, so that MIN node is at most 1. That is below α = 3, so the whole last MAX node (7 and 5) is pruned. root = 3, the same as plain minimax"],[32,"school","Question 8. Bayes rule with a sum in the bottom","It rains on 20 percent of days. The grass is wet on 90 percent of rainy days and on 10 percent of dry days. The grass is wet. P(rain | wet)? P(wet) = 0.9 × 0.2 + 0.1 × 0.8 = 0.18 + 0.08 = 0.26. P(rain | wet) = P(wet | rain) P(rain) ÷ P(wet) = 0.18 ÷ 0.26. ≈ 0.692"],[32,"school","Question 9. One sweep of value iteration","The racing car MDP of chapter 10.1, γ = 0.9, starting from V = 0. Find V₁(cool) and V₁(warm). Cool: slow gives 1. Fast gives 0.5 × 2 + 0.5 × 2 = 2. So V₁(cool) = 2. Warm: slow gives 1. Fast gives −10. So V₁(warm) = 1. The next sweep gives V₂(cool) = 3.35 and V₂(warm) = 2.35, and the values reach 15.5 and 14.5. V₁ = (2, 1) The animation below runs the same kind of sweep on the 4 by 3 grid world of chapter 10.1, one sweep at a time."],[32,"school","Question 10. Convert to CNF","Convert A ⇔ (B ∨ C) to conjunctive normal form. Replace ⇔ by two implications: (A ⇒ B ∨ C) ∧ (B ∨ C ⇒ A). Replace each ⇒: (¬A ∨ B ∨ C) ∧ (¬(B ∨ C) ∨ A). Push ¬ inwards with De Morgan: ¬(B ∨ C) is ¬B ∧ ¬C. Then distribute ∨ over ∧. (¬A ∨ B ∨ C) ∧ (¬B ∨ A) ∧ (¬C ∨ A) Lesson 4 of 6"],[32,"mcq","Tier 3: GATE and NPTEL style papers","In these papers there is no partial credit and often a penalty for a wrong option. Learn the quick checks below and use them to rule out options before you compute. Quick check Use it to BFS and IDS are optimal only when every step costs the same Reject \"BFS is optimal\" for unequal costs DFS is not complete on infinite or cyclic spaces without a check Reject \"DFS always finds a solution\" The max of admissible heuristics is admissible, the sum usually is not Settle multiple select questions on heuristics Alpha beta never changes the root value Reject options where pruning gives a new answer A node is independent of its non descendants given its parents Read independence straight off a net P(A ∨ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∧ B) Avoid adding overlapping cases twice Rejection sampling keeps about N × P(e) samples Spot why it fails for rare evidence Q-learning is off policy, SARSA is on policy Answer the cliff walking question in one line Question 11 (numerical answer). BFS with b = 2 and the goal at depth 3. How many nodes are generated, not counting the root? Depth 1 has 2, depth 2 has 4, depth 3 has 8. 2 + 4 + 8. 14 Question 12 (multiple select). For the 8 puzzle, which are admissible? (a) misplaced tiles h₁ (b) Manhattan distance h₂ (c) max(h₁, h₂) (d) h₁ + h₂ h₁ and h₂ are both exact costs of relaxed problems, so they never overestimate. (a) and (b) are true. The max of two admissible values is still below the true cost. (c) is true. The sum can overestimate: one tile one step away counts 1 in each, giving 2 when the true cost is 1. (d) is false. answer: a, b, c Question 13 (numerical answer). P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4, P(A ∧ B) = 0.2. P(A ∨ B)? P(A ∨ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∧ B). 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.2. 0.7 Question 14 (numerical answer). The evidence has probability 0.01. About how many of 1,000 rejection samples are kept? Each sample agrees with the evidence with chance 0.01. 1,000 × 0.01. about 10. Likelihood weighting keeps all 1,000 and weights them instead Question 15 (multiple choice). In standard first order logic, is ∀x P(x) ⇒ ∃x P(x) valid? Standard semantics needs at least one object in every model. If P holds for every object, it holds for that object, so some x has P(x). valid. It would fail only if empty domains were allowed In the exam Round only at the very end. A numerical answer type key often has a narrow range such as 0.69 to 0.70. Lesson 5 of 6"],[32,"mcq","Tier 4: proofs, derivations and critique","Exams in the style of MIT, Berkeley, Stanford, CMU and IISc reward a complete argument more than a final number. State the conditions, prove the claim, then say when it fails. Chapter 11.1 has five more proofs."],[32,"mcq","Question 16. If h₁ and h₂ are consistent, show that h = max(h₁, h₂) is consistent.","Model answer Take an edge from n to n′ with cost c. For each i, hᵢ(n) ≤ c + hᵢ(n′) ≤ c + h(n′), since h(n′) is at least hᵢ(n′). So both h₁(n) and h₂(n) are at most c + h(n′). Their max, h(n), is too. At the goal both are 0, so h(goal) = 0. So h is consistent, and it dominates each hᵢ, so A* expands no more nodes with it."],[32,"mcq","Question 17. Show that the two parents of a collider are independent when nothing is observed.","Model answer In the net B, E, A with A having parents B and E: P(b, e) = Σ over a of P(b) P(e) P(a | b, e). P(b) P(e) does not depend on a, so it comes out of the sum: P(b) P(e) Σ over a of P(a | b, e). The sum of a conditional distribution over its values is 1. P(b, e) = P(b) P(e), so B ⫫ E"],[32,"mcq","Question 18. Derive the value of a fixed policy in closed form.","Model answer For a fixed policy π, the Bellman equation is linear: V(s) = R(s) + γ Σ P(s′ | s, π(s)) V(s′). In matrix form V = R + γ P_π V, so (I − γ P_π) V = R. For γ < 1 the matrix I − γ P_π can be inverted, because every eigenvalue of P_π has size at most 1. V^π = (I − γ P_π)⁻¹ R, which policy iteration uses for exact evaluation"],[32,"mcq","Question 19. Show that UCS is optimal when every step cost is at least some ε > 0.","Model answer UCS takes nodes out in order of g. Every step adds at least ε, so g grows along any path, and only finitely many nodes have g below any bound. When the goal is taken out with cost C, every node with g < C has already been taken out. Any cheaper path to the goal would have a node with g < C on the frontier, which would come out first. So no cheaper path exists. With zero cost steps, UCS can loop forever on a zero cost cycle. That is why the ε condition is needed for completeness."],[32,"mcq","Critique questions","A critique question gives a claim and asks what is wrong. For example, \"use greedy best first search because it is faster than A*\". The answer: greedy search ignores the cost so far, so it is not optimal, and in tree search it can loop. Another favourite: \"a Bayesian network arrow means cause\". The answer: a network only encodes conditional independence. Many graphs give the same independences, and only a causal model with interventions (chapter 9.4) supports cause and effect. In the exam In proofs, name each condition when you use it: consistency, positive step costs, definite clauses, a finite domain, γ < 1. Examiners often ask what breaks when one fails. One idea, four depths At school you describe agents, follow BFS and DFS on a tree, count rows and cases, and pick a move by minimax. At university you run UCS, A*, alpha beta, AC-3, enumeration and value iteration by hand, writing the frontier or table after each step. In GATE and NPTEL papers you recognise the right fact fast, use quick checks to drop options and round only at the end. At research universities you prove optimality, consistency and independence results, derive closed forms such as V = (I − γP)⁻¹R, and critique methods by their conditions. Lesson 6 of 6"],[32,"sheet","Formula sheet and last minute checks","Topic Formula or fact BFS nodes, IDS nodes b + b² + … + bᵈ, Σ (d + 1 − i) bⁱ Search memory BFS O(bᵈ), DFS O(bm), IDS O(bd) A* f(n) = g(n) + h(n), optimal with admissible h (tree) or consistent h (graph) Consistency h(n) ≤ c(n, n′) + h(n′) and h(goal) = 0 Effective branching factor N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + … + (b*)ᵈ Simulated annealing accept a worse move with probability e^(ΔE ÷ T) Tree CSP O(n d²) Alpha beta best case b^⌈d ÷ 2⌉ + b^⌊d ÷ 2⌋ − 1 leaves Resolution from A ∨ ℓ and B ∨ ¬ℓ infer A ∨ B, prove KB ⊨ α by refuting KB ∧ ¬α STRIPS regression new goal = (goal minus add list) plus preconditions Bayes rule P(h | e) = P(e | h) P(h) ÷ P(e), P(e) = Σ P(e | h) P(h) Bayesian network P(x₁, …, xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)) Adjustment for a cause P(y | do(x)) = Σ over z of P(y | x, z) P(z) Bellman optimality V(s) = max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) (R + γ V(s′)) TD and Q-learning V ← V + α (r + γ V(s′) − V), Q ← Q + α (r + γ max Q(s′, ·) − Q) REINFORCE θ ← θ + α (G − b) ∇ log π(a | s)"],[32,"sheet","Ten mistakes that cost marks","Testing the goal when a node is added in UCS or A*, which can return a costlier path. Calling an admissible heuristic safe for graph search without checking consistency. Adding two admissible heuristics and calling the sum admissible. Forgetting to put arcs back on the AC-3 queue after a domain shrinks. Mixing up α and β in alpha beta: α is the value MAX can already guarantee on the path, β the value MIN can guarantee. Prune only when they cross. Using forward chaining on clauses that are not definite clauses. Unifying x with F(x) without the occurs check. Forgetting the base rate in Bayes rule. Saying a collider path is open with no evidence, or blocked when a descendant is observed. Using max in a SARSA update, or the action actually taken in a Q-learning update."],[33,"","Decision problems and preferences","Decision problems, the three preference relations, completeness, transitivity, cycles and what carries over from weak to strict preference. · decision problem · preference relation · weak preference · strict preference · indifference · completeness · transitivity · rationality Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 1: Rational choice›Preferences Step 1 · Topic 1 of 3 · School and college Write any choice as actions, outcomes and a preference, learn the three preference relations, and see why rational preferences must be complete and transitive. About 40 min4 lessons1 animation9 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a decision problem is 2Weak preference, strict preference and indifference 3Completeness and transitivity 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[33,"problem","What a decision problem is","Every day you choose. Which bus to take, which film to watch, which job offer to accept. The first lecture of the course turns this everyday act into mathematics so that a computer can do it too. A decision problem has three parts. A decision problemExam favourite ( A, X, c, ⪰ ) A is the set of actions you can take. X is the set of outcomes that can happen. c is the map that says which outcome each action leads to. ⪰ is your preference over outcomes. The consequence map c : A → X With certainty, each action leads to exactly one outcome. Later chapters let an action lead to a lottery over outcomes. Action value v(a) = u( c(a) ) We like outcomes, not actions. An action is worth whatever its outcome is worth. Part Everyday example AI example Actions A Go to INOX or go to PVR Move left, right, up or down Outcomes X The film you end up watching The square the robot reaches Consequence c INOX shows Aliens Moving right from (1, 1) reaches (2, 1) Preference ⪰ Aliens is better than Casablanca The charging dock is better than the stairs The professor stressed one distinction. An action is something you choose. An outcome is something you receive. Utility is given to outcomes, and the value of an action is borrowed from the outcome it leads to. Real world scenario: a delivery robot A warehouse robot can take the lift, take the ramp or wait. The lift reaches the shelf in 40 seconds, the ramp in 70 seconds, and waiting reaches nothing. Write A = {{lift, ramp, wait}}, X = {{shelf in 40 s, shelf in 70 s, no delivery}}, and c(lift) = shelf in 40 s. Once the robot can rank the three outcomes, picking an action is a lookup. Lesson 2 of 4"],[33,"relations","Weak preference, strict preference and indifference","A preference relation compares two outcomes at a time. The basic building block is weak preference: x ⪰ y means \"x is at least as good as y\". Everything else is built from it. Weak preferenceExam favourite x ⪰ y x is at least as good as y. It allows a tie. Strict preferenceExam favourite x ≻ y ⟺ x ⪰ y and not y ⪰ x x is better and the reverse does not hold. IndifferenceExam favourite x ∼ y ⟺ x ⪰ y and y ⪰ x Both directions hold, so the two are equally good. Mathematically a relation is just a set of ordered pairs. For X = {A, B, C} with A ≻ B, A ≻ C and B ∼ C the lecture writes the sets out in full. Relation Pairs it contains ⪰ weak (A, A), (B, B), (C, C), (A, B), (A, C), (B, C), (C, B) ≻ strict (A, B), (A, C) ∼ indifference (A, A), (B, B), (C, C), (B, C), (C, B) Worked example from the lecture: A ≻ B ∼ C ≻ D Start with the reflexive pairs, since every outcome is at least as good as itself: (A, A), (B, B), (C, C), (D, D). A beats everything below it: (A, B), (A, C), (A, D). B and C both beat D: (B, D), (C, D). B and C tie, so both directions go in: (B, C), (C, B). |⪰| = 4 + 3 + 2 + 2 = 11 pairs Strict pairs are those whose reverse is missing: (A, B), (A, C), (A, D), (B, D), (C, D), so 5. Indifference pairs are the 4 reflexive ones plus (B, C) and (C, B), so 6. Common mistake Equality and indifference are different ideas. Equality means two things are the same object. Indifference means two different outcomes are judged equally good. Tea and coffee are not equal, but you may be indifferent between them. Lesson 3 of 4"],[33,"rational","Completeness and transitivity","Not every set of pairs is a sensible preference. The course calls a preference rational when it has two properties. CompletenessExam favourite for all x, y ∈ X: x ⪰ y or y ⪰ x You can always compare. You may say better, worse or equally good, but you may not leave a pair unanswered. TransitivityExam favourite x ⪰ y and y ⪰ z ⟹ x ⪰ z Rankings chain together without contradiction. Rational preference rational = complete + transitive A rational agent has such a preference and picks an action whose outcome it likes most."],[33,"rational","When completeness fails","Picture two cars. One is cheaper, less powerful and more reliable. The other is dearer, more powerful and less reliable. If you have no way to trade price against power against reliability, you cannot rank them. That is incompleteness, and it is not the same as indifference. Indifference says \"they are equally good\". Incompleteness says \"I cannot say\"."],[33,"rational","When transitivity fails","Suppose A ≻ B, B ≻ C and C ≻ A. Nothing can be called best, since each outcome loses to another. Worse, someone can exploit you. They sell you B for A plus a coin, then C for B plus a coin, then A for C plus a coin. You end up holding A again with three coins fewer. This is called a money pump. Homework 1: if ⪰ is transitive, is ≻ transitive? Assume x ≻ y and y ≻ z. Then x ⪰ y and y ⪰ z, so transitivity of ⪰ gives x ⪰ z. We still need \"not z ⪰ x\". Suppose instead that z ⪰ x. From z ⪰ x and x ⪰ y transitivity gives z ⪰ y. But y ≻ z says z ⪰ y is false. That is a contradiction. So x ≻ z, and strict preference is transitive. Homework 2: if ⪰ is transitive, is ∼ transitive? Assume x ∼ y and y ∼ z. That gives four facts: x ⪰ y, y ⪰ x, y ⪰ z, z ⪰ y. Chain x ⪰ y and y ⪰ z to get x ⪰ z. Chain z ⪰ y and y ⪰ x to get z ⪰ x. Both directions hold, so x ∼ z. Does it carry over from ⪰? To ≻ To ∼ Transitivity Yes, proof above Yes, proof above Completeness No. If x ∼ y then neither x ≻ y nor y ≻ x No. If x ≻ y then x ∼ y is false and so is y ∼ x Reflexivity No. x ≻ x never holds Yes. x ⪰ x in both directions Going deeper: Why completeness implies reflexivity Completeness says that for every pair x, y at least one of x ⪰ y or y ⪰ x holds. Nothing stops us from choosing y = x. Then the statement reads \"x ⪰ x or x ⪰ x\", so x ⪰ x. A complete relation is therefore always reflexive. This is why every rational weak preference contains the diagonal pairs (A, A), (B, B) and so on. One idea, four depths To choose well you must be able to compare any two options, and your comparisons must not go round in a circle. A preference is a set of ordered pairs. Strict preference and indifference are built from weak preference. A rational preference is complete and transitive. Transitivity of ⪰ passes to ≻ and to ∼, but completeness does not. A complete and transitive relation is a total preorder. Its indifference classes are equivalence classes, and ≻ orders those classes strictly. Dropping completeness gives a partial preorder, which is the starting point of multi criteria decision making and Pareto analysis. Experiments by Tversky (1969) found intransitive choices in people when options differ slightly on several attributes. Research on regret theory, context dependent preferences and incomplete preference models (Aumann 1962, Bewley 1986) asks how much of rational choice survives once these axioms are weakened. Lesson 4 of 4"],[33,"levels","From intuition to research","Preferences are the raw material of every later chapter. This lesson takes the same idea from an everyday picture to an open research question, so you can stop at the depth you need. Real world scenario: the money pump at a juice stall Suppose Riya prefers tea to juice, juice to coffee and coffee to tea. A stall owner hands her tea, then offers juice for a small fee of 1 rupee, then coffee for another rupee, then tea again for a third. Each swap looks good to her, yet after three swaps she holds the same tea and is 3 rupees poorer. Cyclic preferences can be exploited without end, which is why transitivity is treated as a rule of rational choice. Work it out: is this preference transitive? Riya: tea ≻ juice, juice ≻ coffee, coffee ≻ tea. Transitivity would need tea ≻ coffee from the first two. But she states coffee ≻ tea, so the relation has a cycle. Not transitive. Each round of the pump costs her 3 rupees. Going deeper: the analysis A complete and transitive weak preference is called a weak order. With n options there are n! strict rankings with no ties, but more weak orders, because ties are allowed. For 3 options there are 6 strict rankings and 13 weak orders, for 4 options 24 and 75. These are the ordered Bell (Fubini) numbers. Every weak order can be drawn as a ladder of indifference classes, best class on top. Going deeper: the research view Arrow’s impossibility theorem (1951) shows that even when every person is transitive, a group may not be. Three voters with rankings A B C, B C A and C A B make the majority prefer A to B, B to C and C to A: the Condorcet paradox. In AI this matters for preference learning from many annotators, where pooled human comparisons can cycle and a single reward model cannot fit them all. Common mistake Completeness does not mean you like every option. It means you can compare any two, possibly by saying you are indifferent."],[34,"","Utility functions","Utility representation, ordinal invariance, the existence theorem with its construction proof, and counting rankings. · utility function · ordinal utility · cardinal utility · existence theorem · representation · increasing transformation · n factorial Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 1: Rational choice›Utility Step 1 · Topic 2 of 3 · School and college Turn a ranking into numbers, see why only the order of those numbers matters, and prove that every rational preference over a finite set has a utility function. About 40 min4 lessons3 animations6 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1A number for every outcome 2Ordinal and cardinal utility 3When does a utility function exist? 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[34,"represent","A number for every outcome","Comparing outcomes two at a time gets tiring once there are many of them. A utility function does the work once: it gives each outcome a single real number so that bigger means better. Then choosing the best outcome is just finding the largest number. Utility function u : X → ℝ A score for each outcome. It may stand for satisfaction, value, benefit or happiness. It is not money unless the model says so. RepresentationExam favourite x ⪰ y ⟺ u(x) ≥ u(y) u represents ⪰ when the order of the numbers is exactly the order of the preference. The two consequences x ≻ y ⟺ u(x) > u(y), x ∼ y ⟺ u(x) = u(y) Strict preference matches a strictly larger number, and a tie matches equal numbers. Common mistake The slide transcript once read \"u(x) = u(y) ⟺ x ⪰ y\". That is a transcription slip. The correct condition for weak preference uses ≥. Equality belongs to indifference only. Building a utility function from a ranking, one class at a timePress Step: take the best outcomes still left, give them the next value, remove them, repeat. Then try a transformation. Step 3 of 3 Ranking (best first) Value for the best class3 Gap between classes1 Then apply Assigned 3 of 3 classes. Outcomes in one class share one value, values fall as you go down the ranking. Try this Play the construction. Each step removes one indifference class, which is the proof that a finite rational ranking always has a utility function. Set the best value to 100 and the gap to 20. The numbers change, the order does not. Ordinal utility is not unique. Apply v = 2u + 7 or v = u³. Both are strictly increasing, so the order survives. Apply v = −u. The ranking flips, so v no longer represents the same preferences. Set the best value to 1 with gap 2, then apply u². The values 1, −1, −3 square to 1, 1, 9 and the order breaks. u² is increasing only for non negative u. Lecture example: A ≻ B, A ≻ C, B ∼ C We need u(A) larger than both u(B) and u(C), and u(B) = u(C). One choice: u(A) = 10, u(B) = 5, u(C) = 5. Another choice: u(A) = 100, u(B) = −10, u(C) = −10. Both satisfy u(A) > u(B) = u(C), so both represent the same preference. Lesson 2 of 4"],[34,"ordinal","Ordinal and cardinal utility","If two different sets of numbers represent the same preference, which one is right? For a ranking, both are. Only the order matters, not the size of the gaps. Utility that carries only order is called ordinal. Ordinal invarianceExam favourite if f is strictly increasing, v(x) = f( u(x) ) represents the same ⪰ Examples: 2u + 7, u³, eᵘ, and log u when u is positive. A decreasing f such as −u reverses the ranking. Cardinal utility u(A) − u(B) compared with u(C) − u(D) Cardinal utility treats the size of a difference as meaningful. It needs more assumptions than ranking alone, and it returns in chapter 2.2 with lotteries. Proof that an increasing f keeps the ranking Assume u represents ⪰ and f is strictly increasing, which means a ≥ b ⟺ f(a) ≥ f(b). Take any x and y. By representation, x ⪰ y ⟺ u(x) ≥ u(y). Apply f to both sides. Because f is strictly increasing, u(x) ≥ u(y) ⟺ f(u(x)) ≥ f(u(y)). So x ⪰ y ⟺ v(x) ≥ v(y), and v = f ∘ u represents ⪰. Statement about u(A) = 10, u(B) = 5, u(C) = 1 Ordinal meaning Cardinal meaning A is preferred to B Yes, meaningful Yes, meaningful A is twice as good as B Not meaningful Not meaningful either. Ratios change with 2u + 7 The step from B to A is bigger than from C to B Not meaningful Meaningful only when u is cardinal Real world scenario: star ratings A film site shows 1 to 5 stars. Saying a 5 star film is better than a 3 star film uses only order. Saying the 5 star film is worth as much as the 3 star film and the 2 star film together treats the stars as cardinal, which the ratings were never designed to support. Lesson 3 of 4"],[34,"existence","When does a utility function exist?","The main theorem of lecture 1 says when a preference can be turned into numbers. Existence theoremExam favourite X finite and ⪰ complete and transitive ⟹ some u : X → ℝ represents ⪰ Three conditions: finitely many outcomes, completeness, transitivity. Proof by construction, as given in class Find the top ranked outcomes, the x with x ⪰ y for every remaining y. Ties at the top all count. Give every one of them the same number, the largest still unused. Remove them from the set and repeat with what is left. Because X is finite, the process stops after at most |X| rounds. A ≻ B ∼ C ≻ D gives classes {A}, {B, C}, {D} and u = 3, 2, 2, 1 Why a top ranked outcome always exists Suppose no outcome is top ranked. Then every outcome is beaten by some other outcome. Start at any x₁. Some x₂ ≻ x₁. Some x₃ ≻ x₂. Keep going. X is finite, so some outcome must appear twice in this chain. The repeat creates a cycle, and transitivity of ≻ would give that outcome ≻ itself. That is impossible, so a top ranked outcome exists."],[34,"existence","What breaks when a condition fails","Condition dropped What goes wrong Transitivity A ≻ B ≻ C ≻ A would need u(A) > u(B) > u(C) > u(A), so u(A) > u(A). Impossible. Completeness Real numbers are always comparable, so any u would rank pairs the agent cannot rank. Finiteness The construction may never end. Lexicographic preference on the plane has no utility at all."],[34,"existence","How many rankings are there?","With n outcomes and no ties, every ranking is an ordering of n objects, so there are n! of them. Five outcomes give 120 rankings. Twenty outcomes give 20! ≈ 2.43 × 10¹⁸. That is enormous but still finite, and finiteness is what the theorem needs. Allowing ties gives even more rankings. Going deeper: Lexicographic preference: an infinite case with no utility Order points (a, b) of the unit square by a first and use b only to break ties, as a dictionary orders words. This relation is complete and transitive. Suppose u represented it. For each a the interval from u(a, 0) to u(a, 1) is non empty, and the intervals for different a do not overlap. Each interval contains a rational number, so we would have uncountably many different rationals. That is impossible, so no utility function exists. Debreu showed that continuity of preferences is the extra condition that rescues the theorem for infinite sets. One idea, four depths If you can rank your choices without going round in circles, you can give each one a score, and the best choice is the one with the highest score. u represents ⪰ when x ⪰ y exactly when u(x) ≥ u(y). For finite X a rational preference always has such a u, built by peeling off the best class. Any strictly increasing transform gives another valid u. Ordinal utility is unique only up to strictly increasing transformations. Debreu's theorem extends existence to continuous preferences on connected separable spaces. Lexicographic preferences show that completeness and transitivity alone are not enough on infinite sets. In learning from human feedback, a reward model is a learned utility over outputs fitted to pairwise comparisons, for example with the Bradley Terry model. Intransitive human judgments then show up as label noise that no single scalar reward can fit. Lesson 4 of 4"],[34,"levels","From intuition to research","A utility function is a score that reproduces a ranking. Here the same idea is followed from star ratings to the reward models used to train chat assistants. Real world scenario: star ratings for restaurants A food app gives restaurant A 3 stars, C 2 stars and B 1 star. If the app doubled every rating and added 5 (11, 9 and 7), your choice would not change, because only the order matters for picking the best. That is the meaning of ordinal utility: any strictly increasing change of the numbers describes the same preferences. Work it out: which changes keep the same ranking? u(A) = 3, u(C) = 2, u(B) = 1. v = 2u + 5 gives 11, 9, 7: same order. w = u² gives 9, 4, 1: same order, since all values are positive. z = −u gives −3, −2, −1: the order is reversed. Any strictly increasing function keeps the ranking, a decreasing one reverses it Going deeper: the analysis A utility function always exists on a finite or countable set of options with complete and transitive preferences. On an uncountable set it can fail. The lexicographic order on pairs (x, y), compare x first and use y only to break ties, has no utility function: each x would need its own interval of utility values with no overlap, and the real line cannot hold uncountably many disjoint intervals that each contain a rational number. Debreu (1954) showed that adding continuity of preferences restores existence. Going deeper: the research view The Bradley and Terry model turns utilities into choice probabilities: P(a chosen over b) = eu(a) ÷ (eu(a) + eu(b)). With u(a) = 2 and u(b) = 1 this gives 0.731. Reinforcement learning from human feedback trains a reward model exactly this way from pairwise human preferences, then optimises a language model against that learned utility. Common mistake With ordinal utility, differences mean nothing. u(A) − u(B) = 2 does not say A is liked twice as much as some other pair with difference 1."],[35,"","Choosing the best action","Rational choice as arg max, decision trees, the movie, fruit tree and city park problems, and constrained optimization on an interval. · rational choice · arg max · decision tree · problem 1.6 · problem 1.7 · corner solution · satisficing · optimization Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 1: Rational choice›Best action Step 1 · Topic 3 of 3 · School and college Put preferences to work: solve the movie decision tree, the fruit tree problem 1.6 and the city parks problem 1.7, and learn when the best choice sits at a boundary. About 50 min5 lessons3 animations8 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1From preferences to the best action 2Decision trees: the movie problem 3Problem 1.6: the fruit trees 4Problem 1.7: how much to spend on parks 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[35,"rational","From preferences to the best action","Lecture 1 describes a rational individual with four pieces of knowledge. The agent knows the actions A, the outcomes X, which outcome each action leads to, and its own preference. With all four, choosing is a calculation. Value of an actionExam favourite v(a) = u( c(a) ) First find the consequence, then score it. Rational choiceExam favourite a* ∈ arg maxa ∈ Av(a) ⟺ v(a*) ≥ v(a) for every a arg max is a set. If two actions tie for the top, both are rational. Common mistake The transcript sometimes wrote v(a*) > v(a) for every other a. That strict version only fits when the best action is unique. With ties the right condition is ≥."],[35,"rational","Maximizing and satisficing","Maximizing Satisficing Rule Pick an action in arg max v Pick any action with v(a) ≥ T, an aspiration level Search Must compare everything Can stop at the first good enough action Used Throughout this course Herbert Simon's model of real human choice under limited time"],[35,"rational","Machine learning is rational choice","A regression model that minimizes squared error L = (y − ŷ)² is maximizing the utility u = −(y − ŷ)². Changing the loss changes what counts as best. Rationality does not mean everyone wants the same thing. It means each agent consistently maximizes its own objective. Loss as negative utility min (y − ŷ)² ⟺ max −(y − ŷ)² Minimizing a loss and maximizing its negative pick the same answer. Lesson 2 of 5"],[35,"trees","Decision trees: the movie problem","A decision tree draws a choice as a picture. Square nodes are choices, branches are actions, and leaves are outcomes with their payoffs. A tree is a graph with no cycles, so every leaf has exactly one path from the root. Lecture 2 asks you to choose between two cinemas. INOX is 1 mile away and shows Aliens, Blade Runner and Casablanca. PVR is 3 miles away and shows Doctor Strange, Gone with the Wind and The Matrix. You rank the six films alphabetically from best to worst and give them utilities 6 down to 1. Solve it with walking costs of 1 per mile Without walking, the best leaf is Aliens with utility 6, at INOX. Net payoff = film utility − miles walked. INOX: Aliens 6 − 1 = 5, Blade Runner 4, Casablanca 3. PVR: Doctor Strange 3 − 3 = 0, Gone with the Wind −1, The Matrix −2. Best: go to INOX and watch Aliens, net payoff 5 Now drag the PVR distance down to 0 in the animation, or move Aliens to PVR. The tree changes and so can the answer. That is the point of the tree: once the payoffs are written on the leaves, the choice follows mechanically. Real world scenario: route planning A navigation app builds the same kind of tree. Each junction is a decision node, each road a branch, and each arrival a leaf scored by minus the travel time. The cost of walking in the movie problem is the cost of a longer route here. Lesson 3 of 5"],[35,"fruit","Problem 1.6: the fruit trees","You may plant at most two fruit trees. Each tree costs money to maintain but lowers your food bill. You care only about total spending, so lower is better. Tree Maintenance Food bill reduction Net change per tree Apple $100 $130 −30 Orange $70 $90 −20 Pear $120 $145 −25 Spending after a planExam favourite u(plan) = X + Σ maintenance − Σ reduction X is what you spend with no trees. A smaller number is better. Variety version second tree of the same kind saves only half Part (d) of the problem. Mixed pairs keep the full saving. Part (a) and (b): list every action and its spending Actions: none, A, O, P, AA, OO, PP, AO, AP, OP. That is 10, since order does not matter. Single trees: A gives X − 30, O gives X − 20, P gives X − 25. Pairs: AA = X − 60, OO = X − 40, PP = X − 50, AO = X − 50, AP = X − 55, OP = X − 45. Lowest spending is X − 60: plant two apple trees Part (d): a second tree of the same kind saves half AA: maintenance 200, saving 130 + 65 = 195, so X + 5. OO: maintenance 140, saving 90 + 45 = 135, so X + 5. PP: maintenance 240, saving 145 + 72.5 = 217.5, so X + 22.5. Mixed pairs and single trees do not change: AP is still X − 55. Lowest spending is now X − 55: plant one apple and one pear Remember A change in the payoff structure changed the rational choice. Nothing about the person's rationality changed, only the numbers on the leaves. Lesson 4 of 5"],[35,"parks","Problem 1.7: how much to spend on parks","A city has a budget of $20 million. By law, parks may receive at most 5%, which is $1 million. Spending c dollars gives a benefit that grows like a square root but has a cost that grows in a straight line. BenefitExam favourite v(c) = √400c − c80 = 20√c − c80 The action set is the interval A = [0, 1,000,000]. It is continuous, so we cannot list the actions, we must use calculus. First order conditionExam favourite v′(c) = 10√c − 180 = 0 Marginal benefit equals marginal cost. Second order condition v″(c) = −5 c−3/2 < 0 v is strictly concave, so a stationary point inside the interval is the unique maximum. Part (b): solve with √(400c) Set 10 / √c = 1/80. Then √c = 800. Square both sides: c = 640,000. 640,000 lies inside [0, 1,000,000], and v is concave, so this is the maximum. c* = $640,000 and v(c*) = 20 × 800 − 8,000 = 8,000 Part (c): the benefit changes to √(1600c) Now v(c) = 40√c − c/80 and v′(c) = 20/√c − 1/80. Setting v′ = 0 gives √c = 1,600, so c = 2,560,000. That is more than the legal cap of 1,000,000, so it is not allowed. v is concave and its peak lies to the right of the cap, so v is increasing on the whole interval. c* = $1,000,000, a corner solution Going deeper: The recipe for a one variable problem on an interval To maximize v(c) on [L, U]: find every c with v′(c) = 0 inside the interval, then evaluate v at those points and at both ends L and U, and keep the largest. The second derivative tells you whether a stationary point is a peak (v″ < 0) or a valley (v″ > 0). For concave v the answer is the stationary point clipped to the interval: min(max(c₀, L), U). One idea, four depths Write down every choice, work out what each one gives you, and pick the one that gives the most. If there are too many choices to list, use a graph or calculus to find the top. v(a) = u(c(a)) and a* ∈ arg max v. Discrete problems are solved by listing or by a decision tree. Continuous ones are solved with v′ = 0, a check of v″, and a comparison with the boundary. With constraints, the Karush Kuhn Tucker conditions generalize the boundary check: at a corner the derivative need not be zero, but its sign must point out of the feasible set. Concavity turns these necessary conditions into sufficient ones. Bounded rationality asks what to do when computing arg max is itself costly. Satisficing, anytime algorithms and the value of computation in metareasoning (Russell and Wefald) treat thinking time as one more action with a cost. Lesson 5 of 5"],[35,"levels","From intuition to research","Picking the best action means finding the largest value of a function. This lesson goes from listing options by hand to the hidden bias of choosing the maximum of noisy estimates. Real world scenario: choosing how many hours to study Each extra hour of study raises your score, but tiredness grows too. If the benefit of x hours is 10x − x², the first hours help a lot and later hours help less, until extra hours start to hurt. The best plan sits where one more hour adds nothing. Work it out: maximise u(x) = 10x − x² for 0 ≤ x ≤ 8 Derivative u′(x) = 10 − 2x, zero at x = 5. Second derivative −2 < 0, so x = 5 is a maximum: u(5) = 25. Check the ends: u(0) = 0 and u(8) = 80 − 64 = 16. Best action x = 5 with value 25 Going deeper: the analysis The Weierstrass theorem guarantees a best action when the value function is continuous and the action set is closed and bounded, as with 0 ≤ x ≤ 8. On an open set such as 0 < x < 1 with u(x) = x there is no best action, only better and better ones. If u is concave, any point where the derivative is zero is a global maximum, which is why economists like concave payoffs. Going deeper: the research view The optimiser’s curse (Smith and Winkler 2006): when you pick the action with the highest estimated value and the estimates are noisy, the chosen estimate is biased upwards. In reinforcement learning the same effect makes Q-learning overestimate values, and double Q-learning (van Hasselt 2010) fixes it by choosing the action with one estimate and scoring it with another. Common mistake Setting the derivative to zero is not enough. Check the second derivative and the end points of the allowed range."],[36,"","Lotteries and distributions","Lotteries, the CDF and its properties, intervals, densities and continuous outcomes. · lottery · CDF · PDF · nature · interval probability · uniform distribution · stochastic dominance Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 2: Choice under risk›Lotteries Step 2 · Topic 1 of 3 · College Let nature pick the outcome, describe each action by a lottery, and read probabilities from a CDF without the usual traps. About 40 min4 lessons2 animations7 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1When an action has more than one outcome 2The cumulative distribution function 3Densities and continuous outcomes 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[36,"nature","When an action has more than one outcome","So far each action led to one known outcome. Real life is rarely like that. Investing in research may succeed or fail. A crop may yield a lot or a little depending on the rain. The course models this by adding a second player called nature. You choose an action, then nature picks the outcome at random. Nature is not a person. It stands for weather, markets, disease, traffic, and anything else outside your control. What you know is the probability with which nature picks each outcome, and that probability can depend on your action. Lottery of an actionExam favourite P( xi | a ) = pi, 0 ≤ pi ≤ 1, ∑ pi = 1 A simple lottery lists every final outcome with its probability. The probabilities add to 1 because exactly one outcome happens. Deterministic as a special case P( c(a) | a ) = 1 A certain action is a lottery that puts all its probability on one outcome. Deterministic choice Risky choice Action leads to One known outcome A distribution of outcomes Compare u(c(a)) E[u(X) | a] Tree contains Decision nodes only Decision nodes and chance nodes Ranking enough? Yes, ordinal utility works No, cardinal utility is needed (chapter 2.2) Lecture example of a simple lottery Action a gives x₁ with 0.6, x₂ with 0.3, x₃ with 0.1. Each probability lies between 0 and 1. 0.6 + 0.3 + 0.1 = 1, so this is a valid lottery Lesson 2 of 4"],[36,"cdf","The cumulative distribution function","When the outcome can be any number, such as a crop yield of 1.2, 1.21 or 1.2135 tonnes, we cannot list every outcome with its own probability. Instead we ask: what is the chance the outcome is at most x? That function of x is the CDF. CDF of an actionExam favourite Fa(x) = P( X ≤ x | a ) The probability that the outcome is at most x when you take action a. Interval probabilityExam favourite P( r < X ≤ s | a ) = Fa(s) − Fa(r) Everything up to s minus everything up to r."],[36,"cdf","The four properties of every CDF","Property Formula Plain meaning Non decreasing x < y ⟹ F(x) ≤ F(y) More room below means at least as much probability Lower limit F(x) → 0 as x → −∞ Nothing lies below everything Upper limit F(x) → 1 as x → +∞ Everything lies below infinity Right continuous F(x) = lim F(x + h) as h ↓ 0 At a jump the filled dot sits on the upper step Common mistake F(50) = 0.7 means a 70% chance the outcome is at most 50. It does not mean a 70% chance the outcome is exactly 50. This misreading is the most common exam error on CDFs. Practice problem 8: F(50) = 0.8 and F(20) = 0.3. Find P(20 < X ≤ 50) Use the interval rule P(r < X ≤ s) = F(s) − F(r). Here r = 20 and s = 50. P = 0.8 − 0.3 = 0.5 Lesson 3 of 4"],[36,"pdf","Densities and continuous outcomes","For a continuous outcome the CDF has a slope. That slope is the probability density function, the PDF. The density says how crowded the probability is near x. It is not itself a probability, and it can be bigger than 1. From density to CDFExam favourite Fa(x) = ∫−∞xfa(t) dt Add up the density from the far left up to x. From CDF to density fa(x) = ddxFa(x) Wherever the derivative exists. Single points P( X = x ) = 0 for a continuous X Only intervals carry probability. Lottery CDF F(x) Shape Discrete, outcomes xᵢ with pᵢ Sum of pᵢ with xᵢ ≤ x A staircase with jumps of size pᵢ Uniform on [L, U] (x − L) ÷ (U − L) inside A straight ramp from 0 to 1 Exponential with rate λ 1 − e−λx for x ≥ 0 Rises fast then flattens Normal with mean μ and spread σ Φ((x − μ) ÷ σ) An S curve centred on μ Real world scenario: a fertilizer decision A farmer choosing how much fertilizer to buy faces a different yield distribution for each choice. Light fertilizer might give a yield uniform on 20 to 40 tonnes, heavy fertilizer uniform on 15 to 60. Heavy has more upside and more downside. The CDF of each action is what the farmer compares, and chapter 2.2 shows how expected utility turns that comparison into one number per action. Going deeper: First order stochastic dominance Action a dominates action b in the first order sense when Fa(x) ≤ Fb(x) for every x, with strict inequality somewhere. In words, a is less likely than b to fall below any threshold. Every agent who prefers more to less then prefers a, whatever its risk attitude. When the two CDFs cross, the choice depends on the utility function, which is why expected utility is needed. One idea, four depths When you cannot be sure what will happen, list what could happen and how likely each thing is. That list is a lottery. An action under risk gives a distribution P(· | a). The CDF F(x) = P(X ≤ x) is non decreasing, goes from 0 to 1 and is right continuous. Intervals use F(s) − F(r). A density is the slope of F. The CDF determines the distribution completely, and expectations are written as Lebesgue Stieltjes integrals ∫ u dF, which handle discrete, continuous and mixed cases with one formula. First and second order stochastic dominance compare CDFs directly. Distributional reinforcement learning (Bellemare, Dabney and Munos 2017) learns the whole return distribution, often through its quantile function, the inverse of the CDF, rather than only its mean. Risk sensitive policies then optimize a quantile or a tail measure such as CVaR. Lesson 4 of 4"],[36,"levels","From intuition to research","A lottery lists what can happen and how likely each outcome is. The cumulative distribution function packs the same information into one rising curve. Real world scenario: a cricket forecast Before a match a forecaster says: win 0.6, draw 0.1, lose 0.3. That is a lottery over three outcomes. The probabilities add to 1, and a team choosing whether to declare early is really choosing between two such lotteries. Work it out: the CDF of a fair die X takes 1 to 6, each with probability 1/6. F(x) = P(X ≤ x). F(3) = 3/6 = 0.5. F(3.5) is still 0.5, because no outcome lies between 3 and 3.5. P(2 < X ≤ 5) = F(5) − F(2) = 5/6 − 2/6 = 0.5 Going deeper: the analysis Every CDF is non-decreasing, right continuous, tends to 0 on the left and to 1 on the right, and P(a < X ≤ b) = F(b) − F(a). For a discrete lottery F is a staircase whose jumps are the probabilities. For a continuous one F is smooth and its slope is the density f = F′. Going deeper: the research view First order stochastic dominance compares whole distributions: if FA(x) ≤ FB(x) for every x, then every person who prefers more to less prefers A. Machine learning papers now use this test to compare the score distributions of models over many random seeds instead of comparing single averages (Dror et al. 2019, \"Deep Dominance\"). Common mistake A density can be larger than 1. The uniform density on [0, 0.5] equals 2. Only the area under it must be 1."],[37,"","Expected utility and compound lotteries","Expected utility, reducing compound lotteries, the R&D example, the reversal under non affine changes and why utility under risk is cardinal. · expected utility · compound lottery · R&D · affine transformation · cardinal utility · von Neumann Morgenstern · present value Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 2: Choice under risk›Expected utility Step 2 · Topic 2 of 3 · College Score each lottery by its expected utility, reduce the R&D compound lottery step by step, and see why a mere ranking cannot compare risky choices. About 45 min4 lessons2 animations7 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Expected utility 2Compound lotteries: the R&D example 3Why ranking is not enough under risk 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[37,"eu","Expected utility","Once each action is a lottery, how do we compare two lotteries? The course uses the most common answer: weight each outcome's utility by its probability and add. The result is the expected utility of the action, and the rational agent picks the action with the largest. Expected utility, discreteExam favourite EU(a) = ∑iP( xi | a ) u( xi ) Probability times utility, summed over outcomes. Expected utility, continuous EU(a) = ∫ u(x) dFa(x) = ∫ u(x) fa(x) dx The second form needs a density. Rational choice under riskExam favourite a* ∈ arg maxa E[ u(X) | a ] Maximize expected utility, not expected money."],[37,"eu","Expected payoff is not expected outcome","The professor stressed this. Multiply probability by the utility of the outcome, not by the outcome itself. The two agree only when u(x) = x. Practice problems 5 and 6: 100 with 0.3, 40 with 0.7 With u(x) = x: EU = 0.3 × 100 + 0.7 × 40 = 30 + 28 = 58. With u(x) = √x: u(100) = 10 and u(40) = 6.325. EU = 0.3 × 10 + 0.7 × 6.325 = 3 + 4.427 = 7.43. u(E[X]) = √58 = 7.616, which is not E[u(X)] = 7.43 Practice problem 9: compare two risky actions Action A: 20 with 0.4, 0 with 0.6, so EU(A) = 8. Action B: 12 with 0.8, 0 with 0.2, so EU(B) = 9.6. 9.6 > 8, choose B B has the smaller prize but a much better chance of getting it. Lesson 2 of 4"],[37,"compound","Compound lotteries: the R&D example","A firm decides whether to invest in research (action G) or not (action S). Research succeeds with probability 0.625. After success the market is good with probability 0.9. After failure it is good with probability 0.5. A good market pays 10 and a bad one 0. Without research the market is good with probability 0.5. Action G is a compound lottery: a lottery whose prizes are themselves lotteries. To compare it with S, reduce it to a simple lottery over final payoffs. Reduction of compound lotteriesExam favourite P(final outcome) = ∑paths ∏branches on the pathP(branch) Multiply along each path, then add the paths that end at the same outcome. Reduce action G Success and good: 0.625 × 0.9 = 0.5625. Success and bad: 0.625 × 0.1 = 0.0625. Failure and good: 0.375 × 0.5 = 0.1875. Failure and bad: 0.1875. Check: 0.5625 + 0.0625 + 0.1875 + 0.1875 = 1. P(10 | G) = 0.5625 + 0.1875 = 0.75, so EU(G) = 7.5 against EU(S) = 5"],[37,"compound","Other ways to compare lotteries","Rule Looks at R&D answer Expected utility Σ p u G: 7.5 against 5 Optimistic Chance of the best outcome G: 0.75 against 0.5 Pessimistic Chance of the worst outcome, lower is better G: 0.25 against 0.5 Others Median, quantiles, worst case, variance, chance of beating a target Depends on the rule Here every rule agrees. They need not. The course uses expected utility because it has a clean mathematical justification, which is the von Neumann and Morgenstern theorem in the deep box below. Lesson 3 of 4"],[37,"affine","Why ranking is not enough under risk","Chapter 1.2 showed that any increasing transformation of a utility function keeps the ranking. Under risk that is no longer true. Lecture 2 gives a sharp example. Same ranking, opposite choice Outcomes A, B, C, D with u = 9, −1, 10, 0. The ranking is C ≻ A ≻ D ≻ B. G gives A with 0.75 and B with 0.25. S gives C with 0.5 and D with 0.5. EU(G) = 0.75 × 9 + 0.25 × (−1) = 6.5 and EU(S) = 5. Choose G. Now change only u(B) to −8. The ranking C ≻ A ≻ D ≻ B is unchanged. EU(G) = 6.75 − 2 = 4.75 < EU(S) = 5. Now choose S. Positive affine transformationExam favourite v(x) = au(x) + b, a > 0 The only transformations that keep every expected utility comparison. Why it works E[ a u(X) + b ] = a E[ u(X) ] + b Expectation is linear. Scaling by a positive a and shifting by b keeps the order of the expected values. In the exam Moving u(B) from −1 to −8 is not affine: it stretches one gap and leaves the others alone. Under risk the sizes of the gaps matter, so expected utility needs cardinal utility, unique up to a u + b with a > 0. Going deeper: The von Neumann and Morgenstern theorem Suppose a preference over lotteries is complete and transitive, continuous (if L ≻ M ≻ N there is a mix of L and N indifferent to M) and independent (mixing both sides with the same third lottery does not change the comparison). Then there is a utility u over outcomes such that L ⪰ M exactly when EL[u] ≥ EM[u], and u is unique up to positive affine transformations. Independence is the axiom that the Allais paradox shows people often break."],[37,"affine","A look ahead: money later is worth less","If research pays off in t years, its payoff should be discounted. With interest rate r, a future value FV is worth PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)t today. Chapter 3.2 develops this fully. Present value PV = FV(1 + r)t For a risky payoff: E[u(Xt)] ÷ (1 + r)t. One idea, four depths To compare two gambles, multiply what each result is worth to you by how likely it is, add up, and pick the bigger total. EU(a) = Σ P(xᵢ | a) u(xᵢ). Reduce compound lotteries by multiplying along paths. Expected utility choices survive a u + b with a > 0 but not other increasing transforms, so utility under risk is cardinal. The von Neumann and Morgenstern axioms (completeness, transitivity, continuity, independence) are exactly what is needed for an expected utility representation. Reduction of compound lotteries is an extra assumption that experiments on ambiguity question. The Allais paradox breaks independence, and Kahneman and Tversky's prospect theory replaces probabilities with decision weights and utility with a value function around a reference point. Rank dependent utility and Choquet expected utility are formal models that relax independence. Lesson 4 of 4"],[37,"levels","From intuition to research","Expected utility weights the value of each outcome by its probability. Here it is followed from a phone insurance decision to the axioms behind it and the paradoxes that test it. Real world scenario: insuring a phone A phone worth 100 has a 50 percent chance of breaking this year. Expected money without insurance is 50. Many people still pay more than 50 to be covered for sure, because losing everything hurts more than its money value suggests. Expected utility, not expected money, explains this. Work it out: sure 40 or a coin toss for 100 or 0, with u(x) = √x Expected money of the toss: 0.5 × 100 = 50, more than 40. Expected utility of the toss: 0.5 × √100 + 0.5 × 0 = 5. Utility of the sure amount: √40 = 6.32. 6.32 > 5, so this person takes the sure 40 Going deeper: the analysis The von Neumann and Morgenstern theorem: if preferences over lotteries are complete, transitive, continuous and satisfy independence (mixing two lotteries with a third in the same proportion keeps their order), then there is a utility u such that lottery L is preferred to M exactly when EL[u] ≥ EM[u]. The utility is unique up to a positive affine change a·u + b with a > 0, which is why cardinal differences now matter. Going deeper: the research view The Allais paradox shows real people break the independence axiom, and prospect theory (Kahneman and Tversky 1979) models this with reference points and distorted probability weights. In AI, an agent that maximises expected reward is an expected utility maximiser, so choosing the reward function is choosing its vN-M utility. Common mistake Expected value and expected utility differ whenever u is not a straight line. Write which one a question asks for."],[38,"","Risk attitudes and the St Petersburg paradox","Risk attitudes from concavity, certainty equivalent and risk premium, and the St Petersburg paradox with its log utility resolution. · risk aversion · certainty equivalent · risk premium · St Petersburg paradox · log utility · concave utility · Jensen inequality Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 2: Choice under risk›Risk attitudes Step 2 · Topic 3 of 3 · College See how the curve of a utility function makes someone careful, neutral or bold with risk, price any gamble in sure money, and resolve the St Petersburg paradox. About 45 min4 lessons2 animations9 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Three attitudes to risk 2The certainty equivalent 3The St Petersburg paradox 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[38,"attitudes","Three attitudes to risk","Would you rather have 50 rupees for sure, or a coin toss that pays 100 or nothing? Both have the same expected money, 50. Most people take the sure 50. Expected utility explains this through the shape of u. Risk neutralExam favourite E[ u(X) ] = u( E[X] ) u is linear, u(x) = ax + b. Only expected money matters. Risk averseExam favourite E[ u(X) ] ≤ u( E[X] ) u is concave (u″ < 0), for example ln x or √x. The sure amount is preferred. Risk lovingExam favourite E[ u(X) ] ≥ u( E[X] ) u is convex (u″ > 0), for example x². The gamble is preferred. The picture behind this is Jensen's inequality. Draw u and join the two outcomes with a straight chord. The chord's height at the mean is E[u(X)], and the curve's height there is u(E[X]). For a concave curve the chord is below, so the gamble is worth less than its mean. Risk attitude: compare E[u(X)] with u(E[X])A two outcome lottery on the curve u. The chord joins the two outcomes. Where the chord sits against the curve decides the attitude. Utility of money Low outcome x₁0 High outcome x₂100 P(high outcome)0.50 u(x) = √x: E[u(X)] = 5 < u(E[X]) = 7.071, so this agent is risk averse. The certainty equivalent 25 is the sure amount worth exactly as much as the lottery. Try this u = √x is concave: the chord lies under the curve, E[u] < u(E[X]) and the certainty equivalent is below the mean. Risk averse. Switch to u = x². The chord lies above the curve. The certainty equivalent is above the mean. Risk loving, as in practice problem 5. Switch to u = x. The chord is the curve. Certainty equivalent equals the mean. Risk neutral. Practice problem 4: √x with 100 at probability 0.2 and 0 otherwise. EU = 2, so the certainty equivalent is only 4, far below the sure 30. The fair gamble from lecture 3 Pay 1 to enter. Win 2 with probability 0.5, or nothing. Net outcomes: +1 with 0.5 and −1 with 0.5, so expected money is 0. Not playing gives 0 for sure, also expected money 0. Play when 0.5 u(1) + 0.5 u(−1) > u(0) A risk averse agent refuses, a risk neutral one is indifferent, and a risk lover plays. Practice problem 5: u = x², 0 or 10 with 0.5 each E[X] = 5, so u(E[X]) = 25. E[u(X)] = 0.5 × 0 + 0.5 × 100 = 50. 50 > 25, so this agent is risk loving Lesson 2 of 4"],[38,"ce","The certainty equivalent","How much is a gamble worth to you in sure money? That amount is its certainty equivalent. It turns any lottery into one number you can compare with cash. Certainty equivalentExam favourite u(CE) = E[ u(X) ] ⟹ CE = u−1( E[u(X)] ) The sure amount you value exactly as much as the lottery. Risk premium RP = E[X] − CE What you would give up in expectation to remove the risk. Positive for a risk averse agent. Ordering averse: CE ≤ E[X], neutral: CE = E[X], loving: CE ≥ E[X] Practice problem 4: √x, 100 with 0.2 against 30 for sure EU(A) = 0.2 × √100 + 0.8 × √0 = 0.2 × 10 = 2. EU(B) = √30 = 5.477. Choose B. Certainty equivalent of A: √CE = 2, so CE = 4. E[A] = 20 but CE(A) = 4, a risk premium of 16 Utility u″ Attitude CE of 0 or 100 at 50/50 x 0 Neutral 50 √x −¼ x−3/2 < 0 Averse 25 ln(1 + x) −1 ÷ (1 + x)² < 0 Averse 9.05 x² 2 > 0 Loving 70.71 Real world scenario: insurance A home worth 100 lakh has a 1% chance of being destroyed. Expected loss is 1 lakh. A risk averse owner with u = ln(wealth) would pay a premium above 1 lakh to remove the risk, and the insurer, being close to risk neutral across many homes, is happy to sell at that price. The gap between the two is the risk premium, and it is why insurance exists. Lesson 3 of 4"],[38,"stpete","The St Petersburg paradox","A fair coin is tossed until the first tail. If the first tail is on toss n you win 2n−1. How much would you pay to play? Expected moneyExam favourite E[X] = ∑n=1∞12n · 2n−1 = ∑ 12 = ∞ Every toss adds exactly one half to the sum, forever. Expected log utilityExam favourite E[ln X] = ∑ (n − 1) ln 22n = ln 2 Since ∑ (n − 1) ÷ 2ⁿ = 1. Finite. Its certainty equivalent CE = eln 2 = 2 A log utility agent would pay only about 2. The paradox is that expected money says pay any amount, yet nobody pays much. Daniel Bernoulli resolved it in 1738 with a concave, logarithmic utility. Extra money matters less the more you already have. Why Σ (n − 1) ÷ 2ⁿ = 1 Write S = Σn≥1 (n − 1) ÷ 2ⁿ = 0/2 + 1/4 + 2/8 + 3/16 + … Put m = n − 1: S = Σm≥0 m ÷ 2m+1 = ½ Σ m ÷ 2m. The known sum Σ m xᵐ = x ÷ (1 − x)² gives Σ m ÷ 2ᵐ = ½ ÷ ¼ = 2. S = ½ × 2 = 1, so E[ln X] = ln 2 In the exam Concavity is what tames the paradox. With the convex u = x² the terms are 2ⁿ⁻² and the sum still diverges. Even √x converges, to about 1.707, which gives a certainty equivalent near 2.91. Going deeper: Bounded utility and the real resolution Menger showed in 1934 that for any unbounded utility you can design a super St Petersburg game whose payoffs grow fast enough to make expected utility infinite again. With log utility, pay e2ⁿ instead of 2ⁿ. A fully safe theory needs bounded utility. In practice the bank cannot pay more than it owns: with a cap of about 10¹² the expected money is only about 20. One idea, four depths Most people would take a sure 50 over a coin toss for 100 or nothing. Extra money feels less important the more you have, and that makes people careful with risk. Concave u means risk averse (E[u] ≤ u(E[X]), CE ≤ E[X]), linear means neutral, convex means loving. CE = u⁻¹(E[u]). St Petersburg has infinite expected money, but log utility gives E = ln 2 and CE = 2. The Arrow Pratt coefficient −u″(x) ÷ u′(x) measures absolute risk aversion. For a small gamble with variance σ², the risk premium is about ½ σ² times this coefficient. CRRA utilities x1−γ ÷ (1 − γ) give constant relative risk aversion γ, with ln x at γ = 1. Rabin's calibration theorem (2000) shows that turning down small fair gambles at all wealth levels implies absurd aversion to large ones under expected utility, which supports reference dependent models. In reinforcement learning, exponential utility gives risk sensitive control and entropic risk measures. Lesson 4 of 4"],[38,"levels","From intuition to research","Risk attitude is the shape of the utility curve. Concave means careful, convex means adventurous, straight means indifferent to risk. Real world scenario: a farmer choosing a crop A safe crop always earns 25. A risky crop earns 100 in a good year and 0 in a bad one, each with probability 0.5. Expected money favours the risky crop (50), but a farmer whose family must eat every year may prefer the safe one. How much safety is worth is the risk premium. Work it out: certainty equivalent with u(x) = √x Expected utility of the risky crop: 0.5 × 10 + 0.5 × 0 = 5. Certainty equivalent: the sure amount with √CE = 5, so CE = 25. Risk premium = expected money − CE = 50 − 25 = 25. This farmer is just indifferent between the safe crop (25) and the risky one Going deeper: the analysis Jensen’s inequality says that for a concave u, E[u(X)] ≤ u(E[X]), so a risk averse person always values a gamble below its expected money. The Arrow and Pratt measure r(x) = −u″(x) ÷ u′(x) gives the strength of risk aversion at wealth x. For u = √x, r(x) = 1 ÷ (2x): risk aversion falls as wealth grows, matching everyday experience. Going deeper: the research view Risk sensitive reinforcement learning replaces expected return with measures such as conditional value at risk, the average of the worst α percent of outcomes, to build safer agents for finance, robotics and medicine. The Kelly criterion, which bets to maximise expected log wealth, is the log utility case. Common mistake A risk averse person may still gamble. They need the gamble to pay enough more than the sure amount, the risk premium."],[39,"","Backward induction","Backward induction on the R&D and marketing tree, full strategies, the film studio problem and Bellman's principle. · backward induction · decision tree · strategy · dynamic programming · principle of optimality · R&D · expected value Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 3: Decisions over time›Backward induction Step 3 · Topic 1 of 3 · College and masters Solve a decision that unfolds in stages by folding the tree from the end, check it against every full strategy, and see why it scales when listing plans does not. About 40 min4 lessons1 animation3 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Decisions that unfold over time 2Solving the R&D and marketing tree 3Practice problem A: the film studio 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[39,"idea","Decisions that unfold over time","In chapter 2.2 the firm chose once, at the start. Lecture 4 adds a second choice later. After it learns whether research worked, the firm decides whether to spend on a marketing campaign. Because the second decision can depend on what has already happened, the firm can react to news. That is what makes this a decision over time. Path Then Payoffs Skip research (s) Nature picks the market 10 or 0, each with 0.5 Go for research (g), success with 0.625 Choose marketing m or not d, then the market is good with 0.9 m: 13 or −7, d: 9 or −1 Go for research (g), failure with 0.375 Choose m or d, then the market is good with 0.5 m: 13 or −7, d: 9 or −1 Backward inductionExam favourite start at the last decision, replace each chance node by its expected value, keep the best action at each decision node, and move one level back Repeat until you reach the root. At a chance node V(n) = ∑kpk V(childk) Nature moves, so you average. At a decision node V(n) = maxa V(childa) You move, so you take the best. Backward induction on the R&D and marketing treeFold the tree from the right. At an N node take the expected value, at a D node keep the better branch. Press Step. Step 0 of 5 P(R&D succeeds)0.625 P(good market | success)0.90 P(good market | failure)0.50 Market (m): good payoff13 Market (m): bad payoff-7 Do not market (d): good payoff9 Do not market (d): bad payoff-1 Skip R&D: P(10) (else 0)0.50 Start at the last decisions, the marketing choices after success and after failure. Try this The lecture tree. Play it: market after success (11 > 8), do not market after failure (4 > 3), so go for R&D with 8.375 against 5. Make the bad market for a campaign only −2. Now marketing wins after failure too and the plan becomes \"always market\". Drop the success chance to 0.2. The R&D branch falls to 0.2 × 11 + 0.8 × 4 = 5.4, barely above skipping. Lesson 2 of 4"],[39,"solve","Solving the R&D and marketing tree","Backward induction, exactly as in class After success: E[m] = 0.9 × 13 + 0.1 × (−7) = 11 and E[d] = 0.9 × 9 + 0.1 × (−1) = 8. Market, and carry 11 back. After failure: E[m] = 0.5 × 13 + 0.5 × (−7) = 3 and E[d] = 0.5 × 9 + 0.5 × (−1) = 4. Do not market, and carry 4 back. Research chance node: 0.625 × 11 + 0.375 × 4 = 6.875 + 1.5 = 8.375. Skip: 0.5 × 10 + 0.5 × 0 = 5. 8.375 > 5: go for research, market only if it succeeds Common mistake Never compare raw leaf payoffs where nature moves. 13 is not \"better than\" −7 in any useful sense at a chance node, because the firm does not choose between them. Convert every chance node to its expected value first, and compare only at nodes where the firm chooses."],[39,"solve","The cross check: list every full strategy","A strategy is a complete plan that says what to do at every point you might reach, even points you will not reach. Here a strategy says what to do after success and what to do after failure. After success, after failure Expected payoff M, M (always market) 0.625 × 11 + 0.375 × 3 = 8.000 M, D (market only after success) 0.625 × 11 + 0.375 × 4 = 8.375, the best D, M (market only after failure) 0.625 × 8 + 0.375 × 3 = 6.125 D, D (never market) 0.625 × 8 + 0.375 × 4 = 6.500 Both methods give 8.375. The professor's point was about effort. With k binary decision nodes there are 2k full strategies, and listing them quickly becomes hopeless. Backward induction visits each node once. Real world scenario: a vaccine maker How does a firm know research has succeeded? A vaccine maker learns it from clinical trial results. That observable signal is what lets the marketing decision depend on the trial. Planning \"if the trial works we launch a big campaign, if not we keep it small\" is exactly the M, D strategy. Lesson 3 of 4"],[39,"practice","Practice problem A: the film studio","A studio picks a big or a small budget. With a big budget, nature sets the audience buzz: good with 0.4, weak with 0.6. After seeing the buzz the studio decides whether to push extra marketing. A small budget gives 6 or 1 with equal chance. Buzz Push: success chance, payoffs No push: payoffs Good (0.4) 0.8: 18 or 0 0.8: 14 or 2 Weak (0.6) 0.3: 18 or −4 0.3: 10 or 1 Fold the tree back Good buzz: push 0.8 × 18 + 0.2 × 0 = 14.4, no push 0.8 × 14 + 0.2 × 2 = 11.6. Push, value 14.4. Weak buzz: push 0.3 × 18 + 0.7 × (−4) = 2.6, no push 0.3 × 10 + 0.7 × 1 = 3.7. Hold back, value 3.7. Big budget: 0.4 × 14.4 + 0.6 × 3.7 = 5.76 + 2.22 = 7.98. Small budget: 0.5 × 6 + 0.5 × 1 = 3.5. Choose the big budget, push only on good buzz, value 7.98 Going deeper: Why backward induction is correct: the principle of optimality Bellman's principle says that whatever the first decision, the rest of an optimal plan must be optimal for the situation that decision creates. Suppose the best plan used a sub plan that was not best from some node. Replacing it by the better sub plan raises the value of that node without changing the probability of reaching it, so the whole plan improves, which is a contradiction. Backward induction applies this from the leaves upward. Its cost is linear in the size of the tree, while listing strategies is exponential in the number of decision nodes. One idea, four depths Plan from the end. Ask what you would do at the last step in each situation, then use those answers to decide the step before, and so on back to today. Average at chance nodes, take the max at decision nodes, and fold from the leaves to the root. A strategy is a full contingent plan. The R&D tree gives 8.375 by both folding and listing the four strategies. Backward induction is dynamic programming on a finite tree and rests on Bellman's principle of optimality. In games it gives subgame perfect equilibria. With shared sub states the tree becomes a graph and memoisation turns exponential work into polynomial work. Markov decision processes generalize the tree to cycles and infinite horizons, solved by value iteration V(s) = maxa Σ P(s′ | s, a)[r + γ V(s′)]. Monte Carlo tree search, as in AlphaGo, estimates the same backups by sampling when the tree is too large to fold exactly. Lesson 4 of 4"],[39,"levels","From intuition to research","Backward induction solves plans that unfold over time by starting at the end. It is the idea behind dynamic programming and much of reinforcement learning. Real world scenario: planning revision for an exam With the exam on Friday, decide first what Thursday must cover, then what Wednesday must cover to make Thursday possible, and so on back to today. A chess player solving an endgame works the same way: from the checkmate position back to the current move. Work it out: invest 10 now, then launch or sell After a good market (0.6): launch earns 50, selling earns 20. Choose launch, 50. After a bad market (0.4): launch earns −10, selling earns 5. Choose sell, 5. Value of investing: 0.6 × 50 + 0.4 × 5 − 10 = 30 + 2 − 10 = 22. 22 > 0, so invest, then launch if good and sell if bad Going deeper: the analysis Bellman’s principle of optimality: whatever the first decision, the remaining decisions must be optimal for the situation it creates. In equations Vt(s) = maxa(r(s, a) + E[Vt+1(s′)]). Decision nodes take a maximum, chance nodes take an expectation, and the work grows with the number of states rather than with the number of complete plans. Going deeper: the research view Zermelo (1913) used backward induction to show that chess is determined: either White can force a win, Black can, or both can force a draw. Nobody knows which, because the tree is too large. AlphaZero approximates the backed-up values with a neural network and tree search, which is backward induction at scale. Common mistake At chance nodes average with the probabilities. Taking the maximum there assumes luck always goes your way."],[40,"","Discounting and consumption over time","Discount factors and present value, optimal consumption with ln and √x, corner solutions, and the time consistency of exponential discounting. · discount factor · present value · consumption smoothing · time consistency · hyperbolic discounting · corner solution · first order conditions Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 3: Decisions over time›Discounting Step 3 · Topic 2 of 3 · College and masters Price future payments with a discount factor, split an income over two or three periods, and see when the best plan sits at a corner and why exponential plans never need revising. About 50 min4 lessons2 animations7 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why money later is worth less 2Spreading consumption over time 3Corners, homework and time consistency 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[40,"discount","Why money later is worth less","Put 100 rupees in a bank paying interest r. After a year you have 100(1 + r). Now turn the question round. Someone promises you 100 a year from now. What is that promise worth today? It is the amount X that would grow into 100, so X(1 + r) = 100. Discount factorExam favourite δ = 11 + r, 0 < δ < 1 A rupee next period is worth δ rupees today. n periods aheadExam favourite value today of A received after n periods = A δn = A(1 + r)n Two periods: 100 grows to 100(1 + r)², so it is worth 100δ² today. Present value of a stream PV = ∑txt δt Discount each payment by its own delay, then add. δ is not something you choose. It describes how patient you are. The professor's example: if you need money for an operation today, a payment tomorrow is worth very little to you, so your δ is close to 0. Someone with no urgent needs has δ close to 1. Bringing future payments back to todayEach bar is a payment. The violet part is what it is worth today: amount × δᵗ for exponential, amount ⁄ (1 + k t) for hyperbolic. Payments at the end of years 1, 2, 3, … Interest rate r (%)11.1 Discounting k for hyperbolic0.20 δ = 1 ⁄ (1 + r) = 0.9001. Present value = Σ amountₜ × δᵗ = 520.31 from a total of 650. Try this Practice problem B: 200, 150, 300 with δ = 0.9 (r = 11.1%). The present value is about 520.2. Set r = 0. δ = 1 and the future is worth exactly as much as today, a perfectly patient agent. Set r = 100%. δ = 0.5 and each year halves the value, like the impatient patient who needs money for an operation now. Switch to hyperbolic. Early years fall fast but later years fall slowly, the shape that makes plans time inconsistent. Practice problem B: a scholarship pays 200, 150 and 300 at the ends of years 1, 2 and 3, with δ = 0.9 200 × 0.9 = 180. 150 × 0.81 = 121.5. 300 × 0.729 = 218.7. PV = 180 + 121.5 + 218.7 = 520.2 The implied interest rate is r = 1/δ − 1 = 1/0.9 − 1 ≈ 11.1% a year."],[40,"discount","Discounting inside a decision tree","In the discounted R&D tree, research costs 1 today and pays 10 next period if it works. The branch value is 10δ − 1, not (10 − 1)δ. The cost is paid now, so it is not discounted. Only the later gain is. Python # Present value of a stream of payments with discount factor deltadef present_value(payments, delta):return sum(x * delta ** t for t, x in enumerate(payments, start=1))delta = 0.9print(\"scholarship PV =\", round(present_value([200, 150, 300], delta), 2))print(\"implied interest rate r =\", round(1 / delta - 1, 4))for d in (1.0, 0.9, 0.5, 0.1):print(f\"delta = {d}: 100 a year for 5 years is worth {present_value([100] * 5, d):7.2f} today\") Output scholarship PV = 520.2 implied interest rate r = 0.1111 delta = 1.0: 100 a year for 5 years is worth 500.00 today delta = 0.9: 100 a year for 5 years is worth 368.56 today delta = 0.5: 100 a year for 5 years is worth 96.88 today delta = 0.1: 100 a year for 5 years is worth 11.11 today Lesson 2 of 4"],[40,"consume","Spreading consumption over time","You have a total income K to spend over several periods. Each period you enjoy u(x) from spending x, with u′ > 0 (more is better) and u″ < 0 (each extra rupee adds a little less). Later enjoyment is discounted by δ. How should you split K? Two periods, u = ln xExam favourite max ln x₁ + δ ln(K − x₁) ⟹ x₁* = K1 + δ, x₂* = δK1 + δ From 1/x₁ = δ/(K − x₁). Three periods, u = ln xExam favourite x₁* = K1 + δ + δ², x₂* = δK1 + δ + δ², x₃* = δ²K1 + δ + δ² The board solution from lecture 4. Equal discounted marginal utility 1/x₁* = δ/x₂* = δ²/x₃* No small move of money between two periods can raise the total. The whiteboard derivation for three periods Maximize ln x₁ + δ ln x₂ + δ² ln(K − x₁ − x₂). ∂/∂x₁: 1/x₁ − δ²/(K − x₁ − x₂) = 0. ∂/∂x₂: δ/x₂ − δ²/(K − x₁ − x₂) = 0. Both equal the same δ²/x₃, so 1/x₁ = δ/x₂, giving x₁ = x₂/δ. Likewise x₃ = δx₂. ln is increasing, so spend everything: x₂/δ + x₂ + δx₂ = K. x₂*(1 + δ + δ²) = δK, so x₂* = δK/(1 + δ + δ²) Remember Diminishing marginal utility means u″ < 0, not u′ falling to zero. Going from 10 to 11 rupees adds more than going from 110 to 111, but both add something. The first order condition sets the derivative of the whole objective to zero, never u′ itself. Practice problem D: u = √x, two periods Maximize √x₁ + δ√(K − x₁). Derivative: 1/(2√x₁) − δ/(2√(K − x₁)) = 0, so √(K − x₁) = δ√x₁. Square: K − x₁ = δ²x₁, so x₁* = K/(1 + δ²). K = 181, δ = 0.9: x₁* = 181/1.81 = 100 and x₂* = 81 Lesson 3 of 4"],[40,"corners","Corners, homework and time consistency",""],[40,"corners","Homework 1: linear utility","With u(x) = x the objective is x₁ + δx₂ + δ²x₃ with x₁ + x₂ + x₃ = K. The coefficient on x₁ is the largest because 1 > δ > δ². There is no curvature to reward spreading, so all the money goes to the earliest period. Linear utility x* = (K, 0, 0) A linear objective over a budget is maximized at a corner."],[40,"corners","Convex utility: the derivative finds the worst point","With u(x) = x² in two periods, v(x) = x² + δ(K − x)² is convex in x. Setting v′ = 0 finds its minimum, the worst split. The best plan is a corner: compare v(K) = K² with v(0) = δK². Since δ < 1, consume everything now. With δ = 1 the two corners tie, but splitting is still worse. Lecture example: K = 10, δ = 0.8, u = x² v(x) = x² + 0.8(10 − x)². v′(x) = 2x − 1.6(10 − x) = 0 gives x = 16/3.6 = 4.44, where v = 44.4. That is the minimum. Corners: v(10) = 100 and v(0) = 80. Best: x₁ = 10, x₂ = 0, payoff 100"],[40,"corners","Homework 2: is the plan time consistent?","Re-plan in period 2 with what is left After x₁* = K/(1 + δ + δ²), the remaining money is R = Kδ(1 + δ)/(1 + δ + δ²). From period 2 the problem is the two period one with R: x₂ = R/(1 + δ), x₃ = δR/(1 + δ). Substitute R: x₂ = Kδ/(1 + δ + δ²) and x₃ = Kδ²/(1 + δ + δ²). These are the original x₂* and x₃*, so the plan is time consistent Discounting Weight on period t Re-planning later Exponential δt Keeps the original plan (time consistent) Hyperbolic 1/(1 + kt) Wants to change it (time inconsistent): impatience is strongest about the near future Going deeper: Why exponential discounting is time consistent The trade off between periods t and t + 1 has relative weight δt+1/δt = δ, the same no matter when you look at it. So today's view of tomorrow against the day after is the same as tomorrow's view. With hyperbolic weights 1/(1 + kt), the ratio between t and t + 1 seen from today is (1 + kt)/(1 + k(t + 1)), close to 1 for large t, but seen from period t it becomes 1/(1 + k), much smaller. Plans made today for the far future look too patient once that future arrives, which is the economics of procrastination. One idea, four depths A rupee today is worth more than a rupee next year, because today's rupee could earn interest. If more money makes you a little less happy each time, you should spread spending out instead of spending it all at once. δ = 1/(1 + r), and A received in n periods is worth Aδⁿ. With concave u the optimal plan equalizes discounted marginal utilities, so for ln x the shares are 1 : δ : δ². Linear or convex u sends everything to a corner. The intertemporal Euler equation u′(xt) = δ(1 + r) u′(xt+1) generalizes the first order conditions. Exponential discounting is the only time consistent stationary scheme. Quasi hyperbolic β δ discounting (Laibson 1997) models present bias. In reinforcement learning the discount γ in Σ γt rt plays the role of δ, keeping returns finite and trading off near against far rewards. Work on hyperbolic discounting in RL (Fedus et al. 2019) learns values for many γ at once and combines them into a hyperbolic weighting. Lesson 4 of 4"],[40,"levels","From intuition to research","Discounting compares money and happiness at different times. The same factor δ appears in savings decisions and in every reinforcement learning agent. Real world scenario: 1000 rupees now or 1100 next year If a bank pays 8 percent interest, 1000 rupees today grows to 1080 in a year. An offer of 1100 next year is therefore better than 1000 today. The present value of 1100 is what you would need to deposit today to reach it. Work it out: present value at r = 0.08 Discount factor δ = 1 ÷ (1 + r) = 1 ÷ 1.08 = 0.9259. Present value of 1100 = 1100 × 0.9259. = 1018.52 > 1000, so waiting is better At r = 0.12 the present value is 1100 ÷ 1.12 = 982.14, and taking the money now is better. Going deeper: the analysis For lifetime utility ∑ δt u(ct) with interest rate r, the first order condition is the Euler equation u′(ct) = δ(1 + r) u′(ct+1). With δ(1 + r) = 1 marginal utilities are equal across periods, so a concave u gives equal consumption: people smooth spending. Exponential discounting is time consistent, a plan made today is still optimal tomorrow. Going deeper: the research view Reinforcement learning maximises ∑ γt rt, and 1 ÷ (1 − γ) is the effective planning horizon: γ = 0.99 looks about 100 steps ahead. People often discount hyperbolically, 1 ÷ (1 + kt), which makes plans time inconsistent (Laibson 1997): the gym plan made on Sunday is dropped on Monday. Common mistake The discount rate r and the discount factor δ = 1 ÷ (1 + r) are different numbers. A higher rate means a lower factor."],[41,"","The value of information","The value of perfect and imperfect information, the MBA and venue examples, and why information is never worth less than zero. · value of information · perfect information · oracle · Bayes rule · MBA · forecast · decision analysis Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 3: Decisions over time›Value of information Step 3 · Topic 3 of 3 · College and masters Work out how much it is worth to learn the state of the world before you decide, prove it can never be negative, and price imperfect forecasts with Bayes' rule. About 40 min4 lessons2 animations4 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What is it worth to know first? 2Information is never worth less than zero 3Imperfect information and Bayes 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[41,"mba","What is it worth to know first?","You must decide whether to do an MBA, which costs 10, before you know how the job market will look when you graduate. Nature makes the market good with 0.25, medium with 0.5 and poor with 0.25. Market (probability) With MBA, gross With MBA, net of 10 Without MBA Good (0.25) 32 22 12 Medium (0.5) 16 6 8 Poor (0.25) 12 2 4 Case A: decide before the market is known E[MBA] = 0.25 × 22 + 0.5 × 6 + 0.25 × 2 = 5.5 + 3 + 0.5 = 9. E[no MBA] = 0.25 × 12 + 0.5 × 8 + 0.25 × 4 = 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. Get the MBA, expected payoff 9 Case B: an oracle tells you the market first Good: max(22, 12) = 22, get the MBA. Medium: max(6, 8) = 8, skip it. Poor: max(2, 4) = 4, skip it. E[with information] = 0.25 × 22 + 0.5 × 8 + 0.25 × 4 = 10.5 Value of perfect informationExam favourite VOI = E[ maxau(a, s) ] − maxa E[ u(a, s) ] Best with the oracle (choose per state, then average) minus best without (average per action, then choose). MBA exampleExam favourite VOI = 10.5 − 9 = 1.5 Pay an oracle anything below 1.5. Above 1.5 you are better off deciding now. What is perfect information worth?Without information you pick one action for every state. With an oracle you pick the best action state by state. The gap is the value of information. Action names State, probability, payoff of each action Price the oracle asks1.00 No information: choose MBA with 9. With the oracle: 10.5. Value of information = 1.5. At a price of 1 the oracle is worth buying (you keep 0.5). Try this The MBA example (net of its cost 10). No information: 9 against 8, get the MBA. With the oracle: 10.5. Information is worth 1.5. Practice problem C: outdoor or indoor venue. 27.5 without a forecast, 37.5 with one, so a perfect forecast is worth 10. Make one action best in every state. The oracle never changes the decision and information is worth exactly 0, never less. Raise the oracle price above the value of information. Paying would leave you worse off than deciding now. Python # Value of perfect information: best expected payoff with the oracle minus best without itstates = [(\"good\", 0.25), (\"medium\", 0.5), (\"poor\", 0.25)]pay = {\"MBA\": {\"good\": 32 - 10, \"medium\": 16 - 10, \"poor\": 12 - 10},\"no MBA\": {\"good\": 12, \"medium\": 8, \"poor\": 4}}without = {a: sum(p * pay[a][s] for s, p in states) for a in pay}best_without = max(without.values())with_info = sum(p * max(pay[a][s] for a in pay) for s, p in states)print(\"decide first :\", without, \" best =\", best_without)print(\"oracle first :\", with_info)print(\"value of information =\", with_info - best_without) Output decide first : {'MBA': 9.0, 'no MBA': 8.0} best = 9.0 oracle first : 10.5 value of information = 1.5 Common mistake A student in class computed 0.25 × 22 + 0.5 × 8 + 0.25 × 4 as the no information value. That mixes actions across states, which you can only do once you know the state. Without information you must use one action in every state. Lesson 2 of 4"],[41,"never","Information is never worth less than zero","With the oracle you could always ignore what it says and take the action you would have taken anyway. So the best plan with information is at least as good as the best plan without it. Proof that VOI ≥ 0 Let a* be the best action without information, so the no information value is E[u(a*, s)]. For every state s, maxa u(a, s) ≥ u(a*, s), since a* is one of the options. Take the expectation of both sides over s. E[maxa u(a, s)] ≥ E[u(a*, s)], so VOI ≥ 0 Equality holds exactly when one action is best in every state. The oracle then never changes your choice. Practice problem C: book an outdoor or indoor venue before knowing the weather Sunny 0.5, rainy 0.5. Outdoor pays 50 or −20. Indoor pays 30 or 25. No forecast: E[outdoor] = 15 and E[indoor] = 27.5, so book indoor, value 27.5. Perfect forecast: sunny pick outdoor 50, rainy pick indoor 25, so 0.5 × 50 + 0.5 × 25 = 37.5. VOI = 37.5 − 27.5 = 10 Real world scenario: exploratory drilling An oil company can buy a lease at price X. From geology it already has probabilities for how oily the site is, which give an expected value Y without any survey. A survey costs money but reveals more. If X is far below Y the deal is good enough already and the survey adds little. If the survey is cheap compared with what it might change, survey first. The probabilities are known before drilling, exactly like the market probabilities in the MBA example. This is also one reason firms negotiate options on two or three sites instead of surveying one in depth. Lesson 3 of 4"],[41,"imperfect","Imperfect information and Bayes","Real forecasts are not oracles. Suppose a forecast is right with probability q. When it says \"sunny\", update your belief with Bayes' rule, choose the best action for the updated belief, and average over what the forecast might say. Bayes updateExam favourite P(s | signal) = P(signal | s) P(s)P(signal) The forecast shifts the probabilities, not the payoffs. Value of imperfect information VOI = ∑signalsP(signal) maxa E[u(a, s) | signal] − maxa E[u(a, s)] Between 0 and the value of perfect information. Venue problem with a forecast that is right 90% of the time Forecast says sunny with probability 0.5. Then P(sunny | says sunny) = 0.45/0.5 = 0.9. Outdoor: 0.9 × 50 + 0.1 × (−20) = 43. Indoor: 0.9 × 30 + 0.1 × 25 = 29.5. Choose outdoor, 43. Forecast says rainy: P(sunny) = 0.1. Outdoor 5 − 18 = −13, indoor 3 + 22.5 = 25.5. Choose indoor, 25.5. Expected value with the forecast: 0.5 × 43 + 0.5 × 25.5 = 34.25. VOI = 34.25 − 27.5 = 6.75, less than 10 for a perfect forecast Going deeper: A forecast that is right half the time is worth nothing With q = 0.5 the posterior after either signal equals the prior, 0.5 and 0.5. The best action after each signal is the same as without the forecast, so the value is exactly 0. Information has value only if it can change what you do. Below q = 0.5 the forecast is still useful: reverse what it says. One idea, four depths Finding out before you decide can only help, because you can always ignore what you learn. It is worth paying for only when it might change your choice. VOI = E[best per state] − best E[per action]. MBA: 10.5 − 9 = 1.5. Venue: 37.5 − 27.5 = 10. VOI ≥ 0, and it is 0 when one action wins in every state. For imperfect signals, compute posteriors by Bayes and the value of sample information. Blackwell's theorem (1953) orders information structures: one experiment is more informative than another exactly when every decision maker values it at least as much. In active learning and Bayesian optimization, the next query is chosen to maximize expected information or knowledge gradient, a direct use of value of information. Exploration bonuses in reinforcement learning, such as information directed sampling, trade the value of information against the immediate regret. Lesson 4 of 4"],[41,"levels","From intuition to research","Information is worth paying for only if it can change what you do. This lesson computes that worth and shows why it is never negative. Real world scenario: a weather forecast before a picnic An outdoor picnic is worth 100 if sunny and 0 if it rains. An indoor party is worth 60 whatever the weather. Rain has probability 0.3. How much should you pay for a perfect forecast? Work it out: the value of perfect information Without a forecast: outdoors gives 0.7 × 100 = 70, indoors 60. Choose outdoors, 70. With a forecast: sunny (0.7) choose outdoors 100, rain (0.3) choose indoors 60. Expected value with the forecast: 0.7 × 100 + 0.3 × 60 = 88. Value of perfect information = 88 − 70 = 18 Going deeper: the analysis Information is never worth less than zero: with information you could always make the same choice as without it, so E[maxa V(a | signal)] ≥ maxa E[V(a | signal)]. The gap is zero exactly when the best action is the same for every signal. Imperfect information is valued the same way, with posterior probabilities from Bayes’ rule. Going deeper: the research view Active learning and Bayesian optimisation pick the next experiment or label by its expected value of information or information gain. AI assistants that ask a clarifying question before acting use the same calculation: ask only if the answer could change the action enough to repay the cost of asking. Common mistake If every forecast would lead to the same choice, the forecast is worth 0 to you, however accurate it is."],[42,"","Static games and the normal form","Static games of complete information, common knowledge, the normal form, the Prisoner's Dilemma and best responses. · game theory · normal form · static game · common knowledge · Prisoner's Dilemma · best response · bimatrix Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 4: Games between rational agents›Normal form Step 4 · Topic 1 of 3 · College and masters Move from one decision maker to several: what static and complete information really mean, how to write a game as players, strategies and payoffs, and how to read best responses off a table. About 40 min4 lessons1 animation7 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Many agents: static games of complete information 2The normal form of a game 3Best responses and unilateral deviation 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[42,"static","Many agents: static games of complete information","Up to now one agent faced nature. From lecture 5 on, several rational agents face each other, and each one's payoff depends on what everyone does. The course places such interactions on two axes. Axis One end Other end What do players know about each other? Complete information Incomplete information When do players act? Static: choose without seeing others' choices Dynamic: some players see earlier moves This step studies the corner that is static with complete information. \"Static\" does not mean everyone acts at the same clock time. It means no player sees what any other player actually chose before choosing. Thinking about what the other might do is allowed. Seeing what they did is not. Practice problem D: static or dynamic? Sealed tenders opened together after both are submitted: static. Neither bidder sees the other bid. Chess: dynamic. Every move is seen before the next one. Two candidates reveal platforms at the same moment, drafted without seeing each other: static. Poker betting, where each player sees earlier bets: dynamic"],[42,"static","Complete information and common knowledge","Complete information means four things are common knowledge: every player's possible actions, the possible outcomes, how each combination of actions leads to an outcome, and every player's preferences. Common knowledge is stronger than everyone knowing. Everyone knows it, everyone knows that everyone knows it, and so on without end. Common knowledgeExam favourite E is common knowledge ⟺ (everyone knows)nE for every n = 1, 2, 3, … Knowing a fact privately is mutual knowledge. Common knowledge needs the whole infinite chain. Real world scenario: the class boycott The professor used the course itself. Grades are relative. Suppose the whole class secretly agrees that nobody writes anything in the exam. For that to hold, each student must be sure every other student is committed, and sure that everyone else is sure, and so on. The moment anyone suspects one classmate might study, writing a little becomes very attractive under relative grading. Without common knowledge of commitment, the boycott unravels. Lesson 2 of 4"],[42,"normal","The normal form of a game","A static game of complete information is written as a normal form game, also called the strategic form. It has three ingredients. Normal form gameExam favourite ⟨ N, {{Si}}, {{vi}} ⟩ N = {1, …, n} players. Sᵢ = pure strategies of player i. vᵢ = payoff of player i. Strategy profile s = (s₁, …, sn) ∈ S₁ × … × Sn One choice per player. In a static game a strategy is simply an action. Payoff functionExam favourite vi : S₁ × … × Sn → ℝ Your payoff depends on the whole profile, not only on your own choice. Everyone else s−i = (s₁, …, si−1, si+1, …, sn) Read \"s minus i\". We write s = (sᵢ, s₋ᵢ). A game is finite when there are finitely many players and each has finitely many strategies. If even one player has infinitely many, such as any quantity in [0, ∞), the game is infinite."],[42,"normal","The bimatrix convention","For two players with finite strategies, draw a table. Rows are player 1's strategies and columns player 2's. Each cell holds two numbers: the row player's payoff first, then the column player's."],[42,"normal","The Prisoner's Dilemma","Two suspects are held in separate rooms. Each can stay mum (M) or confess (C). Both mum: 2 years each. Both confess: 4 years each. One confesses: the confessor gets 1 year and the other 5. A year in prison is worth −1. Player 1 (rows), Player 2 (columns) M C M (−2, −2) (−5, −1) C (−1, −5) (−4, −4) Lesson 3 of 4"],[42,"br","Best responses and unilateral deviation","A best response is your best choice when you take the others' choices as fixed. Best responseExam favourite si ∈ BRi(s−i) ⟺ vi(si, s−i) ≥ vi(s′i, s−i) for all s′i Hold everyone else fixed and maximize your own payoff. Unilateral deviation from s to (s′i, s−i) Only player i changes. A profile where no unilateral deviation helps anyone is self enforcing. Best responses in the Prisoner's Dilemma If player 2 plays M, player 1 compares −2 (M) with −1 (C). C is better. If player 2 plays C, player 1 compares −5 (M) with −4 (C). C is better again. So C is player 1's best reply whatever player 2 does. The game is symmetric, so the same holds for player 2. Both confess and get (−4, −4), although (−2, −2) was better for both Remember A best response is computed against fixed choices of the others. It is not a guess about what they will do. Whether the others actually play those choices is what a solution concept, in chapter 4.3, decides. Going deeper: Static games and extensive form A static game is a normal form game. A dynamic game is drawn as a tree, the extensive form, with information sets that group nodes a player cannot tell apart. A static game is the special case where each player's whole move sits in one information set, because she has seen nothing. Every extensive form game also has a normal form, whose strategies are full contingent plans, as in the R&D tree of chapter 3.1. One idea, four depths In a game, what you get depends on what others choose too. A table lists every combination of choices and what each player gets from it. A normal form game has players N, strategy sets Sᵢ and payoffs vᵢ on profiles. Static means unobserved choices, not same time. Complete information makes actions, outcomes, the map and preferences common knowledge. Best responses hold s₋ᵢ fixed. Common knowledge is the fixed point of the everyone knows operator. Aumann's agreement theorem and the muddy children puzzle show how public announcements create it. Normal forms of extensive games can be reduced by merging equivalent strategies. Multi agent reinforcement learning builds empirical normal form games from trained policies (PSRO, Lanctot et al. 2017) and computes best responses with RL. Epistemic game theory studies which solution concepts follow from which assumptions about knowledge and rationality. Lesson 4 of 4"],[42,"levels","From intuition to research","A game in normal form is a table of payoffs for every combination of choices. The idea runs from two friends picking a restaurant to training generative networks. Real world scenario: two friends choosing a restaurant Asha prefers the dosa place, Ben prefers the pizza place, but both would rather eat together than alone. Neither knows what the other picks. The outcome for each depends on both choices, which is what makes it a game rather than a single person decision. Work it out: best responses in the restaurant game Payoffs (Asha, Ben): both dosa (2, 1), both pizza (1, 2), apart (0, 0). If Ben picks dosa, Asha’s best response is dosa (2 > 0). If Ben picks pizza, it is pizza (1 > 0). Ben’s best responses mirror this. Two stable outcomes: both dosa and both pizza Going deeper: the analysis A normal form game is G = (N, (Si), (ui)): players, strategy sets and payoff functions. The table has ∏|Si| cells: three players with 2, 3 and 4 strategies give 24 strategy profiles, each with three payoffs. A profile is stable when no player gains by a unilateral deviation. Going deeper: the research view Generative adversarial networks are a two player game between a generator and a discriminator, and multi agent reinforcement learning trains many agents whose payoffs depend on each other. Nash (1950) proved that every finite game has an equilibrium in mixed strategies, but finding one is computationally hard in general (the PPAD class). Common mistake Payoff pairs are written (row player, column player). Swapping them is the most common slip in exam tables."],[43,"","Modelling games: Cournot, Bertrand and the streetlamp","The modelling recipe, Cournot and Bertrand duopoly, and the streetlamp public good with free riding. · Cournot · Bertrand · public good · streetlamp · best response function · duopoly · free rider Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 4: Games between rational agents›Modelling games Step 4 · Topic 2 of 3 · College and masters Turn three real stories into games: firms choosing quantities, firms choosing prices, and neighbours deciding whether to fund a streetlamp. About 50 min5 lessons3 animations7 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How to write any game down 2Cournot: competing in quantities 3Bertrand: competing in prices 4The streetlamp: a public good 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[43,"recipe","How to write any game down","Lecture 6 opens with a recipe. Before you solve a game you must state it precisely, and almost every mistake in class came from skipping a step. The modelling recipe Players: who makes a decision? Strategies: what exactly does each player choose, a quantity, a price, contribute or not? What is the smallest and largest allowed value? Payoffs: split into cases. In each case write what the player receives minus what it costs. Game = ⟨N, {{Sᵢ}}, {{vᵢ}}⟩ This chapter applies the recipe to three games used throughout the course: Cournot competition in quantities, Bertrand competition in prices, and the streetlamp public good. Lesson 2 of 5"],[43,"cournot","Cournot: competing in quantities","Two airlines, say IndiGo and SpiceJet, decide how many seats to put on the same route. They do not name a price. The market sets the price from the total supply through the inverse demand function. A potato farmer is in the same position: he can grow as much as he likes, but he cannot also fix the price. Inverse demand P(q) = a − bq if this is ≥ 0, and 0 otherwise q is the total quantity of both firms. a and b are common knowledge. Cournot payoffExam favourite vi(si, sj) = (a − b(si + sj)) si − Ci(si), or −Ci(si) if the price would be negative Strategy set Sᵢ = [0, ∞), so the game is infinite. Best response, linear cost cᵢExam favourite si = a − ci − b sj2b From ∂vᵢ/∂sᵢ = a − 2bsᵢ − bsⱼ − cᵢ = 0. Your best output falls by half of the rival's. Symmetric equilibriumExam favourite s* = a − c3b Put sⱼ = s* in the best response: 3bs* = a − c. Lecture numbers: a = 100, b = 1, c = 10 Each firm: s* = (100 − 10) ÷ 3 = 30. Total Q = 60, so the price is 100 − 60 = 40. Profit of each firm = (40 − 10) × 30. = 900 each Practice problem F: different costs, a = 60, b = 1, c₁ = 12, c₂ = 18 Best responses: s₁ = (48 − s₂) ÷ 2 and s₂ = (42 − s₁) ÷ 2. Substitute: s₁ = 13.5 + s₁ ÷ 4, so s₁* = 18 and s₂* = 12. General formula: s₁* = (a − 2c₁ + c₂) ÷ 3b = 54 ÷ 3 = 18. Price 30, profits 18 × 18 = 324 and 12 × 12 = 144 The cheaper firm makes more and earns more. Constant costs do not mean equal costs. Lesson 3 of 5"],[43,"bertrand","Bertrand: competing in prices","Now the firms choose prices and the market decides how much each sells. Buyers go only to the cheaper firm, and split evenly when prices are equal. In class, students first said the strategy was a quantity, then \"0 to infinity\", then \"MRP\". The professor corrected each in turn. Step 2 of the recipe: the strategy set A firm chooses a price pᵢ, not a quantity. A price cannot be negative, so pᵢ ≥ 0. The highest price the market can produce is at q = 0, which gives p = a. Sᵢ = [0, a] Prices above a sell nothing, exactly like a, so they add nothing. MRP, a legal price cap, plays no part in this model. Quantity from price q = a − pb Turn the demand rule around: p = a − bq gives bq = a − p. Bertrand payoffExam favourite vi = pia − pib − Ci(a − pib) if cheaper, half the market (divide by 2b) if tied, −Ci(0) if dearer Three cases. The −Cᵢ(0) term is a fixed cost paid even when nothing sells. p₁ (with p₂ = 40) Case Quantity Profit 30 cheaper 70 30 × 70 − 700 = 1,400 39.9 cheaper 60.1 2,397.99 − 601 = 1,796.99 40 tie 30 1,200 − 300 = 900 50 dearer 0 0 The payoff jumps. Just below 40 the firm earns about 1,800, exactly at 40 it earns 900, and just above it earns 0. Undercutting by one paisa roughly doubles profit. That is why price competition is so fierce. In the exam The Bertrand paradox: with equal unit cost c, the only stable prices are p₁ = p₂ = c. Two firms are enough to drive profit to zero, while Cournot firms keep the price at 40, well above cost 10. Lesson 4 of 5"],[43,"lamp","The streetlamp: a public good","Three neighbours each decide whether to pay 1 towards a streetlamp. It is built only if at least two pay, and once built it is worth 3 to everyone, including anyone who did not pay. There is no refund if it is not built. Streetlamp payoffExam favourite vi(s) = 3 · 1[ ∑jsj ≥ 2 ] − si, si ∈ {{0, 1}} The indicator 1[·] is 1 when the statement is true and 0 otherwise. Split on the sum, not on the eight profiles one by one. Others who pay You pay You do not pay Better for you 0 −1 (not built) 0 Do not pay 1 2 (you complete it) 0 Pay, you are pivotal 2 2 3 (free ride) Do not pay Neither choice is always best, so the game has no dominant strategy. From (1, 1, 1) each neighbour gains by stopping alone, 3 against 2, so everyone paying is not self enforcing. The self enforcing profiles are the three with exactly two payers, and the unhappy (0, 0, 0), where a lone payer would just lose 1. Real world scenario: all or nothing crowdfunding A crowdfunding campaign that only charges backers if a target is reached is a threshold public good. Everyone wants the project, but each backer hopes others will cover the target. Open source maintenance and herd immunity from vaccination have the same structure: a good that nobody can be excluded from and that needs enough contributors. Going deeper: Comparing the three models Cournot payoffs are smooth and concave in a firm's own quantity, so calculus finds the best response, and the price stays above cost. Bertrand payoffs jump at pᵢ = pⱼ, so there is no smooth maximum and price falls to cost. The streetlamp is finite, with two strategies each, and its interest lies in coordination and free riding rather than in calculus. Choosing the right strategy variable changes the prediction completely. One idea, four depths To study a game, first write down who plays, what each can choose, and what each gets in every case. Firms that choose amounts behave differently from firms that choose prices, and shared goods tempt people to let others pay. Cournot: sᵢ = (a − cᵢ − bsⱼ)/2b and s* = (a − c)/3b, so 30 each, price 40, profit 900. Bertrand: Sᵢ = [0, a] and a three case payoff, and price falls to cost. Streetlamp: vᵢ = 3·1[Σ ≥ 2] − sᵢ. With n Cournot firms s* = (a − c)/((n + 1)b), and the price tends to c as n grows, so Cournot converges to perfect competition. Bertrand with capacity limits (Edgeworth) or differentiated products restores positive margins. Learning agents in repeated pricing games can learn to keep prices above the Bertrand level without communicating (Calvano et al. 2020, algorithmic collusion), which is now a question for competition law. Mechanism design asks how to set rules so that public goods are funded despite free riding. Lesson 5 of 5"],[43,"levels","From intuition to research","Writing a real situation as a game is a skill. Here it is practised on two tea stalls, with the numbers worked out and a modern twist with pricing algorithms. Real world scenario: two tea stalls choosing how many cups to make Two stalls sell tea on the same street. The more cups both make, the lower the price customers will pay. Each owner decides how many cups to prepare, knowing the other is doing the same. This is a Cournot game. Work it out: Cournot with P = 100 − q₁ − q₂ and cost 10 per cup Profit of stall 1: (100 − q₁ − q₂ − 10) q₁. Best response q₁ = (90 − q₂) ÷ 2. Symmetric equilibrium: q = (90 − q) ÷ 2, so q = 30 each, price 40. Profit each: (40 − 10) × 30 = 900. A single owner would make 45 cups at price 55 for 2025, or 1012.5 each if shared, more than 900 Going deeper: the analysis Cournot competition gives more output and lower prices than a monopoly but less than perfect competition. Bertrand competition in prices with identical goods drives the price down to cost with just two firms, the Bertrand paradox, because undercutting by a tiny amount captures the whole market. The streetlamp shows the public good problem: each person’s contribution helps everyone, so each contributes too little. Going deeper: the research view Calvano et al. (2020) showed that simple Q-learning pricing algorithms, playing a repeated Bertrand game, learn to keep prices above the competitive level without any communication. This has become a question for competition law as more prices are set by AI. Common mistake The Cournot equilibrium is not the joint best outcome for the firms. Each firm ignores that its extra output lowers the other’s price."],[44,"","Solution concepts and Pareto optimality","What solution concepts are, the assumptions behind them, and Pareto optimality with worked games and the zero sum proof. · solution concept · Pareto optimality · equilibrium · zero sum · altruism · self enforcement · tragedy of the commons Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 4: Games between rational agents›Pareto Step 4 · Topic 3 of 3 · College and masters Learn what a solution concept must deliver and what equilibrium analysis assumes, then judge outcomes with Pareto optimality on the Prisoner's Dilemma, the Marketing War and zero sum games. About 45 min4 lessons2 animations3 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a solution concept is for 2Pareto optimality 3Zero sum games, altruism and the practice problems 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[44,"concepts","What a solution concept is for","Writing down a game does not tell us what will happen. A solution concept is a method that narrows all possible profiles down to the reasonable ones, either to advise players or to predict them. Whatever profile it selects is called an equilibrium of that concept. The professor warned against the physics picture of equilibrium, a state a system returns to after a push. In game theory an equilibrium is simply what a chosen concept selects. There is no implied motion towards it. Candidate concept Prisoner's Dilemma answer Verdict \"Anything can happen\" All four profiles Always exists, but gives no advice or prediction Play a strategy that is best whatever others do (C, C) Sharp and useful, but not always available Nash equilibrium (later in the course) (C, C) Exists far more often"],[44,"concepts","The four assumptions of equilibrium analysis","Assumption Meaning in this course Rational Each player maximizes her own (expected) payoff Intelligent Each player knows every action, the outcome map and everyone's preferences, as well as the analyst does Common knowledge That everyone is rational and intelligent is itself common knowledge Self enforcement The equilibrium must hold without any outside enforcer Real world scenario: the gang lawyer Suppose each suspect in the Prisoner's Dilemma gets a lawyer from the same gang who threatens his family if he confesses. Both stay mum and (M, M) results. That outcome is not self enforcing. It holds only because of a third party. Remove the threat and each suspect gains by confessing alone."],[44,"concepts","What we want from a good concept","Property Question it asks Existence Does it give an answer for every game? Uniqueness Does it pick a small set, ideally one profile? \"Anything can happen\" fails badly here. Invariance Does a small change in payoffs that keeps every ranking leave the answer the same? Lesson 2 of 4"],[44,"pareto","Pareto optimality","Pareto optimality is not a solution concept. It does not predict anything. It is a yardstick for judging the answer a solution concept gives: does the equilibrium waste payoff that everyone could have shared? Pareto dominanceExam favourite s Pareto dominates s′ ⟺ vi(s) ≥ vi(s′) for all i, and > for at least one j Nobody is worse off and someone is strictly better off. Pareto optimalExam favourite s′ is not Pareto dominated by any profile Also called Pareto efficient. Test How to apply it Direct test Search for a profile that is at least as good for everyone and strictly better for someone. If one exists, the candidate is not optimal. Improvement test Try to make one player strictly better off. If every such move hurts someone, the candidate is optimal. Prisoner's Dilemma (M, M) = (−2, −2) beats (C, C) = (−4, −4) for both players, so (C, C) is not Pareto optimal. Nothing beats (M, M): (M, C) is worse for player 1, (C, M) worse for player 2, (C, C) worse for both. (M, C) and (C, M) hold the best payoff for one player, so nothing can dominate them. Pareto optimal: (M, M), (M, C), (C, M). Not optimal: (C, C), the dominant strategy outcome Marketing Campaign War (rows and columns L, M, H) Payoffs: (L,L) 6,6. (L,M) 2,8. (L,H) 0,4. (M,L) 8,2. (M,M) 4,4. (M,H) 1,3. (H,L) 4,0. (H,M) 3,1. (H,H) 2,2. (L, L) = (6, 6) beats (M, M) and every profile with H, strictly for both players. (L, M) and (M, L) contain 8, the largest single payoff, so nothing dominates them. Nothing dominates (6, 6) either. Pareto optimal: (L, L), (L, M), (M, L) Common mistake Pareto optimality says nothing about fairness. (2, 8) and (8, 2) are Pareto optimal next to the balanced (6, 6). It only rules out waste. Lesson 3 of 4"],[44,"zerosum","Zero sum games, altruism and the practice problems",""],[44,"zerosum","Rock, Paper, Scissors and a general proof","In Rock, Paper, Scissors the winner gets 1 and the loser −1, and a tie gives 0 to both. The two payoffs always add to 0, so the game is zero sum. Checking nine cells one by one caused confusion in class. A short proof settles every cell at once. Claim: in a two player zero sum game every profile is Pareto optimal Suppose s Pareto dominates s′. Then v₁(s) ≥ v₁(s′) and v₂(s) ≥ v₂(s′), with one strict. Zero sum means v₂ = −v₁, so the second inequality says −v₁(s) ≥ −v₁(s′), that is v₁(s) ≤ v₁(s′). Together with v₁(s) ≥ v₁(s′) this forces v₁(s) = v₁(s′), and then v₂(s) = v₂(s′) too. No inequality is strict, a contradiction. Every profile is Pareto optimal."],[44,"zerosum","The Prisoner's Dilemma with altruism","Altruistic payoffExam favourite vialt(s) = vi(s) + ½ vj(s) Your own jail term at full weight plus half of the other's. Not an extra penalty added on top. Profile Original With altruism ½ (M, M) (−2, −2) (−3, −3) (M, C) (−5, −1) (−5.5, −3.5) (C, M) (−1, −5) (−3.5, −5.5) (C, C) (−4, −4) (−6, −6) With weight ½, staying mum becomes the better reply to both choices: −3 beats −3.5 and −5.5 beats −6. Caring about the other person turns the dilemma into a game whose sensible outcome is also Pareto optimal. Chapter 5.1 finds the exact weight, ⅓, where this switch happens. Practice problem A: two farmers and one aquifer, pump Heavily (H) or Moderately (Mod) Payoffs: (H,H) 2,2. (H,Mod) 5,0. (Mod,H) 0,5. (Mod,Mod) 3,3. H beats Mod for player 1 against H (2 > 0) and against Mod (5 > 3). The table is symmetric. So both pump heavily and get (2, 2). But (Mod, Mod) = (3, 3) Pareto dominates it. Pareto optimal: (Mod, Mod), (H, Mod), (Mod, H). The predicted outcome is the wasteful one. Practice problem C: two ride hailing apps choose driver incentives Small, Medium, Large Payoffs: (S,S) 5,5. (S,Md) 9,2. (S,Lg) 3,3. (Md,S) 2,9. (Md,Md) 6,6. (Md,Lg) 1,4. (Lg,S) 3,3. (Lg,Md) 4,1. (Lg,Lg) 2,2. (Md, Md) = (6, 6) dominates (S, S) and all profiles with Lg. (S, Md) and (Md, S) contain 9, the largest payoff. Pareto optimal: (S, Md), (Md, S), (Md, Md) Going deeper: The tragedy of the commons The farmers game is a two player preview of the tragedy of the commons, studied with n players and continuous effort next in the course. When each user of a shared resource gets the full benefit of extra use but bears only a share of the damage, self interest pushes total use above the level that would be best for the group. Elinor Ostrom's field work showed communities avoiding this with monitoring and graduated sanctions, which change the game rather than the players. One idea, four depths A rule for predicting games should always give an answer, a clear one, and not change wildly over tiny details. Separately, we can ask whether the result wastes anything that would make someone better off for free. Equilibrium analysis assumes rational, intelligent players, common knowledge of both, and self enforcement. Pareto dominance is ≥ for all and > for one. In the Prisoner's Dilemma the dominant outcome (C, C) is the only profile that is not Pareto optimal. In zero sum games all profiles are optimal. The Pareto frontier of a game is the set of undominated payoff vectors. Folk theorems for repeated games show that patient players can sustain Pareto superior outcomes such as (M, M) as equilibria, which links efficiency to the shadow of the future. Measuring the price of anarchy (Koutsoupias and Papadimitriou 1999) quantifies how far equilibria can fall below the social optimum, for example in routing networks and congestion games. Multi agent learning research studies how to steer self interested learners towards Pareto efficient outcomes, through reward shaping, communication or opponent shaping. Lesson 4 of 4"],[44,"levels","From intuition to research","Pareto optimality asks whether an outcome wastes anything. Equilibria often do, and the size of that waste has a name in research: the price of anarchy. Real world scenario: splitting a pizza Two friends split a pizza 5 slices to 3. Moving one slice from one to the other helps one and hurts the other, so the split is Pareto optimal. Throwing a slice away hurts someone and helps no one, so any split that wastes slices is not. Pareto optimal does not mean fair: 8 slices to 0 is Pareto optimal too. Work it out: the prisoner’s dilemma Payoffs: both cooperate (−1, −1), one defects (0, −3) or (−3, 0), both defect (−2, −2). Defect is better for each player whatever the other does, so the equilibrium is (defect, defect). Both cooperating gives (−1, −1), better for both. The equilibrium is Pareto dominated: total cost 4 against the optimum 2 Going deeper: the analysis A good solution concept should exist for a wide class of games, give a small set of predictions, and not change under irrelevant details. Pareto optimality is a separate test of efficiency. The two can conflict, as the prisoner’s dilemma shows, which is the formal reason why contracts, repeated play and regulation exist. Going deeper: the research view The price of anarchy (Koutsoupias and Papadimitriou 1999) is the ratio between the cost of the worst equilibrium and the optimal cost. In the dilemma above it is 4 ÷ 2 = 2. For selfish routing with linear delays it is at most 4/3 (Roughgarden and Tardos 2002), and Braess’s paradox shows adding a road can make every driver slower. Common mistake Pareto optimal and fair are different ideas. Pareto says nothing about how the total is shared."],[45,"","Dominance and the dominant strategy equilibrium","Strict and weak dominance, the four dominance words, the dominant strategy equilibrium with its uniqueness proof, and its limits. · dominance · strictly dominated · dominant strategy · dominant strategy equilibrium · partial order · altruism · rationality Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 5: Dominance and iterated elimination›Dominance Step 5 · Topic 1 of 3 · Masters Spot strategies that are worse whatever others do, learn the exact vocabulary, prove that a dominant strategy equilibrium is unique, and see where this first solution concept fails. About 50 min4 lessons2 animations4 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Strict domination 2Dominating, dominated, dominant, submissive 3The dominant strategy equilibrium 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[45,"strict","Strict domination","Sometimes one of your strategies is better than another whatever the others do. Then you never need to guess what they will do to know you should not play the worse one. Strict dominationExam favourite si ▷is′i ⟺ vi(si, s−i) > vi(s′i, s−i) for every s−i Only player i's own payoffs are compared, once for each situation the others can create. Weak domination si ⊵is′i ⟺ ≥ for every s−i Textbooks add \"and > for at least one s₋ᵢ\". State which version you use in an exam. Common mistake The mistake made twice in class: checking player 1 by mixing in the second number of each cell, or by moving down a column. Rule: for the row player compare first numbers, moving across columns. For the column player compare second numbers, moving down rows. Moving along the other player's strategies is exactly \"for every s₋ᵢ\". Lecture examples Prisoner's Dilemma: against M, C gives −1 > −2. Against C, −4 > −5. So C ▷₁ M, and by symmetry C ▷₂ M. With altruism ½ the table is (−3, −3), (−5.5, −3.5), (−3.5, −5.5), (−6, −6). Now −3 > −3.5 and −5.5 > −6, so M ▷₁ C. The direction reversed. Rock, Paper, Scissors: each strategy is the only best reply to something, so nothing dominates anything, not even weakly. Marketing War: M ▷₁ L (8 > 6, 4 > 2, 1 > 0), but M and H are not comparable (1 < 2 against H) Does one strategy dominate another?Pick a player and two of their strategies. Compare only that player's own payoffs, one opponent move at a time. Game Payoff table (edit any number)Each cell is: row player payoff, column player payoff. Player Strategy number to test as the winner2 Strategy number to test as the loser1 M strictly dominates L. Strict dominance needs > against every move of the opponent. A strategy is deleted by IESDS if at least one other strategy strictly dominates it. Try this Marketing War, player 1, M against L: 8 > 6, 4 > 2, 1 > 0. M strictly dominates L. Now M against H: 8 > 4, 4 > 3, but 1 < 2. One reversal is enough to kill dominance. Prisoner's Dilemma, C against M: C wins against both moves. With two strategies, dominating is the same as dominant. Slide 11, player 2, R against C: 2 > 1, 6 > 4, 8 > 6. C is strictly dominated, so it is deleted first in IESDS. Weak dominance game, player 1, M against T: 1 = 1 and 2 > 0. Weak but not strict. Python # Strict and weak dominance for the row player of a bimatrixmkt = [[(6, 6), (2, 8), (0, 4)], [(8, 2), (4, 4), (1, 3)], [(4, 0), (3, 1), (2, 2)]]names = [\"L\", \"M\", \"H\"]def strict(t, a, b): # row a strictly dominates row breturn all(t[a][c][0] > t[b][c][0] for c inrange(len(t[0])))def weak(t, a, b): # textbook version: >= everywhere and > somewhere ge = all(t[a][c][0] >= t[b][c][0] for c inrange(len(t[0])))return ge and any(t[a][c][0] > t[b][c][0] for c inrange(len(t[0])))for a inrange(3):for b inrange(3):if a != b and strict(mkt, a, b):print(names[a], \"strictly dominates\", names[b])print(\"M vs H:\", strict(mkt, 1, 2), \" H vs M:\", strict(mkt, 2, 1), \" so M and H are not comparable\") Output M strictly dominates L M vs H: False H vs M: False so M and H are not comparable Lesson 2 of 4"],[45,"vocab","Dominating, dominated, dominant, submissive","Term Needs Meaning for strategy sᵢ Dominating at least one sᵢ beats some other strategy Dominated at least one some other strategy beats sᵢ Dominant all others sᵢ beats every other strategy Submissive all others every other strategy beats sᵢ Strategy Dominating Dominated Dominant Submissive PD: C yes no yes no PD: M no yes no yes Marketing: M yes no no, does not beat H no Marketing: L no yes no no, H does not beat it Marketing: H no no no no RPS: any no no no no The difference matters. A dominant strategy, needed by the first solution concept, is rare. A dominated strategy, which is what elimination deletes, is common. A student thought a column could only be deleted if every other column beat it. One dominator is enough, because a rational player would switch to it. With only two strategies dominating equals dominant, which is why the Prisoner's Dilemma hides the difference. Theorem 1: strict domination is a strict partial order Irreflexive: sᵢ ▷ sᵢ would need a number strictly bigger than itself. Asymmetric: sᵢ ▷ s′ᵢ and s′ᵢ ▷ sᵢ would give x > y and y > x at the same s₋ᵢ. Transitive: for every s₋ᵢ, a > b and b > c give a > c. Not complete: M and H in the Marketing War, or any pair in RPS, are incomparable Going deeper: Domination by a mixed strategy Player 1 has U = (3, 0), M = (0, 3) and D = (1, 1) against (L, R). Neither U nor M dominates D. But the 50/50 mix of U and M earns 1.5 against L and 1.5 against R, more than D's 1 in both. D is strictly dominated by a mixture. This course uses pure strategies for now, and a fuller elimination later removes such strategies too. Real world scenario: the voting correction In class, voting for your favourite candidate was offered as a weakly dominant strategy in plurality voting with alphabetical tie breaks. It is not. Voter 3 ranks c ≻ b ≻ a. Voter 1 votes a and voter 2 votes b. Voting sincerely for c makes a three way tie that a wins, her worst outcome. Voting for b makes b win. What is true is that voting for your least favourite is weakly dominated. The Gibbard Satterthwaite theorem says every non dictatorial rule with three or more candidates sometimes rewards insincere voting. Lesson 3 of 4"],[45,"dse","The dominant strategy equilibrium","Strict dominant strategy equilibriumExam favourite sD with siD strictly dominant for every player i Everyone plays the strategy that is best against everything. No one needs to predict anyone. Rational players and dominated strategiesExam favourite Eμ[vi(si)] − Eμ[vi(s′i)] = ∑ μ(s−i)[vi(si, s−i) − vi(s′i, s−i)] > 0 Theorem 4: whatever the belief μ, a strictly dominated strategy has lower expected payoff, so a rational player never uses it. Examples: the Prisoner's Dilemma gives (C, C) with (−4, −4). With altruism it gives (M, M) with (−3, −3). Rock, Paper, Scissors and the Marketing War have none. Theorem 3: a strict dominant strategy equilibrium is unique (the proof many students missed the first time) Assume two different ones, s and s′. Then some player j has sⱼ ≠ s′ⱼ. In s, sⱼ is strictly dominant, so it beats s′ⱼ: vⱼ(sⱼ, s₋ⱼ) > vⱼ(s′ⱼ, s₋ⱼ) for all s₋ⱼ. In s′, s′ⱼ is strictly dominant, so vⱼ(s′ⱼ, s₋ⱼ) > vⱼ(sⱼ, s₋ⱼ) for all s₋ⱼ. Fix one s₋ⱼ. The first line says x > y and the second y > x. Impossible. So there is at most one. With ≥ instead of > the argument breaks, and weak ones can be many. Property Strict DSE Why Existence Fails RPS, Marketing War, Cournot, Bertrand, streetlamp have none Uniqueness Holds Theorem 3 (fails for the weak version) Invariance Fails Altruism ½ flips (C, C) to (M, M) Pareto optimality Fails PD picks (C, C), the only wasteful profile Going deeper: Which payoff changes cannot affect dominance Dominance compares player i's own payoffs with s₋ᵢ held fixed. So it is unchanged by any strictly increasing f(vᵢ), and by adding any term h(s₋ᵢ) that depends only on the others: f(vᵢ(sᵢ, s₋ᵢ)) + h(s₋ᵢ) > f(vᵢ(s′ᵢ, s₋ᵢ)) + h(s₋ᵢ) exactly when vᵢ(sᵢ, s₋ᵢ) > vᵢ(s′ᵢ, s₋ᵢ). Altruism is not of this form because vⱼ depends on player i's own choice too, which is why it could flip the prediction. One idea, four depths If one choice is better than another no matter what anyone else does, never make the worse choice. If one choice beats all the others, make it. sᵢ ▷ s′ᵢ needs vᵢ larger for every s₋ᵢ. Dominant beats all, dominated is beaten by one. A strict DSE is unique when it exists, but often does not exist, is not robust, and can be Pareto dominated. Strict domination by pure or mixed strategies is equivalent to never being a best response to any belief (for two players, via linear programming duality). Dominant strategy implementation is the strongest form of incentive compatibility in mechanism design. Strategy proof mechanisms, where truth telling is dominant, include the Vickrey auction and its generalization VCG. The Gibbard Satterthwaite theorem limits them for voting, and work on large markets and approximate strategy proofness studies how close practical systems such as school choice can get. Lesson 4 of 4"],[45,"levels","From intuition to research","A dominated strategy is never the right choice. This lesson adds a case the pure check misses and shows where dominance drives real systems. Real world scenario: the free umbrella If carrying an umbrella costs nothing and keeps you dry when it rains, carrying it is at least as good as leaving it in every kind of weather, and strictly better when it rains. Leaving it is weakly dominated. Work it out: domination by a mixture Row payoffs against two columns: A (3, 0), B (0, 3), C (1, 1). No pure strategy beats C in both columns: A loses to C in column 2, B loses in column 1. Mix A and B with probability 0.5 each: (1.5, 1.5). 1.5 > 1 in both columns, so C is strictly dominated by the mixture Going deeper: the analysis A strategy can survive every pure dominance check and still be dominated by a mixed strategy. A rational player never uses a strictly dominated strategy, and in two player games a strategy is never a best response to any belief exactly when it is strictly dominated, possibly by a mixture. This equivalence is the link between dominance and rationalisability. Going deeper: the research view Mechanism design builds rules where telling the truth is a dominant strategy, called dominant strategy incentive compatibility. The Vickrey-Clarke-Groves mechanism achieves it for efficient allocation, and variants run in online advertising and spectrum auctions. Common mistake Strict and weak dominance behave differently. Removing weakly dominated strategies can remove equilibria and depends on the order of removal."],[46,"","Iterated elimination of dominated strategies","The IESDS algorithm, the slide 11 and Marketing War examples, theorems about IESDS and Nash equilibrium, and why weak elimination depends on order. · IESDS · iterated elimination · dominated strategies · common knowledge · Nash equilibrium · order independence · rationalizability Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 5: Dominance and iterated elimination›IESDS Step 5 · Topic 2 of 3 · Masters Use common knowledge of rationality to cross out dominated strategies round after round, solve the slide 11 game cell by cell, and learn the four theorems that make the method trustworthy. About 50 min4 lessons2 animations3 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why we may eliminate again and again 2Worked examples: the Marketing War and slide 11 3Theorems about IESDS 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[46,"why","Why we may eliminate again and again","Chapter 5.1 showed that a rational player never uses a strictly dominated strategy. Common knowledge of rationality lets us go further, one layer at a time. Layer Reasoning on the slide 11 game 0 Player 2 is rational, so she never plays column C (R beats it) 1 Player 1 knows player 2 is rational, so he ignores C. In the smaller game M and D become dominated 2 Player 2 knows player 1 knows she is rational, so she expects only row U. Now R is dominated k Each round of elimination uses one more layer of \"I know that you know that…\" IESDS, round kExam favourite DSik = {{ si ∈ Sik : some s′i ∈ Sik beats it against every s−i ∈ S−ik }} Compare only against strategies that still survive. UpdateExam favourite Sik+1 = Sik ∖ DSik Remove the elements. Stop when no player has a dominated strategy. Every profile of survivors is a prediction. Remember Two small slip ups on slide 9: the dominator must range over the current set Sᵢᵏ, and the update is Sᵢᵏ minus DSᵢᵏ, not minus a set that contains DSᵢᵏ. Slide 6 also says \"strictly\" where it means \"weakly\" in two places. The formulas are the correct part. Real world scenario: the blue eyed islanders Islanders must leave on the night they work out that their own eyes are blue. A visitor announces \"at least one of you has blue eyes\". Everyone already knew it, yet the announcement makes it common knowledge, and with k blue eyed people all leave on night k. Each night peels off one layer of \"knows that knows\", just like each round of IESDS. In \"guess two thirds of the average\" experiments, real people stop after one to three layers, which is why predictions needing many rounds are less reliable in practice. Lesson 2 of 4"],[46,"slide11","Worked examples: the Marketing War and slide 11","Marketing War (slide 10) Round 1: M ▷₁ L (8 > 6, 4 > 2, 1 > 0) and by symmetry M ▷₂ L. Delete row L and column L together. Round 2 on {{M, H}}: against M, 4 > 3, but against H, 1 < 2. No domination for either player. Survivors {{M, H}} × {{M, H}}: four profiles. IESDS says \"nobody goes low\" but no more Among the four, only (M, M) and (H, H) are self enforcing. Finding them needs Nash equilibrium. Slide 11 game L C R U 4, 3 5, 1 6, 2 M 2, 1 8, 4 3, 6 D 3, 0 9, 6 2, 8 Slide 11, cell by cell Round 1, player 1: U is best against L (4) and D is best against C (9), so neither can be dominated. M is beaten by U against L but wins against C, so nothing goes. Round 1, player 2: R = (2, 6, 8) against C = (1, 4, 6): 2 > 1, 6 > 4, 8 > 6, so R ▷₂ C. Delete C. One dominator is enough. Round 2, player 1 against L and R only: U = (4, 6) beats M = (2, 3) and D = (3, 2). Delete M and D. Round 3, player 2 with only row U: L gives 3, R gives 2. Delete R. IESDS equilibrium (U, L) with payoffs (4, 3). Order: C, then M and D, then R Neither player has a dominant strategy here, so the dominant strategy equilibrium says nothing, yet IESDS pins down one answer. Is it self enforcing? Yes: player 1 moving down column L gets 2 or 3, less than 4, and player 2 moving along row U gets 1 or 2, less than 3. Is it the best? No. (D, C) = (9, 6) Pareto dominates it, and the Pareto optimal profiles are (9, 6) and (2, 8). Solving each player's problem separately, holding the others fixed, need not maximize anything for the group. Lecture 5 practice problem E Round 1: Z is dominated by Y for firm A (6 > 1, 5 > 0, 2 > 0). Delete Z. Round 2: with rows X and Y, R is dominated by P for firm B (3 > 1, 7 > 0). Delete R. Round 3: with columns P and Q, X is dominated by Y (6 > 4, 5 > 3). Delete X. Round 4: in row Y, P gives 7 against Q's 6. IESDS gives (Y, P) = (6, 7) Lesson 3 of 4"],[46,"theorems","Theorems about IESDS","Theorem Statement 5 If a strict dominant strategy equilibrium exists, IESDS leaves exactly it after one round 6 Every Nash equilibrium survives IESDS 7 In a finite game, if IESDS leaves one profile, it is the unique Nash equilibrium 8 For strict dominance in finite games, the order of deletion does not change the survivors (Gilboa, Kalai and Zemel 1990) Nash equilibrium (preview)Exam favourite vi(si*, s*−i) ≥ vi(si, s*−i) for every i and every si No player gains by a unilateral deviation. The course defines it fully in later lectures. Proof of theorem 6: Nash equilibria are never deleted Suppose some part of a Nash equilibrium s* is deleted. Let round k be the first time, and sᵢ* the strategy deleted. Before round k nothing of s* was deleted, so s*₋ᵢ is still available in round k. Deletion means some s′ᵢ beats sᵢ* against every surviving s₋ᵢ, in particular against s*₋ᵢ. So vᵢ(s′ᵢ, s*₋ᵢ) > vᵢ(sᵢ*, s*₋ᵢ), and player i gains by deviating from s*. That contradicts s* being a Nash equilibrium."],[46,"theorems","With weak dominance, order matters","Order game L R T 1, 1 0, 0 M 1, 1 2, 1 B 0, 0 2, 1 Two orders, two answers M weakly dominates both T and B for player 1. Delete T first: on rows M and B, R = (1, 1) weakly beats L = (1, 0). Delete L. Payoff (2, 1). Delete B first: on rows T and M, L = (1, 1) weakly beats R = (0, 1). Delete R. Payoff (1, 1). Different orders give different predictions, one reason the course uses strict dominance Property IESDS Why Existence Holds A finite strict partial order always has an undominated element Uniqueness Fails Marketing War leaves four profiles, RPS deletes nothing Stronger than DSE Yes Finds any DSE in one round and solves slide 11, which has none Needs Common knowledge One more layer of \"knows that knows\" per round Pareto optimal Fails Slide 11 ends at (4, 3), dominated by (9, 6) Going deeper: Proof idea of theorem 8, order independence Removing the opponents' strategies only shrinks the list of s₋ᵢ to check, so a strategy strictly dominated in a game stays strictly dominated in every smaller game that keeps its dominator. If the dominator was deleted first, its own dominator takes over, by transitivity, and that chain ends at a survivor because the game is finite. So a deletion can be postponed but never lost, and every maximal sequence of deletions ends at the same sets. With weak dominance a tie can make the replacement fail, which is exactly what the order game shows. One idea, four depths Cross out the choices nobody sensible would make, then look again: with fewer options left, more choices become silly. Keep going until nothing more can be crossed out. IESDS deletes all strictly dominated strategies, compared only within the current game, round after round. Slide 11 ends at (U, L) = (4, 3). Strict IESDS is order independent, always leaves something, finds any DSE, and keeps every Nash equilibrium. Weak IESDS depends on order. With mixed strategy domination, IESDS gives the rationalizable strategies in two player games (Bernheim 1984, Pearce 1984). Rationalizability is exactly what common knowledge of rationality implies, a weaker prediction than Nash equilibrium. Level k and cognitive hierarchy models (Stahl and Wilson, Camerer, Ho and Chong 2004) replace common knowledge with a small number of reasoning steps and fit laboratory data better. In AI, iterated best response and double oracle methods for large games are computational relatives of iterated elimination. Lesson 4 of 4"],[46,"levels","From intuition to research","Iterated elimination keeps removing choices that no sensible player would make. The guessing game below shows the logic and how real people stop early. Real world scenario: guess two thirds of the average Everyone in a class writes a number from 0 to 100. The winner is closest to two thirds of the class average. No one should write more than 66.7, since two thirds of any average is at most that. If everyone knows this, no one should write more than 44.4, and so on. Work it out: the bound after k rounds of elimination Round 1: guesses above 100 × 2/3 = 66.67 are dominated. Round 2: above 100 × (2/3)² = 44.44. After k rounds the bound is 100 × (2/3)k. After 5 rounds: 100 × 32 ÷ 243 = 13.17, and in the limit 0 Going deeper: the analysis For strict dominance the order of elimination does not matter: every order reaches the same surviving set. Any Nash equilibrium survives the process, so if exactly one profile survives it is the unique equilibrium. For weak dominance neither statement holds in general, which is why exam questions specify strict elimination. Going deeper: the research view In real experiments (Nagel 1995) average guesses cluster near 33 and 22, one or two rounds of reasoning, not 0. Level-k and cognitive hierarchy models capture this limited depth, and researchers now use the same game to test how many reasoning steps large language model agents perform. Common mistake IESDS does not always leave one outcome. In many games, such as the restaurant game, nothing is dominated and every strategy survives."],[47,"","Dominance at work: Cournot, auctions and voting","Cournot solved by iterated elimination, truthful bidding in second price auctions, the median voter, and where weak dominance misleads. · Cournot · IESDS · Vickrey auction · first price auction · Hotelling · median voter · mechanism design Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 5: Dominance and iterated elimination›Applications Step 5 · Topic 3 of 3 · Masters Watch IESDS solve the Cournot duopoly by halving intervals, prove that truthful bidding is dominant in a second price auction, and see candidates move to the median voter. About 45 min4 lessons5 animations5 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1IESDS solves the Cournot duopoly 2Auctions: when truth telling is dominant 3Voting, prices and puzzles 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[47,"cournot","IESDS solves the Cournot duopoly","Cournot firms have no dominant quantity. Yet some quantities are strictly dominated, and IESDS solves the whole game. This was the professor's homework at the end of lecture 6. Profit with the rival fixed πi = (a − c − bqj) qi − b qi² An upside down parabola in qᵢ, with its peak at the best response. Best responseExam favourite BRi(qj) = max{{ 0, a − c − bqj2b }} With a = 100, b = 1, c = 10 this is max{0, (90 − qⱼ)/2}. Without calculus Kq − bq² = −b(q − K2b)² + K²4b Completing the square shows the peak at q = K/2b, where K = a − c − bqⱼ. The rounds of elimination Round 1: the peak is largest when the rival makes 0, at the monopoly quantity 45. Any q above 45 is beaten by 45 whatever the rival does. Survivors [0, 45]. Round 2: the rival makes at most 45, so every peak is at least BR(45) = 22.5. Any q below 22.5 is beaten by 22.5. Survivors [22.5, 45]. Round 3: the rival makes at least 22.5, so every peak is at most BR(22.5) = 33.75. Survivors [22.5, 33.75]. Round 4: BR(33.75) = 28.125. Survivors [28.125, 33.75]. The width halves each round. Limit q* = (a − c)/3b = 30, the only survivor IESDS in the Cournot duopoly: the interval halvesRound 1 deletes quantities above the monopoly amount. Each later round uses the best reply to the surviving extremes. The square shrinks to one point. Step 0 of 8 Demand intercept a100 Unit cost c (both firms)10 Demand slope b1.0 BR(q) = max{0, (a − c − b q) ⁄ 2b}. The monopoly amount 45 caps round 1. Each new end is BR of the old one, and BR(q) − q* = −½ (q − q*), so the distance to q* = (a − c) ⁄ 3b = 30 halves every round. Try this The class homework: a = 100, c = 10. Intervals [0, 45], [22.5, 45], [22.5, 33.75], … close in on 30. Practice E1: a = 130, c = 10. Five rounds give [37.5, 41.25] and the limit is 40. Double b. Every quantity halves but the shape of the process stays the same: each round halves the distance to the limit. Python # IESDS in the Cournot duopoly: the surviving interval [lo, hi] shrinks towards (a - c) / 3ba, b, c = 100, 1, 10def br(q):return max(0.0, (a - c - b * q) / (2 * b))lo, hi = 0.0, float(\"inf\")hi = br(0) # round 1: nothing above the monopoly quantityprint(\"round 1:\", [lo, hi])for k inrange(2, 9):if k % 2 == 0: lo = br(hi) # too small is now dominatedelse: hi = br(lo) # too large is now dominatedprint(f\"round {k}:\", [round(lo, 4), round(hi, 4)])print(\"limit (a - c) / 3b =\", (a - c) / (3 * b)) Output round 1: [0.0, 45.0] round 2: [22.5, 45.0] round 3: [22.5, 33.75] round 4: [28.125, 33.75] round 5: [28.125, 30.9375] round 6: [29.5312, 30.9375] round 7: [29.5312, 30.2344] round 8: [29.8828, 30.2344] limit (a - c) / 3b = 30.0 Practice E1: p = 130 − Q and c = 10 BR(q) = (120 − q)/2. Round 1: [0, 60]. Round 2: BR(60) = 30, so [30, 60]. Round 3: BR(30) = 45, so [30, 45]. Round 4: BR(45) = 37.5. Round 5: BR(37.5) = 41.25. Limit 120/3 = 40, price 130 − 80 = 50, profit 40 × 40 = 1,600 each Lesson 2 of 4"],[47,"auctions","Auctions: when truth telling is dominant","In a sealed bid auction each bidder privately values the object at v and submits a bid b. Let B be the highest rival bid. Second price (Vickrey)Exam favourite payoff = v − B if b > B, otherwise 0 The winner pays the second highest bid. Bidding b = v weakly dominates every other bid. First priceExam favourite payoff = v − b if b > B, otherwise 0 The winner pays her own bid. Overbidding is weakly dominated, but bidding v is not dominant. Second price, value v Rival bid B below v B between v and the overbid B above both Bid v win, v − B lose, 0 lose, 0 Overbid b > v win, v − B (same) win, v − B < 0 (worse) lose, 0 Why underbidding is never better in a second price auction If B is below the low bid, both bids win and pay B: same payoff. If B is between the low bid and v, the truthful bid wins v − B > 0 while the low bid loses. If B is above v, both lose. Never worse, sometimes strictly better: b = v is weakly dominant In a first price auction, winning with b = v gives v − v = 0. Shading the bid below v can win with a positive surplus, so truthful bidding is not dominant and bidders shade. No dominant strategy exists there, which is why first price auctions need the Nash equilibrium analysis later in the course. Real world scenario: online ad auctions Search engines sell ad slots in automated auctions millions of times a day. Early systems used a first price rule and bidders kept adjusting their bids against each other. The move to second price style rules was meant to make simple truthful bidding safe. Display advertising later moved back to first price with automated bid shading, which shows the trade offs are still debated. Lesson 3 of 4"],[47,"auctions","Voting, prices and puzzles",""],[47,"auctions","Practice D5: where should a candidate stand?","Two candidates choose a position 1 to 5 on a line. One voter sits at each position and votes for the nearer candidate, splitting ties. The payoff is the number of votes. IESDS in the location game Round 1: position 2 beats 1 against every rival position (4 > 2.5, 2.5 > 1, 2 > 1.5, 2.5 > 2, 3 > 2.5). By symmetry 4 beats 5. Delete 1 and 5. Round 2: against rivals in {{2, 3, 4}}, position 3 earns (3, 2.5, 3), beating both 2 and 4, which earn (2.5, 2, 2.5). Both candidates stand at 3, the median voter"],[47,"auctions","Practice D6: Bertrand with whole number prices","Prices 1 to 5, unit cost 1, 10 buyers Against a rival price of 1 every price earns 0, so no price beats another strictly in every column. Strict IESDS deletes nothing. Weak elimination: 2 weakly beats 1 and 4 weakly beats 5. Then 3 beats 4, then 2 beats 3. Weak IESDS ends at (2, 2), one step above cost But (1, 1), (2, 2) and (3, 3) are all self enforcing. Weak elimination threw two of them away."],[47,"auctions","Practice E4: below cost Bertrand prices","Pricing at cost c always earns 0. Pricing below cost earns a loss when you are cheapest or tied, and 0 when the rival is cheaper still. So pᵢ = c weakly but not strictly dominates pᵢ < c, and strict IESDS cannot start. The Bertrand prediction needs Nash equilibrium."],[47,"auctions","Puzzles F1 and F2","Puzzle Answer F1. Streetlamp with refunds when the lamp is not built Contributing ties with not contributing when nobody else pays, wins with one other payer, and loses with two. Still no dominance. Refunds remove the risk but not the free riding. F2. Two players guess an integer in [0, 100]. Closer to two thirds of the average wins The lower guess always wins, so 0 weakly dominates every other guess, but not strictly: if the rival guesses above you, both 0 and your guess win. Going deeper: Mechanism design in one line The Vickrey auction and the subsidy in the Marketing War (chapter 5.2) share an idea: instead of predicting play in a given game, change the rules so that the outcome you want becomes dominant. That reverse question, choosing a game to get a desired equilibrium, is mechanism design, and the Gibbard Satterthwaite theorem marks where it runs out for voting. One idea, four depths Even when no single choice is always best, some choices are always bad. Removing them again and again can lead to one clear answer, as with two shops on a street both moving to the middle. Cournot IESDS halves the surviving interval each round to (a − c)/3b. In a second price auction bidding your value is weakly dominant. The location game ends at the median. Weak elimination can delete self enforcing outcomes, as in discrete Bertrand. For Cournot with more than two firms, IESDS no longer pins down a single quantity, while the Nash equilibrium is still unique. The revenue equivalence theorem shows first and second price auctions give the same expected revenue with independent private values. Generalized second price auctions for search ads are not truthful, which led to work on their equilibria (Edelman, Ostrovsky and Schwarz 2007, Varian 2007). Learning agents bidding repeatedly in first price auctions are now studied as online learning problems with regret guarantees. Lesson 4 of 4"],[47,"levels","From intuition to research","Dominance shows up wherever rules can be designed. The second price auction is the cleanest case, and it is worth checking by hand. Real world scenario: a sealed bid auction for a used bicycle Each bidder writes a bid in an envelope. The highest bidder wins but pays the second highest bid. Your value for the bicycle is 70. Should you write 70, or try to be clever? Work it out: why bidding your value is safe Others’ highest bid 60: bid 70, win and pay 60, gain 10. Bidding 50 loses, gain 0. Others’ highest bid 80: bid 70, lose, gain 0. Bidding 90 wins but pays 80, gain −10. Your bid only decides whether you win, never the price you pay. Bidding your true value is weakly dominant Going deeper: the analysis Proof sketch: let b be the highest other bid. If b < v, any bid above b wins and pays b, and bidding v does so. If b > v, winning means paying more than v, and bidding v avoids it. If b = v you get 0 either way. In no case does another bid do strictly better, and in some cases it does strictly worse. Going deeper: the research view Search engines first ran generalised second price auctions for ad slots, which are not truthful, and much research compares them with the truthful VCG auction. Revenue equivalence says that under standard assumptions first price, second price and many other auctions earn the same expected revenue. Common mistake In a second price auction you do not pay your own bid. Students who forget this conclude that shading the bid is wise."],[48,"","Quiz 1 solved: all four questions","Worked answers to Quiz 1: B, A, A and B under the standard reading, and (K, 0, 0). · Quiz 1 · solutions · Cournot · lottery · consumption · marginal value · CSL 7580 Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 6: Quiz and exam revision›Quiz 1 solved Step 6 · Topic 1 of 2 · All levels Every question of Advanced AI Quiz 1 with the full working, an animation to test each answer, and the traps that cost marks. About 45 min4 lessons4 animations9 formulas10 worked problems10 quiz questionsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Question 1: Cournot with a curved demand 2Question 2: bananas and candy 3Question 3: buying a lottery ticket 4Question 4: consumption with a convex payoff Lesson 1 of 4"],[48,"q1","Question 1: Cournot with a curved demand","The question (10 points). Market demand is p(q) = a/(q + 1) with a > 0. Two firms each have cost C(q) = cq with c > 0, and neither may choose qᵢ = 0. S1: if the firms choose quantities simultaneously to maximize profit, in the Nash equilibrium each firm produces (a − c)/3 and the price is a ÷ (2(a − c)/3 + 1). S2: if the firms collude as a monopoly with joint cost C*(q) = 2cq, no total quantity maximizes total profit. Options: A both true, B both false, C only S1 true, D only S2 true. Profit of firm i πi = a qiqi + qj + 1 − c qi Price times own quantity, minus cost. First order conditionExam favourite a(qj + 1)(qi + qj + 1)² = c The derivative of a qᵢ/(qᵢ + qⱼ + 1) is a(qⱼ + 1)/(Q + 1)². Symmetric equilibriumExam favourite q* = (a − 4c) + √a² + 8ac8c From a(q + 1) = c(2q + 1)², a quadratic 4cq² + (4c − a)q + (c − a) = 0. Joint monopolyExam favourite Q* = √a2c − 1 From a/(Q + 1)² = 2c. Positive exactly when a > 2c. Statement S1 Set qᵢ = qⱼ = q in the first order condition: a(q + 1) = c(2q + 1)². Expand: 4cq² + (4c − a)q + (c − a) = 0. The positive root is q* = ((a − 4c) + √(a² + 8ac)) ÷ 8c. Try a = 10, c = 1: q* = (6 + √180) ÷ 8 = 2.4271, while (a − c)/3 = 3. Check directly: if the rival makes 3, producing 2.5 earns more than producing 3, so (3, 3) is not an equilibrium. S1 is false. The formula (a − c)/3b belongs to linear demand a − bq, not to a/(q + 1) Statement S2 Joint profit Π(Q) = aQ/(Q + 1) − 2cQ for Q > 0. Π′(Q) = a/(Q + 1)² − 2c = 0 gives Q* = √(a/2c) − 1, and Π″ = −2a/(Q + 1)³ < 0, so it is a maximum. Q* > 0 whenever a > 2c. With a = 10, c = 1, Q* = 1.236. Only when a ≤ 2c does profit fall from the start, so that no positive Q is best, since Q = 0 is ruled out. S2 claims no maximizer in general, which is false. Answer B: both statements are false Quiz 1, question 1: Cournot with demand p = a ⁄ (q + 1)Left: both reaction curves and the true equilibrium against the claimed (a − c) ⁄ 3. Right: joint profit of a monopoly with cost 2cQ. a10.0 c1.0 Equilibrium from a(q + 1) = c(2q + 1)²: q* = 2.4271 each, against the claimed 3. The monopoly has a best total Q = √(a ⁄ 2c) − 1 = 1.2361, so S2 fails. Both statements are false: option B. Try this a = 10, c = 1. The curves cross at about 2.427, not at (a − c) ⁄ 3 = 3. Statement S1 is false. The monopoly curve with a = 10, c = 1 peaks at Q = √(a ⁄ 2c) − 1 = 1.236. A maximum exists, so S2 is false here. Make a ≤ 2c, for example a = 1.5, c = 1. Joint profit falls from the start, and since Q = 0 is not allowed, no best quantity exists. S2 is true only in this case. Python # Quiz 1, question 1: symmetric Cournot equilibrium with p = a / (q + 1) and cost c qimport matha, c = 10, 1q = ((a - 4 * c) + math.sqrt(a * a + 8 * a * c)) / (8 * c) # root of 4c q^2 + (4c - a) q + (c - a) = 0print(\"equilibrium q per firm:\", round(q, 4), \" claimed (a - c) / 3:\", round((a - c) / 3, 4))print(\"check a(q + 1) = c(2q + 1)^2:\", round(a * (q + 1), 4), round(c * (2 * q + 1) ** 2, 4))print(\"price:\", round(a / (2 * q + 1), 4))# a firm's best reply to q = 3 is not 3, so (3, 3) cannot be an equilibriumprofit = lambda qi, qj: a * qi / (qi + qj + 1) - c * qiprint(\"profit at 3 vs 2.5 when the rival plays 3:\", round(profit(3, 3), 4), round(profit(2.5, 3), 4))Q = math.sqrt(a / (2 * c)) - 1# joint monopoly with cost 2cQprint(\"monopoly quantity:\", round(Q, 4), \" exists because a > 2c:\", a > 2 * c) Output equilibrium q per firm: 2.4271 claimed (a - c) / 3: 3.0 check a(q + 1) = c(2q + 1)^2: 34.2705 34.2705 price: 1.7082 profit at 3 vs 2.5 when the rival plays 3: 1.2857 1.3462 monopoly quantity: 1.2361 exists because a > 2c: True In the exam The trap in S1 is pattern matching. (a − c)/3 is the textbook Cournot answer, but it comes from demand a − bq. With a different demand curve you must redo the first order condition. Lesson 2 of 4"],[48,"q2","Question 2: bananas and candy","The question (10 points). A banana costs $0.50 and a candy $0.25. You have $1.25 and you value money. The first banana is worth $1.20 to you and each further one half the previous ($0.60, $0.30, …). The first candy is worth $0.40, then $0.20, $0.10, … The two values do not affect each other. Should you spend all your money? Options: A yes, B no, C not enough information. Buy one more unit whenExam favourite marginal value ≥ price Because you value money, a unit is worth buying only if it is worth more to you than what it costs. Unit Marginal value Price Worth buying? Banana 1 $1.20 $0.50 Yes, +0.70 Banana 2 $0.60 $0.50 Yes, +0.10 Banana 3 $0.30 $0.50 No, −0.20 Candy 1 $0.40 $0.25 Yes, +0.15 Candy 2 $0.20 $0.25 No, −0.05 Solve it Every unit worth more than its price: two bananas and one candy. Cost: 2 × 0.50 + 0.25 = $1.25, exactly your cash. Value: 1.20 + 0.60 + 0.40 = $2.20. Net gain = 2.20 − 1.25 = $0.95, the best of all affordable baskets The best basket uses all the money, so the answer is A: yes, spend it all. Remember The answer depends on the numbers, not on a rule like \"always spend\" or \"never spend\". With $3 you would still stop at $1.25, because the third banana and the second candy are worth less than they cost. Lesson 3 of 4"],[48,"q3","Question 3: buying a lottery ticket","The question (10 points). A decision maker with u(w) = √w and wealth ₹400 is offered a lottery that pays ₹900 with probability 0.4 and ₹10 with probability 0.6, sold at ₹p. Options: A she will definitely buy if p < 18, B she will definitely buy if p = 18, C she will definitely buy if p > 18, D she will never buy. Buy whenExam favourite 0.4 √400 − p + 900 + 0.6 √400 − p + 10 ≥ √400 = 20 Expected utility of final wealth after paying p and receiving the prize, against keeping ₹400. Break even priceExam favourite p* ≈ 267.99 Found by bisection. Below p* she buys, above it she does not. Solve it At p = 18: 0.4 × √1282 + 0.6 × √392 = 0.4 × 35.805 + 0.6 × 19.799 = 14.322 + 11.879 = 26.20. 26.20 > 20, so she buys at 18. The left side falls as p rises, so she buys at every p < 18 too. The left side reaches 20 at about p* = 267.99. At p = 300 she refuses, so C fails, and D fails as well. A is true and B is true under this reading Common mistake This question has two true options under the standard reading (prizes added to wealth after paying p). If only one option may be marked, A is the safer choice because it covers every price below 18, and B is a special case. It is worth asking the instructor whether the question allows more than one answer. Going deeper: Other readings and why they do not change the verdict If wealth is ignored, the lottery alone has E[√X] = 0.4 × 30 + 0.6 × 3.162 = 13.90, a certainty equivalent of about ₹193, still far above 18. If she were risk neutral, the break even price would be the expected prize, 0.4 × 900 + 0.6 × 10 = ₹366. Under every sensible reading the price 18 is well below the break even price, so A and B hold and C and D fail. Lesson 4 of 4"],[48,"q4","Question 4: consumption with a convex payoff","The question (10 points). A decision maker lives for 3 periods and splits ₹K among them. Each period's payoff is u(xₜ) = xₜ², discounted by δ = ½, so v(x₁, x₂, x₃) = x₁² + ½x₂² + ¼x₃² with 0 ≤ xₜ ≤ K and x₁ + x₂ + x₃ = K. Find the optimal plan. Objective v = x₁² + ½ x₂² + ¼ x₃² A convex function on the triangle x₁ + x₂ + x₃ = K. AnswerExam favourite (x₁, x₂, x₃) = (K, 0, 0), v = K² Spend everything in period 1. Solve it u = x² has u″ = 2 > 0, so v is convex. Setting the derivatives to zero finds a minimum, not a maximum. A convex function on a triangle is maximized at a corner: (K, 0, 0), (0, K, 0) or (0, 0, K). The corner values are K², ½K² and ¼K². Best: (K, 0, 0) with total payoff K² Why not spread? Moving ε from period 1 to period 2 changes v by −2Kε + ε² + ½ε², which is negative for small ε. What the Lagrange method would have given Interior first order conditions: 2x₁ = λ, x₂ = λ, ½x₃ = λ, so x₁ : x₂ : x₃ = 1 : 2 : 4. With K = 7 that is (1, 2, 4) and v = 1 + 2 + 4 = 7. The corner gives K² = 49. The stationary point is the minimum, as in lecture 4's x² example One idea, four depths Read the question carefully, do not trust a formula you remember from a different setting, and check the answer with numbers. Q1: redo the first order conditions for the new demand. Q2: buy while marginal value ≥ price. Q3: compare expected utility of final wealth with the sure wealth. Q4: convex payoffs go to a corner. Each question tests the second order condition in disguise: concavity guarantees that first order conditions find a maximum, and its failure (Q4) or the boundary (Q1, S2 with a ≤ 2c) changes the answer. Existence of Cournot equilibria with general demand needs conditions such as decreasing marginal revenue (Novshek 1985). Convex intertemporal objectives appear in models of lumpy consumption and in risk seeking reinforcement learning, where policies collapse to extreme actions."],[49,"","Exam revision: from school to research universities","Exam style questions from school level to research universities, with worked answers, a formula sheet and a mock quiz. · exam · revision · formula sheet · mock quiz · numericals · proofs · IIT · NPTEL · GATE · MIT · Cambridge Brainy BytesAdvanced AI learning path Advanced AI›Advanced AI›Step 6: Quiz and exam revision›Exam revision Step 6 · Topic 2 of 2 · All levels Practise the question shapes of every level with worked answers: expected values and money pumps, trees, lotteries and Cournot by hand, quick multiple choice checks, and proofs with their assumptions. About 60 min6 lessons5 animations3 formulas13 worked problems12 quiz questionsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How Advanced AI is examined 2Tier 1: school and first year 3Tier 2: university semester and IIT course exams 4Tier 3: multiple choice and numerical answer papers 5Tier 4: proofs, derivations and critique 6Formula sheet and last minute checks Lesson 1 of 6"],[49,"map","How Advanced AI is examined","This chapter is for the week before an exam. It does not teach new material. It shows the shapes questions take at each level, with worked answers, and points back to the chapter where each idea is taught. The questions are original practice questions written in the style of each tier, not copies of real past papers. Tier Typical format What earns the marks Where to practise School and first year Short answers, expected values, simple tables A clear definition with an example, careful arithmetic Lesson 2 University semester and IIT course exams Numericals by hand on trees, lotteries and games Formula first, every step shown, the answer stated with its meaning Lesson 3 NPTEL and GATE style papers Multiple choice, multiple select and numerical answer type Speed, spotting the trap option, exact rounding Lesson 4 MIT and Cambridge style Proofs, derivations and critique of a model Assumptions named, a complete argument, when it breaks Lesson 5"],[49,"map","The twelve ideas examined most often","Completeness and transitivity, and why a cycle allows a money pump (chapter 1.1). Ordinal utility and its existence on a finite set (chapter 1.2). Choosing the best action, with constraints (chapter 1.3). Lotteries, CDFs and first order stochastic dominance (chapter 2.1). Expected utility, compound lotteries and affine invariance (chapter 2.2). Risk attitudes, certainty equivalent and risk premium (chapter 2.3). Backward induction on a decision tree (chapter 3.1). Discounting and the consumption problem (chapter 3.2). The value of information (chapter 3.3). Normal form games, best responses and Pareto optimality (chapters 4.1 to 4.3). Strict and weak dominance, and the dominant strategy equilibrium (chapter 5.1). IESDS and its order independence, Cournot by elimination (chapters 5.2 and 5.3). Lesson 2 of 6"],[49,"school","Tier 1: school and first year","Questions at this level check that you can read a preference, compute an average over chances and spot a choice that is always better. Expected valueExam favourite E[X] = Σ pixi Weight each prize by its chance and add. Present valueExam favourite PV = Σ δtxt A payment t periods away is worth δt of its face value today. Strict orderings n! The number of ways to rank n outcomes with no ties. Question 1. Riya prefers tea to coffee and coffee to juice, but juice to tea. Is she rational in the sense of this course? Rational preferences must be complete and transitive. Transitivity needs tea preferred to juice, but she says juice is preferred to tea. So she is not rational. A trader could sell her each swap for a small fee and take her money round the cycle, the money pump of chapter 1.1. Question 2. A raffle ticket wins 100 rupees with probability 0.3 and nothing otherwise. What is its expected value? E = 0.3 × 100 + 0.7 × 0. = 30 rupees. A risk neutral buyer would pay up to 30 for it. A risk averse buyer would pay less. Question 3. In how many ways can you rank 4 films with no ties? The first place has 4 choices, then 3, then 2, then 1. 4 × 3 × 2 × 1. = 24 strict orderings. Question 4. Two players each pick Quiet or Confess. Confessing is better for you whatever the other does. Why do both confess? Confess strictly dominates Quiet for each player. A rational player never plays a strictly dominated strategy. So (Confess, Confess) is played, even though (Quiet, Quiet) is better for both. That gap is the lesson of the Prisoner's Dilemma. Remember Try the presets stag and pennies in the game above. Stag has two equilibria and pennies has none in pure strategies. Lesson 3 of 6"],[49,"semester","Tier 2: university semester and IIT course exams","Here the examiner gives a tree, a lottery or a game and asks you to work it by hand. State the formula, then show each step. Question 5. With u(w) = √w, find the certainty equivalent and risk premium of a lottery paying 100 or 0 with equal chance. EU = 0.5 × √100 + 0.5 × √0 = 5. CE solves √CE = 5, so CE = 25. EV = 50, so the risk premium is 50 − 25 = 25. The person would swap the lottery for a sure 25. Concave utility means risk aversion. Question 6. Two firms face p = 100 − (q₁ + q₂) with unit cost 10. Find the Cournot equilibrium. Firm 1 maximises (100 − q₁ − q₂ − 10) q₁. The first order condition is 90 − 2q₁ − q₂ = 0. Best response q₁ = (90 − q₂) ÷ 2, and the same for firm 2. By symmetry q = (90 − q) ÷ 2, so q* = 30 each. Price 100 − 60 = 40, profit (40 − 10) × 30 = 900 each. Question 7. Action A pays 10 if the market is good and 0 if bad. Action B pays 4 for sure. The market is good with probability 0.6. What is the value of perfect information? Without information: E(A) = 6, E(B) = 4, so pick A for 6. With information: if good pick A for 10, if bad pick B for 4. Expected value with information = 0.6 × 10 + 0.4 × 4 = 7.6. VOI = 7.6 − 6 = 1.6. Never pay more than 1.6 for the forecast. Question 8. Split K = 100 over two periods to maximise ln x₁ + δ ln x₂ with δ = 0.9. The first order conditions give 1 ÷ x₁ = δ ÷ x₂, so x₂ = δ x₁. Budget x₁ + δ x₁ = 100, so x₁ = 100 ÷ 1.9. x₁ = 52.63, x₂ = 47.37 A more patient person (δ closer to 1) spreads consumption more evenly. Lesson 4 of 6"],[49,"mcq","Tier 3: multiple choice and numerical answer papers","In these papers there is no partial credit and often a penalty for a wrong option. Learn the quick checks below and use them to rule options out before you compute. Quick check Use it to A strictly dominant strategy equilibrium, if it exists, is unique Reject any option listing two Affine change a u + b with a > 0 keeps every expected utility ranking Reject options where a rescaled utility flips a choice Concave u means CE < EV and a positive risk premium Spot a risk averse answer at a glance VOI is never negative Reject a negative VOI at once IESDS order does not change the result, iterated weak dominance can Answer order questions in one line Linear Cournot q* = (a − c) ÷ 3b, Bertrand price = c Compute in seconds Question 9 (numerical answer). A payment of 100 arrives in 2 years with δ = 0.9. Its present value is PV = δ² × 100. = 0.81 × 100. = 81. Question 10 (multiple select). Which are true for u(w) = w²? (a) risk loving (b) CE > EV (c) risk premium < 0 (d) concave u is convex, so the person is risk loving. (a) is true. For a convex u, Jensen gives u(EV) ≤ EU, so CE ≥ EV. (b) is true for a lottery with real risk. Risk premium = EV − CE < 0. (c) is true. (d) is false. Answer: a, b, c. Question 11 (numerical answer). A player has 3 decision nodes with 2 actions at each. How many pure strategies? A strategy names one action at every node, even nodes it never reaches. 2 × 2 × 2. = 8. The R&D tree of chapter 3.1 had 2 nodes and 4 strategies, MM, MD, DM and DD. Question 12 (numerical answer). A lottery pays 2k with probability 2−k for k = 1 to 10. Its expected value is Each term is 2−k × 2k = 1. Ten terms. = 10. Without the cap the sum grows without bound, the St Petersburg paradox. In the exam Round only at the very end. A numerical answer type key often has a narrow range such as 52.6 to 52.7. Lesson 5 of 6"],[49,"mcq","Tier 4: proofs, derivations and critique","Exams in the style of MIT and Cambridge reward a complete argument more than a final number. State the assumptions, prove the claim, then say when it fails."],[49,"mcq","Question 13. Show that every complete and transitive preference on a finite set has a utility function.","Model answer For each outcome x, let u(x) be the number of outcomes y with x ⪰ y. If x ⪰ z then, by transitivity, every y below z is also below x, so u(x) ≥ u(z). If x ≻ z then z ⪰ x fails, so x is counted for x but not for z, and u(x) > u(z). Completeness makes sure every pair is compared, so u represents ⪰. It fails on some infinite sets, for example lexicographic preferences on the plane have no real valued utility."],[49,"mcq","Question 14. Show that a concave utility gives a positive risk premium.","Model answer Jensen: for concave u, E[u(X)] ≤ u(E[X]). The certainty equivalent satisfies u(CE) = E[u(X)] ≤ u(E[X]). u is increasing, so CE ≤ E[X], and the risk premium E[X] − CE ≥ 0. Equality holds only for a lottery with no real risk or a linear u."],[49,"mcq","Question 15. Show that expected utility rankings survive a positive affine change.","Model answer Let v = a u + b with a > 0. E[v] = a E[u] + b because expectation is linear. So E[u(L₁)] ≥ E[u(L₂)] if and only if E[v(L₁)] ≥ E[v(L₂)]. A non affine change such as u² can flip the ranking, which is why vNM utility is cardinal (chapter 2.2)."],[49,"mcq","Question 16. Show that a strictly dominant strategy equilibrium is unique.","Model answer Suppose s and t are both strictly dominant for player i and s ≠ t. Since s strictly dominates every other strategy, ui(s, x) > ui(t, x) for every x. Since t is strictly dominant, ui(t, x) > ui(s, x). Both cannot hold. So each player has at most one, and the equilibrium is unique. Weak dominance does not give this."],[49,"mcq","Critique questions","A critique question gives a model and asks what is wrong with it. For example, \"a firm uses expected value to price insurance\". The answer names the missing piece: the buyer is risk averse, so the willingness to pay exceeds the expected loss by the risk premium. Another favourite: \"the players will reach (Quiet, Quiet) because it is Pareto better\". The answer: Pareto optimality says nothing about what rational players choose. Each player still gains by deviating. In the exam In proofs, name each assumption when you use it: completeness, transitivity, the vNM axioms, common knowledge of rationality. Examiners often ask what breaks when one fails. One idea, four depths At school you read preferences, compute expected values and spot a choice that is always better. At university you solve trees, lotteries and games by hand, step by step, and explain what the number means. In competitive papers you must recognise the right formula fast, use quick checks to drop options and round only at the end. At research universities you prove existence of utility, Jensen based risk results, affine invariance and the uniqueness and order independence results for dominance. Lesson 6 of 6"],[49,"sheet","Formula sheet and last minute checks","Topic Formula Rational preference complete and transitive Utility representation x ⪰ y if and only if u(x) ≥ u(y) CDF F(x) = P(X ≤ x), non decreasing, from 0 to 1 First order stochastic dominance FA(x) ≤ FB(x) for all x Expected utility E[u] = Σ pi u(xi) Certainty equivalent, risk premium u(CE) = E[u], RP = E[X] − CE Present value PV = Σ δt xt, perpetuity δ ÷ (1 − δ) per unit from next period ln consumption, two periods x₁ = K ÷ (1 + δ), x₂ = δ K ÷ (1 + δ) Value of information VOI = E[max with information] − max E[without] Linear Cournot duopoly q* = (a − c) ÷ 3b, monopoly Q = (a − c) ÷ 2b Bertrand with equal cost p* = c Pareto optimal no other profile makes someone better and nobody worse"],[49,"sheet","Ten mistakes that cost marks","Calling a preference rational without checking transitivity. Treating ordinal utility as cardinal, for example adding utilities of different people. Computing expected value when the question asks for expected utility. Reporting expected utility as the certainty equivalent without applying u inverse. Forgetting chance nodes when folding back a tree. Listing a strategy that skips nodes off the path. Discounting the first payment when it is received today. Reporting a negative value of information. Confusing a Pareto optimal profile with an equilibrium. Deleting weakly dominated strategies when the question asks for strict IESDS."],[50,"","Cheatsheet","Every formula, key idea, table, memory aid and common mistake from both sections on one page. Core AI runs from agents and BFS to A*, local search, CSPs, minimax and alpha beta, logic and resolution, planning, Bayesian networks, causality and reinforcement learning. Advanced AI runs from preferences and utility to expected utility, risk, discounting, games, dominance and IESDS. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · formula sheet · revision · PDF · download · quick reference · A* · admissible heuristic · alpha beta · arc consistency · resolution · unification · STRIPS · Bayes rule · d-separation · Bellman equation · Q learning · utility · expected utility · IESDS"],[50,"step-core-1","Step 1Core AI · Intelligence, agents and problems","1.1"],[50,"what-is-ai","What AI is and intelligent agents","Open chapter The four approaches to AI, rational agents, PEAS, environment types, the social machine and the five kinds of agent program. Key ideas Definition AI builds agents that perceive and act. This course uses the acting rationally view: choose the action with the best expected performance. PEAS Describe any task by Performance measure, Environment, Actuators, Sensors. Environment types Fully or partially observable, deterministic or stochastic, episodic or sequential, static or dynamic, discrete or continuous, single or multi agent. Agent programs Simple reflex, model based, goal based, utility based, learning. Table driven needs ∑ |P|t rows, so it is impossible. Rational, not perfect Rationality depends on the performance measure, prior knowledge, available actions and percepts so far. Bad luck is not irrational. Social machine Needs etiquette, intuitive psychology, common sense, intuitive physics and layers from instinctive to self conscious (Minsky, Society of Mind). Formulas Agent functionExam favourite f : P* → A Maps every percept sequence (everything the agent has sensed so far, P*) to an action in the set A. It is the mathematical description of behaviour. Lesson: Agents, environments and PEAS Agent program agent = architecture + program The program runs on the physical machine (the architecture) and computes the agent function one percept at a time. Lesson: Agents, environments and PEAS Rational agentExam favourite choose a* = argmaxa E[performance | percepts so far, a] For every percept sequence, pick the action that is expected to give the best performance measure, given what the agent knows. Lesson: Agents, environments and PEAS Performance over a lifetime U = ∑t=1T rt Add up the score rt earned at each time step t. In the vacuum world rt = number of clean squares (minus 1 per move if moving costs energy). Lesson: The maths behind a rational agent Expected performance of an actionExam favourite E[U | a] = ∑s′ P(s′ | a) U(s′) Weight each possible result s′ by its probability. A rational agent picks the action with the largest value. Lesson: The maths behind a rational agent Size of a table driven agentExam favourite rows = ∑t=1T |P|t = |P|(|P|T − 1) ⁄ (|P| − 1) |P| is the number of different percepts, T the lifetime. A geometric series, so it grows exponentially in T. Lesson: The maths behind a rational agent Tables Table: · like a human · rationally (the right thing) Like a human Rationally (the right thing) Thinking Thinking humanly: model how people think (cognitive science, brain studies). Thinking rationally: follow correct rules of reasoning (logic, the \"laws of thought\"). Acting Acting humanly: behave so that people cannot tell it is a machine (the Turing test). Acting rationally: choose the action expected to do best (the rational agent approach, used in this course). Lesson: What intelligence and AI mean Table: skill · what it means in plain words · example in the scene Skill What it means in plain words Example in the scene Social etiquette Knowing polite and expected behaviour Waiting for the person to finish speaking Intuitive psychology Sensing that someone is in trouble or worried, and wanting to help Noticing the person is struggling and offering a hand Awareness of its own limits (proprioception) Knowing what its own body can and cannot do Not trying to lift something too heavy Common sense Everyday facts everyone knows Glass breaks if dropped Domain knowledge Expert facts about the task How a machine is repaired Intuitive physics A feel for how objects move and fall A ball rolls down a slope Lesson: A social machine: the man machine symbiosis scene Table: agent · performance · environment · actuators Agent Performance Environment Actuators Sensors Self driving taxi Safe, fast, legal, comfortable, profit Roads, traffic, people, weather Steering, brake, accelerator, horn, display Cameras, radar, GPS, speedometer Medical diagnosis Healthy patient, low cost Patient, hospital, staff Questions,"],[50,"what-is-ai","What AI is and intelligent agents","tests, treatments Symptoms, test results, answers Spam filter Spam caught, real mail kept Email stream, users Move to spam, deliver Words, sender, links Vacuum robot Clean squares over time, little energy Two squares A and B with dirt Left, Right, Suck, NoOp Location sensor, dirt sensor Lesson: Agents, environments and PEAS Table: question · easy answer · hard answer · example of the hard case Question Easy answer Hard answer Example of the hard case Can the agent see everything that matters? Fully observable Partially observable Poker: you cannot see the other cards Is the next state fixed by the action? Deterministic Stochastic (random) A dice game, a slippery floor Does each decision stand alone? Episodic Sequential Chess: a move now matters later Does the world change while the agent thinks? Static Dynamic Taxi driving Are states and times countable steps? Discrete Continuous Steering angle of a car How many agents? Single agent Multi agent (competitive or cooperative) Chess (competitive), football team (cooperative) Lesson: Kinds of environment and kinds of agent Table: task · observable · deterministic · episodic Task Observable Deterministic Episodic Static Discrete Agents Crossword puzzle Fully Yes Sequential Static Discrete Single Chess with a clock Fully Yes Sequential Semi Discrete Multi Taxi driving Partially Stochastic Sequential Dynamic Continuous Multi Image classification Fully Yes Episodic Static Continuous Single Lesson: Kinds of environment and kinds of agent Table: approach · strength · weakness · used today for Approach Strength Weakness Used today for Thinking humanly Explains human minds Hard to test, we do not fully know how people think Cognitive science, user models Thinking rationally Exact, provable logic Not everything is certain, and logic alone can be slow Theorem provers, verification Acting humanly Clear test (Turing test) Copies human mistakes too, says little about how Chatbots, games Acting rationally Measurable, general, works with uncertainty Needs a good performance measure Almost all modern AI systems Lesson: How the rational agent view compares Table: agent · memory · looks ahead? · good when Agent Memory Looks ahead? Good when Table driven Huge table No Never in practice, only as a thought experiment Simple reflex None No Fully observable, simple rules Model based Internal state No Partially observable worlds Goal based State + goal Yes, by search or planning Many steps are needed to reach a goal Utility based State + utility Yes, weighs trade offs Several goals, risk, uncertainty Learning Changes itself Depends on the base agent The world is unknown or changing Lesson: How the rational agent view compares Tips and traps Remember In class the four boxes were explained like this: thinking humanly is how we think, thinking rationally is making correct decisions in our head, acting humanly is doing what we think, and acting rationally is working it out so that it leads to a real, measurable result.Lesson: What intelligence and AI mean Common mistake A table driven agent stores one action for every possible percept sequence. With 4 percepts and a lifetime of T steps it needs 4 + 4² + ... + 4T rows. For a taxi camera this number is larger than the atoms in the universe. Agent programs exist to produce good behaviour from a small program instead of a huge table.Lesson: Kinds of environment and kinds of agent In the exam A rational agent is not one that is always right. It is one that chooses the best action given what it knows. Bad luck does not make a choice irrational.Lesson: From intuition to research 1.2"],[50,"search-problems","Problems as search","Open chapter States, actions, start and goal, nodes and the frontier, tree against graph search, and the four properties of search. Key ideas Search problem State space, actions with costs C(x, a, y) ≥ ε > 0, initial state, goal test (a condition). Heuristics are optional. Node STATE, PARENT, ACTION, PATH-COST g(n). Follow PARENT links to rebuild the path. Tree against graph search Tree search can loop on cycles. Graph search keeps an explored set and skips seen states. Four properties Completeness, optimality, time and space, measured with b, d and m. Full tree: (bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1) nodes. Water jugs 14 of 20 states reachable from (0, 0). (1, 2) unreachable. Getting 2 gallons in the 4 gallon jug takes 6 actions. Formulation Search cannot choose the states for you. A good abstraction can shrink the space enormously (8 queens: 1.8 × 10¹⁴ to 2,057). Formulas Successor functionExam favourite S(x) = {⟨y, a⟩ : action a takes x to y} All states one action away from x, each tagged with the action used. Two different actions may reach the same state. Lesson: Inputs, outputs and nodes of a search algorithm Action cost C(x, a, y) ≥ ε > 0 The cost of going from x to y with action a. C = ∞ if a does not lead from x to y. Every cost is at least a small positive ε. Lesson: Inputs, outputs and nodes of a search algorithm Path costExam favourite g(n) = ∑i C(si, ai, si+1) Add the costs along the path from the start to node n. Lesson: Inputs, outputs and nodes of a search algorithm Branching factor and depth b = most children of any node, d = depth of the shallowest goal, m = largest depth These three numbers measure every algorithm. Lesson: The selection rule and the four properties Nodes in a full treeExam favourite 1 + b + b² + ... + bd = (bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1) = O(bd) The number of nodes up to depth d. It grows exponentially with d. Lesson: The selection rule and the four properties Size of a state space |S| = product of the number of values of each part of the state Jugs: 4 values for gal3 (0 to 3) × 5 for gal4 (0 to 4) = 20 states, of which 14 are reachable from (0, 0). Lesson: The maths behind search problems 8 puzzle reachable statesExam favourite 9! ⁄ 2 = 181,440 Nine tiles (with the blank) in 9! orders, but only half can be reached from a given start, because a move never changes the parity of the arrangement. Lesson: The maths behind search problems Paths are much bigger than statesExam favourite tree nodes up to depth d ≈ bd while states may be few In a grid with 4 moves, a tree search to depth d makes 4d paths but only about 2d² distinct cells are within d steps. This is why graph search remembers explored states. Lesson: The maths behind search problems Tables Table: why search · limits of search Why search Limits of search Game playing programs based on search beat world champions. Search does not tell you how to formulate the problem. Choosing the states and actions is up to you. Many problems have no special algorithm. Casting them as search is often the easiest way. Search only solves a problem once it is correctly formulated. Local search gives good approximations for huge optimisation problems. Without good knowledge (heuristics) it can be very slow. Problem specific heuristics let search use extra knowledge, and planning can be cast as search. Lesson: Why search, and what a search problem is Table: part · meaning · romania trip · water jugs Part Meaning Romania trip Water jugs State space All configurations the problem can be in, kept as computer data The city you are in Pairs (gal3, gal4), gallons in each jug Actions (transitions) Moves from one state to another Drive to a neighbouring city Fill, empty, or pour one jug into the other Initial state and goal Where you start and the condition you want In Arad, goal: be in Bucharest Start (0, 0), goal e.g. (0, 2) or (*, 3) Heuristics Hints that guide the search Straight line distance to Bucharest How far each jug is from the target amount Lesson: The formalism: states, actions,"],[50,"search-problems","Problems as search","start, goal Table: property · question it answers Property Question it answers Completeness If a solution exists, will the algorithm always find one? Optimality Will it find the cheapest solution (when actions have costs)? Time complexity How many nodes does it generate before it finds a solution? Space complexity What is the largest number of nodes it must keep in memory? Lesson: The selection rule and the four properties Table: · tree search · graph search Tree search Graph search Remembers explored states? No Yes (explored set) Can loop for ever on cycles? Yes No, on a finite state space Memory Only the frontier Frontier plus every explored state Best when The space is a true tree, or memory is very tight Many paths lead to the same state (maps, puzzles) Lesson: How problem formulation choices compare Table: formulation · states · comment Formulation States Comment Add one queen at a time anywhere 64 × 63 × ... × 57 ≈ 1.8 × 10¹⁴ sequences Very wasteful Add one queen per column, in a row not attacked 2,057 states Same problem, tiny space Complete state: 8 queens, one per column, move a queen in its column 8⁸ ≈ 16.8 million states Used by local search (chapter 4.1, 5.3) Lesson: How problem formulation choices compare Tips and traps Remember The goal can be a condition, not one state. \"(*, 3)\" means any state where the 4 gallon jug holds 3, whatever the 3 gallon jug holds. The goal test is a function that returns true or false.Lesson: The formalism: states, actions, start, goal Common mistake Cycles can cause non termination. Arad → Sibiu → Arad → Sibiu ... is a valid path in tree search, so a search may never stop. Graph search fixes this by remembering explored states.Lesson: Inputs, outputs and nodes of a search algorithm In the exam Exam questions often ask you to formulate a problem. Always list all four parts: states, actions (with costs), initial state, goal test. Say how big the state space is.Lesson: From intuition to research"],[50,"step-core-2","Step 2Core AI · Uninformed search","2.1"],[50,"bfs-ucs","Breadth first and uniform cost search","Open chapter BFS with a FIFO queue, UCS with a priority queue on g(n), when each is optimal, their cost, and why UCS tests the goal late. Key ideas BFS FIFO queue. Complete (finite b). Fewest actions, optimal only for equal costs. Time and space O(bd). UCS Priority queue on g(n). Complete and optimal if every cost ≥ ε > 0. O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋). Goal test timing BFS: test at generation. UCS: test at expansion (Sibiu: 310 is replaced by 278). Romania BFS 450 via Fagaras. UCS 418 via Rimnicu Vilcea and Pitesti, after taking out 13 cities. Optimality proof Lemma 1: costs taken out never decrease. Lemma 2: cheaper paths come first. Lemma 3: first path to a state is cheapest. Memory b = 10, d = 10 needs about 10 TB. Memory runs out before time. Formulas Time of BFSExam favourite 1 + b + b² + ... + bd = O(bd) With the goal test when a node is generated. If you test only when a node is taken out, the last level is generated too and the time is O(bd+1). Lesson: Is BFS complete and optimal, and what does it cost Space of BFSExam favourite O(bd−1) explored + O(bd) frontier = O(bd) Every node stays in memory. Memory, not time, is the real limit of BFS. Lesson: Is BFS complete and optimal, and what does it cost Complete and optimal if every action cost C(x, a, y) ≥ ε > 0 With zero cost actions UCS can loop for ever on a zero cost cycle. Lesson: Uniform cost search Time and space of UCSExam favourite O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) C* is the cost of the optimal solution and ε the smallest action cost. The search may go about C*⁄ε levels deep. When all costs are equal this is bd+1. Lesson: Uniform cost search Geometric seriesExam favourite ∑i=0d bi = (bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1) ≤ bd · b ⁄ (b − 1) The last level holds most of the nodes. For b = 10 it is about 90 percent of them. Lesson: The maths: geometric series and the cost bound Fraction of nodes in the last level bd ⁄ ((bd+1 − 1) ⁄ (b − 1)) → (b − 1) ⁄ b For b = 2 half the nodes are in the last level. For b = 10, 90 percent. Lesson: The maths: geometric series and the cost bound UCS depth bound depth ≤ ⌊C* ⁄ ε⌋ A path of more than C*⁄ε actions costs more than C*, so UCS never needs to expand it before the goal. Lesson: The maths: geometric series and the cost bound Tables Table: step · open Step OPEN 0 ⟨(0,0)⟩ 1 ⟨(0,0),(3,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4)⟩ 2 ⟨(0,0),(0,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(3,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,3)⟩ 3 ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(3,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(3,0),(0,3)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4),(0,0)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4),(3,4)⟩, ⟨(0,0),(0,4),(3,1)⟩ Lesson: Uninformed search and breadth first search Table: depth d · nodes (b = 10) · time at 1 million nodes a second · memory at 1 kb a node Depth d Nodes (b = 10) Time at 1 million nodes a second Memory at 1 KB a node 2 110 0.11 ms 107 KB 4 11,110 11 ms 10.6 MB 6 10⁶ 1.1 s 1 GB 8 10⁸ 2 minutes 103 GB 10 10¹⁰ 3 hours 10 TB 12 10¹² 13 days 1 PB 14 10¹⁴ 3.5 years 99 PB Lesson: Is BFS complete and optimal, and what does it cost Table: · breadth first · uniform cost Breadth first Uniform cost Frontier FIFO queue Priority queue on g(n) Finds Fewest actions Lowest total cost Goal test At generation (saves a level) At expansion (needed for optimality) Complete Yes, if b is finite Yes, if every cost ≥ ε > 0 Time and space O(bd) O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) Romania answer 450 (3 roads) 418 (4 roads) Lesson: BFS against UCS against Dijkstra Table: · ucs · dijkstra UCS Dijkstra Stops When the goal is taken out After the cheapest path to every node is known Graph given Implicitly, by a successor function Usually explicitly, all nodes known Frontier starts with Only the start Often every node at ∞ Lesson: BFS against UCS against Dijkstra Tips and traps Common mistake Two lessons from the table. Memory runs out long before time does. And no amount of faster hardware saves you: each extra level multiplies the cost by b.Lesson: Is BFS complete and optimal, and what does it cost In the exam This is why UCS tests the goal when a path is taken OUT, not when it is generated. In the Sibiu example, Bucharest was"],[50,"bfs-ucs","Breadth first and uniform cost search","generated with cost 310 before the 278 path existed. Testing at generation would have returned the wrong answer.Lesson: Why uniform cost search is optimal: the three lemmas 2.2"],[50,"dfs-ids","Depth first, iterative deepening and bidirectional search","Open chapter DFS, DLS, IDS and bidirectional search, their memory and time, path checking, and the full comparison table. Key ideas DFS Stack. O(b m) space, O(bm) time. Not complete on infinite or cyclic spaces, not optimal. DLS DFS with depth limit D. Returns solution, failure, or cutoff. Complete only if d ≤ D. IDS DLS with D = 0, 1, 2, .... Complete, optimal for equal costs, O(bd) time, O(b d) space. IDS overhead ∑ (d + 1 − i) bi, about b ⁄ (b − 1) times BFS. b = 10, d = 5: 123,456 against 111,111. Path check Drop a child already on the current path. Removes cycles, keeps O(b m) memory. Bidirectional Search from both ends, O(bd⁄2). Needs reversible actions and one explicit goal. Formulas Nodes generated by IDSExam favourite N(IDS) = (d + 1)·1 + d·b + (d − 1)·b² + ... + 1·bd = ∑i=0d (d + 1 − i) bi Level i is generated in every iteration from i to d, that is d + 1 − i times. Lesson: Is iterative deepening wasteful Overhead of IDS over BFSExam favourite N(IDS) ⁄ N(BFS) ≈ b ⁄ (b − 1) For b = 10 that is 1.11, only 11 percent more work. For b = 2 it is 2 times. Lesson: Is iterative deepening wasteful Cost of IDS time O(bd), space O(b d) Complete when b is finite, and optimal when all actions cost the same. Lesson: Is iterative deepening wasteful Cost of bidirectional searchExam favourite 2 · bd⁄2 = O(bd⁄2) instead of O(bd) For b = 10 and d = 6 that is 2,000 nodes against 1,000,000. Lesson: Bidirectional search Tables Table: property · dfs · why Property DFS Why Complete No on infinite or cyclic spaces (tree search). Yes on finite spaces with graph search or a path check. It can follow an infinite branch for ever. Optimal No It returns the first goal it meets, which may be deep and costly. Time O(bm) m is the largest depth. It may explore the whole tree, and m can be much bigger than d. Space O(b m) Only one path plus the untried children of each node on it. Lesson: Depth first search Table: check · what it does · memory · what it misses Check What it does Memory What it misses Parent check Do not go straight back to the state you came from None Longer cycles like A → B → C → A Path check Do not add a child whose state is already on the current path O(m), the path is already stored Two different paths to the same state (redundant paths) Full cycle check (graph search) Do not add a child whose state was ever explored O(number of states) Nothing, but memory grows like BFS Lesson: Path checking and cycle checking Table: dls result · meaning DLS result Meaning A solution A goal was found at depth ≤ D Failure No solution exists at all (nothing was cut off) Cutoff No solution within the limit, but deeper nodes were cut off, so one may exist deeper Lesson: Depth limited and iterative deepening search Table: criterion · bfs · ucs · dfs Criterion BFS UCS DFS DLS IDS Bidirectional Complete Yes (finite b) Yes (costs ≥ ε) No No (only if d ≤ l) Yes (finite b) Yes (with BFS both ways) Optimal Equal costs only Yes No No Equal costs only Equal costs only Time O(bd) O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) O(bm) O(bl) O(bd) O(bd⁄2) Space O(bd) O(b1 + ⌊C*⁄ε⌋) O(b m) O(b l) O(b d) O(bd⁄2) Lesson: All five uninformed methods side by side Tips and traps Remember Backtracking search, a version of DFS that generates one child at a time and undoes changes when it goes back, needs only O(m) memory. It is the basis of CSP solvers (chapter 5.2) and Prolog.Lesson: Depth first search In the exam A path check makes DFS complete on any finite state space, while keeping memory at O(b m). It can still visit the same state many times along different paths, so the time may be much more than the number of states.Lesson: Path checking and cycle checking Remember IDS is the preferred uninformed method when the search space is large and the depth of the solution is not known (slide 49).Lesson: Depth limited and iterative deepening search Common mistake When the frontiers first meet, the joined path is not always the shortest. With uniform costs, finish the current level before stopping, then"],[50,"dfs-ids","Depth first, iterative deepening and bidirectional search","pick the best meeting point.Lesson: Bidirectional search"],[50,"dfs-ids","Step 3Core AI · Informed search","3.1"],[50,"greedy-astar","Greedy best first and A* search","Open chapter Greedy best first, A*, admissible and consistent heuristics, why A* is optimal, and weighted A*. Key ideas Greedy best first Order by h. Not optimal (Romania 450, slide 58 trap 110). Can loop in tree search. A* Order by f = g + h. Romania: Arad 366, Sibiu 393, RV 413, Fagaras 415, Pitesti 417, Bucharest 418. Admissible h(n) ≤ h*(n). Makes A* tree search optimal. Consistent h(n1) ≤ C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2). f never decreases, graph search optimal, implies admissible. Optimally efficient A* expands every node with f < C* and none with f > C*. No optimal method with the same h does better. Weighted A* f = g + w h, cost ≤ w C*. Formulas Evaluation function of A*Exam favourite f(n) = g(n) + h(n) g(n) is the cost of the path of node n, h(n) the estimate from n to a goal. With h = 0 A* is UCS. With g ignored it is greedy. Lesson: A* search: f(n) = g(n) + h(n) AdmissibleExam favourite h(n) ≤ h*(n) for every n h*(n) is the true cost of an optimal path from n to a goal (∞ if there is none). An admissible h never overestimates: it is optimistic. Also h(goal) = 0. Lesson: Admissible and consistent heuristics Consistent (monotone)Exam favourite h(n1) ≤ C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2) for every action a from n1 to n2 A triangle inequality: the estimate cannot drop by more than the cost of a step. It must hold for every action between n1 and n2. Lesson: Admissible and consistent heuristics Consistency implies admissibility (∀ n1, n2, a) h(n1) ≤ C(n1, a, n2) + h(n2) ⇒ (∀ n) h(n) ≤ h*(n) Proof by induction on the number of steps to the goal (below). The other direction is false. Lesson: Admissible and consistent heuristics What A* expands surely every n with f(n) < C*, maybe some with f(n) = C*, never any with f(n) > C* With a consistent h. Lesson: Is A* optimal, and how fast is it Optimally efficientExam favourite no other optimal algorithm using the same h expands fewer nodes (up to ties) Any algorithm that skips a node with f(n) < C* risks missing a cheaper solution. Lesson: Is A* optimal, and how fast is it Effective branching factor N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + ... + (b*)d If A* generates N nodes for a solution at depth d, b* measures how well h focuses the search. b* close to 1 is ideal. Lesson: Is A* optimal, and how fast is it Weighted A* f(n) = g(n) + w · h(n), w ≥ 1 w = 1 is A*. A larger w trusts h more, so the search is faster and greedier. Lesson: The maths: weighted A* and the cost of a bad h Bounded suboptimalityExam favourite cost found ≤ w · C* With an admissible h, weighted A* is at most w times worse than the optimum. w = 1.5 means at most 50 percent more cost. Lesson: The maths: weighted A* and the cost of a bad h Error and work if |h* − h| ≤ c for a constant c, the work is about bc, not bd Constant absolute error still costs exponential in c. Relative error ε = (h* − h) ⁄ h* gives about bε d. Lesson: The maths: weighted A* and the cost of a bad h Tables Table: property · greedy best first Property Greedy best first Complete No in tree search (Iasi, Neamt loop). Yes in graph search on a finite space. Optimal No (450 against 418, 110 against 30) Time and space O(bm) in the worst case, but often far less with a good h Lesson: Greedy best first search Table: · ucs · greedy best first · a* UCS Greedy best first A* Order by g(n) h(n) g(n) + h(n) Looks at The past only The future only Both Optimal Yes No Yes, with admissible h (tree) or consistent h (graph) Complete Yes (costs ≥ ε) Only in graph search on finite spaces Yes (costs ≥ ε, finite b) Romania, Arad to Bucharest 418, 13 cities taken out 450, 4 cities 418, 6 cities Slide 58 trap 30 110 30 Lesson: Greedy against UCS against A* Tips and traps In the exam Food for thought IV asks for necessary and sufficient conditions. For optimal A*: admissibility is sufficient for tree search, consistency is sufficient for graph search. Neither is necessary: a slightly overestimating h can still return the optimal path by luck, but then nothing is guaranteed.Lesson: Heuristics: knowing roughly"],[50,"greedy-astar","Greedy best first and A* search","where the goal is Common mistake The time of A* is still exponential in general, and the memory is its real weak point: it keeps every generated node. That is why chapter 3.3 builds IDA*, RBFS and SMA*.Lesson: Is A* optimal, and how fast is it 3.2"],[50,"heuristic-design","Designing and comparing heuristics","Open chapter 8 puzzle heuristics, relaxed problems, dominance, max, pattern databases and the effective branching factor. Key ideas 8 puzzle heuristics h1 misplaced tiles, h2 Manhattan distance. Textbook start: h1 = 8, h2 = 18, true cost 26. Relaxed problem Remove rules. The exact relaxed cost is admissible and consistent. Dominance h2 ≥ h1 and both admissible: A* with h2 expands no more nodes. Combining max of admissible heuristics is admissible and dominates each one. Effective branching factor N + 1 = 1 + b* + ... + (b*)d. Close to 1 is good. Pattern database Exact costs of a sub problem, looked up during search. Disjoint PDBs can be added. Formulas h1: misplaced tiles h1(n) = number of tiles not in their goal square Every misplaced tile needs at least one move. Lesson: Two heuristics for the 8 puzzle h2: Manhattan distanceExam favourite h2(n) = ∑tiles (|row − goal row| + |column − goal column|) Every tile needs at least as many moves as its distance in rows plus columns. Lesson: Two heuristics for the 8 puzzle DominanceExam favourite h2 dominates h1 if h2(n) ≥ h1(n) for every n, and both are admissible Then A* with h2 expands no more nodes than A* with h1 (apart from ties). Lesson: Dominance and combining heuristics Why A* surely expands every n with f(n) < C*, that is h(n) < C* − g(n) A node with h2(n) < C* − g(n) also has h1(n) < C* − g(n). So every node expanded with h2 is also expanded with h1. Lesson: Dominance and combining heuristics CombiningExam favourite h(n) = max(h1(n), ..., hk(n)) The max of admissible heuristics is admissible, and it dominates each of them. The max of consistent heuristics is consistent. Lesson: Dominance and combining heuristics Disjoint pattern databasesExam favourite h = PDB(tiles 1 to 4) + PDB(tiles 5 to 8) Adding two PDBs is admissible only if each counts only the moves of its own tiles. Then no move is counted twice. Lesson: Pattern databases and learned heuristics Size of a PDB for k pattern tiles plus the blank on 9 squares: 9! ⁄ (9 − k − 1)! entries For k = 4: 9 × 8 × 7 × 6 × 5 = 15,120 entries. Lesson: Pattern databases and learned heuristics Effective branching factor b*Exam favourite N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + ... + (b*)d N nodes generated for a solution at depth d. b* is the branching factor a uniform tree of depth d would need to hold N + 1 nodes. A good heuristic has b* close to 1. Lesson: The maths: effective branching factor Tables Table: real rule: a tile can move from square a to square b if · remove this part · heuristic you get Real rule: a tile can move from square A to square B if Remove this part Heuristic you get A is next to B and B is blank \"and B is blank\" h2: Manhattan distance A is next to B and B is blank \"A is next to B\" Gaschnig's heuristic: number of swaps with the blank A is next to B and B is blank both conditions h1: misplaced tiles (each tile jumps in one move) Lesson: Relaxed problems: where heuristics come from Table: problem · relaxation · heuristic Problem Relaxation Heuristic Romania road trip You may fly in a straight line Straight line distance hSLD Robot on a grid with walls No walls Manhattan or octile distance Water jugs, get 2 litres Pour any amount you like 0 if a jug holds 2, else 1 Travelling salesperson The tour need not be a cycle Cost of a minimum spanning tree Lesson: Relaxed problems: where heuristics come from Table: depth d · bfs nodes · a*(h1) nodes · a*(h2) nodes Depth d BFS nodes A*(h1) nodes A*(h2) nodes b* BFS b* h1 b* h2 2 7 6 6 2.15 2.00 2.00 6 81 17 15 1.83 1.31 1.26 10 602 72 34 1.74 1.35 1.21 12 1,580 164 57 1.72 1.38 1.23 16 10,198 953 207 1.68 1.42 1.27 20 47,880 5,508 725 1.63 1.45 1.29 Lesson: Dominance and combining heuristics Table: heuristic · admissible · value at the start state · cost to compute Heuristic Admissible Value at the start state Cost to compute Nodes at d = 20 h = 0 (uniform cost) Yes 0 None about 48,000 (BFS) h1 misplaced tiles Yes 8 9 checks 5,508 h2 Manhattan Yes 18 9 small sums 725 max(h1, h2) Yes 18"],[50,"heuristic-design","Designing and comparing heuristics","(= h2, since h2 dominates) Both 725 Pattern database Yes At least as large as one group of tiles needs One table lookup, after building the table Much fewer h* (perfect) Yes 26 As hard as the problem 26 (straight to the goal) Lesson: Comparing heuristics Tips and traps Remember The relaxed problem must be easy to solve, or computing h will cost more than the search saves. Manhattan distance is a sum of independent tile problems, which takes 9 small steps.Lesson: Relaxed problems: where heuristics come from Common mistake Larger is better only while h stays admissible. 2 × h2 is larger, but it overestimates, so A* may then return a longer solution.Lesson: Comparing heuristics 3.3"],[50,"memory-bounded","Memory bounded heuristic search: IDA*, RBFS and SMA*","Open chapter IDA* with f cutoffs, RBFS with f-limits and backed up values, SMA*, and the memory against time trade. Key ideas Why A* stores every node and runs out of memory first. Memory bounded methods trade memory for repeated work. IDA* DFS with an f cutoff, next cutoff = smallest f over the old one. Romania: 366, 393, 413, 415, 417, 418. RBFS f-limit = min(limit, best alternative). Unwind when best f > limit and back up that f. Linear memory. SMA* A* until memory is full, then drop the highest f leaf and back up its f. Optimal if the solution fits. Costs IDA* and RBFS: O(b d) memory. IDA* worst case O(N²) time with many distinct f values. Formulas Iterations of IDA*Exam favourite one per distinct f value up to C* With integer costs and a good h that is few. With many different real valued costs, each iteration may add only one new node, and IDA* can take about N² time for N nodes A* would expand. Lesson: IDA*: iterative deepening A* MemoryExam favourite A*: O(bd) IDA*, RBFS: O(b d) SMA*: M, the memory you give it b branching factor, d solution depth. Lesson: The maths: memory and repeated work IDA* number of iterations k = number of distinct f values in (h(start), C*] Romania: 366, 393, 413, 415, 417, 418 gives 6 iterations. Lesson: The maths: memory and repeated work Worst case of IDA* 1 + 2 + ... + N = N(N + 1) ⁄ 2 = O(N²) If each iteration adds just one new node to the N nodes A* expands. Lesson: The maths: memory and repeated work Tables Table: method · idea · memory Method Idea Memory IDA* Iterative deepening, but the cutoff is on f = g + h instead of depth O(b d), linear RBFS Best first search written as a recursion that remembers only the f of the best alternative O(b d), linear SMA* A* that uses all the memory it has, and drops the worst leaf when memory is full Whatever you give it Lesson: Why A* runs out of memory Table: property · sma* Property SMA* Complete Yes, if the depth of the shallowest goal is less than the memory size (the path must fit) Optimal Yes, if an optimal solution fits in memory. Otherwise it returns the best solution that fits. Weak point When memory is very tight, it can thrash: switch back and forth between paths and regenerate them many times Lesson: SMA*: use all the memory you have Table: · bfs · ids · a* BFS IDS A* IDA* RBFS SMA* Uses h No No Yes Yes Yes Yes Optimal Equal costs Equal costs Yes Yes (admissible h) Yes (admissible h) If the solution fits Memory O(bd) O(b d) O(bd) O(b d) O(b d) M Repeated work None Top levels per iteration None One iteration per f value Each time it switches back Only for dropped nodes Romania, Arad to Bucharest 450 450 418 418, 6 iterations 418, 2 switches 418 Lesson: BFS against IDS against IDA* against RBFS Tips and traps In the exam The minor exam of this course was announced as a coding task, such as writing RBFS. The code in lesson 3 is short enough to learn by heart, and the notebook lets you practise it.Lesson: Why A* runs out of memory Remember All three methods are a trade between time and memory. In practice, SMA* style methods are the most robust when memory is limited but not tiny.Lesson: SMA*: use all the memory you have 3.4"],[50,"and-or-search","AND OR search and AO*","Open chapter AND OR trees, conditional and cyclic plans, AND OR search on the erratic vacuum world, and AO* on the slide example. Key ideas AND and OR nodes OR: the agent chooses, one child must work. AND: nature chooses, every child must work. Contingency plan Erratic vacuum from state 1: [Suck, if State = 5 then [Right, Suck] else []]. AND OR search DFS that fails a branch on a state already on its path. Complete on finite spaces. Cyclic plan Slippery world: [Suck, L1: Right, if State = 5 then L1 else Suck]. Needs fairness. AO* Expand the best partial graph, AND = sum, OR = min, revise upward. Slide example: 6, then 9 against 8, ends at 5. Optimal only with admissible h. Formulas A cyclic planExam favourite [Suck, L1: Right, if State = 5 then L1 else Suck] L1 is a label. \"if State = 5 then L1\" means go back to the label and try Right again. In code it is a while loop: while State = 5: Right. Lesson: Slippery worlds and cyclic plans When a cyclic plan is a solution every leaf is a goal, and a goal can be reached from every point of the plan Each try must succeed with some chance. If the floor is so slippery that Right never works, no plan helps. Lesson: Slippery worlds and cyclic plans Cost at an OR node cost(n) = minconnectors k cost(n, k) The agent picks the cheapest way. Lesson: The maths: cost of a solution graph and complexity Cost of an AND connectorExam favourite cost(n, k) = ∑children c of k (C(n, c) + cost(c)) All parts must be done, so their costs add. Lesson: The maths: cost of a solution graph and complexity Admissibility for AO* h(n) ≤ the true cost of the best solution graph below n With such an h, AO* returns an optimal solution graph (on graphs without cycles). Lesson: The maths: cost of a solution graph and complexity Tables Table: · or node · and node OR node AND node Who chooses The agent Nature (the environment) Solved when At least one child is solved Every child is solved Cost The cheapest child (plus the edge) The sum over all children (plus the edges) Drawn as Separate branches Branches joined by an arc Lesson: When actions do not always do the same thing Table: · a* or bfs · and or search (dfs) · ao* A* or BFS AND OR search (DFS) AO* Actions Deterministic Non deterministic, or problems split into parts Same as AND OR, with costs Solution A path (a list of actions) A tree: a conditional plan The cheapest solution graph Uses h A*: yes No Yes Handles loops Explored set Fails on a repeated state, cyclic plans need labels Classic AO* assumes no cycles (LAO* handles them) Example Romania route Erratic vacuum plan Slide graph, cost 5 Lesson: AND OR search against ordinary search Table: · and or (contingency) · markov decision process (chapter 10.1) AND OR (contingency) Markov decision process (chapter 10.1) Outcomes A set of possible states States with probabilities Goal Reach a goal in every outcome Maximise expected reward Result Conditional plan Policy for every state Lesson: AND OR search against ordinary search Tips and traps Remember Failing on a state already on the path does not miss solutions: if a plan exists from a repeated state, it also exists from its first occurrence. That keeps AND OR search complete on finite state spaces.Lesson: The erratic vacuum world and AND OR search Common mistake A cyclic plan only works under a fairness assumption: an action that can succeed will succeed if you try it often enough. If the failure has a hidden cause (the wheel is broken), trying again is useless and the agent must learn the cause instead.Lesson: Slippery worlds and cyclic plans Common mistake Look closely at the slide numbers: h(D) = 4 but D really costs only 1 (D → J). So this h overestimates and is not admissible. Here AO* still ends at the best answer 5, but try the second tip (h(H) = 6): AO* stops at 9 while the true best is 7. Like A*, AO* is only guaranteed optimal when h never overestimates.Lesson: AO*: best first search on AND OR graphs"],[50,"step-core-4","Step 4Core AI · Local and evolutionary search","4.1"],[50,"hill-climbing","Local search: hill climbing and simulated annealing","Open chapter Hill climbing and its traps, sideways moves, restarts, beam search, and simulated annealing with cooling schedules. Key ideas Local search One current state, an objective, neighbours. No path, constant memory, works on huge spaces. Hill climbing Best neighbour until none is better. Stuck at local maxima, ridges and plateaux. 8 queens: about 14 percent solved. Fixes Sideways moves (about 94 percent), random restarts (1 ⁄ p runs), stochastic and first choice, local beam search. Simulated annealing Accept worse with eΔE⁄T. High T explores, low T exploits. Cooling schedules Geometric T ← αT (0.8 ≤ α ≤ 1), linear T0 − kΔT, logarithmic C ⁄ ln(k + 2), constant, exponential T0 αk. Formulas Random restartExam favourite expected restarts = 1 ⁄ p p is the chance one run succeeds. For 8 queens p ≈ 0.148, so about 7 runs: 6 failures (about 3 steps each) and 1 success (about 4 steps), roughly 22 steps in all. Lesson: Ways out: sideways moves, randomness and restarts Acceptance probabilityExam favourite P(accept) = eΔE ⁄ T for ΔE < 0, and 1 for ΔE ≥ 0 High T: P near 1, almost any move is accepted. Low T: P near 0, only uphill moves. Lesson: Simulated annealing Why the slide table as T grows, ΔE ⁄ T → 0 and eΔE⁄T → e0 = 1 The slide lists ex for x = 1, 1⁄2, 1⁄4, ..., 1⁄1024: 2.718, 1.649, 1.284, ..., 1.001. As the exponent shrinks, the value approaches 1. Lesson: Simulated annealing Gradient ascent for continuous statesExam favourite x ← x + η ∇f(x) In continuous spaces, move a small step η along the gradient. Too big η overshoots, too small is slow. Lesson: The maths: acceptance, restarts and gradients Newton Raphson step x ← x − Hf(x)−1 ∇f(x) Uses the second derivatives (Hessian H) to choose the step. Very fast near an optimum. Lesson: The maths: acceptance, restarts and gradients Probability of success with r restarts 1 − (1 − p)r p = 0.148, r = 10: 1 − 0.852¹⁰ ≈ 0.80. Lesson: The maths: acceptance, restarts and gradients Tables Table: feature of the landscape · what it is · why it is a problem Feature of the landscape What it is Why it is a problem Local maximum A peak higher than all its neighbours but lower than the global maximum Every move goes down, so a greedy climber stops Ridge A narrow line of peaks with slopes on both sides No single move goes up along the ridge, so the climber zig zags slowly Plateau: flat local maximum A flat area with no uphill exit No neighbour is better Plateau: shoulder A flat area from which you can still go up later The climber cannot see that, and stops unless it may move sideways Lesson: Local search: forget the path, improve the state Table: method on random 8 queens boards (our run, 2,000 boards) · solved · average steps when it works · average steps when stuck Method on random 8 queens boards (our run, 2,000 boards) Solved Average steps when it works Average steps when stuck Steepest ascent hill climbing 14.8 percent 4.0 3.1 With up to 100 sideways moves 93.8 percent 19.3 60.9 Lesson: Hill climbing Table: variant · idea · helps with Variant Idea Helps with Sideways moves Allow moves to an equal valued neighbour, up to a limit (to avoid looping on a flat top) Shoulders Stochastic hill climbing Pick a random uphill move, with more chance for steeper ones Some ridges, slower but sometimes better First choice hill climbing Generate random neighbours until one is better, take it States with thousands of neighbours Random restart hill climbing Run hill climbing again from new random starts, keep the best result Local maxima. Complete with probability approaching 1 Local beam search Keep k states. Generate all their successors, keep the best k overall Shares information between the k searches Stochastic beam search Keep k successors chosen at random, better ones more likely Stops all k states crowding onto one hill Lesson: Ways out: sideways moves, randomness and restarts Table: cooling schedule (slide 83) · formula · comment Cooling schedule (slide 83) Formula Comment Geometric Tk+1 = α Tk,"],[50,"hill-climbing","Local search: hill climbing and simulated annealing","0.8 ≤ α ≤ 1 The most common. α near 1 cools slowly and explores longer. Linear Tk = T0 − k ΔT Drops by the same amount each step. Logarithmic Tk = C ⁄ ln(k + 2) C = the highest energy barrier to cross. Very slow, but with it annealing finds the global optimum with probability approaching 1. Constant Tk = C This is the Metropolis algorithm, no cooling. Exponential Tk = T0 αk The closed form of geometric cooling. Lesson: Simulated annealing Table: · hill climbing · simulated annealing · genetic algorithm Hill climbing Simulated annealing Genetic algorithm Breadth of the search One state, narrow One state, wanders widely while hot A population, broad Tests worse solutions Never Yes, with probability eΔE⁄T Yes, weak individuals may still breed Avoids local optima No (only with restarts) Yes, if cooled slowly Often, through crossover and mutation Memory One state One state k states Speed Fastest per run Medium, depends on the schedule Slowest per generation Prefer it when The landscape is smooth or you can restart cheaply There are many local optima and one good answer is enough Good solutions are made of good parts that can be combined Lesson: Comparing local search methods Tips and traps Remember Local beam search is not k independent restarts. If one state finds a great region, its children can fill all k places, so the search moves its effort to where the progress is.Lesson: Ways out: sideways moves, randomness and restarts Remember Choose T0 so that most worse moves are accepted at the start, often from the average cost difference between neighbours. Too fast cooling turns annealing into plain hill climbing.Lesson: Simulated annealing 4.2"],[50,"genetic-algorithms","Genetic algorithms","Open chapter Population, fitness, selection, crossover and mutation, the slide x² example, and when a GA beats other local search. Key ideas GA words Gene, chromosome, population, fitness, selection, crossover, mutation. Roulette wheel P(i) = fi ⁄ ∑f. Expected copies fi ⁄ f̄. Slide example Fitness 169, 576, 64, 361, total 1170, average 292.5. Children 01100, 11001, 11011 (729), 10000. Other selections Tournament, rank, elitism. Rank and tournament ignore the scale of fitness. When GAs help When good solutions are built from blocks crossover can combine. Watch diversity to avoid premature convergence. Formulas Fitness for 8 queens fitness = 28 − (number of attacking pairs) 28 is the number of pairs among 8 queens, 8 × 7 ⁄ 2. Lesson: A GA for 8 queens Search space for the digit string 88 ≈ 16.8 million The same as for hill climbing on 8 queens. Lesson: A GA for 8 queens Roulette wheel probabilityExam favourite P(i) = fi ⁄ ∑j fj Expected copies of i in a population of n: n P(i) = fi ⁄ f̄. Lesson: The maths: selection pressure and schemata Schema theorem (Holland) E[m(H, t + 1)] ≥ m(H, t) · (f(H) ⁄ f̄) · [1 − pc δ(H) ⁄ (l − 1) − o(H) pm] m(H, t) individuals match schema H (like 1***1). f(H) is their average fitness, δ(H) the defining length, o(H) the order (fixed positions), l the string length, pc and pm the crossover and mutation rates. Lesson: The maths: selection pressure and schemata Chance a string escapes mutation (1 − pm)l With pm = 0.01 and l = 5: 0.99⁵ ≈ 0.951. Lesson: The maths: selection pressure and schemata Tables Table: word · meaning · in the x² example Word Meaning In the x² example Gene One encoded decision value One bit Chromosome (individual) A full candidate solution, a string of genes 01101 Population The set of individuals in one generation 4 strings Fitness function How good an individual is (higher is better) f(x) = x², with x the number the bits encode Selection Choosing parents, fitter ones more often Roulette wheel Crossover Swap the tails of two parents after a cut point 0110|1 and 1100|0 give 01100 and 11001 Mutation A small random change Flip one bit Lesson: Search by evolution Table: string · x · f(x) = x² · p(pick) = f ⁄ ∑f String x f(x) = x² P(pick) = f ⁄ ∑f Expected copies = f ⁄ average Actual copies 01101 13 169 0.14 0.58 1 11000 24 576 0.49 1.97 2 01000 8 64 0.05 (the slide rounds to 0.06) 0.22 0 10011 19 361 0.31 1.23 1 Total 1170 1.00 4.00 4 Average 292.5 (the slide rounds to 293) Maximum 576 Lesson: The slide example: maximise x² with 5 bits Table: selection method · how it works · strength · weakness Selection method How it works Strength Weakness Roulette wheel (fitness proportionate) P(i) = fi ⁄ ∑f. Spin a wheel with slices sized by fitness Simple, matches the theory One very fit individual can take over too early. Needs positive fitness Tournament Pick k individuals at random, the fittest of them wins Easy to tune with k, works with any fitness scale Large k is very greedy Rank Sort by fitness, pick by rank, not by raw value Not fooled by huge fitness gaps Ignores how much better the best is Elitism Copy the best few unchanged into the next generation Never loses the best found so far Can reduce diversity Lesson: Selection, crossover and mutation in detail Table: operator (slide 84) · example · note Operator (slide 84) Example Note One point crossover AB|CD and MN|OP give ABOP and MNCD The cut point is chosen at random Two point and uniform crossover Swap a middle segment, or each gene with chance 0.5 Mix more freely Flip mutation 01101 → 01001 For bit strings Switch (swap) mutation ABCD → ADCB Keeps a permutation valid, for example a tour Scramble mutation ABCD → ACBD (shuffle a segment) Bigger jump Lesson: Selection, crossover and mutation in detail Table: feature · hill climbing · simulated annealing · genetic algorithm Feature Hill climbing Simulated annealing Genetic algorithm Inspiration Climbing a hill Annealing in metallurgy Natural selection and evolution Search space Local"],[50,"genetic-algorithms","Genetic algorithms","Broad (with controlled randomness) Very broad (population based) Moves to worse solutions No Yes (probabilistic) Yes (via mutation and crossover) Avoids local optima No Yes Yes Memory usage Low Medium High Parameter tuning needed Minimal High (temperature, cooling) High (population, mutation rate) Speed Fast Moderate Slower (population evolution) Complexity Simple Moderate High Best use case Small or simple problems Problems with many local optima Complex optimisation problems Lesson: Comparing hill climbing, annealing and genetic algorithms Tips and traps Common mistake The slide table lists x = 10 for the string 10000. In binary 10000 is 16, and 16² = 256 matches the f column, so x should read 16.Lesson: The slide example: maximise x² with 5 bits Common mistake For problems like the travelling salesperson, plain one point crossover makes invalid tours (a city twice). Use order crossover or swap mutation, which always give valid permutations.Lesson: Selection, crossover and mutation in detail Remember Crossover helps when nearby genes form meaningful blocks, like the left half of the board. If the encoding scatters related decisions, crossover mostly breaks good solutions, and a GA is no better than random restarts.Lesson: A GA for 8 queens Remember Short, low order schemata with above average fitness grow exponentially. These are the \"building blocks\" that crossover combines.Lesson: The maths: selection pressure and schemata"],[50,"step-core-5","Step 5Core AI · Constraint satisfaction","5.1"],[50,"csp-basics","Constraint satisfaction problems and arc consistency","Open chapter CSP formulation, constraint types, node and arc consistency, AC-3 and its three outcomes. Key ideas CSP Variables X, domains D, constraints C. Solution: complete and consistent assignment. Examples Australia: 37 = 2,187 assignments, 18 solutions. N queens with Xij ∈ {0, 1}. Car assembly with precedence T1 + d1 ≤ T2 and disjunctive constraints. Arc consistency ∀ x ∈ dom(X) ∃ y ∈ dom(Y): r(x, y). AC-3 costs O(c d³). Three outcomes Empty domain: no solution. All singletons: unique solution. Otherwise search. Forward checking against AC-3 FC checks arcs from the newly assigned variable only. AC-3 checks every arc and finds failures earlier. Formulas Arc consistencyExam favourite ∀ x ∈ dom(X) ∃ y ∈ dom(Y): r(x, y) Every value of X has a supporting value of Y. A network is arc consistent when all its arcs are. Lesson: Node and arc consistency The three outcomes (slide 6) a domain is empty ⇒ no solution. Every domain has one value ⇒ the unique solution. Some domains have several values ⇒ search is still needed Arc consistency alone does not always decide the problem. Lesson: Node and arc consistency Cost of AC-3Exam favourite O(c d³) c binary constraints, domains of size at most d. Each arc can be added to the queue at most d times (once per value deleted from its head), and REVISE costs d² checks. Lesson: The AC-3 algorithm Number of complete assignmentsExam favourite dn n variables, domain size d. Australia: 37 = 2,187. Lesson: The maths: counting assignments and solutions Naive search treeExam favourite n! · dn leaves If any unassigned variable can be chosen at each level: n d choices at the top, (n − 1) d next, and so on. Fixing the variable order (assignments commute) brings it back to dn. Lesson: The maths: counting assignments and solutions Solutions of the Australia map 3 × 2 × 3 = 18 Pick SA (3 ways). WA, NT, Q, NSW, V form a chain around SA and must alternate the other two colours (2 ways). T is free (3 ways). Lesson: The maths: counting assignments and solutions Tables Table: part · value Part Value Variables WA, NT, SA, Q, NSW, V, T Domains Di = {red, green, blue} Constraints Neighbouring regions differ: WA ≠ NT, WA ≠ SA, NT ≠ SA, NT ≠ Q, SA ≠ Q, SA ≠ NSW, SA ≠ V, Q ≠ NSW, NSW ≠ V A solution WA = red, NT = green, SA = blue, Q = red, NSW = green, V = red, T = green Lesson: What a constraint satisfaction problem is Table: part · formulation Part Formulation Variables Xij for 0 ≤ i, j < N, one for each square (row i, column j) Domains {0, 1}: 1 means a queen stands on the square Same row ∀ i, j, k: (Xij, Xik) ∈ {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)}, at most one queen Same column ∀ i, j, k: (Xij, Xkj) ∈ {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)} Diagonals (Xij, Xi+k, j+k) and (Xij, Xi+k, j−k) ∈ {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)} Count ∑i, j Xij = N, exactly N queens Lesson: More CSPs: N queens and job scheduling Table: part · formulation Part Formulation Variables AxleF, AxleB, WheelRF, WheelLF, WheelRB, WheelLB, Nuts for each wheel, Cap for each wheel, Inspect: 15 tasks, each value is a start time Precedence T1 + d1 ≤ T2: task 1 must finish before task 2 starts. AxleF + 10 ≤ WheelRF, WheelRF + 1 ≤ NutsRF, NutsRF + 2 ≤ CapRF, ... Disjunctive The two axles share one tool: AxleF + 10 ≤ AxleB or AxleB + 10 ≤ AxleF Inspection last X + dX ≤ Inspect for every other task X, and Inspect takes 3 minutes Deadline 30 minutes Di = {1, 2, 3, ..., 27}, so even Inspect ends by minute 30 Lesson: More CSPs: N queens and job scheduling Table: kind of constraint · example Kind of constraint Example Unary (one variable) SA ≠ green, B ≠ 3 Binary (two variables) WA ≠ NT, A < B Global (any number) Alldiff(all cells in a Sudoku row) Preference (soft) Prof. R prefers morning lectures. Breaking it costs, but is allowed (constrained optimisation) Lesson: More CSPs: N queens and job scheduling Table: · forward checking · arc consistency (ac-3) Forward checking Arc consistency (AC-3) What it checks Only arcs from the variable just assigned to its unassigned neighbours Every arc in"],[50,"csp-basics","Constraint satisfaction problems and arc consistency","the network, again and again until nothing changes Strength Weaker Stronger: it also catches conflicts between two unassigned variables Cost per step Cheap: O(d) per neighbour Heavier: up to O(c d³) Australia, after WA = red and Q = green NT = {blue}, SA = {blue}, and no failure is seen yet Checks NT against SA, finds both must be blue, empties a domain: failure detected at once Lesson: Forward checking against arc consistency Table: level · meaning Level Meaning Node (1 consistency) Each value meets its unary constraints Arc (2 consistency) Each value of a variable has a partner in every neighbour Path (3 consistency) Each consistent pair (x, y) can be extended to any third variable z k consistency Any consistent assignment to k − 1 variables extends to any k-th variable Strong n consistency k consistent for every k ≤ n: then a solution can be found with no backtracking, but reaching it costs exponential time in general Lesson: Forward checking against arc consistency Tips and traps Common mistake Arc consistency cannot see that 2 colours are not enough for a triangle WA, NT, SA: each arc on its own is fine ({red, blue} against {red, blue}). Path consistency catches it.Lesson: Forward checking against arc consistency 5.2"],[50,"csp-backtracking","Backtracking search for CSPs","Open chapter Backtracking, MRV, degree, LCV, forward checking, MAC, backjumping and learning. Key ideas Backtracking DFS, one variable per level (assignments commute), undo on a broken constraint. dn leaves. MRV and degree MRV: fewest legal values (fail first). Degree: most constraints on unassigned variables, breaks ties. SA first. LCV Value ruling out fewest neighbour values first. WA = red, NT = green: Q = red, not blue. FC and MAC FC prunes neighbours of the assigned variable. MAC runs AC-3 and catches more. Backjumping and learning Jump to the latest variable in the conflict set. Record no goods so the same failure never repeats. Formulas Worst case of backtrackingExam favourite O(dn) Every partial assignment may be extended until the last variable. General CSPs are NP complete (3 colouring is), so no method avoids this in the worst case. Lesson: The maths: branching and pruning Forward checking cost per assignment O(e d) at most, e = number of neighbours Each neighbour loses at most the conflicting values. Lesson: The maths: branching and pruning Symmetry k colours ⇒ k! equivalent solutions Fixing the first colour divides the search by k, fixing an ordering of all colours by k!. Lesson: The maths: branching and pruning Tables Table: heuristic · rule · why it helps · slide example Heuristic Rule Why it helps Slide example Minimum remaining values (MRV), also \"most constrained variable\" or \"fail first\" Choose the variable with the fewest legal values left If some variable has no values left, MRV picks it and the failure is found at once, before wasting time on others After WA and NT are coloured, SA has only one colour left, so colour SA next Degree heuristic Choose the variable involved in the most constraints on other unassigned variables Lowers the branching factor of future choices. Used to break ties of MRV, and at the very start when all domains are equal Choose SA first: it has degree 5 Least constraining value (LCV) Try first the value that rules out the fewest choices for neighbouring variables Leaves the most room for the rest, so a solution is found sooner After WA = red and NT = green, Q = blue would remove SA's last value, so LCV prefers Q = red Lesson: Which variable next, which value first Table: method · idea Method Idea Backjumping Jump back to the most recent variable in the conflict set of the failed variable (the assigned variables that removed its values) Conflict directed backjumping When jumping back from X to Y, add X's conflict set (minus Y) to Y's, so the next jump from Y can also skip unrelated variables Constraint learning (no goods) Record the smallest set of assignments that caused the failure, for example {Q = red, NSW = green, V = blue}, and never try that combination again Symmetry breaking In map colouring, any solution with colours permuted is also a solution. Fix WA = red at the start to cut the work by 3! = 6 Lesson: Smarter backtracking: backjumping and learning Table: method · order · inference · backtracks Method Order Inference Backtracks Plain backtracking Fixed WA, NT, NSW, Q, SA, V, T None 2 With forward checking Fixed Forward checking 2, but each dead end is found one level earlier MRV with degree and LCV SA first Forward checking 0 2 colours with MAC SA first MAC Proves no solution after trying both colours for SA Lesson: Comparing CSP search strategies Table: · search over states (chapter 2) · csp backtracking Search over states (chapter 2) CSP backtracking State Black box Partial assignment with visible structure Pruning Only by the goal test or h Constraints prune at every step, inference removes values ahead Heuristics Problem specific h General ones that work for any CSP: MRV, degree, LCV Lesson: Comparing CSP search strategies Tips and traps In the exam Variable ordering is fail first: pick the hardest variable so a dead end shows up early. Value ordering is succeed first: pick the value most likely to work. When you want all solutions, or none exists, value ordering does"],[50,"csp-backtracking","Backtracking search for CSPs","not matter, because every value gets tried anyway.Lesson: Which variable next, which value first Common mistake Forward checking misses conflicts between two unassigned variables. After WA = red and Q = green, NT and SA both have only blue left and are neighbours. Forward checking does not see it. MAC does, because it also checks the arc NT, SA.Lesson: Forward checking and maintaining arc consistency Remember Forward checking already finds every conflict that simple backjumping would jump over, so backjumping adds little to forward checking. Conflict directed backjumping and learning do add power, and they are the core of modern SAT solvers.Lesson: Smarter backtracking: backjumping and learning 5.3"],[50,"csp-local","Local search for CSPs and problem structure","Open chapter Min-conflicts local search, tabu and weighting, tree CSPs, cutset conditioning and tree decomposition. Key ideas Min-conflicts Complete random assignment, move a random conflicted variable to its least conflicting value. About 50 steps for a million queens (AIMA). Escaping traps Sideways moves, tabu search, constraint weighting, restarts. Local search cannot prove no solution. Tree CSP O(n d²): directional arc consistency backward, then assign forward, no backtracking. Cutset conditioning O(dc(n − c)d²). Australia: cutset {SA}. Tree decomposition O(n dw+1), w = tree width. Formulas Independent sub problemsExam favourite connected components solved separately: O(dc · n ⁄ c) instead of O(dn) Tasmania is not connected to the mainland, so colour it on its own. If n = 80 splits into 4 parts of 20 with d = 2: 4 × 2²⁰ ≈ 4 million against 2⁸⁰ ≈ 10²⁴. Lesson: Using the shape of the problem: independent parts and trees Tree structured CSPExam favourite solvable in O(n d²) If the constraint graph is a tree (no loops), there is no need to backtrack at all. Lesson: Using the shape of the problem: independent parts and trees Cutset conditioningExam favourite O(dc · (n − c) d²) c is the size of the cutset. Australia: removing SA leaves a tree (a chain plus T), so c = 1. Lesson: Cutset conditioning and tree decomposition Tree decomposition O(n dw+1) w is the tree width: the size of the largest cluster minus 1. CSPs with small tree width are easy. Lesson: Cutset conditioning and tree decomposition Backtracking worst case O(dn) For any CSP. Lesson: The maths of problem structure Tree CSP O(n d²) Linear in n. Lesson: The maths of problem structure Cutset O(dc (n − c) d²) Exponential only in the cutset size c. Lesson: The maths of problem structure Tables Table: fix · idea Fix Idea Sideways moves Allow moves to a value with the same number of conflicts, so the search can cross a plateau Tabu search Keep a short list of recently visited states (or recently changed variables) and do not go back to them, so the search does not bounce between the same few states Constraint weighting Give every constraint a weight, starting at 1. After each step, add 1 to the weight of every broken constraint. Minimise the total weight. Hard constraints that keep breaking become expensive, which changes the landscape and pushes the search out of local minima Random restarts and random walk Start again, or sometimes make a random move instead of the best one Online repair When the problem changes (a flight is cancelled), start min-conflicts from the old solution: only a few variables need to change Lesson: Plateaux, tabu search and constraint weighting Table: · backtracking with propagation · min-conflicts (local search) · structure based (tree, cutset) Backtracking with propagation Min-conflicts (local search) Structure based (tree, cutset) Starts from An empty assignment A complete random assignment The shape of the constraint graph Complete (finds a solution if one exists) Yes No Yes Can prove no solution Yes No Yes Memory O(n) for the path O(n), one assignment O(n d) for the domains Best on Tight problems with few solutions Large problems with many solutions spread out (N queens, scheduling) Graphs that are trees or nearly trees N queens with a million queens Very slow About 50 steps (AIMA) Not a tree Lesson: Backtracking against min-conflicts against structure Tips and traps Common mistake Local search cannot prove that a CSP has no solution. It just fails to find one. Backtracking with propagation can prove it, for example that 2 colours are not enough for Australia.Lesson: Plateaux, tabu search and constraint weighting"],[50,"step-core-6","Step 6Core AI · Adversarial search","6.1"],[50,"minimax","Games and minimax","Open chapter Game formalism, minimax values, the slide examples, the cost of minimax and evaluation functions. Key ideas Game S0, PLAYER, ACTIONS, RESULT, TERMINAL-TEST, UTILITY. Zero sum: one player's gain is the other's loss. Minimax MAX takes max, MIN takes min, terminals give utility. Root value = what MAX can guarantee. Slide examples 27 leaves: B1 = 5, B2 = 2, B3 = 3, A = 5. Tic-tac-toe: O takes the centre. Cost Complete (finite), optimal against perfect play, O(bm) time, O(b m) space. Chess ≈ 10154. Cutoffs Depth limit with EVAL = ∑ wi fi. Quiescence search, horizon effect. Formulas Minimax valueExam favourite MINIMAX(s) = UTILITY(s) if s is terminal, maxa MINIMAX(RESULT(s, a)) if MAX moves, mina MINIMAX(RESULT(s, a)) if MIN moves A recursive definition, computed depth first. Lesson: The minimax algorithm Depth limited minimaxExam favourite replace TERMINAL-TEST by CUTOFF-TEST(s, d) and UTILITY by EVAL(s) Search to a fixed depth d, then use a heuristic evaluation of the position. Lesson: Properties, and what to do when the tree is too big Weighted linear evaluation EVAL(s) = w1 f1(s) + w2 f2(s) + ... + wn fn(s) Chess material: pawn 1, knight 3, bishop 3, rook 5, queen 9, counted for MAX minus for MIN. Lesson: Properties, and what to do when the tree is too big Zero sumExam favourite UTILITY(s, MAX) + UTILITY(s, MIN) = constant Chess: 1 + 0, 0 + 1 or ½ + ½. So MIN minimising MAX's utility is the same as maximising its own. Lesson: The maths: zero sum games and evaluation Multiplayer games each node holds a vector (u1, ..., uk), and the player to move picks the child with the best uown With three players, alliances can appear and break, which plain minimax cannot express. Lesson: The maths: zero sum games and evaluation Size of a game tree bm Tic-tac-toe has at most 9! = 362,880 move sequences (fewer, because games end early), and only 5,478 legal positions. Lesson: The maths: zero sum games and evaluation Tables Table: part (slide 2) · meaning · tic-tac-toe Part (slide 2) Meaning Tic-tac-toe S0 The initial state The empty board PLAYER(s) Whose turn it is in state s X and O take turns ACTIONS(s) The legal moves in s The empty squares RESULT(s, a) The transition model: the state after move a The board with the new mark TERMINAL-TEST(s) True when the game is over Three in a row, or a full board UTILITY(s, p) The final payoff for player p in a terminal state s +1 win, −1 loss, 0 draw Lesson: Games as search problems Table: · deterministic · chance Deterministic Chance Perfect information Chess, Go, tic-tac-toe, checkers Backgammon, Monopoly Imperfect information Battleship, Kriegspiel Poker, bridge, Scrabble Lesson: Games as search problems Table: property · minimax Property Minimax Complete Yes, if the tree is finite Optimal Yes, against an optimal opponent. Against a weak one it is safe but may miss chances Time O(bm): it visits the whole tree to depth m Space O(b m) when it explores depth first and generates all moves at once, O(m) when it generates one move at a time Data structures A recursion stack (or explicit stack) of states, the list of moves at each level, and the best value and move so far Lesson: Properties, and what to do when the tree is too big Table: problem with cutting off · fix Problem with cutting off Fix Non quiet positions: the evaluation changes wildly in the next move (a capture is about to happen) Quiescence search: keep searching captures until the position is quiet Horizon effect: a delaying move pushes an unavoidable loss beyond the search depth, so it looks avoided Singular extensions: search a clearly best move one level deeper Too many moves to search Forward pruning: skip moves that look bad (beam search on moves). Risky but used in practice Lesson: Properties, and what to do when the tree is too big Table: · greedy (best immediate result) · minimax · expectimax (chapter 6.3) Greedy (best immediate result) Minimax Expectimax (chapter 6.3) Assumes about the opponent Nothing, ignores replies"],[50,"minimax","Games and minimax","Plays perfectly against you Plays at random with known chances Safe against a perfect opponent No Yes No Exploits a weak opponent Sometimes No, it is cautious Yes, if the chances are right Cost O(b) O(bm) O(bm) Slide tic-tac-toe Can pick a losing corner Picks the centre, wins Picks the centre too Lesson: Minimax against other ways to choose a move Tips and traps Remember Boards 8, 10, 13 and 14 show what happens if X does not take the centre: O wins. Minimax ignores those lines because a perfect X would not play them.Lesson: The tic-tac-toe example of the slides 6.2"],[50,"alpha-beta","Alpha beta pruning","Open chapter Alpha and beta, the prune rule, the slide example, move ordering and the cost of alpha beta. Key ideas α and β α = best MAX can guarantee so far (starts −∞). β = best MIN can guarantee so far (starts +∞). Rule α, β go parent to child. Values go child to parent. Prune when α ≥ β. Root value = minimax. Slide example Leaves 2 3 5 9 0 1 7 5. Prune the 9 under E and all of G. A = 3 after 5 of 8 leaves. Cost Best O(bm/2), random about O(b3m/4), worst O(bm). Space O(b m). Same time, twice the depth. Ordering Best first: b⌈d/2⌉ + b⌊d/2⌋ − 1 leaves. Iterative deepening, killer moves, transposition tables. Formulas Fewest leaves alpha beta can read (Knuth and Moore)Exam favourite b⌈d/2⌉ + b⌊d/2⌋ − 1 For a uniform tree with branching b and depth d, with perfect ordering. b = 2, d = 3: 4 + 2 − 1 = 5. b = 3, d = 3: 9 + 3 − 1 = 11. Lesson: Move ordering decides how much is pruned Best order costExam favourite O(bm/2) = O((√b)m) The effective branching factor drops from b to √b. Chess: √35 ≈ 5.9. Lesson: The maths: how much faster, and how much deeper Depth for the same work bd = (√b)2d In the time minimax searches depth d, alpha beta with good ordering searches depth 2d. Lesson: The maths: how much faster, and how much deeper Random order cost about O(b3m/4) Still a big saving: the depth grows by about a third. Lesson: The maths: how much faster, and how much deeper Negamax form max(a, b) = −min(−a, −b) So one function can serve both players: value = max over children of −value(child), with the window (−β, −α). Lesson: The maths: how much faster, and how much deeper Tables Table: value (slide 7) · meaning · starts at · changed at Value (slide 7) Meaning Starts at Changed at α The highest value MAX can guarantee so far on the path to this node. A lower bound −∞ MAX nodes: α = max(α, value) β The lowest value MIN can guarantee so far on the path to this node. An upper bound +∞ MIN nodes: β = min(β, value) Lesson: Why we can skip parts of the tree Table: direction (slide 7) · what moves Direction (slide 7) What moves Parent to child The current α and β values Child to parent The node value, which then updates α (at a MAX parent) or β (at a MIN parent) Lesson: Why we can skip parts of the tree Table: property · alpha beta Property Alpha beta Same answer as minimax Yes, always, at the root. Pruning never changes the decision Complete and optimal Same as minimax: complete for finite trees, optimal against a perfect opponent Time, best order O(bm/2) Time, random order About O(b3m/4) for moderate b Time, worst order O(bm), no pruning at all Space O(b m), like depth first minimax. Only the current path and its α, β values are stored Data structures The recursion stack. Each frame holds a state, its list of moves, v, α and β. Real programs add a transposition table (a hash map of positions already searched) Lesson: The algorithm Table: tree · leaves · read by alpha beta · pruned Tree Leaves Read by alpha beta Pruned Slide example, slide order (b = 2, depth 3) 8 5 3 Same values, worst order 8 8 0 27 leaf slide tree, slide order (b = 3, depth 3) 27 11 16 Same 27 values, worst order 27 27 0 Lesson: Move ordering decides how much is pruned Table: how real programs get a good order · idea How real programs get a good order Idea Iterative deepening Search depth 1, 2, 3 and so on. The best move found at depth d − 1 is tried first at depth d Killer moves A move that caused a prune at the same depth elsewhere is tried early, since it is likely good again Captures first In chess, try captures and threats before quiet moves Transposition table Store each searched position with its value and best move, so a position reached by a different move order is not searched twice Lesson: Move ordering decides how much is pruned Table: question (slide 10) · answer Question (slide 10) Answer Time and space complexity Time O(bm/2) with best ordering, about O(b3m/4) with random ordering, O(bm) in the worst case. Space O(b m) Data structures A recursion stack"],[50,"alpha-beta","Alpha beta pruning","with α and β per frame, a move list per level, and in practice a hash table of positions (transposition table) and a small table of killer moves per depth Real life uses Chess, checkers, Othello and Connect Four engines. Adversarial planning such as security patrols, where the attacker answers the defender. Puzzle game AI in mobile apps Utility functions Chess: material, king safety, mobility, pawn structure, weighted and added. Connect Four: number of open lines of 2 and 3. Security: value of each target minus the cost of guarding it Lesson: Food for thought from the slides Table: · minimax · alpha beta · depth limited alpha beta Minimax Alpha beta Depth limited alpha beta Monte Carlo tree search (6.3) Same answer as full minimax Yes Yes Only as good as EVAL Approaches it with many rollouts Leaves read All bm bm/2 to bm, depends on order bd/2 to bd for depth d Chosen by statistics, not by depth Needs an evaluation function No (full tree) No (full tree) Yes No, uses random playouts Good for Tiny games Small to medium games Chess, checkers Go, games with b in the hundreds Handles chance No No No Yes, naturally Lesson: Alpha beta against other game search methods Table: improvement · what it adds Improvement What it adds Aspiration windows Start with a narrow window [v − δ, v + δ] around the last value instead of [−∞, +∞]. More prunes, research if the result falls outside Principal variation search (NegaScout) Search the first move with the full window, the rest with a zero width window to prove they are worse. Research only when the proof fails Null move pruning Let the opponent move twice. If MAX is still above β, the real position is surely good enough, so prune Transposition tables Reuse values of positions already seen through another move order Lesson: Alpha beta against other game search methods Tips and traps Common mistake The slide text says \"the initial value of alpha is +∞\" in the line about beta. That is a typing slip: it is β that starts at +∞, and α starts at −∞.Lesson: Why we can skip parts of the tree In the exam A pruned node gets a bound, not an exact value: E is \"at least 5\" and C is \"at most 1\". These bounds are enough to choose the move, which is all we need at the root.Lesson: The worked example of the slides Remember In class we noted that when the order of nodes is poor, or when you add your own thresholds to prune more, you may cut away a good move. Plain alpha beta never does that: its pruning is exact. Only extra rules such as forward pruning or chance thresholds can lose the best move.Lesson: Food for thought from the slides 6.3"],[50,"expectimax","Games with chance: expectimax and Monte Carlo tree search","Open chapter Expectimax, expectiminimax, their problems, and Monte Carlo tree search with UCB1. Key ideas Chance node V(s) = ∑ p(s′ | s) V(s′). exp-value starts at v = 0 and adds p × value. Slide 11 3 12 9, 2 4 6, 15 6 0 with 1/3 each: 8, 4, 7. MAX = 8. Slide 12 MIN 2, 1, 3 with 0.5, 0.4, 0.1: 1.7. Layers MAX, CHANCE, MIN, CHANCE. Problems Need probabilities, cost O(bm nm), value scale matters (only a x + b with a > 0 is safe), pruning needs bounds. MCTS Select by UCB1 = w ⁄ n + C √(ln N ⁄ n), expand, simulate, back up. Play the most visited move. Formulas Expectimax value (slide 11)Exam favourite V(s) = maxs′ ∈ successors(s) V(s′) for agent controlled states. V(s) = ∑s′ ∈ successors(s) p(s′ | s) V(s′) for chance states. V(s) = known for terminal states The same recursion as minimax, with the MIN rule replaced by an expectation. Lesson: When dice decide part of the game ExpectiminimaxExam favourite EMM(s) = UTILITY(s) if terminal, maxa EMM(RESULT(s, a)) if MAX, mina EMM(RESULT(s, a)) if MIN, ∑r P(r) EMM(RESULT(s, r)) if CHANCE r is a chance outcome, such as a dice roll. Lesson: Expectiminimax: chance and an opponent UCB1 (upper confidence bound)Exam favourite UCB1(i) = wi ⁄ ni + C √(ln N ⁄ ni) wi wins in ni visits of move i, N visits of the parent, C balances the two parts. The first part is exploitation (how good the move looks), the second is exploration (how unsure we still are). C = √2 ≈ 1.4 is the usual start. Lesson: Monte Carlo tree search Expected valueExam favourite E[X] = ∑x P(x) × x The average result over many plays. The probabilities of the outcomes of one chance node add up to 1. Lesson: The maths: expected value, UCB1 and cost Linearity E[a X + b] = a E[X] + b for a > 0 This is why a positive linear change of all values keeps the expectimax choice, while other changes may not. Lesson: The maths: expected value, UCB1 and cost Cost of expectiminimax O(bm nm) b moves and n chance outcomes per level, m levels of each. Lesson: The maths: expected value, UCB1 and cost Where UCB1 comes from P(true mean > sample mean + ε) ≤ e−2 n ε² (Hoeffding) Choose ε so the chance of being wrong falls like 1 ⁄ N⁴: ε = √(2 ln N ⁄ n). That is UCB1 with C = √2. Lesson: The maths: expected value, UCB1 and cost Tables Table: problem · why it hurts · solution strategies Problem Why it hurts Solution strategies We must know the probabilities In class: a coin tossed 1,000 times is never exactly 50:50, a coin may be biased, and probabilities from experience are skewed towards what we have seen Estimate from data, update the estimates as you play, or test how much the decision changes when the probabilities change a little The tree is much bigger Time O(bm nm), where n is the number of chance outcomes. Backgammon has 21 distinct rolls of two dice and about 20 moves per roll Depth limit with an evaluation function, or sample a few rolls instead of all (Monte Carlo) The size of the values matters Minimax only cares about the order of the leaves. Expectimax averages, so stretching some values can change the choice (see below) The evaluation must be a positive linear function of the true expected payoff, for example the chance of winning Pruning is harder One unseen child of a chance node can change the average by any amount If the values are bounded, say between 0 and 10, a chance node gets an upper bound after a few children, and alpha beta style pruning works again The model of the opponent matters Expectimax against a clever opponent can walk into a trap. Minimax against a random opponent is too careful Pick the model that fits: MIN nodes for an adversary, chance nodes for random behaviour Lesson: What problems do you see here? Table: · minimax and alpha beta · expectimax · expectiminimax Minimax and alpha beta Expectimax Expectiminimax MCTS Other side modelled as A perfect opponent Random events with known chances An opponent plus random events Whatever the rollout policy does Value at a non MAX node min Weighted average min or weighted"],[50,"expectimax","Games with chance: expectimax and Monte Carlo tree search","average Win rate from rollouts Needs an evaluation function Yes, at the depth limit Yes, on the right scale Yes, on the right scale No Pruning Alpha beta, exact Only with bounded values Only with bounded values Not needed: it grows only good parts Typical games Chess, checkers, tic-tac-toe Single player games with randomness, Pac-Man ghosts that move at random Backgammon Go, many board and card games, planning Lesson: Which game search to use Tips and traps Remember If the same tree had MIN nodes instead of chance nodes, minimax would give min values 3, 2 and 0, and MAX would still pick the first move, but with value 3. The third move looks worst to minimax (0) and second best to expectimax (7). The two methods can choose different moves when the risk differs.Lesson: The expectimax example of the slides Remember MCTS also handles chance naturally: a dice roll in a rollout is just one more random step. That is one reason it replaced expectimax in many games with dice and cards.Lesson: Monte Carlo tree search"],[50,"expectimax","Step 7Core AI · Logic and knowledge","7.1"],[50,"propositional-logic","Knowledge, logic and propositional logic","Open chapter Knowledge based agents, entailment, propositional logic and model checking. Key ideas KB agent TELL percepts, ASK for an action, TELL the action. Inference happens inside ASK. Entailment KB ⊨ α iff M(KB) ⊆ M(α). Sound: derives only entailed. Complete: derives all entailed. Connectives ¬ ∧ ∨ ⇒ ⇔, in that order of precedence. P ⇒ Q false only for P = T, Q = F. Wumpus KB R1 ¬P11, R2 B11 ⇔ (P12 ∨ P21), R3 B21 ⇔ (P11 ∨ P22 ∨ P31), R4 ¬B11, R5 B21. 3 of 128 models. Entails ¬P12. Validity KB ⊨ α iff KB ⇒ α is valid iff KB ∧ ¬α is unsatisfiable. SAT is NP-complete. Formulas EntailmentExam favourite KB ⊨ α if and only if M(KB) ⊆ M(α) The more a KB says, the fewer models it has. If all of them make α true, α follows. Lesson: Logic: syntax, semantics, models and entailment Deduction theoremExam favourite KB ⊨ α if and only if (KB ⇒ α) is valid Entailment can be checked as validity. Lesson: Validity, satisfiability and equivalence Proof by contradiction (refutation)Exam favourite KB ⊨ α if and only if (KB ∧ ¬α) is unsatisfiable Assume α is false and show this is impossible. Resolution (chapter 7.2) works this way. Lesson: Validity, satisfiability and equivalence Number of modelsExam favourite 2n for n symbols 3 symbols: 8 models. 7 symbols: 128. 30 symbols: over 10⁹. Lesson: The maths: counting models Number of different sentences up to equivalence 22n Each sentence is fixed by the set of models where it is true, and there are 22n such sets. For n = 2 there are 16 different truth tables. Lesson: The maths: counting models Entailment as sets KB ⊨ α ⟺ M(KB) ⊆ M(α) ⟺ M(KB) ∩ M(¬α) = ∅ The last form is proof by contradiction. Lesson: The maths: counting models Tables Table: peas part · wumpus world PEAS part Wumpus world Performance +1000 for climbing out with the gold, −1000 for falling into a pit or being eaten, −1 for each action, −10 for using the arrow Environment 4 × 4 grid of rooms, agent starts in [1,1]. The wumpus and the gold are placed at random in other rooms, and each other room has a pit with probability 0.2 Actuators Forward, TurnLeft, TurnRight, Grab, Shoot, Climb Sensors Stench (wumpus next door), Breeze (pit next door), Glitter (gold here), Bump (walked into a wall), Scream (the wumpus died) Lesson: The wumpus world Table: idea · meaning · example Idea Meaning Example Syntax Which strings are well formed sentences x + y = 4 is a sentence, x4y+ = is not Semantics When a sentence is true. A sentence is true or false in each possible world x + y = 4 is true in the world where x = 2, y = 2 Model A possible world, fixed exactly. In propositional logic: a true or false value for every symbol P = T, Q = F, R = T M(α) The set of all models in which α is true M(P ∨ Q) has 3 of the 4 models of P, Q Entailment KB ⊨ α α is true in every model where the KB is true {x = 0} ⊨ x y = 0 Lesson: Logic: syntax, semantics, models and entailment Table: property of an inference procedure i · meaning Property of an inference procedure i Meaning KB ⊢i α Procedure i derives α from the KB Sound (truth preserving) It derives only entailed sentences: if KB ⊢i α then KB ⊨ α Complete It can derive every entailed sentence: if KB ⊨ α then KB ⊢i α Grounding The link between the sentences and the real world. It comes from the sensors and from learning, not from logic Lesson: Logic: syntax, semantics, models and entailment Table: connective · name · read as · true when Connective Name Read as True when ¬P negation not P P is false P ∧ Q conjunction P and Q both are true P ∨ Q disjunction P or Q at least one is true P ⇒ Q implication if P then Q P is false, or Q is true. False only for P = T, Q = F P ⇔ Q biconditional P if and only if Q both have the same value Lesson: Propositional logic Table: name · sentence · meaning Name Sentence Meaning R1 ¬P1,1 no pit in the start room R2 B1,1 ⇔ (P1,2 ∨ P2,1) a room is breezy if and only if a neighbour has a pit R3 B2,1 ⇔ (P1,1 ∨ P2,2 ∨ P3,1) the same rule for [2,1] R4 ¬B1,1 percept: no breeze in [1,1] R5"],[50,"propositional-logic","Knowledge, logic and propositional logic","B2,1 percept: breeze in [2,1] Lesson: A knowledge base for the wumpus world, and model checking Table: term · meaning · example Term Meaning Example Valid (a tautology) True in every model P ∨ ¬P Satisfiable True in at least one model P ∧ Q Unsatisfiable True in no model P ∧ ¬P Equivalent, α ≡ β True in exactly the same models. Same as α ⊨ β and β ⊨ α P ⇒ Q ≡ ¬P ∨ Q Lesson: Validity, satisfiability and equivalence Table: equivalence · name Equivalence Name α ∧ β ≡ β ∧ α, α ∨ β ≡ β ∨ α commutativity (α ∧ β) ∧ γ ≡ α ∧ (β ∧ γ), and the same for ∨ associativity ¬(¬α) ≡ α double negation α ⇒ β ≡ ¬β ⇒ ¬α contraposition α ⇒ β ≡ ¬α ∨ β implication elimination α ⇔ β ≡ (α ⇒ β) ∧ (β ⇒ α) biconditional elimination ¬(α ∧ β) ≡ ¬α ∨ ¬β, ¬(α ∨ β) ≡ ¬α ∧ ¬β De Morgan α ∧ (β ∨ γ) ≡ (α ∧ β) ∨ (α ∧ γ), α ∨ (β ∧ γ) ≡ (α ∨ β) ∧ (α ∨ γ) distributivity Lesson: Validity, satisfiability and equivalence Table: · atomic states (search) · propositional logic · first order logic (7.3) Atomic states (search) Propositional logic First order logic (7.3) Probability (step 9) A state is one name, no inside parts true or false values for fixed facts objects, relations and functions a distribution over possible worlds Can say \"pits cause breezes in every room\" in one sentence No No, one sentence per room Yes Yes, with a rule per variable Handles uncertainty No Only by listing cases with ∨ Only by listing cases Yes, with numbers Inference Search Model checking, resolution, chaining Unification, chaining, resolution Bayes rule, Bayesian networks Decidable Yes Yes (but NP-complete) Only semidecidable Yes for finite models Lesson: Logic against other ways to store knowledge Tips and traps Remember Room names are [column, row]. [2,1] is one step right of the start, [1,2] is one step up.Lesson: The wumpus world Common mistake P ⇒ Q is true whenever P is false. \"If 5 is even, then Sam is smart\" is true in propositional logic, whatever Sam is like. Implication does not mean that P causes Q.Lesson: Propositional logic 7.2"],[50,"propositional-inference","Reasoning patterns: rules, resolution and chaining","Open chapter Inference rules, resolution, CNF, forward and backward chaining, DPLL and WalkSAT. Key ideas Rules Modus Ponens: α ⇒ β, α ⊢ β. And-Elimination. Equivalences as rules. Monotonic. Resolution ℓ ∨ α, ¬ℓ ∨ β ⊢ α ∨ β. One pair at a time. Refutation complete. CNF steps Remove ⇔, remove ⇒, move ¬ in (De Morgan, double negation), distribute ∨ over ∧. Chaining Definite clause: exactly one positive literal. Forward from facts, backward from goal. Linear time. SAT DPLL: early termination, pure symbol, unit clause. WalkSAT: local search, incomplete. CDCL in practice. Formulas The resolution ruleExam favourite from ℓ1 ∨ ... ∨ ℓk and m1 ∨ ... ∨ mn, where ℓi and mj are opposite literals, conclude the clause of all the other literals Example: from P ∨ Q and ¬Q ∨ R, conclude P ∨ R. Copies of the same literal are kept once (factoring). Lesson: Resolution and conjunctive normal form Why it is sound if ℓi is true then mj is false, so the rest of the second clause must be true. If ℓi is false, the rest of the first clause must be true Either way, the joined rest is true. Lesson: Resolution and conjunctive normal form Possible clauses over n symbolsExam favourite 3n Each symbol is positive, negative or absent in a clause. So resolution always stops: there are at most 3n distinct clauses to add. Lesson: The maths: how many clauses, and how fast Clauses from distributing (a ∧ b) ∨ (c ∧ d) gives (a ∨ c) ∧ (a ∨ d) ∧ (b ∨ c) ∧ (b ∨ d) An OR of k ANDs with 2 parts each gives 2k clauses: CNF can blow up. The Tseitin trick adds new symbols to keep it linear. Lesson: The maths: how many clauses, and how fast Forward chaining cost O(n) for a KB of total size n Each premise of each rule is counted down at most once. Lesson: The maths: how many clauses, and how fast Random 3-SAT hardness m ⁄ n ≈ 4.26 With m clauses over n symbols, problems are hardest near this ratio. Below it almost all are satisfiable, above it almost none. Lesson: The maths: how many clauses, and how fast Tables Table: rule · from · conclude Rule From Conclude Modus Ponens α ⇒ β and α β And-Elimination α ∧ β α (and β) Biconditional elimination α ⇔ β (α ⇒ β) ∧ (β ⇒ α) Contraposition α ⇒ β ¬β ⇒ ¬α De Morgan ¬(α ∨ β) ¬α ∧ ¬β Unit resolution α ∨ β and ¬β α Lesson: Inference rules and proofs Table: clause type · at most / exactly · example · as an implication Clause type At most / exactly Example As an implication Definite clause exactly one positive literal ¬L ∨ ¬M ∨ P L ∧ M ⇒ P Fact one positive literal and nothing else A A Horn clause at most one positive literal ¬A ∨ ¬B A ∧ B ⇒ False (a goal clause) Lesson: Horn clauses, forward and backward chaining Table: · forward chaining · backward chaining Forward chaining Backward chaining Driven by Data (the facts) The goal Good for Watching a stream of new facts, such as sensors Answering one question, such as \"what should I do now?\" Wasted work May derive many facts unrelated to the query Only touches relevant rules Cost O(size of KB), each rule counted down at most once per premise Often much less than the KB size Sound and complete for definite clauses Yes Yes, with the loop check Lesson: Horn clauses, forward and backward chaining Table: method · complete · typical use Method Complete Typical use Truth table (TT-ENTAILS) Yes Teaching, tiny problems Resolution Yes (refutation complete) Theorem provers, the basis of first order provers Forward or backward chaining Yes, for definite clauses only Rule based systems, Prolog DPLL and its modern form CDCL Yes Industrial SAT solving with millions of clauses WalkSAT No: it can find a model, but it cannot prove there is none Large satisfiable problems Lesson: Fast satisfiability: DPLL and WalkSAT Table: · model checking · inference rules by hand · resolution Model checking Inference rules by hand Resolution Chaining DPLL and WalkSAT Works on Any sentences Any sentences CNF Definite clauses only CNF Sound and complete Yes Depends on the rules chosen Refutation complete Yes, for definite clauses"],[50,"propositional-inference","Reasoning patterns: rules, resolution and chaining","DPLL yes, WalkSAT not for proving Time 2n always Search over proofs Can be exponential Linear Exponential worst case, fast in practice Easy for people to follow Yes, but long Yes Fairly Yes No Lesson: Which inference method to use Tips and traps Remember Logic is monotonic: adding sentences to a KB never removes something it already entails. If KB ⊨ α then KB ∧ β ⊨ α. So a rule can be used whenever its premises are in the KB, whatever else is there.Lesson: Inference rules and proofs In the exam The ground resolution theorem: if a set of clauses is unsatisfiable, resolution will reach the empty clause. So resolution is a complete refutation procedure for propositional logic. It cannot list every entailed sentence directly, but it can always confirm or refute a given one.Lesson: Resolution and conjunctive normal form 7.3"],[50,"first-order-logic","First order logic: syntax and semantics","Open chapter Objects, relations, functions, quantifiers, models and how to write knowledge in FOL. Key ideas Syntax Constants, predicates, functions, variables. Terms name objects, atoms are true or false. Semantics Model = domain + interpretation. Infinitely many models, so no truth tables. Quantifier traps ∀ with ⇒, ∃ with ∧. ∀x ∃y ≠ ∃y ∀x. ¬∀x P ≡ ∃x ¬P. Equality x = y means same object. Needed for \"at least two\". Database semantics: unique names, closed world, domain closure. Engineering Questions, knowledge, vocabulary, general axioms, instance facts, queries, debug. Formulas De Morgan for quantifiersExam favourite ∀x ¬P ≡ ¬∃x P, ¬∀x P ≡ ∃x ¬P, ∀x P ≡ ¬∃x ¬P, ∃x P ≡ ¬∀x ¬P \"Nobody likes parsnips\" = \"everybody dislikes parsnips\". Lesson: Quantifiers and their traps ∀ as a big AND, ∃ as a big OR ∀x P(x) ≡ P(o1) ∧ P(o2) ∧ ..., ∃x P(x) ≡ P(o1) ∨ P(o2) ∨ ... Over a finite domain with objects o1, o2, ... Lesson: Quantifiers and their traps Interpretations over a fixed domain of d objectsExam favourite dc × (2d)u × (2d²)b c constants (each names one of d objects), u unary predicates (each is any subset of the d objects), b binary predicates (any subset of the d² pairs). Functions add more. Lesson: The maths: counting interpretations A k-ary relation 2dk possible extensions There are dk tuples, each in or out. Lesson: The maths: counting interpretations Grounding ∀ ∀x P(x) becomes d ground sentences Over a finite domain, FOL can be turned into propositional logic. Functions make the set of ground terms infinite. Lesson: The maths: counting interpretations Tables Table: language (aima figure 8.1) · what exists in the world (ontological commitment) · what an agent believes about facts (epistemological commitment) Language (AIMA figure 8.1) What exists in the world (ontological commitment) What an agent believes about facts (epistemological commitment) Propositional logic facts true, false, unknown First order logic facts, objects, relations true, false, unknown Temporal logic facts, objects, relations, times true, false, unknown Probability theory facts a degree of belief between 0 and 1 Fuzzy logic facts with a degree of truth between 0 and 1 a known interval value Lesson: Why we need more than propositional logic Table: element · what it names · examples Element What it names Examples Constant symbol one object Richard, John, M1, 2 Predicate symbol a relation (true or false for its arguments) Brother(·, ·), King(·), Adjacent(·, ·) Function symbol a function: objects in, one object out LeftLeg(·), Mother(·), + Variable any object, to be fixed by a quantifier x, y, s Term a constant, a variable, or a function applied to terms John, x, LeftLeg(John), Mother(Mother(x)) Atomic sentence a predicate applied to terms, or term = term Brother(Richard, John), Father(x) = John Complex sentence atoms joined by ¬ ∧ ∨ ⇒ ⇔ King(Richard) ∨ King(John) Quantified sentence ∀x S or ∃x S ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x) Lesson: Syntax: terms, atoms and sentences Table: sentence · read as · true when Sentence Read as True when ∀x King(x) ⇒ Person(x) all kings are persons for every object, if it is a king then it is a person ∃x Crown(x) ∧ OnHead(x, John) some crown is on John's head at least one object is a crown and on John's head ∀x ∃y Loves(x, y) everybody loves somebody for each x there is a y (maybe different for each x) ∃y ∀x Loves(x, y) there is someone whom everybody loves one y works for all x Lesson: Quantifiers and their traps Table: rule of thumb · why Rule of thumb Why ∀ goes with ⇒ ∀x King(x) ∧ Person(x) says everything is a king and a person, which is far too strong ∃ goes with ∧ ∃x Crown(x) ⇒ OnHead(x, John) is true as soon as one object is not a crown, so it says almost nothing Order matters for mixed quantifiers ∀x ∃y and ∃y ∀x mean different things The same kind can be swapped ∀x ∀y ≡ ∀y ∀x, ∃x ∃y ≡ ∃y ∃x Lesson: Quantifiers and their traps Table: kinship axiom · meaning Kinship axiom Meaning ∀m, c Mother(c) = m ⇔ Female(m) ∧ Parent(m, c) a"],[50,"first-order-logic","First order logic: syntax and semantics","mother is a female parent ∀w, h Husband(h, w) ⇔ Male(h) ∧ Spouse(h, w) a husband is a male spouse ∀p, c Parent(p, c) ⇔ Child(c, p) parent and child are inverse relations ∀g, c Grandparent(g, c) ⇔ ∃p Parent(g, p) ∧ Parent(p, c) a grandparent is a parent of a parent ∀x, y Sibling(x, y) ⇔ x ≠ y ∧ ∃p Parent(p, x) ∧ Parent(p, y) siblings share a parent and are different people Lesson: Using first order logic: kinship and the wumpus world Table: wumpus world in fol · meaning Wumpus world in FOL Meaning ∀x, y, a, b Adjacent([x, y], [a, b]) ⇔ (x = a ∧ (y = b − 1 ∨ y = b + 1)) ∨ (y = b ∧ (x = a − 1 ∨ x = a + 1)) which rooms are neighbours, for any cave size ∀s Breezy(s) ⇔ ∃r Adjacent(r, s) ∧ Pit(r) one sentence for every room ∀t, s At(Agent, s, t) ∧ Breeze(t) ⇒ Breezy(s) what a percept at time t tells about room s Lesson: Using first order logic: kinship and the wumpus world Table: · propositional · first order · higher order Propositional First order Higher order Description logics Talks about fixed facts objects, relations, functions also relations of relations classes and properties Can quantify over nothing objects relations and functions too objects, in limited patterns Entailment decidable Yes Semidecidable: a yes answer is found if true, but a search may never end if false No complete proof method exists Yes, for most variants Compactness Low: one sentence per case High Highest High for taxonomies Used in SAT, hardware checking Theorem provers, Prolog, databases Proof assistants such as Coq and Lean Web ontologies (OWL), medical terms (SNOMED) Lesson: First order logic against its neighbours Tips and traps Remember In this course and in the animations, names that start with a lower case letter are variables, and names that start with a capital letter are constants, predicates or functions. Prolog uses the opposite convention.Lesson: Syntax: terms, atoms and sentences Common mistake First order logic has infinitely many models: any domain size from 1 upwards, and every interpretation of every symbol. So entailment cannot be checked by listing models, as we did with truth tables.Lesson: Semantics: models and interpretations 7.4"],[50,"fol-inference","Inference in first order logic: unification, chaining and resolution","Open chapter Instantiation, unification, GMP, forward and backward chaining, and resolution in FOL. Key ideas UI and EI UI: any ground term, many times. EI: new Skolem constant, once. Propositionalisation: kv copies. Semidecidable. Unify {x/Jane}, {y/John, x/Bill}, {y/John, x/Mother(John)}, fail for Knows(x, Elizabeth). Occurs check. Standardise apart. MGU. GMP and FC Generalised Modus Ponens with θ. Crime: round 1 Weapon, Sells, Hostile, round 2 Criminal(West). Datalog stops. BC Goal driven, depth first, left to right (Prolog). Left recursion loops. Negation as failure. FOL resolution CNF: remove ⇒, ¬ in, standardise, Skolemise (∃y inside ∀x gives F(x)), drop ∀, distribute. Refutation complete. Formulas Generalised Modus Ponens (GMP)Exam favourite from p1′, ..., pn′ and (p1 ∧ ... ∧ pn ⇒ q), infer SUBST(θ, q), where SUBST(θ, pi′) = SUBST(θ, pi) for every i King(John), Greedy(y) and King(x) ∧ Greedy(x) ⇒ Evil(x) with θ = {x/John, y/John} give Evil(John). Lesson: Unification and lifting UNIFY UNIFY(p, q) = θ where SUBST(θ, p) = SUBST(θ, q) θ is written as a set of bindings variable/term. Lesson: Unification and lifting Binary resolutionExam favourite from ℓ1 ∨ ... ∨ ℓk and m1 ∨ ... ∨ mn with UNIFY(ℓi, ¬mj) = θ, infer SUBST(θ, all other literals) Example: [Animal(F(x)) ∨ Loves(G(x), x)] and [¬Loves(u, v) ∨ ¬Kills(u, v)] with θ = {u/G(x), v/x} give Animal(F(x)) ∨ ¬Kills(G(x), x). Lesson: Resolution in first order logic Ground instances of one ruleExam favourite kv k constants, v variables. The crime law has v = 3 and the KB has k = 4 constants (West, M1, Nono, America): 64 copies, of which forward chaining needs 1. Lesson: The maths: instances, ground terms and proof size Ground terms with one unary function F and one constant A, up to depth d d + 1 A, F(A), F(F(A)), and so on. Lesson: The maths: instances, ground terms and proof size Ground terms with c constants and one binary function, depth at most 1 c + c² Each argument is a constant. Depth 2 grows much faster. Lesson: The maths: instances, ground terms and proof size Tables Table: rule · what it does · example Rule What it does Example Universal Instantiation (UI) From ∀v α, infer α with v replaced by any ground term g From ∀x King(x) ∧ Greedy(x) ⇒ Evil(x), infer King(John) ∧ Greedy(John) ⇒ Evil(John) Existential Instantiation (EI) From ∃v α, infer α with v replaced by a brand new constant k (a Skolem constant) From ∃x Crown(x) ∧ OnHead(x, John), infer Crown(C1) ∧ OnHead(C1, John) Lesson: From first order inference to propositional inference Table: p · q · θ = unify(p, q) p q θ = UNIFY(p, q) Knows(John, x) Knows(John, Jane) {x/Jane} Knows(John, x) Knows(y, Bill) {x/Bill, y/John} Knows(John, x) Knows(y, Mother(y)) {y/John, x/Mother(John)} Knows(John, x) Knows(x, Elizabeth) fail: x cannot be John and Elizabeth Knows(John, x) Knows(x17, Elizabeth) {x17/John, x/Elizabeth}, after standardising apart Lesson: Unification and lifting Table: sentence in definite clauses · meaning Sentence in definite clauses Meaning American(x) ∧ Weapon(y) ∧ Sells(x, y, z) ∧ Hostile(z) ⇒ Criminal(x) the law Owns(Nono, M1), Missile(M1) Nono has a missile, M1 (EI on \"Nono has some missiles\") Missile(x) ∧ Owns(Nono, x) ⇒ Sells(West, x, Nono) all of Nono's missiles were sold by West Missile(x) ⇒ Weapon(x) missiles are weapons Enemy(x, America) ⇒ Hostile(x) an enemy of America counts as hostile American(West), Enemy(Nono, America) facts Lesson: Forward chaining: the crime example Table: fact about first order forward chaining · detail Fact about first order forward chaining Detail Sound and complete for first order definite clauses Every entailed atom is eventually derived Datalog Definite clauses with no function symbols. Forward chaining then stops after polynomially many steps Pattern matching is hard Finding all θ that match a rule's premises to the facts is like solving a CSP, and is NP-hard in general. Order the premises, most constrained first Incremental forward chaining In round t, only try rules"],[50,"fol-inference","Inference in first order logic: unification, chaining and resolution","with a premise that matches a fact new in round t − 1 Rete Store partial matches in a network so new facts update them cheaply (used in rule engines) Lesson: Forward chaining: the crime example Table: prolog feature · what it means Prolog feature What it means Syntax criminal(X) :- american(X), weapon(Y), sells(X, Y, Z), hostile(Z). Upper case letters are variables Search Depth first, rules in the order written, premises left to right Negation as failure not P (written with a special operator in Prolog) succeeds if P cannot be proved. This is the closed world assumption, not real negation No occurs check Faster unification, but unsound in rare cases Incomplete A left recursive rule such as path(X, Z) :- path(X, Y), link(Y, Z) can loop for ever. Writing link first fixes it Tabling Remembering solved subgoals (memoisation) avoids repeated work and many loops Lesson: Backward chaining and Prolog Table: fact about first order resolution · detail Fact about first order resolution Detail Refutation complete (Robinson, 1965) If KB ⊨ α, resolution with factoring derives { } from KB ∧ ¬α Factoring Merge two literals of one clause that unify, for example P(x) ∨ P(A) becomes P(A) Equality Needs extra rules: demodulation (rewrite with x = y) and paramodulation Strategies Unit preference (resolve with short clauses), set of support (always use a clause from ¬α or derived from it), input resolution, subsumption (delete clauses implied by shorter ones) Used in Theorem provers such as Vampire and E, and software verification Lesson: Resolution in first order logic Table: · propositionalisation · forward chaining · backward chaining (prolog) Propositionalisation Forward chaining Backward chaining (Prolog) Resolution Works on Any FOL KB Definite clauses Definite clauses Any FOL KB in CNF Complete Yes, with growing term depth Yes No with plain depth first search (loops), yes with tabling Yes, refutation complete Main cost kv copies of every rule Matching premises to facts Repeated subgoals Many useless resolvents Best for Small finite domains Databases, rule engines, Datalog Answering queries, logic programming Mathematics, software verification Lesson: Which first order inference method to use Tips and traps Common mistake The occurs check (does the variable appear inside the term?) makes unification cost quadratic in the size of the terms. Prolog leaves it out for speed, so Prolog can unify x with F(x) and build an endless term.Lesson: Unification and lifting Remember Backward chaining is the reason Prolog programs can be read as logic and run as programs. But the order of rules and premises changes what happens, which pure logic does not care about.Lesson: Backward chaining and Prolog"],[50,"step-core-8","Step 8Core AI · Planning","8.1"],[50,"situation-calculus","Situation calculus and deductive planning","Open chapter Situations, fluents, action axioms, the frame problem, successor state axioms and deductive planning. Key ideas Situations S0, Result(a, s). Fluents take s as last argument. Atemporal predicates do not. Axioms Possibility: Poss(a, s) ⇔ preconditions. Effect: Poss(a, s) ⇒ effect in Result(a, s). Frame problem Effect axioms do not say what stays. A × F frame axioms, or F successor state axioms (Reiter). SSA form F(Result(a, s)) ⇔ a made F true ∨ (F(s) ∧ a did not make F false). Needs unique action axioms. Deductive planning Green 1969: prove ∃s Goal(s), extract s. Slow, so STRIPS (1971). Formulas Successor state axiom (one per fluent F)Exam favourite Poss(a, s) ⇒ [F(Result(a, s)) ⇔ (a made F true) ∨ (F(s) ∧ a did not make F false)] A fluent is true after an action if the action made it true, or if it was already true and the action did not make it false. Lesson: The frame problem and successor state axioms Frame axioms neededExam favourite A × F One for each action and fluent pair that the action does not change. 20 actions and 50 fluents: up to 1,000. Lesson: The maths: counting axioms and situations Successor state axioms neededExam favourite F One per fluent, however many actions there are. 50 fluents: 50 axioms. Lesson: The maths: counting axioms and situations Situations up to depth d 1 + b + b² + ... + bd b actions possible in each situation. Situations are terms, so two different action sequences are always two different situations, even when the world looks the same. Lesson: The maths: counting axioms and situations Tables Table: planning language · states · actions · main use Planning language States Actions Main use Situation calculus (McCarthy, 1963) situations: terms such as Result(Go(B), S0) axioms in first order logic Deductive planning, theory of action STRIPS (Fikes and Nilsson, 1971) sets of ground facts precondition, add list, delete list Fast state space planners PDDL (1998) like STRIPS, with types and more action schemas with parameters The standard language of planning competitions Lesson: What planning is Table: part · meaning · example Part Meaning Example Initial situation S0 the start At(Robot, A, S0), At(Gold, B, S0) Result(a, s) the situation after action a in s S1 = Result(Go(B), S0) Fluent a relation or function whose value changes, with the situation as last argument Holding(Gold, s) Atemporal (eternal) predicate true or false in every situation Gold(G1), Adjacent(A, B) Possibility axiom when an action can be done Poss(Grab, s) ⇔ ∃x At(Gold, x, s) ∧ At(Robot, x, s) ∧ ¬Holding(Gold, s) Effect axiom what an action changes Poss(Grab, s) ⇒ Holding(Gold, Result(Grab, s)) Lesson: Situation calculus Table: version of the problem · what goes wrong Version of the problem What goes wrong Representational frame problem Writing a frame axiom such as At(Gold, x, s) ∧ ¬Holding(Gold, s) ⇒ At(Gold, x, Result(Go(y), s)) for every pair of action and fluent: A × F axioms Inferential frame problem Carrying every unchanged fact forward one action at a time costs time for every fact at every step Qualification problem Listing every condition for an action to succeed is endless: the robot can grab the gold unless it is glued down, too heavy, ... Ramification problem Indirect effects: when the robot moves while holding the gold, the gold moves too Lesson: The frame problem and successor state axioms Table: strength of deductive planning · weakness Strength of deductive planning Weakness Very expressive: quantifiers, functions, partial knowledge General theorem provers are slow and have no planning heuristics A proof that the plan works No guarantee of a short plan Clear meaning (semantics) The frame problem must be handled with care, and the search space of proofs is huge Lesson: Deductive planning: a plan is a proof Table: · situation calculus · strips and pddl · event calculus Situation calculus STRIPS and PDDL Event calculus Logic Full first order Ground facts, no quantifiers in states First order, time"],[50,"situation-calculus","Situation calculus and deductive planning","points Frame problem Solved by successor state axioms Solved by the STRIPS assumption: facts not in the delete list stay Solved by circumscription over events Actions at the same time Hard No (sequential plans) Yes, events happen at time points Planner speed Slow (theorem proving) Fast (heuristic search) Slow Used for Theory, GOLOG robot programs, reasoning about what is known Practical planners, competitions Narratives, commonsense reasoning Lesson: Situation calculus against other ways to describe actions Tips and traps Remember The search above is not a resolution prover. It looks through the same situation terms breadth first, which is what a fair prover ends up doing for this small problem, and it finds the same answer term.Lesson: Deductive planning: a plan is a proof 8.2"],[50,"strips-planning","STRIPS planning and sub goals","Open chapter STRIPS actions, forward and backward search, planning heuristics, sub goals and the Sussman anomaly. Key ideas STRIPS State = set of facts. Action = PRE, ADD, DEL. RESULT = (s − DEL) ∪ ADD. Unmentioned facts stay. Regression g′ = (g − ADD) ∪ PRE, for actions that add part of g and delete none of it. Heuristics Ignore preconditions, ignore delete lists (h+ admissible, hFF greedy), sub goal independence. Sussman anomaly C on A, goal A on B and B on C. Either sub goal order undoes the other. Shortest plan 3: C to table, B on C, A on B. Complexity STRIPS planning is PSPACE-complete. Blocks world states: 13 for 3 blocks, 73 for 4. Formulas Applying an actionExam favourite RESULT(s, a) = (s − DEL(a)) ∪ ADD(a), if PRE(a) ⊆ s Delete first, then add. Lesson: STRIPS: actions with preconditions, add lists and delete lists RegressionExam favourite g′ = (g − ADD(a)) ∪ PRE(a) a is relevant if it adds some fact of g, and consistent if it deletes none. Lesson: Searching forwards and backwards Ground actions in the blocks world with n blocksExam favourite n(n − 1) MoveToTable + n(n − 1) Move from the table + n(n − 1)(n − 2) Move between blocks n = 3: 6 + 6 + 6 = 18. n = 4: 12 + 12 + 24 = 48. Lesson: The maths: how big planning problems are States of the blocks world 1, 3, 13, 73, 501, ... for n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 The ways to arrange n named blocks into stacks. It grows faster than n!. Lesson: The maths: how big planning problems are Complexity PlanSAT (is there any plan?) is PSPACE-complete for STRIPS Bylander, 1994. Bounded PlanSAT (is there a plan of length ≤ k?) is also PSPACE-complete in general, and NP-complete for many restricted forms. Lesson: The maths: how big planning problems are Tables Table: domain · action schema (pddl style) Domain Action schema (PDDL style) Blocks world Move(b, x, y): PRE On(b, x) ∧ Clear(b) ∧ Clear(y). ADD On(b, y) ∧ Clear(x). DEL On(b, x) ∧ Clear(y) Blocks world MoveToTable(b, x): PRE On(b, x) ∧ Clear(b). ADD On(b, Table) ∧ Clear(x). DEL On(b, x) Air cargo Fly(p, from, to): PRE At(p, from) ∧ Plane(p) ∧ Airport(from) ∧ Airport(to). ADD At(p, to). DEL At(p, from) Air cargo Load(c, p, a): PRE At(c, a) ∧ At(p, a) ∧ Cargo(c) ∧ Plane(p) ∧ Airport(a). ADD In(c, p). DEL At(c, a) Spare tyre PutOn(t, Axle): PRE Tire(t) ∧ At(t, Ground) ∧ ¬At(Flat, Axle). ADD At(t, Axle). DEL At(t, Ground) Lesson: STRIPS: actions with preconditions, add lists and delete lists Table: · forward (progression) search · backward (regression) search Forward (progression) search Backward (regression) search Starts from the initial state the goal A step apply an action whose preconditions hold regress the goal through a relevant action Branching every applicable action, often many irrelevant ones only actions that achieve part of the goal Search nodes complete states sets of goal facts (partial descriptions) Heuristics easy to compute from a full state harder, since nodes are sets of states Lesson: Searching forwards and backwards Table: relaxation · heuristic · admissible Relaxation Heuristic Admissible Ignore preconditions number of unsatisfied goal facts (when each action adds one goal fact), or a set cover Yes in its exact form, the set cover version is hard to compute exactly Ignore delete lists h+, the cost of the best relaxed plan Yes, but finding h+ is NP-hard Ignore delete lists, greedy relaxed plan hFF (FF planner) No, but very informative Sub goal independence sum (or max) of the costs of the goals solved one at a time Max is admissible, sum is not Lesson: Heuristics for planning Table: · forward search + heuristic · backward search · linear sub goal planning Forward search + heuristic Backward search Linear sub goal planning Partial order planning (8.3) GraphPlan and SATPlan Searches over states goal sets sub goals, one at a time partial plans a planning graph, or a SAT formula Handles interacting sub goals Yes Yes No (gives long plans or fails) Yes Yes Plan form sequence sequence sequence partial order: flexible sequence"],[50,"strips-planning","STRIPS planning and sub goals","of parallel steps Strength Fastest today (FF, Fast Downward) Few relevant actions Simple Least commitment, easy to explain Good for plans with parallel actions Weakness Many irrelevant actions without a heuristic Goal sets can be impossible Incomplete for interacting goals Slower than heuristic state search Large graphs or formulas Lesson: Ways to plan with STRIPS actions Tips and traps Remember PDDL (Planning Domain Definition Language, 1998) is the modern form of STRIPS. It adds types, negative preconditions, equality and more, and is used in the International Planning Competition.Lesson: STRIPS: actions with preconditions, add lists and delete lists Common mistake Regressing On(A, B) ∧ On(B, C) through Move(B, Table, C) gives a goal with both On(A, B) and Clear(B), which no state can satisfy. Backward planners need extra rules (state constraints) to throw such goals away early.Lesson: Searching forwards and backwards 8.3"],[50,"partial-order-planning","Partial order planning","Open chapter Partial order plans, causal links, threats, promotion and demotion, and the POP algorithm. Key ideas Partial plan Steps, orderings A ≺ B, causal links A →p→ B, open preconditions. Start gives the initial state, Finish needs the goal. Threat C deletes p and could run between A and B of the link A →p→ B. Fixes Demotion: C ≺ A. Promotion: B ≺ C. Keep the orderings free of cycles, else go back. Solution No open preconditions, no threats, no cycles. Every linearization then reaches the goal. Counting Independent chains n₁, ..., n_k give (Σ n_i)! ÷ Π n_i! linearizations. Socks and shoes: 6. Sussman by POP MoveToTable(C, A) ≺ Move(B, Table, C) ≺ Move(A, Table, B), found by two promotions. Formulas ThreatExam favourite C deletes p, and neither C ≺ A nor B ≺ C follows from the orderings C could sit between A and B. Lesson: Threats, promotion and demotion Demotion add C ≺ A C runs before p is made. Lesson: Threats, promotion and demotion Promotion add B ≺ C C runs after p is used. Lesson: Threats, promotion and demotion Linearizations of independent chainsExam favourite (n₁ + n₂ + ... + n_k)! ÷ (n₁! n₂! ... n_k!) k chains of steps, chain i has n_i steps in a fixed order, and the chains do not constrain each other. This is the multinomial coefficient. Lesson: The maths: counting orders and checking threats Two chains of length 2 4! ÷ (2! × 2!) = 24 ÷ 4 = 6 The socks and shoes plan allows 6 total orders. Lesson: The maths: counting orders and checking threats Steps with no orderings at all n! Every order is allowed. Lesson: The maths: counting orders and checking threats Causal links in a finished plan number of links = total number of preconditions of all steps (including Finish) Each precondition gets exactly one link. Lesson: The maths: counting orders and checking threats Cost of one threat check links × steps pairs, each needing a reachability test in the ordering graph Reachability is a graph search, about O(steps + orderings) each time. Lesson: The maths: counting orders and checking threats Tables Table: part · meaning · socks and shoes example Part Meaning Socks and shoes example Steps actions in the plan, plus Start and Finish RightSock, RightShoe, LeftSock, LeftShoe Ordering A ≺ B A must run before B RightSock ≺ RightShoe Causal link A →p→ B A makes p true for B, and nothing may undo p between them RightSock →RightSockOn→ RightShoe Open precondition a precondition with no causal link yet at the start: RightShoeOn and LeftShoeOn of Finish Lesson: Partial plans: steps, orderings and causal links Table: · partial order planning · forward state search with heuristics · linear sub goal planning Partial order planning Forward state search with heuristics Linear sub goal planning GraphPlan Hierarchical task networks (HTN) Searches over partial plans world states one sub goal at a time a layered planning graph ways to break big tasks into smaller ones Plan form partial order, flexible total order total order layers of parallel actions partial or total order Interacting sub goals handled by threat fixes handled by search often fails or gives long plans handled by mutex links handled by the method author Speed today slower on large problems fastest general method (FF, Fast Downward) fast but incomplete good on small parallel problems very fast with good methods Best use flexible plans to explain or to run in parallel large benchmark problems teaching, independent goals parallel plans, heuristics real systems with expert knowledge Lesson: POP compared with other planners Tips and traps Remember A partial plan is consistent when its orderings have no cycle (A ≺ B and B ≺ A cannot both hold) and no causal link is threatened. A consistent plan with no open preconditions is a solution: every linearization of it reaches the goal.Lesson: Partial plans: steps, orderings and causal links Remember Some textbooks add a third fix for lifted plans with variables: separation, a constraint such as x ≠ C that stops the threatening step from"],[50,"partial-order-planning","Partial order planning","deleting this exact fact.Lesson: Threats, promotion and demotion Remember POP is sound (every plan it returns works) and complete (it finds a plan if one exists) when the step and threat choices are searched fully, for example breadth first or with iterative deepening on the number of steps. The animation uses iterative deepening, so it reports the step limit it needed.Lesson: The POP algorithm"],[50,"partial-order-planning","Step 9Core AI · Probability and Bayesian networks","9.1"],[50,"probability-basics","Probability for AI agents","Open chapter Probability axioms, joint distributions, Bayes’ rule, independence and naive Bayes. Key ideas Axioms 0 ≤ P(ω) ≤ 1, Σ P(ω) = 1. P(a ∨ b) = P(a) + P(b) − P(a ∧ b). P(a | b) = P(a ∧ b) ÷ P(b). Enumeration P(X | e) = α Σ_y P(X, e, y). Marginalise, keep matching worlds, normalise. Full joint: 2ⁿ entries. Bayes' rule P(c | e) = P(e | c) P(c) ÷ P(e), P(e) = Σ_c P(e | c) P(c). Odds form: posterior odds = prior odds × LR. Base rate Prior 0.01, sensitivity 0.9, false positive 0.09 gives P(ill | +) ≈ 0.092. Conditional independence P(a, b | c) = P(a | c) P(b | c). Naive Bayes: P(C | e) = α P(C) Π P(eᵢ | C), 2n + 1 numbers. Dentist numbers P(cavity) = 0.2, P(toothache) = 0.2, P(cavity | toothache) = 0.6, P(cavity | toothache, catch) ≈ 0.871. Formulas Kolmogorov axiomsExam favourite 0 ≤ P(ω) ≤ 1 for every world ω, and the sum of P(ω) over all worlds is 1 Everything else follows from these. Lesson: Why agents need probability Probability of a sentence P(φ) = Σ P(ω) over worlds ω where φ is true A sentence is a set of worlds. Lesson: Why agents need probability Inclusion and exclusion P(a ∨ b) = P(a) + P(b) − P(a ∧ b) Do not count the shared worlds twice. Lesson: Why agents need probability Conditional probabilityExam favourite P(a | b) = P(a ∧ b) ÷ P(b), when P(b) > 0 Belief in a after learning only b. Lesson: Why agents need probability Marginalisation P(Y) = Σ over z of P(Y, z) Sum out the other variables Z. Lesson: The full joint distribution and inference by enumeration Inference by enumerationExam favourite P(X | e) = α P(X, e) = α Σ over y of P(X, e, y) X query, e evidence, y hidden variables. Lesson: The full joint distribution and inference by enumeration Size of the full joint 2ⁿ entries for n true or false variables (2ⁿ − 1 free numbers) This is why we need Bayesian networks. Lesson: The full joint distribution and inference by enumeration Bayes' ruleExam favourite P(c | e) = P(e | c) P(c) ÷ P(e) posterior = likelihood × prior ÷ evidence. Lesson: Bayes' rule Total probability P(e) = P(e | c) P(c) + P(e | ¬c) P(¬c) The bottom line of Bayes’ rule. Lesson: Bayes' rule Odds form posterior odds = prior odds × likelihood ratio, where the likelihood ratio is P(e | c) ÷ P(e | ¬c) Easy to update one test at a time. Lesson: Bayes' rule Independence P(a ∧ b) = P(a) P(b) Same as P(a | b) = P(a). Lesson: Independence, conditional independence and naive Bayes Conditional independenceExam favourite P(a ∧ b | c) = P(a | c) P(b | c) Written a ⫫ b | c. Lesson: Independence, conditional independence and naive Bayes Naive Bayes P(Cause, e₁, ..., eₙ) = P(Cause) × Π P(eᵢ | Cause) Then P(Cause | e) = α P(Cause) Π P(eᵢ | Cause). Lesson: Independence, conditional independence and naive Bayes Laplace (add one) smoothing P(w | c) = (count(w, c) + 1) ÷ (total words in c + vocabulary size) A word never seen with class c no longer gives probability 0. Lesson: Independence, conditional independence and naive Bayes Product rule P(a ∧ b) = P(a | b) P(b) = P(b | a) P(a) Bayes’ rule comes from these two forms. Lesson: The maths: rules every probability step uses Chain ruleExam favourite P(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π over i of P(xᵢ | x₁, ..., xᵢ₋₁) Always true. Bayesian networks shorten each condition. Lesson: The maths: rules every probability step uses Bayes with background evidence P(c | e, b) = P(e | c, b) P(c | b) ÷ P(e | b) Keep b on every term. Lesson: The maths: rules every probability step uses Expected value E[X] = Σ x P(X = x) Used again for decisions and for reinforcement learning. Lesson: The maths: rules every probability step uses Variance Var[X] = E[X²] − (E[X])² How spread out X is. Lesson: The maths: rules every probability step uses Tables Table: · probability theory · logic with default rules · certainty factors (mycin) Probability theory Logic with default rules Certainty factors (MYCIN) Fuzzy logic Dempster Shafer theory What a number means degree of belief that a fact is true no numbers, rules hold \"by default\" change in belief from one rule"],[50,"probability-basics","Probability for AI agents","degree to which a vague fact holds (\"tall\") belief and plausibility, an interval Combines evidence by Bayes' rule withdrawing conclusions fixed formulas per rule min and max Dempster's rule Sound for chains of reasoning Yes Partly No (can double count evidence) It answers a different question (vagueness) Yes, but costly Used today Everywhere: medicine, robotics, speech, spam Knowledge bases Historic Control systems Sensor fusion niches Lesson: Probability compared with other ways to handle uncertainty Tips and traps Remember Why these rules and no others: de Finetti (1931) showed that an agent whose beliefs break the axioms can be offered a set of bets it accepts and always loses (a Dutch book). So a rational agent that bets on its beliefs must follow the axioms.Lesson: Why agents need probability Common mistake P(a | b) is not \"if b then a with probability p\". It is belief in a when b is all the agent knows. If more evidence c arrives, the agent must use P(a | b ∧ c).Lesson: Why agents need probability Common mistake The base rate fallacy: most people guess about 90 percent for the test above. Healthy people are so many that their few false positives outnumber the true positives.Lesson: Bayes' rule Remember Naive Bayes is called naive because the effects are rarely truly independent given the class. It still classifies well in practice (spam filters, document topics), because the class with the highest score is often right even when the probabilities themselves are off.Lesson: Independence, conditional independence and naive Bayes 9.2"],[50,"bayesian-networks","Bayesian networks and Markov networks","Open chapter Directed and undirected graphical models: CPTs, d-separation, Markov blankets, factors and Z. Key ideas Bayesian network DAG + CPT per node. P(x₁..xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | parents). Size Σ 2^(parents). Burglary: 10 vs 31. d-separation Chain and fork blocked if the middle is observed. Collider blocked unless the middle or a descendant is observed. Markov blanket Parents, children, children’s other parents. Given it, a node is independent of all others. Noisy OR P(¬x | causes) = Π q_j over present causes. n numbers instead of 2ⁿ. Markov network P(x) = (1 ÷ Z) Π φ_c(x_c), Z = Σ_x Π φ_c. Separation in the graph gives independence. Moralise a BN: marry parents, drop arrows. Key numbers P(j, m, a, ¬b, ¬e) ≈ 0.000628. Misconception network Z = 7,201,840. Formulas Network semanticsExam favourite P(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π over i of P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)) One table entry per variable. Lesson: Bayesian networks: a graph plus small tables Size of a CPT a true or false node with k true or false parents needs 2ᵏ numbers One number per row, since P(false) = 1 − P(true). Lesson: Bayesian networks: a graph plus small tables Size of the network Σ over nodes of 2^(number of parents) n nodes with at most k parents: at most n 2ᵏ, against 2ⁿ − 1. Lesson: Bayesian networks: a graph plus small tables Markov blanketExam favourite parents, children, and the children’s other parents Given its blanket, a node is independent of every other node. Lesson: Reading independence from the graph: d-separation Noisy ORExam favourite P(¬x | parents) = Π over true parents j of q_j q_j is the chance that cause j alone fails. Lesson: Compact tables: deterministic nodes, noisy OR and continuous variables Linear Gaussian P(x | h) = N(x | mean a h + b, variance σ²) A continuous child of a continuous parent h. Lesson: Compact tables: deterministic nodes, noisy OR and continuous variables Gibbs distributionExam favourite P(x) = (1 ÷ Z) Π over cliques c of φ_c(x_c) Factors need not add up to 1. Lesson: Undirected models: Markov networks Partition function Z = Σ over all worlds x of Π φ_c(x_c) A sum with 2ⁿ terms: computing it is the hard part. Lesson: Undirected models: Markov networks Hammersley Clifford theorem a positive distribution has the independencies of the graph exactly when it factorises over the cliques Links the graph to the formula. Lesson: Undirected models: Markov networks Log linear (energy) form P(x) = (1 ÷ Z) exp(Σ wᵢ fᵢ(x)) Used by conditional random fields and Boltzmann machines. Lesson: Undirected models: Markov networks Parameters with at most k parentsExam favourite n 2ᵏ, against 2ⁿ − 1 for the full joint n = 30, k = 5: 960 against about 1.07 × 10⁹. Lesson: The maths: counting parameters and checking independence Local Markov property X ⫫ NonDescendants(X) | Parents(X) Equivalent to the product formula for a Bayesian network. Lesson: The maths: counting parameters and checking independence Markov blanket property P(X | everything else) = P(X | MB(X)) ∝ P(X | parents) × Π over children Y of P(y | parents(Y)) Used by Gibbs sampling in chapter 9.3. Lesson: The maths: counting parameters and checking independence Moral ancestral graph test X ⫫ Y | Z exactly when X and Y are not joined in the moralised graph of the ancestors of X, Y and Z, after removing Z Lauritzen and others, 1990. This is the algorithm in the code above. Lesson: The maths: counting parameters and checking independence Tables Table: node · parents · table (p of true) Node Parents Table (P of true) Burglary none 0.001 Earthquake none 0.002 Alarm Burglary, Earthquake b e: 0.95, b ¬e: 0.94, ¬b e: 0.29, ¬b ¬e: 0.001 JohnCalls Alarm a: 0.90, ¬a: 0.05 MaryCalls Alarm a: 0.70, ¬a: 0.01 Lesson: Bayesian networks: a graph plus small tables Table: pattern · example · blocked when · plain meaning Pattern Example Blocked when Plain meaning Chain A → M → B Burglary → Alarm → JohnCalls M is observed once you know the alarm rang, the burglary tells nothing more about John Fork A ← M → B JohnCalls ← Alarm →"],[50,"bayesian-networks","Bayesian networks and Markov networks","MaryCalls M is observed both calls depend only on the alarm Collider A → M ← B Burglary → Alarm ← Earthquake M and all its descendants are not observed two causes are independent until we see their common effect (explaining away) Lesson: Reading independence from the graph: d-separation Table: · bayesian network (directed) · markov network (undirected) Bayesian network (directed) Markov network (undirected) Parts CPTs P(X | parents) factors φ on cliques Normalised each CPT row adds up to 1, so the product is a probability only after dividing by Z Independence test d-separation plain graph separation Can express explaining away (colliders) cycles of mutual influence, such as A - B - C - D - A Learning the numbers counting, one table at a time needs Z, so iterative methods Lesson: Undirected models: Markov networks Table: · full joint table · naive bayes · bayesian network Full joint table Naive Bayes Bayesian network Markov network Factor graph Structure none one cause, independent effects directed graph, no cycles undirected graph variables joined to factors Size 2ⁿ − 1 2n + 1 Σ 2^(parents) Σ clique table sizes Σ factor table sizes Strength answers anything tiny and fast causal, readable, easy to learn from counts symmetric relations, images, text labels one form for both directed and undirected models, used by message passing Weakness exponential size ignores links between effects cannot show some symmetric dependencies Z is hard, numbers are hard to read less intuitive for experts Lesson: Graphical models compared Tips and traps Remember Build a network in causal order: causes first, then effects. The diagnostic order (MaryCalls, JohnCalls, Alarm, Burglary, Earthquake) is still correct but needs more arrows and harder numbers, such as P(Earthquake | Burglary, Alarm).Lesson: Bayesian networks: a graph plus small tables Remember Explaining away: if the alarm rang, a burglary is likely. If we then learn an earthquake happened, the burglary becomes less likely, because the earthquake already explains the alarm. The two causes were independent before we saw the alarm.Lesson: Reading independence from the graph: d-separation Remember To turn a Bayesian network into a Markov network, moralise it: join the parents of each child (they \"marry\"), then drop the arrow heads. The result may lose some independencies, such as Burglary ⫫ Earthquake.Lesson: Undirected models: Markov networks 9.3"],[50,"bn-inference","Inference in Bayesian networks","Open chapter Enumeration, variable elimination, complexity, rejection sampling, likelihood weighting and Gibbs sampling. Key ideas Enumeration P(X | e) = α Σ_y Π P(xᵢ | parents). O(2ⁿ) time, O(n) space. P(b | j, m) ≈ 0.284. Variable elimination Factors from CPTs with evidence. Multiply factors with X, sum out X. Cost exponential in the largest factor (treewidth). Drop non ancestors. Complexity Polytree: linear. General: #P-hard (Cooper 1990). Samplers Rejection: N(x, e) ÷ N(e). LW: w = Π P(eᵢ | parents). Gibbs: sample from P(x | mb(X)). Error ≈ √(p(1 − p) ÷ N). Key numbers LW sample weight 0.1 × 0.99 = 0.099. P(Rain | s, w) = 0.3204. P(Rain | s) = 0.3. Formulas EnumerationExam favourite P(B | j, m) = α Σ_e Σ_a P(B) P(e) P(a | B, e) P(j | a) P(m | a) Hidden variables E and A are summed out. Lesson: Exact inference by enumeration Moving factors outside α P(B) Σ_e P(e) Σ_a P(a | B, e) P(j | a) P(m | a) P(B) does not depend on e or a, so it moves out of both sums. Lesson: Exact inference by enumeration Cost time O(2ⁿ), memory O(n) Depth first over the hidden variables. Lesson: Exact inference by enumeration Pointwise product f(X, Y) × g(Y, Z) = h(X, Y, Z), with h(x, y, z) = f(x, y) g(y, z) The result covers the union of the variables. Lesson: Variable elimination Summing outExam favourite h(X, Z) = Σ_y f(X, y, Z) Add the entries for each value of the removed variable. Lesson: Variable elimination Cost exponential in the size of the largest factor made, which depends on the order The best possible largest factor size, minus one, is the treewidth of the graph. Lesson: Variable elimination Rejection sampling P(X | e) ≈ N(X, e) ÷ N(e) Consistent, but wastes the fraction 1 − P(e) of samples. Lesson: Approximate inference by sampling Likelihood weightExam favourite w = Π over evidence variables Eᵢ of P(eᵢ | parents(Eᵢ)) Estimate: sum of weights with X true ÷ sum of all weights. Lesson: Approximate inference by sampling Gibbs step P(x | mb(X)) = α P(x | parents(X)) Π over children Y of P(y | parents(Y)) Only the Markov blanket is needed. Lesson: Approximate inference by sampling Error of an estimate standard error ≈ √(p (1 − p) ÷ N) Four times the samples halves the error. Lesson: Approximate inference by sampling Prior sampling is consistent S(x₁, ..., xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)) = P(x₁, ..., xₙ) Each world is drawn with exactly its probability. Lesson: The maths: why the samplers give the right answer Likelihood weighting is consistentExam favourite S_WS(z, e) w(z, e) = Π P(zᵢ | parents) × Π P(eᵢ | parents) = P(z, e) The sampling distribution times the weight is the true joint. Lesson: The maths: why the samplers give the right answer Gibbs sampling the posterior P(x | e) is the stationary distribution of the Markov chain Detailed balance: π(x) q(x → x′) = π(x′) q(x′ → x). Lesson: The maths: why the samplers give the right answer Variable elimination size eliminating X makes a factor over all variables that share a factor with X Bad orders build large factors. Lesson: The maths: why the samplers give the right answer Tables Table: · enumeration · variable elimination · junction tree Enumeration Variable elimination Junction tree Rejection sampling Likelihood weighting Gibbs sampling (MCMC) Loopy belief propagation Exact Yes Yes Yes No (converges) No (converges) No (converges) No (may not converge) Cost O(2ⁿ) time, O(n) memory exponential in the largest factor exponential in the largest cluster grows as 1 ÷ P(e) cheap per sample cheap per step cheap per pass Good for tiny networks, checking one query, small treewidth all marginals at once likely evidence evidence near the roots large networks, any evidence large networks with loops, error correcting codes Weak when more than about 25 variables large treewidth large treewidth rare evidence evidence at the leaves near deterministic tables (slow mixing) strong loops Lesson: Inference methods compared Tips and traps Common mistake Enumeration repeats work: P(j | a) P(m | a) is computed"],[50,"bn-inference","Inference in Bayesian networks","again for each value of e. Variable elimination stores such parts once.Lesson: Exact inference by enumeration Remember Irrelevant variables: every variable that is not an ancestor of the query or the evidence can be removed before starting. Summing it out only gives factors full of 1s, as the second tip of the animation shows.Lesson: Variable elimination Common mistake Likelihood weighting suffers when evidence comes late in the order (at the leaves): the samples ignore it while choosing the earlier variables, so most of them get tiny weights.Lesson: Approximate inference by sampling 9.4"],[50,"causality","Causality: seeing, doing and imagining","Open chapter Causal networks, the do operator, back door adjustment, Simpson’s paradox and counterfactuals. Key ideas Ladder Seeing P(y | x), doing P(y | do(x)), imagining counterfactuals. do operator Cut arrows into X, drop its CPT (truncated product). Root X: do equals see. Back door Z has no descendant of T and blocks all T ← ... paths: P(r | do(t)) = Σ_z P(r | t, z) P(z). Simpson's paradox Kidney stones: seeing A 0.78 < B 0.826, doing A 0.833 > B 0.779. Adjust for confounders, not mediators or colliders. Counterfactual Abduction (find U), action (set X), prediction (recompute Y). Front door: Σ_m P(m | x) Σ_x′ P(y | m, x′) P(x′). Sprinkler P(Rain | s) = 0.3 but P(Rain | do(s)) = 0.5. P(WetGrass | do(s)) = 0.945. Formulas Truncated factorisationExam favourite P(v | do(x)) = Π over i with Vᵢ not X of P(vᵢ | parents(Vᵢ)), with X = x The table of X is removed. Lesson: The do operator and graph surgery Root causes if X has no parents, P(y | do(x)) = P(y | x) Nothing to cut, so seeing equals doing. Lesson: The do operator and graph surgery Average causal effect ACE = P(y | do(x)) − P(y | do(¬x)) The quantity a randomised trial estimates. Lesson: The do operator and graph surgery Back door adjustmentExam favourite P(r | do(t)) = Σ_z P(r | t, z) P(z) Weight each group by its share of everyone, not its share among those treated. Lesson: Confounders, the back door criterion and Simpson's paradox Compare with seeing P(r | t) = Σ_z P(r | t, z) P(z | t) The only difference is P(z | t) in place of P(z). Lesson: Confounders, the back door criterion and Simpson's paradox Back doorExam favourite P(y | do(x)) = Σ_z P(y | x, z) P(z) Z blocks every back door path and has no descendants of X. Lesson: The maths: adjustment, front door and effects Front door P(y | do(x)) = Σ_m P(m | x) Σ_x′ P(y | m, x′) P(x′) M carries all of the effect and has no hidden common cause with X. Lesson: The maths: adjustment, front door and effects Inverse probability weighting P(y | do(x)) = Σ over units with X = x of 1 ÷ (N P(x | z)) Same answer as back door adjustment, used with many variables in Z. Lesson: The maths: adjustment, front door and effects Tables Table: rung · question · written as · example Rung Question Written as Example 1. Association (seeing) What if I see x? P(y | x) Patients who took the drug recovered more often. 2. Intervention (doing) What if I do x? P(y | do(x)) Would recovery rise if we gave everyone the drug? 3. Counterfactual (imagining) What if I had done x instead? P(y_x | x′, y′) This patient took the drug and recovered. Would they have recovered without it? Lesson: Seeing, doing and imagining: the ladder of causation Table: role of z · graph · adjust for z? Role of Z Graph Adjust for Z? Confounder T ← Z → R Yes: it closes the back door Mediator T → Z → R No: it would remove part of the effect you want Collider T → Z ← R No: conditioning on it creates a false link (Berkson’s paradox) Lesson: Confounders, the back door criterion and Simpson's paradox Table: · randomised trial · back door adjustment · front door adjustment Randomised trial Back door adjustment Front door adjustment Instrumental variables Causal discovery from data Needs the power to assign treatment all confounders measured a mediator that carries the whole effect a variable that moves T but affects R only through T assumptions such as faithfulness Gives P(r | do(t)) directly P(r | do(t)) from observations P(r | do(t)) even with a hidden confounder the effect under linear or monotone models a set of possible graphs Weak when costly, slow or unethical a confounder is missed the mediator has other causes shared with T the instrument is weak data are few or variables hidden Example drug trials kidney stones smoking, tar and cancer distance to a hospital as an instrument the PC algorithm (Spirtes and Glymour, 1991) Lesson: Ways to learn what causes what Tips and traps Common mistake A plain Bayesian network can be read in a non causal order and still give the right P(y |"],[50,"causality","Causality: seeing, doing and imagining","x). It can only answer rung 2 questions when its arrows are causal.Lesson: Seeing, doing and imagining: the ladder of causation Remember A randomised controlled trial (Fisher, 1935) performs do(X) in the real world: a coin, not the patient or the doctor, decides the treatment, so every arrow into the treatment is cut.Lesson: The do operator and graph surgery Common mistake Simpson’s paradox is not a fault in arithmetic. Which answer is right, the pooled one or the one inside groups, depends on the causal graph. With stone size as a confounder, the group answer (A is better) is right. If Z were a mediator, the pooled answer would be right.Lesson: Confounders, the back door criterion and Simpson's paradox"],[50,"causality","Step 10Core AI · Reinforcement learning","10.1"],[50,"mdp","Markov decision processes","Open chapter MDPs, the Bellman equation, value iteration, policy iteration and Q-values. Key ideas MDP States, actions, P(s′ | s, a), R, γ. Solution: policy π(s). Utility = E[Σ γᵗ Rₜ] ≤ R_max ÷ (1 − γ). Bellman U(s) = R(s) + γ max_a Σ P(s′ | s, a) U(s′). Q(s, a) = Σ P [R + γ max Q(s′, a′)]. U = max_a Q. Value iteration Repeat the update. Contraction by γ. Stop when δ < ε(1 − γ) ÷ γ. 4 × 3: U(1,1) = 0.705. Policy iteration Evaluate (linear equations, O(n³)), improve greedily, stop when unchanged. Few rounds. Racing car γ = 0.9: V₁ = (2, 1), V₂ = (3.35, 2.35). Optimal: cool fast, warm slow, V = (15.5, 14.5). Formulas Utility of a reward sequenceExam favourite U = R₀ + γ R₁ + γ² R₂ + ... = Σ γᵗ Rₜ γ between 0 and 1. With γ < 1 the sum is at most R_max ÷ (1 − γ). Lesson: Markov decision processes Utility of a state under π U^π(s) = E[Σ γᵗ R(Sₜ) | S₀ = s, actions from π] Expected discounted reward when starting in s. Lesson: Markov decision processes Optimal policy π*(s) = argmax over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U(s′) One step look ahead with the true utilities. Lesson: Markov decision processes Bellman equationExam favourite U(s) = R(s) + γ max over a of Σ over s′ of P(s′ | s, a) U(s′) One equation per state. Lesson: The Bellman equation and value iteration Bellman update U_(i+1)(s) ← R(s) + γ max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) U_i(s′) Value iteration repeats this. Lesson: The Bellman equation and value iteration Contraction ‖U_(i+1) − U‖ ≤ γ ‖U_i − U‖ Each sweep shrinks the error by at least γ (in the max norm), so value iteration always converges for γ < 1. Lesson: The Bellman equation and value iteration Stopping rule if ‖U_(i+1) − U_i‖ < ε (1 − γ) ÷ γ then ‖U_(i+1) − U‖ < ε A safe test for \"close enough\". Lesson: The Bellman equation and value iteration Policy evaluationExam favourite U^π(s) = R(s) + γ Σ P(s′ | s, π(s)) U^π(s′) n linear equations, O(n³) to solve exactly. Lesson: Policy iteration and Q-values Q-value Q(s, a) = Σ P(s′ | s, a) [R(s, a, s′) + γ max over a′ of Q(s′, a′)] U(s) = max over a of Q(s, a). Lesson: Policy iteration and Q-values Termination there are |A|^|S| policies and each round is at least as good as the last So policy iteration ends after a finite number of rounds. Lesson: Policy iteration and Q-values Bound on utilitiesExam favourite |U(s)| ≤ R_max ÷ (1 − γ) Geometric series: R_max (1 + γ + γ² + ...). Lesson: The maths: bounds and counts Sweeps needed N = ⌈log(2 R_max ÷ (ε (1 − γ))) ÷ log(1 ÷ γ)⌉ Enough sweeps for error below ε, starting from 0. It grows fast as γ nears 1. Lesson: The maths: bounds and counts Policy loss if ‖U_i − U‖ < ε then ‖U^(π_i) − U‖ < 2 ε γ ÷ (1 − γ) The greedy policy is good even before the values have fully converged. Lesson: The maths: bounds and counts Number of policies |A|^|S| 4 × 3 world: 4⁹ = 262,144 policies for the 9 squares where a choice matters. Lesson: The maths: bounds and counts Tables Table: part · 4 × 3 world · racing car Part 4 × 3 world Racing car States 11 squares cool, warm, overheated (end) Actions Up, Down, Left, Right slow, fast Transitions intended move 0.8, each side 0.1 cool + fast: cool or warm (0.5 each), warm + fast: overheated Rewards R = −0.04 per step, +1 and −1 at the end squares slow +1, fast +2, overheating −10 Discount γ = 1 γ = 0.9 Lesson: Markov decision processes Table: · value iteration · policy iteration · modified policy iteration Value iteration Policy iteration Modified policy iteration Linear programming Expectimax search (6.3) Works on utilities policies policies, with a few sweeps for evaluation utilities as LP variables a tree from the current state Cost per round O(|S|² |A|) O(|S|³) for exact evaluation plus O(|S|² |A|) O(k |S|²) plus O(|S|² |A|) polynomial overall exponential in the depth Rounds many when γ is near 1 few few one LP one search per move Best for simple and general small state spaces the usual choice in practice theory: MDPs are solvable in polynomial time huge state spaces, online decisions Lesson: MDP solvers compared Tips"],[50,"mdp","Markov decision processes","and traps Remember Why discount: a reward now is worth more than the same reward later, an agent may stop at any time, and with γ < 1 infinite sums stay finite. γ close to 1 means a patient agent.Lesson: Markov decision processes 10.2"],[50,"passive-rl","Passive reinforcement learning","Open chapter Direct utility estimation, adaptive dynamic programming and temporal difference learning for a fixed policy. Key ideas Passive RL Fixed π, unknown P and R, learn U^π from trials. Direct estimation, ADP, TD. TD(0) U(s) ← U(s) + α (R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s)). δ = TD error. Robbins Monro: Σα = ∞, Σα² < ∞. ADP P̂(s′ | s, a) = N(s, a, s′) ÷ N(s, a), then policy evaluation. Fewest trials, most work per step. Bias and variance Monte Carlo return: unbiased, high variance. TD target: biased, low variance. TD(λ) mixes. Key numbers Three trials: U(1,1) ≈ 0.0933 (true 0.705). P̂((2,3) | (1,3)) = 2/3. Random walk values k ÷ 6. Formulas TD(0) updateExam favourite U(s) ← U(s) + α (R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s)) α is the learning rate. Lesson: Temporal difference learning TD error δ = R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s) Positive: things went better than expected. Lesson: Temporal difference learning Learning rate schedule Σ αₙ = ∞ and Σ αₙ² < ∞, for example αₙ = 1 ÷ n or 60 ÷ (59 + n) Robbins and Monro (1951). Then TD converges. Lesson: Temporal difference learning TD(λ) U(x) ← U(x) + α δ e(x), with traces e(x) that decay by γ λ each step λ = 0 is TD(0), λ = 1 is like averaging whole trials. Lesson: Temporal difference learning Learned modelExam favourite P̂(s′ | s, a) = N(s, a, s′) ÷ N(s, a) Maximum likelihood from counts. Lesson: Adaptive dynamic programming Monte Carlo target Gₜ = Rₜ + γ Rₜ₊₁ + γ² Rₜ₊₂ + ... Unbiased, but high variance: it depends on every later random step. Lesson: The maths: bias, variance and fixed points TD targetExam favourite Rₜ + γ U(Sₜ₊₁) Biased while U is wrong, but low variance: it depends on one step. Lesson: The maths: bias, variance and fixed points Eligibility trace e(s) ← γ λ e(s), then e(Sₜ) ← e(Sₜ) + 1 Credit fades by γλ per step. Lesson: The maths: bias, variance and fixed points Batch TD answer the values of the maximum likelihood Markov model of the data Batch Monte Carlo gives the averages of the observed returns instead. Lesson: The maths: bias, variance and fixed points Tables Table: method · what it learns · update · speed Method What it learns Update Speed Direct utility estimation U(s) as an average average of all rewards to go seen from s slow: ignores links between states Adaptive dynamic programming (ADP) P̂(s′ | s, π(s)) and R(s) solve U(s) = R(s) + γ Σ P̂ U(s′) after each step fastest per trial, costly per step Temporal difference (TD) U(s) directly U(s) ← U(s) + α (R(s) + γ U(s′) − U(s)) cheap per step, a bit slower than ADP Lesson: Passive learning: judge a fixed policy without a model Table: · direct estimation (monte carlo) · adp · td(0) Direct estimation (Monte Carlo) ADP TD(0) TD(λ) Needs a model No learns one No No Work per step tiny solve a system of equations one update update all traced states Data needed most least medium medium, often less than TD(0) Uses the Markov links No Yes, fully Yes, one step Yes, mixed Famous use Monte Carlo evaluation model based RL TD-Gammon (Tesauro, 1995) TD-Gammon also used TD(λ) Lesson: Passive learners compared Tips and traps Remember TD learning from experience is the same idea as the Bellman update, but it uses the one next state that actually happened instead of an average over all next states. Over many steps, states are reached in proportion to P(s′ | s, a), so the averages come out right.Lesson: Temporal difference learning 10.3"],[50,"active-rl","Active reinforcement learning","Open chapter Exploration, Q-learning, SARSA, function approximation and deep Q-networks. Key ideas Explore or exploit ε-greedy, exploration function f(u, n) = R⁺ if n < N_e else u, UCB1 x̄ + √(2 ln n ÷ nᵢ). GLIE. Q-learning Q ← Q + α [r + γ max Q(s′, a′) − Q]. Off policy, model free. Converges with every pair tried and Robbins Monro α. SARSA Q ← Q + α [r + γ Q(s′, a′) − Q] with the a′ actually taken. On policy. Safer path on the cliff. Approximation θ ← θ + α [target − Q̂] ∇Q̂. DQN: replay buffer + target network. Deadly triad. Key numbers ε-greedy best action: 1 − ε + ε/|A|. Corridor Q*: 1, 0.9, 0.81. Cliff: Q-learning path 13 steps. Formulas ε-greedy with probability ε a random action, otherwise argmax over a of Q(s, a) Simple and widely used. Lesson: Active learning: explore or exploit Exploration function (AIMA)Exam favourite f(u, n) = R⁺ if n < N_e, else u Optimistic about actions tried fewer than N_e times. Lesson: Active learning: explore or exploit UCB1 pick argmax over i of x̄ᵢ + √(2 ln n ÷ nᵢ) Mean so far plus a bonus for uncertainty. Its regret grows only like log n. Lesson: Active learning: explore or exploit GLIE try every action infinitely often, and become greedy in the limit A condition under which the learned policy becomes optimal. Lesson: Active learning: explore or exploit Q-learning updateExam favourite Q(s, a) ← Q(s, a) + α [r + γ max over a′ of Q(s′, a′) − Q(s, a)] Off policy: the target uses the best next action. Lesson: Q-learning SARSA update Q(s, a) ← Q(s, a) + α [r + γ Q(s′, a′) − Q(s, a)] On policy: a′ is the action the agent really takes next. Lesson: Q-learning Greedy policy π(s) = argmax over a of Q(s, a) No model needed. Lesson: Q-learning Linear approximation Û_θ(s) = θ₀ + θ₁ f₁(s) + ... + θₙ fₙ(s) f are features, such as the x and y of a square. Lesson: Function approximation and deep Q-networks Gradient TD updateExam favourite θᵢ ← θᵢ + α [target − Q̂(s, a)] ∂Q̂ ÷ ∂θᵢ For a linear Q̂ the derivative is just the feature value. Lesson: Function approximation and deep Q-networks Bellman optimality for QExam favourite Q*(s, a) = Σ P(s′ | s, a) [R(s, a, s′) + γ max over a′ of Q*(s′, a′)] Q-learning is a sampled version of this. Lesson: The maths: the Bellman equation for Q and convergence ε-greedy action chances best action: 1 − ε + ε ÷ |A|, each other action: ε ÷ |A| The random choice can also pick the best action. Lesson: The maths: the Bellman equation for Q and convergence Convergence of Q-learning every (s, a) tried infinitely often, Σ αₜ = ∞, Σ αₜ² < ∞ Watkins and Dayan, 1992, for tables. Lesson: The maths: the Bellman equation for Q and convergence UCB1 regret expected regret after n pulls is O(log n) Lai and Robbins (1985) showed log n is the best possible order. Lesson: The maths: the Bellman equation for Q and convergence Tables Table: · q-learning · sarsa Q-learning SARSA Learns Q* of the greedy policy (off policy) Q of the ε-greedy policy it follows (on policy) Cliff walking path shortest, along the edge (13 steps) safer, one or two rows up Reward while learning lower: it falls more while exploring higher Can learn from any data, even another agent’s its own current policy only Lesson: SARSA, on policy and off policy Table: · greedy adp · adp + exploration function · q-learning Greedy ADP ADP + exploration function Q-learning SARSA DQN Model learned learned none none none Exploration none, gets stuck optimistic values ε-greedy or similar ε-greedy or similar ε-greedy Learns a policy that may be poor near optimal policy Q* (off policy) Q of its own policy (on policy) Q* with a neural network Data use best best one update per step one update per step replays old steps many times Weak when always big state spaces (must solve the model) huge state spaces without approximation same as Q-learning needs millions of frames, can be unstable Lesson: Active learners compared Tips and traps Remember The same trade off appears in the expectimax chapter (6.3) in UCT, which uses UCB1 inside Monte"],[50,"active-rl","Active reinforcement learning","Carlo tree search to pick which move to try next.Lesson: Active learning: explore or exploit Common mistake With function approximation, Q-learning can diverge: off policy data, bootstrapping and approximation together (the deadly triad). DQN’s replay buffer and target network were added to keep training stable.Lesson: Function approximation and deep Q-networks 10.4"],[50,"policy-search","Policy search and policy gradients","Open chapter Policy gradients, REINFORCE, baselines, actor critic and learning from experts. Key ideas Policy search Parameterise π_θ, maximise ρ(θ) = E[Σ γᵗ Rₜ]. Use stochastic (softmax) policies so ρ is smooth. Policy gradient ∇ρ = E[∇ log π_θ(a | s) Q^π(s, a)]. Softmax: ∂ log π(a) ÷ ∂θ_k = 1[k = a] − π(k). REINFORCE θ ← θ + α (G − b) ∇ log π(a | s). Baseline unbiased since E[∇ log π] = 0. Actor critic δ = r + γV(s′) − V(s). Actor θ += α δ ∇ log π, critic w += β δ ∇V. PPO clips the ratio. From experts Behaviour cloning copies actions. Inverse RL learns the reward (Ng and Russell 2000). Formulas Policy valueExam favourite ρ(θ) = E[Σ γᵗ Rₜ | actions from π_θ] What policy search maximises. Lesson: Policy search: tune the policy directly Softmax policy π_θ(a | s) = e^(θ · f(s, a)) ÷ Σ over b of e^(θ · f(s, b)) Every action keeps some chance, so the policy explores. Lesson: Policy search: tune the policy directly Finite difference gradient ∂ρ ÷ ∂θᵢ ≈ (ρ(θ + h eᵢ) − ρ(θ)) ÷ h Works for any policy, but needs many trials per parameter and is noisy. Lesson: Policy search: tune the policy directly Policy gradient theoremExam favourite ∇ρ(θ) = E[∇_θ log π_θ(a | s) Q^π(s, a)] Only the gradient of the policy appears. Lesson: The policy gradient and REINFORCE REINFORCE update θ ← θ + α (Gₜ − b) ∇_θ log π_θ(aₜ | sₜ) b is a baseline that does not depend on the action. Lesson: The policy gradient and REINFORCE Softmax gradient ∂ log π(a) ÷ ∂θ_k = 1[k = a] − π(k) For a softmax over preferences θ. Lesson: The policy gradient and REINFORCE Why a baseline is safe E[∇ log π(a)] = Σ π(a) ∇ log π(a) = ∇ Σ π(a) = ∇ 1 = 0 So subtracting b changes no expected value. Lesson: The policy gradient and REINFORCE Actor criticExam favourite δ = r + γ V(s′) − V(s), θ ← θ + α δ ∇ log π_θ(a | s) δ estimates the advantage of the action taken. Lesson: Actor critic and other refinements Advantage A(s, a) = Q(s, a) − V(s) How much better a is than the policy’s average action. Lesson: Actor critic and other refinements PPO clipped objective E[min(r_t(θ) Â, clip(r_t(θ), 1 − ε, 1 + ε) Â)], with r_t = π_θ ÷ π_old Keeps each policy update small and safe. Lesson: Actor critic and other refinements Log derivative trickExam favourite ∇ π(a) = π(a) ∇ log π(a) Turns a gradient of a sum into an expectation. Lesson: The maths: deriving the policy gradient One step (bandit) case ∇ρ = ∇ Σ π(a) R(a) = Σ π(a) ∇ log π(a) R(a) = E[∇ log π(a) R(a)] Sample a, multiply the gradient of log π(a) by its reward. Lesson: The maths: deriving the policy gradient Exact bandit gradient for a softmax ∂ρ ÷ ∂θ_k = π(k) (μ_k − ρ) An action with above average reward gets pushed up. Lesson: The maths: deriving the policy gradient Variance with a baseline Var[(G − b) ∇ log π] is smallest near b = E[G] The usual choice is an estimate of V(s). Lesson: The maths: deriving the policy gradient Tables Table: · q-learning (value based) · reinforce · actor critic Q-learning (value based) REINFORCE Actor critic Finite differences or evolution Imitation and inverse RL Learns Q(s, a), acts greedily π_θ directly π_θ and V_w π_θ by trying perturbed θ π from an expert, or a reward Actions discrete (needs a max) discrete or continuous discrete or continuous any any Variance low high medium high, unless correlated sampling low (supervised) Data efficiency good (off policy, replay) poor (on policy) better poor good with an expert Known for DQN simple, unbiased A3C, PPO, SAC PEGASUS, evolution strategies (Salimans and others, 2017) self driving data, helicopters Lesson: Value based and policy based methods compared Tips and traps Common mistake With a greedy policy (always the best action under some score) ρ(θ) jumps when the best action changes, so its gradient is zero almost everywhere. That is why policy gradient methods use stochastic policies.Lesson: Policy search: tune the policy directly"],[50,"step-core-11","Step 11Core AI · Challenge and revision","11.1"],[50,"core-challenge","Challenge problems across Core AI","Open chapter Hard exam style problems across every Core AI step, with proofs and a method guide. Key ideas Five moves Name the model, write the formula, check its conditions, work step by step, say what it means. Search traps Admissible is not enough for graph search without reopening. IDS costs about b ÷ (b − 1) times BFS. CSPs and games Tree CSP O(n d²). Alpha beta best case b^⌈d/2⌉ + b^⌊d/2⌋ − 1. Expectimax needs real values. Logic and planning Resolution refutes KB ∧ ¬α. Occurs check. Regress: (goal − add) ∪ pre. Promotion or demotion. Uncertainty and RL Base rates. Observed descendant opens a collider. ‖BU − BU′‖ ≤ γ ‖U − U′‖. Q += α (r + γ max Q′ − Q). Tables Table: move · ask yourself · example Move Ask yourself Example 1. Name the model What are the states, actions, variables or rules? A route finder is a search problem with a step cost. 2. Write the formula Which equation decides the answer? f(n) = g(n) + h(n) for A*. 3. Check the conditions Does the theorem’s condition hold here? Is h consistent, or only admissible? 4. Work it step by step Can a reader follow each line? Write the frontier after every expansion. 5. Say what it means Is the answer sensible? A probability must lie between 0 and 1. Lesson: How to attack a hard Core AI problem Table: problem looks like · use · why · watch out for Problem looks like Use Why Watch out for Find a path, all step costs equal BFS or IDS Optimal for equal costs, IDS uses little memory BFS memory grows as bᵈ Find the cheapest path, a good estimate known A* (IDA* or SMA* if memory is short) Optimal with a consistent h, focused search Inconsistent h in graph search Actions may fail or have several outcomes AND-OR search, AO* Returns a conditional plan Cycles need loops in the plan Only the final state matters Hill climbing, annealing, genetic algorithms Little memory, works on huge spaces Local maxima and plateaus Assign values under constraints Backtracking with MRV, forward checking, AC-3, or min-conflicts Prunes whole sets of assignments Variable order changes the time a lot An opponent moves against you Minimax with alpha beta, MCTS for huge trees Assumes the opponent plays well Evaluation function quality Chance moves in a game Expectimax, expectiminimax Uses averages over chance Needs real values, not only their order Facts and rules, need a proof Forward or backward chaining, resolution Sound and complete for their logic First order logic is only semi decidable Reach a goal by a sequence of actions STRIPS with forward or backward search, partial order planning Factored states, reuse of heuristics Sub-goal interaction Uncertain facts Bayesian networks, exact or sampling inference Compact, answers any query Exact inference is NP hard in general Cause and effect, an intervention Causal graphs and do-calculus Separates seeing from doing Hidden confounders Decide over time, model known Value or policy iteration Exact optimal policy Large state spaces Decide over time, model unknown TD learning, Q-learning, SARSA, policy gradients Learn from experience Exploration and slow learning Lesson: Choosing the right method Tips and traps Remember Each challenge below names the chapter that teaches the idea, so you can go back if a step is not clear.Lesson: How to attack a hard Core AI problem In the exam The fix: use a consistent heuristic, or let graph search reopen a closed node when a cheaper path to it turns up. Tree search with an admissible heuristic is always optimal, because it never closes anything.Lesson: Challenge set 1: search and heuristics In the exam Forward chaining is complete for definite clauses only. Resolution is refutation complete for any clauses. A favourite exam trap gives a KB with a disjunction such as A ∨ B and asks for forward chaining.Lesson: Challenge set 4: logic and inference In the exam The answer P(B | j, m) ≈ 0.284 from chapter 9.3 is worth knowing by heart. Exams often give the burglary tables and ask for it, or for P(J | b) by summing out A.Lesson:"],[50,"core-challenge","Challenge problems across Core AI","Challenge set 6: probability and Bayesian networks 11.2"],[50,"core-revision","Exam revision: from school to research universities","Open chapter Core AI exam questions from school level to research universities, with worked answers, a formula sheet and a mock quiz. Formulas Rows of a truth tableExam favourite 2ⁿ n symbols, each true or false. Lesson: Tier 1: school and first year Nodes at depth dExam favourite bᵈ Each node has b children. Lesson: Tier 1: school and first year Chance of an event P(A) = favourable cases ÷ all equally likely cases Only when every case is equally likely. Lesson: Tier 1: school and first year Tables Table: tier · typical format · what earns the marks · where to practise Tier Typical format What earns the marks Where to practise School and first year Short answers, small trees, simple chances A clear definition with an example, careful counting Lesson 2 University semester and IIT course exams Run an algorithm by hand on a small graph, net or MDP The frontier or table after every step, the answer stated with its meaning Lesson 3 GATE and NPTEL style papers Multiple choice, multiple select and numerical answer type Speed, quick checks that rule out options, exact rounding Lesson 4 MIT, Berkeley, Stanford, CMU and IISc style Proofs, derivations and critique of a method Conditions named, a complete argument, when it breaks Lesson 5 Lesson: How Core AI is examined Table: quick check · use it to Quick check Use it to BFS and IDS are optimal only when every step costs the same Reject \"BFS is optimal\" for unequal costs DFS is not complete on infinite or cyclic spaces without a check Reject \"DFS always finds a solution\" The max of admissible heuristics is admissible, the sum usually is not Settle multiple select questions on heuristics Alpha beta never changes the root value Reject options where pruning gives a new answer A node is independent of its non descendants given its parents Read independence straight off a net P(A ∨ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∧ B) Avoid adding overlapping cases twice Rejection sampling keeps about N × P(e) samples Spot why it fails for rare evidence Q-learning is off policy, SARSA is on policy Answer the cliff walking question in one line Lesson: Tier 3: GATE and NPTEL style papers Table: topic · formula or fact Topic Formula or fact BFS nodes, IDS nodes b + b² + … + bᵈ, Σ (d + 1 − i) bⁱ Search memory BFS O(bᵈ), DFS O(bm), IDS O(bd) A* f(n) = g(n) + h(n), optimal with admissible h (tree) or consistent h (graph) Consistency h(n) ≤ c(n, n′) + h(n′) and h(goal) = 0 Effective branching factor N + 1 = 1 + b* + (b*)² + … + (b*)ᵈ Simulated annealing accept a worse move with probability e^(ΔE ÷ T) Tree CSP O(n d²) Alpha beta best case b^⌈d ÷ 2⌉ + b^⌊d ÷ 2⌋ − 1 leaves Resolution from A ∨ ℓ and B ∨ ¬ℓ infer A ∨ B, prove KB ⊨ α by refuting KB ∧ ¬α STRIPS regression new goal = (goal minus add list) plus preconditions Bayes rule P(h | e) = P(e | h) P(h) ÷ P(e), P(e) = Σ P(e | h) P(h) Bayesian network P(x₁, …, xₙ) = Π P(xᵢ | parents(Xᵢ)) Adjustment for a cause P(y | do(x)) = Σ over z of P(y | x, z) P(z) Bellman optimality V(s) = max over a of Σ P(s′ | s, a) (R + γ V(s′)) TD and Q-learning V ← V + α (r + γ V(s′) − V), Q ← Q + α (r + γ max Q(s′, ·) − Q) REINFORCE θ ← θ + α (G − b) ∇ log π(a | s) Lesson: Formula sheet and last minute checks Tips and traps In the exam Round only at the very end. A numerical answer type key often has a narrow range such as 0.69 to 0.70.Lesson: Tier 3: GATE and NPTEL style papers In the exam In proofs, name each condition when you use it: consistency, positive step costs, definite clauses, a finite domain, γ < 1. Examiners often ask what breaks when one fails.Lesson: Tier 4: proofs, derivations and critique"],[50,"step-advanced-1","Step 1Advanced AI · Rational choice","1.1"],[50,"decision-problems","Decision problems and preferences","Open chapter Decision problems, the three preference relations, completeness, transitivity, cycles and what carries over from weak to strict preference. Key ideas Decision problem (A, X, c, ⪰): actions, outcomes, consequence map c : A → X, preference. Utility goes on outcomes, v(a) = u(c(a)). Three relations Weak x ⪰ y. Strict x ≻ y means x ⪰ y and not y ⪰ x. Indifference x ∼ y means both directions. Rationality Complete (every pair compared) plus transitive (rankings chain). Cycles have no best element and allow a money pump. What carries over Transitivity of ⪰ passes to ≻ and ∼. Completeness does not pass to either. Indifference is not incompleteness Indifferent: equally good. Incomplete: cannot compare. Equality: the same object. Formulas A decision problemExam favourite ( A, X, c, ⪰ ) A is the set of actions you can take. X is the set of outcomes that can happen. c is the map that says which outcome each action leads to. ⪰ is your preference over outcomes. Lesson: What a decision problem is The consequence map c : A → X With certainty, each action leads to exactly one outcome. Later chapters let an action lead to a lottery over outcomes. Lesson: What a decision problem is Action value v(a) = u( c(a) ) We like outcomes, not actions. An action is worth whatever its outcome is worth. Lesson: What a decision problem is Weak preferenceExam favourite x ⪰ y x is at least as good as y. It allows a tie. Lesson: Weak preference, strict preference and indifference Strict preferenceExam favourite x ≻ y ⟺ x ⪰ y and not y ⪰ x x is better and the reverse does not hold. Lesson: Weak preference, strict preference and indifference IndifferenceExam favourite x ∼ y ⟺ x ⪰ y and y ⪰ x Both directions hold, so the two are equally good. Lesson: Weak preference, strict preference and indifference CompletenessExam favourite for all x, y ∈ X: x ⪰ y or y ⪰ x You can always compare. You may say better, worse or equally good, but you may not leave a pair unanswered. Lesson: Completeness and transitivity TransitivityExam favourite x ⪰ y and y ⪰ z ⟹ x ⪰ z Rankings chain together without contradiction. Lesson: Completeness and transitivity Rational preference rational = complete + transitive A rational agent has such a preference and picks an action whose outcome it likes most. Lesson: Completeness and transitivity Tables Table: part · everyday example · ai example Part Everyday example AI example Actions A Go to INOX or go to PVR Move left, right, up or down Outcomes X The film you end up watching The square the robot reaches Consequence c INOX shows Aliens Moving right from (1, 1) reaches (2, 1) Preference ⪰ Aliens is better than Casablanca The charging dock is better than the stairs Lesson: What a decision problem is Table: relation · pairs it contains Relation Pairs it contains ⪰ weak (A, A), (B, B), (C, C), (A, B), (A, C), (B, C), (C, B) ≻ strict (A, B), (A, C) ∼ indifference (A, A), (B, B), (C, C), (B, C), (C, B) Lesson: Weak preference, strict preference and indifference Table: does it carry over from ⪰? · to ≻ · to ∼ Does it carry over from ⪰? To ≻ To ∼ Transitivity Yes, proof above Yes, proof above Completeness No. If x ∼ y then neither x ≻ y nor y ≻ x No. If x ≻ y then x ∼ y is false and so is y ∼ x Reflexivity No. x ≻ x never holds Yes. x ⪰ x in both directions Lesson: Completeness and transitivity Tips and traps Common mistake Equality and indifference are different ideas. Equality means two things are the same object. Indifference means two different outcomes are judged equally good. Tea and coffee are not equal, but you may be indifferent between them.Lesson: Weak preference, strict preference and indifference Common mistake Completeness does not mean you like every option. It means you can compare any two, possibly by saying you are indifferent.Lesson: From intuition to research 1.2"],[50,"utility-functions","Utility functions","Open chapter Utility representation, ordinal invariance, the existence theorem with its construction proof, and counting rankings. Key ideas Representation x ⪰ y ⟺ u(x) ≥ u(y). Strict gives >, indifference gives =. Not \"=\" for weak preference. Ordinal Only order matters. Any strictly increasing f gives f(u), another valid utility. Gaps and ratios mean nothing. Existence Finite X + complete + transitive ⟹ a utility exists. Build it by peeling off top classes: A ≻ B ∼ C ≻ D gives 3, 2, 2, 1. Best exists In finite X an endless chain of improvements must cycle, which transitivity forbids. Ties at the top are allowed. Counting n strict outcomes give n! rankings (5! = 120). With ties: 1, 3, 13, 75 weak orders. Formulas Utility function u : X → ℝ A score for each outcome. It may stand for satisfaction, value, benefit or happiness. It is not money unless the model says so. Lesson: A number for every outcome RepresentationExam favourite x ⪰ y ⟺ u(x) ≥ u(y) u represents ⪰ when the order of the numbers is exactly the order of the preference. Lesson: A number for every outcome The two consequences x ≻ y ⟺ u(x) > u(y), x ∼ y ⟺ u(x) = u(y) Strict preference matches a strictly larger number, and a tie matches equal numbers. Lesson: A number for every outcome Ordinal invarianceExam favourite if f is strictly increasing, v(x) = f( u(x) ) represents the same ⪰ Examples: 2u + 7, u³, eᵘ, and log u when u is positive. A decreasing f such as −u reverses the ranking. Lesson: Ordinal and cardinal utility Cardinal utility u(A) − u(B) compared with u(C) − u(D) Cardinal utility treats the size of a difference as meaningful. It needs more assumptions than ranking alone, and it returns in chapter 2.2 with lotteries. Lesson: Ordinal and cardinal utility Existence theoremExam favourite X finite and ⪰ complete and transitive ⟹ some u : X → ℝ represents ⪰ Three conditions: finitely many outcomes, completeness, transitivity. Lesson: When does a utility function exist? Tables Table: statement about u(a) = 10, u(b) = 5, u(c) = 1 · ordinal meaning · cardinal meaning Statement about u(A) = 10, u(B) = 5, u(C) = 1 Ordinal meaning Cardinal meaning A is preferred to B Yes, meaningful Yes, meaningful A is twice as good as B Not meaningful Not meaningful either. Ratios change with 2u + 7 The step from B to A is bigger than from C to B Not meaningful Meaningful only when u is cardinal Lesson: Ordinal and cardinal utility Table: condition dropped · what goes wrong Condition dropped What goes wrong Transitivity A ≻ B ≻ C ≻ A would need u(A) > u(B) > u(C) > u(A), so u(A) > u(A). Impossible. Completeness Real numbers are always comparable, so any u would rank pairs the agent cannot rank. Finiteness The construction may never end. Lexicographic preference on the plane has no utility at all. Lesson: When does a utility function exist? Tips and traps Common mistake The slide transcript once read \"u(x) = u(y) ⟺ x ⪰ y\". That is a transcription slip. The correct condition for weak preference uses ≥. Equality belongs to indifference only.Lesson: A number for every outcome Common mistake With ordinal utility, differences mean nothing. u(A) − u(B) = 2 does not say A is liked twice as much as some other pair with difference 1.Lesson: From intuition to research 1.3"],[50,"choosing-best-action","Choosing the best action","Open chapter Rational choice as arg max, decision trees, the movie, fruit tree and city park problems, and constrained optimization on an interval. Key ideas Rational choice v(a) = u(c(a)). a* ∈ arg max v, i.e. v(a*) ≥ v(a) for all a. Ties are all rational. Movie tree Utilities 6 to 1, minus 1 per mile. INOX 1 mile, PVR 3. Best: INOX Aliens, net 5. Fruit trees 1.6 Plain: AA at X − 60. Second same tree saves half: AA, OO become X + 5, PP X + 22.5, best AP at X − 55. Parks 1.7 v = √(400c) − c/80 gives √c = 800, c* = 640,000 under the 1,000,000 cap. With √(1600c) the peak 2,560,000 is infeasible, so c* = 1,000,000. Other ideas Satisficing picks the first v ≥ T. Minimizing loss = maximizing −loss. Check feasibility and boundaries. Formulas Value of an actionExam favourite v(a) = u( c(a) ) First find the consequence, then score it. Lesson: From preferences to the best action Rational choiceExam favourite a* ∈ arg maxa ∈ Av(a) ⟺ v(a*) ≥ v(a) for every a arg max is a set. If two actions tie for the top, both are rational. Lesson: From preferences to the best action Loss as negative utility min (y − ŷ)² ⟺ max −(y − ŷ)² Minimizing a loss and maximizing its negative pick the same answer. Lesson: From preferences to the best action Spending after a planExam favourite u(plan) = X + Σ maintenance − Σ reduction X is what you spend with no trees. A smaller number is better. Lesson: Problem 1.6: the fruit trees Variety version second tree of the same kind saves only half Part (d) of the problem. Mixed pairs keep the full saving. Lesson: Problem 1.6: the fruit trees BenefitExam favourite v(c) = √400c − c80 = 20√c − c80 The action set is the interval A = [0, 1,000,000]. It is continuous, so we cannot list the actions, we must use calculus. Lesson: Problem 1.7: how much to spend on parks First order conditionExam favourite v′(c) = 10√c − 180 = 0 Marginal benefit equals marginal cost. Lesson: Problem 1.7: how much to spend on parks Second order condition v″(c) = −5 c−3/2 < 0 v is strictly concave, so a stationary point inside the interval is the unique maximum. Lesson: Problem 1.7: how much to spend on parks Tables Table: · maximizing · satisficing Maximizing Satisficing Rule Pick an action in arg max v Pick any action with v(a) ≥ T, an aspiration level Search Must compare everything Can stop at the first good enough action Used Throughout this course Herbert Simon's model of real human choice under limited time Lesson: From preferences to the best action Table: tree · maintenance · food bill reduction · net change per tree Tree Maintenance Food bill reduction Net change per tree Apple $100 $130 −30 Orange $70 $90 −20 Pear $120 $145 −25 Lesson: Problem 1.6: the fruit trees Tips and traps Common mistake The transcript sometimes wrote v(a*) > v(a) for every other a. That strict version only fits when the best action is unique. With ties the right condition is ≥.Lesson: From preferences to the best action Remember A change in the payoff structure changed the rational choice. Nothing about the person's rationality changed, only the numbers on the leaves.Lesson: Problem 1.6: the fruit trees Common mistake Setting the derivative to zero is not enough. Check the second derivative and the end points of the allowed range.Lesson: From intuition to research"],[50,"step-advanced-2","Step 2Advanced AI · Choice under risk","2.1"],[50,"lotteries-cdf","Lotteries and distributions","Open chapter Lotteries, the CDF and its properties, intervals, densities and continuous outcomes. Key ideas Lottery Action a gives P(xᵢ | a) = pᵢ with pᵢ in [0, 1] and Σ pᵢ = 1. Nature picks the outcome. CDF F(x) = P(X ≤ x | a). Non decreasing, 0 at −∞, 1 at +∞, right continuous. F(50) = 0.7 is \"at most 50\". Intervals P(r < X ≤ s) = F(s) − F(r). Notes example 0.8 − 0.3 = 0.5. Density F = ∫ f, f = F′. P(X = x) = 0 for continuous X. Densities can exceed 1. Compound paths Multiply along each path, add the paths that reach the same outcome: 0.6 × 0.8 + 0.4 × 0.3 = 0.6. Formulas Lottery of an actionExam favourite P( xi | a ) = pi, 0 ≤ pi ≤ 1, ∑ pi = 1 A simple lottery lists every final outcome with its probability. The probabilities add to 1 because exactly one outcome happens. Lesson: When an action has more than one outcome Deterministic as a special case P( c(a) | a ) = 1 A certain action is a lottery that puts all its probability on one outcome. Lesson: When an action has more than one outcome CDF of an actionExam favourite Fa(x) = P( X ≤ x | a ) The probability that the outcome is at most x when you take action a. Lesson: The cumulative distribution function Interval probabilityExam favourite P( r < X ≤ s | a ) = Fa(s) − Fa(r) Everything up to s minus everything up to r. Lesson: The cumulative distribution function From density to CDFExam favourite Fa(x) = ∫−∞xfa(t) dt Add up the density from the far left up to x. Lesson: Densities and continuous outcomes From CDF to density fa(x) = ddxFa(x) Wherever the derivative exists. Lesson: Densities and continuous outcomes Single points P( X = x ) = 0 for a continuous X Only intervals carry probability. Lesson: Densities and continuous outcomes Tables Table: · deterministic choice · risky choice Deterministic choice Risky choice Action leads to One known outcome A distribution of outcomes Compare u(c(a)) E[u(X) | a] Tree contains Decision nodes only Decision nodes and chance nodes Ranking enough? Yes, ordinal utility works No, cardinal utility is needed (chapter 2.2) Lesson: When an action has more than one outcome Table: property · formula · plain meaning Property Formula Plain meaning Non decreasing x < y ⟹ F(x) ≤ F(y) More room below means at least as much probability Lower limit F(x) → 0 as x → −∞ Nothing lies below everything Upper limit F(x) → 1 as x → +∞ Everything lies below infinity Right continuous F(x) = lim F(x + h) as h ↓ 0 At a jump the filled dot sits on the upper step Lesson: The cumulative distribution function Table: lottery · cdf f(x) · shape Lottery CDF F(x) Shape Discrete, outcomes xᵢ with pᵢ Sum of pᵢ with xᵢ ≤ x A staircase with jumps of size pᵢ Uniform on [L, U] (x − L) ÷ (U − L) inside A straight ramp from 0 to 1 Exponential with rate λ 1 − e−λx for x ≥ 0 Rises fast then flattens Normal with mean μ and spread σ Φ((x − μ) ÷ σ) An S curve centred on μ Lesson: Densities and continuous outcomes Tips and traps Common mistake F(50) = 0.7 means a 70% chance the outcome is at most 50. It does not mean a 70% chance the outcome is exactly 50. This misreading is the most common exam error on CDFs.Lesson: The cumulative distribution function Common mistake A density can be larger than 1. The uniform density on [0, 0.5] equals 2. Only the area under it must be 1.Lesson: From intuition to research 2.2"],[50,"expected-utility","Expected utility and compound lotteries","Open chapter Expected utility, reducing compound lotteries, the R&D example, the reversal under non affine changes and why utility under risk is cardinal. Key ideas Expected utility EU(a) = Σ P(xᵢ | a) u(xᵢ), or ∫ u dF. Choose arg max EU, not arg max E[X]. R&D compound lottery P(10 | G) = 0.625 × 0.9 + 0.375 × 0.5 = 0.75, EU(G) = 7.5 > EU(S) = 5. Multiply along paths, add paths. Reversal u = 9, −1, 10, 0: EU(G) 6.5 > 5. u(B) = −8: 4.75 < 5. Same ranking, opposite choice. Affine invariance Only v = a u + b with a > 0 keeps every EU comparison. Utility under risk is cardinal. Other rules and PV Optimistic (P best), pessimistic (P worst). PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ᵗ. vNM axioms give EU. Formulas Expected utility, discreteExam favourite EU(a) = ∑iP( xi | a ) u( xi ) Probability times utility, summed over outcomes. Lesson: Expected utility Expected utility, continuous EU(a) = ∫ u(x) dFa(x) = ∫ u(x) fa(x) dx The second form needs a density. Lesson: Expected utility Rational choice under riskExam favourite a* ∈ arg maxa E[ u(X) | a ] Maximize expected utility, not expected money. Lesson: Expected utility Reduction of compound lotteriesExam favourite P(final outcome) = ∑paths ∏branches on the pathP(branch) Multiply along each path, then add the paths that end at the same outcome. Lesson: Compound lotteries: the R&D example Positive affine transformationExam favourite v(x) = au(x) + b, a > 0 The only transformations that keep every expected utility comparison. Lesson: Why ranking is not enough under risk Why it works E[ a u(X) + b ] = a E[ u(X) ] + b Expectation is linear. Scaling by a positive a and shifting by b keeps the order of the expected values. Lesson: Why ranking is not enough under risk Present value PV = FV(1 + r)t For a risky payoff: E[u(Xt)] ÷ (1 + r)t. Lesson: Why ranking is not enough under risk Tables Table: rule · looks at · r&d answer Rule Looks at R&D answer Expected utility Σ p u G: 7.5 against 5 Optimistic Chance of the best outcome G: 0.75 against 0.5 Pessimistic Chance of the worst outcome, lower is better G: 0.25 against 0.5 Others Median, quantiles, worst case, variance, chance of beating a target Depends on the rule Lesson: Compound lotteries: the R&D example Tips and traps In the exam Moving u(B) from −1 to −8 is not affine: it stretches one gap and leaves the others alone. Under risk the sizes of the gaps matter, so expected utility needs cardinal utility, unique up to a u + b with a > 0.Lesson: Why ranking is not enough under risk Common mistake Expected value and expected utility differ whenever u is not a straight line. Write which one a question asks for.Lesson: From intuition to research 2.3"],[50,"risk-attitudes","Risk attitudes and the St Petersburg paradox","Open chapter Risk attitudes from concavity, certainty equivalent and risk premium, and the St Petersburg paradox with its log utility resolution. Key ideas Three attitudes Concave u (ln, √): averse, E[u] ≤ u(E[X]). Linear: neutral. Convex (x²): loving. Jensen with the chord picture. Certainty equivalent u(CE) = E[u(X)]. Risk premium = E[X] − CE. √x with 100 at 0.2: EU = 2, CE = 4, sure 30 wins. Fair gamble +1 or −1 at 50/50 has mean 0. Play when 0.5u(1) + 0.5u(−1) > u(0). Averse agents refuse. St Petersburg Pays 2ⁿ⁻¹ w.p. 1/2ⁿ. E[X] = Σ ½ = ∞. Log utility: E = ln 2, CE = 2. x² still diverges. Practice 5 u = x², 0 or 10: E[X] = 5, u(E[X]) = 25, E[u] = 50, so risk loving. Formulas Risk neutralExam favourite E[ u(X) ] = u( E[X] ) u is linear, u(x) = ax + b. Only expected money matters. Lesson: Three attitudes to risk Risk averseExam favourite E[ u(X) ] ≤ u( E[X] ) u is concave (u″ < 0), for example ln x or √x. The sure amount is preferred. Lesson: Three attitudes to risk Risk lovingExam favourite E[ u(X) ] ≥ u( E[X] ) u is convex (u″ > 0), for example x². The gamble is preferred. Lesson: Three attitudes to risk Certainty equivalentExam favourite u(CE) = E[ u(X) ] ⟹ CE = u−1( E[u(X)] ) The sure amount you value exactly as much as the lottery. Lesson: The certainty equivalent Risk premium RP = E[X] − CE What you would give up in expectation to remove the risk. Positive for a risk averse agent. Lesson: The certainty equivalent Ordering averse: CE ≤ E[X], neutral: CE = E[X], loving: CE ≥ E[X] Lesson: The certainty equivalent Expected moneyExam favourite E[X] = ∑n=1∞12n · 2n−1 = ∑ 12 = ∞ Every toss adds exactly one half to the sum, forever. Lesson: The St Petersburg paradox Expected log utilityExam favourite E[ln X] = ∑ (n − 1) ln 22n = ln 2 Since ∑ (n − 1) ÷ 2ⁿ = 1. Finite. Lesson: The St Petersburg paradox Its certainty equivalent CE = eln 2 = 2 A log utility agent would pay only about 2. Lesson: The St Petersburg paradox Tables Table: utility · u″ · attitude · ce of 0 or 100 at 50/50 Utility u″ Attitude CE of 0 or 100 at 50/50 x 0 Neutral 50 √x −¼ x−3/2 < 0 Averse 25 ln(1 + x) −1 ÷ (1 + x)² < 0 Averse 9.05 x² 2 > 0 Loving 70.71 Lesson: The certainty equivalent Tips and traps In the exam Concavity is what tames the paradox. With the convex u = x² the terms are 2ⁿ⁻² and the sum still diverges. Even √x converges, to about 1.707, which gives a certainty equivalent near 2.91.Lesson: The St Petersburg paradox Common mistake A risk averse person may still gamble. They need the gamble to pay enough more than the sure amount, the risk premium.Lesson: From intuition to research"],[50,"step-advanced-3","Step 3Advanced AI · Decisions over time","3.1"],[50,"backward-induction","Backward induction","Open chapter Backward induction on the R&D and marketing tree, full strategies, the film studio problem and Bellman's principle. Key ideas Method From the last decision back: chance node = Σ p × value, decision node = max. Never compare raw leaves at a chance node. R&D tree Success: m 11 > d 8. Failure: m 3 < d 4. Go = 0.625 × 11 + 0.375 × 4 = 8.375 > skip 5. Full strategies MM 8.0, MD 8.375, DM 6.125, DD 6.5. Same answer as folding, but 2ᵏ plans for k binary nodes. Practice A Good buzz push 14.4, weak buzz hold 3.7, big budget 7.98 > small 3.5. Why it works Bellman's principle of optimality: each tail of an optimal plan is optimal. Folding is linear in the tree size. Formulas Backward inductionExam favourite start at the last decision, replace each chance node by its expected value, keep the best action at each decision node, and move one level back Repeat until you reach the root. Lesson: Decisions that unfold over time At a chance node V(n) = ∑kpk V(childk) Nature moves, so you average. Lesson: Decisions that unfold over time At a decision node V(n) = maxa V(childa) You move, so you take the best. Lesson: Decisions that unfold over time Tables Table: path · then · payoffs Path Then Payoffs Skip research (s) Nature picks the market 10 or 0, each with 0.5 Go for research (g), success with 0.625 Choose marketing m or not d, then the market is good with 0.9 m: 13 or −7, d: 9 or −1 Go for research (g), failure with 0.375 Choose m or d, then the market is good with 0.5 m: 13 or −7, d: 9 or −1 Lesson: Decisions that unfold over time Table: after success, after failure · expected payoff After success, after failure Expected payoff M, M (always market) 0.625 × 11 + 0.375 × 3 = 8.000 M, D (market only after success) 0.625 × 11 + 0.375 × 4 = 8.375, the best D, M (market only after failure) 0.625 × 8 + 0.375 × 3 = 6.125 D, D (never market) 0.625 × 8 + 0.375 × 4 = 6.500 Lesson: Solving the R&D and marketing tree Table: buzz · push: success chance, payoffs · no push: payoffs Buzz Push: success chance, payoffs No push: payoffs Good (0.4) 0.8: 18 or 0 0.8: 14 or 2 Weak (0.6) 0.3: 18 or −4 0.3: 10 or 1 Lesson: Practice problem A: the film studio Tips and traps Common mistake Never compare raw leaf payoffs where nature moves. 13 is not \"better than\" −7 in any useful sense at a chance node, because the firm does not choose between them. Convert every chance node to its expected value first, and compare only at nodes where the firm chooses.Lesson: Solving the R&D and marketing tree Common mistake At chance nodes average with the probabilities. Taking the maximum there assumes luck always goes your way.Lesson: From intuition to research 3.2"],[50,"discounting-consumption","Discounting and consumption over time","Open chapter Discount factors and present value, optimal consumption with ln and √x, corner solutions, and the time consistency of exponential discounting. Key ideas Discount factor δ = 1/(1 + r), 0 < δ < 1, patience not a choice. A after n periods is worth Aδⁿ. Practice B: PV = 520.2. Timing Discount each piece by when it happens: R&D branch 10δ − 1, not (10 − 1)δ. ln consumption 2 periods K/(1 + δ), δK/(1 + δ). 3 periods K, δK, δ²K over 1 + δ + δ². Equal discounted marginal utility. Corners Linear u: (K, 0, 0). Convex u = x²: FOC finds the minimum, best is a corner. √x: x₁ = K/(1 + δ²), K = 181 gives 100, 81. Time consistency Exponential: re-planning keeps x₂*, x₃*. Hyperbolic 1/(1 + kt) is time inconsistent. Formulas Discount factorExam favourite δ = 11 + r, 0 < δ < 1 A rupee next period is worth δ rupees today. Lesson: Why money later is worth less n periods aheadExam favourite value today of A received after n periods = A δn = A(1 + r)n Two periods: 100 grows to 100(1 + r)², so it is worth 100δ² today. Lesson: Why money later is worth less Present value of a stream PV = ∑txt δt Discount each payment by its own delay, then add. Lesson: Why money later is worth less Two periods, u = ln xExam favourite max ln x₁ + δ ln(K − x₁) ⟹ x₁* = K1 + δ, x₂* = δK1 + δ From 1/x₁ = δ/(K − x₁). Lesson: Spreading consumption over time Three periods, u = ln xExam favourite x₁* = K1 + δ + δ², x₂* = δK1 + δ + δ², x₃* = δ²K1 + δ + δ² The board solution from lecture 4. Lesson: Spreading consumption over time Equal discounted marginal utility 1/x₁* = δ/x₂* = δ²/x₃* No small move of money between two periods can raise the total. Lesson: Spreading consumption over time Linear utility x* = (K, 0, 0) A linear objective over a budget is maximized at a corner. Lesson: Corners, homework and time consistency Tables Table: discounting · weight on period t · re-planning later Discounting Weight on period t Re-planning later Exponential δt Keeps the original plan (time consistent) Hyperbolic 1/(1 + kt) Wants to change it (time inconsistent): impatience is strongest about the near future Lesson: Corners, homework and time consistency Tips and traps Remember Diminishing marginal utility means u″ < 0, not u′ falling to zero. Going from 10 to 11 rupees adds more than going from 110 to 111, but both add something. The first order condition sets the derivative of the whole objective to zero, never u′ itself.Lesson: Spreading consumption over time Common mistake The discount rate r and the discount factor δ = 1 ÷ (1 + r) are different numbers. A higher rate means a lower factor.Lesson: From intuition to research 3.3"],[50,"value-of-information","The value of information","Open chapter The value of perfect and imperfect information, the MBA and venue examples, and why information is never worth less than zero. Key ideas VOI formula VOI = E[max_a u(a, s)] − max_a E[u(a, s)]. Choose per state then average, minus average per action then choose. MBA Cost 10, states 0.25/0.5/0.25. No info: 9 (MBA) vs 8. Oracle: 22, 8, 4 gives 10.5. VOI = 1.5. Never negative You can ignore the oracle, so VOI ≥ 0. It is 0 when one action is best in every state. Venue practice C Outdoor 50/−20, indoor 30/25 at 0.5 each. 27.5 without, 37.5 with, VOI = 10. 90% forecast: 6.75. Imperfect signals Update by Bayes, pick the best per signal, average. Worth 0 if it cannot change the decision (q = 0.5). Formulas Value of perfect informationExam favourite VOI = E[ maxau(a, s) ] − maxa E[ u(a, s) ] Best with the oracle (choose per state, then average) minus best without (average per action, then choose). Lesson: What is it worth to know first? MBA exampleExam favourite VOI = 10.5 − 9 = 1.5 Pay an oracle anything below 1.5. Above 1.5 you are better off deciding now. Lesson: What is it worth to know first? Bayes updateExam favourite P(s | signal) = P(signal | s) P(s)P(signal) The forecast shifts the probabilities, not the payoffs. Lesson: Imperfect information and Bayes Value of imperfect information VOI = ∑signalsP(signal) maxa E[u(a, s) | signal] − maxa E[u(a, s)] Between 0 and the value of perfect information. Lesson: Imperfect information and Bayes Tables Table: market (probability) · with mba, gross · with mba, net of 10 · without mba Market (probability) With MBA, gross With MBA, net of 10 Without MBA Good (0.25) 32 22 12 Medium (0.5) 16 6 8 Poor (0.25) 12 2 4 Lesson: What is it worth to know first? Tips and traps Common mistake A student in class computed 0.25 × 22 + 0.5 × 8 + 0.25 × 4 as the no information value. That mixes actions across states, which you can only do once you know the state. Without information you must use one action in every state.Lesson: What is it worth to know first? Common mistake If every forecast would lead to the same choice, the forecast is worth 0 to you, however accurate it is.Lesson: From intuition to research"],[50,"step-advanced-4","Step 4Advanced AI · Games between rational agents","4.1"],[50,"normal-form-games","Static games and the normal form","Open chapter Static games of complete information, common knowledge, the normal form, the Prisoner's Dilemma and best responses. Key ideas Two axes Complete vs incomplete information. Static (no one sees others' choices) vs dynamic. Static is about observation, not clock time. Common knowledge Everyone knows, everyone knows everyone knows, forever. Complete info: actions, outcomes, map, preferences are common knowledge. Normal form ⟨N, Sᵢ, vᵢ⟩ with vᵢ on full profiles. Finite if all Sᵢ finite. Bimatrix cell = (row payoff, column payoff). Prisoner's Dilemma (M,M) = (−2,−2), (M,C) = (−5,−1), (C,M) = (−1,−5), (C,C) = (−4,−4). C is the best reply to both. Best response BRᵢ(s₋ᵢ) maximizes vᵢ(·, s₋ᵢ). A profile with no profitable unilateral deviation is self enforcing. Formulas Common knowledgeExam favourite E is common knowledge ⟺ (everyone knows)nE for every n = 1, 2, 3, … Knowing a fact privately is mutual knowledge. Common knowledge needs the whole infinite chain. Lesson: Many agents: static games of complete information Normal form gameExam favourite ⟨ N, {{Si}}, {{vi}} ⟩ N = {1, …, n} players. Sᵢ = pure strategies of player i. vᵢ = payoff of player i. Lesson: The normal form of a game Strategy profile s = (s₁, …, sn) ∈ S₁ × … × Sn One choice per player. In a static game a strategy is simply an action. Lesson: The normal form of a game Payoff functionExam favourite vi : S₁ × … × Sn → ℝ Your payoff depends on the whole profile, not only on your own choice. Lesson: The normal form of a game Everyone else s−i = (s₁, …, si−1, si+1, …, sn) Read \"s minus i\". We write s = (sᵢ, s₋ᵢ). Lesson: The normal form of a game Best responseExam favourite si ∈ BRi(s−i) ⟺ vi(si, s−i) ≥ vi(s′i, s−i) for all s′i Hold everyone else fixed and maximize your own payoff. Lesson: Best responses and unilateral deviation Unilateral deviation from s to (s′i, s−i) Only player i changes. A profile where no unilateral deviation helps anyone is self enforcing. Lesson: Best responses and unilateral deviation Tables Table: axis · one end · other end Axis One end Other end What do players know about each other? Complete information Incomplete information When do players act? Static: choose without seeing others' choices Dynamic: some players see earlier moves Lesson: Many agents: static games of complete information Table: player 1 (rows), player 2 (columns) · m · c Player 1 (rows), Player 2 (columns) M C M (−2, −2) (−5, −1) C (−1, −5) (−4, −4) Lesson: The normal form of a game Tips and traps Remember A best response is computed against fixed choices of the others. It is not a guess about what they will do. Whether the others actually play those choices is what a solution concept, in chapter 4.3, decides.Lesson: Best responses and unilateral deviation Common mistake Payoff pairs are written (row player, column player). Swapping them is the most common slip in exam tables.Lesson: From intuition to research 4.2"],[50,"modelling-games","Modelling games: Cournot, Bertrand and the streetlamp","Open chapter The modelling recipe, Cournot and Bertrand duopoly, and the streetlamp public good with free riding. Key ideas Recipe Players, strategies (with their range), payoffs split into cases. Game = ⟨N, Sᵢ, vᵢ⟩. Cournot P = a − bq. sᵢ = (a − cᵢ − bsⱼ)/2b, s* = (a − c)/3b. a = 100, c = 10: 30 each, P = 40, profit 900. Asymmetric Cournot s₁* = (a − 2c₁ + c₂)/3b. Practice F: 18 and 12, P = 30, profits 324 and 144. Bertrand Sᵢ = [0, a], q = (a − p)/b. Cheaper takes all, tie splits (divide by 2b), dearer gets −Cᵢ(0). Paradox: p = c. Streetlamp vᵢ = 3·1[Σ ≥ 2] − sᵢ, 8 profiles. Pay only when pivotal. Stable: exactly two payers, or nobody. Formulas Inverse demand P(q) = a − bq if this is ≥ 0, and 0 otherwise q is the total quantity of both firms. a and b are common knowledge. Lesson: Cournot: competing in quantities Cournot payoffExam favourite vi(si, sj) = (a − b(si + sj)) si − Ci(si), or −Ci(si) if the price would be negative Strategy set Sᵢ = [0, ∞), so the game is infinite. Lesson: Cournot: competing in quantities Best response, linear cost cᵢExam favourite si = a − ci − b sj2b From ∂vᵢ/∂sᵢ = a − 2bsᵢ − bsⱼ − cᵢ = 0. Your best output falls by half of the rival's. Lesson: Cournot: competing in quantities Symmetric equilibriumExam favourite s* = a − c3b Put sⱼ = s* in the best response: 3bs* = a − c. Lesson: Cournot: competing in quantities Quantity from price q = a − pb Turn the demand rule around: p = a − bq gives bq = a − p. Lesson: Bertrand: competing in prices Bertrand payoffExam favourite vi = pia − pib − Ci(a − pib) if cheaper, half the market (divide by 2b) if tied, −Ci(0) if dearer Three cases. The −Cᵢ(0) term is a fixed cost paid even when nothing sells. Lesson: Bertrand: competing in prices Streetlamp payoffExam favourite vi(s) = 3 · 1[ ∑jsj ≥ 2 ] − si, si ∈ {{0, 1}} The indicator 1[·] is 1 when the statement is true and 0 otherwise. Split on the sum, not on the eight profiles one by one. Lesson: The streetlamp: a public good Tables Table: p₁ (with p₂ = 40) · case · quantity · profit p₁ (with p₂ = 40) Case Quantity Profit 30 cheaper 70 30 × 70 − 700 = 1,400 39.9 cheaper 60.1 2,397.99 − 601 = 1,796.99 40 tie 30 1,200 − 300 = 900 50 dearer 0 0 Lesson: Bertrand: competing in prices Table: others who pay · you pay · you do not pay · better for you Others who pay You pay You do not pay Better for you 0 −1 (not built) 0 Do not pay 1 2 (you complete it) 0 Pay, you are pivotal 2 2 3 (free ride) Do not pay Lesson: The streetlamp: a public good Tips and traps In the exam The Bertrand paradox: with equal unit cost c, the only stable prices are p₁ = p₂ = c. Two firms are enough to drive profit to zero, while Cournot firms keep the price at 40, well above cost 10.Lesson: Bertrand: competing in prices Common mistake The Cournot equilibrium is not the joint best outcome for the firms. Each firm ignores that its extra output lowers the other’s price.Lesson: From intuition to research 4.3"],[50,"solution-concepts-pareto","Solution concepts and Pareto optimality","Open chapter What solution concepts are, the assumptions behind them, and Pareto optimality with worked games and the zero sum proof. Key ideas Solution concept Narrows profiles to reasonable ones. Its pick is an equilibrium. Not a physics equilibrium. Four assumptions Rational (maximize), intelligent (know the game), common knowledge of both, self enforcement (no outside enforcer). Good concept Existence, uniqueness, invariance. \"Anything can happen\" exists but is not unique. Pareto s dominates s′: ≥ for all, > for one. PD: only (C, C) fails. Marketing War: (L,L), (L,M), (M,L). Zero sum: all optimal. Altruism vᵢ + ½vⱼ gives (−3,−3), (−5.5,−3.5), (−3.5,−5.5), (−6,−6). Mum becomes best. Pareto is not fairness. Formulas Pareto dominanceExam favourite s Pareto dominates s′ ⟺ vi(s) ≥ vi(s′) for all i, and > for at least one j Nobody is worse off and someone is strictly better off. Lesson: Pareto optimality Pareto optimalExam favourite s′ is not Pareto dominated by any profile Also called Pareto efficient. Lesson: Pareto optimality Altruistic payoffExam favourite vialt(s) = vi(s) + ½ vj(s) Your own jail term at full weight plus half of the other's. Not an extra penalty added on top. Lesson: Zero sum games, altruism and the practice problems Tables Table: candidate concept · prisoner's dilemma answer · verdict Candidate concept Prisoner's Dilemma answer Verdict \"Anything can happen\" All four profiles Always exists, but gives no advice or prediction Play a strategy that is best whatever others do (C, C) Sharp and useful, but not always available Nash equilibrium (later in the course) (C, C) Exists far more often Lesson: What a solution concept is for Table: assumption · meaning in this course Assumption Meaning in this course Rational Each player maximizes her own (expected) payoff Intelligent Each player knows every action, the outcome map and everyone's preferences, as well as the analyst does Common knowledge That everyone is rational and intelligent is itself common knowledge Self enforcement The equilibrium must hold without any outside enforcer Lesson: What a solution concept is for Table: property · question it asks Property Question it asks Existence Does it give an answer for every game? Uniqueness Does it pick a small set, ideally one profile? \"Anything can happen\" fails badly here. Invariance Does a small change in payoffs that keeps every ranking leave the answer the same? Lesson: What a solution concept is for Table: test · how to apply it Test How to apply it Direct test Search for a profile that is at least as good for everyone and strictly better for someone. If one exists, the candidate is not optimal. Improvement test Try to make one player strictly better off. If every such move hurts someone, the candidate is optimal. Lesson: Pareto optimality Table: profile · original · with altruism ½ Profile Original With altruism ½ (M, M) (−2, −2) (−3, −3) (M, C) (−5, −1) (−5.5, −3.5) (C, M) (−1, −5) (−3.5, −5.5) (C, C) (−4, −4) (−6, −6) Lesson: Zero sum games, altruism and the practice problems Tips and traps Common mistake Pareto optimality says nothing about fairness. (2, 8) and (8, 2) are Pareto optimal next to the balanced (6, 6). It only rules out waste.Lesson: Pareto optimality Common mistake Pareto optimal and fair are different ideas. Pareto says nothing about how the total is shared.Lesson: From intuition to research"],[50,"step-advanced-5","Step 5Advanced AI · Dominance and iterated elimination","5.1"],[50,"dominance","Dominance and the dominant strategy equilibrium","Open chapter Strict and weak dominance, the four dominance words, the dominant strategy equilibrium with its uniqueness proof, and its limits. Key ideas Strict domination sᵢ ▷ s′ᵢ: vᵢ(sᵢ, s₋ᵢ) > vᵢ(s′ᵢ, s₋ᵢ) for every s₋ᵢ. Row player: first numbers across columns. Column player: second numbers down rows. Vocabulary Dominating/dominated: beats/is beaten by at least one. Dominant/submissive: beats/is beaten by all. One dominator is enough to delete. Partial order ▷ is irreflexive, asymmetric, transitive, not complete (RPS, M and H in the Marketing War). Weak: ≥ (textbook adds > once). DSE Everyone plays a strictly dominant strategy. Unique if it exists. PD (C, C), altruistic PD (M, M), RPS and Marketing War none. Rationality A rational player never plays a strictly dominated strategy under any belief. Increasing transforms and h(s₋ᵢ) do not change dominance. Formulas Strict dominationExam favourite si ▷is′i ⟺ vi(si, s−i) > vi(s′i, s−i) for every s−i Only player i's own payoffs are compared, once for each situation the others can create. Lesson: Strict domination Weak domination si ⊵is′i ⟺ ≥ for every s−i Textbooks add \"and > for at least one s₋ᵢ\". State which version you use in an exam. Lesson: Strict domination Strict dominant strategy equilibriumExam favourite sD with siD strictly dominant for every player i Everyone plays the strategy that is best against everything. No one needs to predict anyone. Lesson: The dominant strategy equilibrium Rational players and dominated strategiesExam favourite Eμ[vi(si)] − Eμ[vi(s′i)] = ∑ μ(s−i)[vi(si, s−i) − vi(s′i, s−i)] > 0 Theorem 4: whatever the belief μ, a strictly dominated strategy has lower expected payoff, so a rational player never uses it. Lesson: The dominant strategy equilibrium Tables Table: term · needs · meaning for strategy sᵢ Term Needs Meaning for strategy sᵢ Dominating at least one sᵢ beats some other strategy Dominated at least one some other strategy beats sᵢ Dominant all others sᵢ beats every other strategy Submissive all others every other strategy beats sᵢ Lesson: Dominating, dominated, dominant, submissive Table: strategy · dominating · dominated · dominant Strategy Dominating Dominated Dominant Submissive PD: C yes no yes no PD: M no yes no yes Marketing: M yes no no, does not beat H no Marketing: L no yes no no, H does not beat it Marketing: H no no no no RPS: any no no no no Lesson: Dominating, dominated, dominant, submissive Table: property · strict dse · why Property Strict DSE Why Existence Fails RPS, Marketing War, Cournot, Bertrand, streetlamp have none Uniqueness Holds Theorem 3 (fails for the weak version) Invariance Fails Altruism ½ flips (C, C) to (M, M) Pareto optimality Fails PD picks (C, C), the only wasteful profile Lesson: The dominant strategy equilibrium Tips and traps Common mistake The mistake made twice in class: checking player 1 by mixing in the second number of each cell, or by moving down a column. Rule: for the row player compare first numbers, moving across columns. For the column player compare second numbers, moving down rows. Moving along the other player's strategies is exactly \"for every s₋ᵢ\".Lesson: Strict domination Common mistake Strict and weak dominance behave differently. Removing weakly dominated strategies can remove equilibria and depends on the order of removal.Lesson: From intuition to research 5.2"],[50,"iesds","Iterated elimination of dominated strategies","Open chapter The IESDS algorithm, the slide 11 and Marketing War examples, theorems about IESDS and Nash equilibrium, and why weak elimination depends on order. Key ideas Algorithm Each round delete every strictly dominated strategy of every player, comparing only survivors. Stop when nothing is dominated. Common knowledge Round k uses k layers of \"I know that you know\". Real people manage about 1 to 3 layers. Slide 11 R ▷ C, then U ▷ M and D, then L ▷ R. Outcome (U, L) = (4, 3), self enforcing but Pareto dominated by (9, 6). Theorems DSE found in one round. Every NE survives. Unique survivor = unique NE. Strict IESDS is order independent. Limits Marketing War leaves {M, H}², RPS deletes nothing. Weak IESDS depends on order: (2, 1) or (1, 1). Formulas IESDS, round kExam favourite DSik = {{ si ∈ Sik : some s′i ∈ Sik beats it against every s−i ∈ S−ik }} Compare only against strategies that still survive. Lesson: Why we may eliminate again and again UpdateExam favourite Sik+1 = Sik ∖ DSik Remove the elements. Stop when no player has a dominated strategy. Every profile of survivors is a prediction. Lesson: Why we may eliminate again and again Nash equilibrium (preview)Exam favourite vi(si*, s*−i) ≥ vi(si, s*−i) for every i and every si No player gains by a unilateral deviation. The course defines it fully in later lectures. Lesson: Theorems about IESDS Tables Table: layer · reasoning on the slide 11 game Layer Reasoning on the slide 11 game 0 Player 2 is rational, so she never plays column C (R beats it) 1 Player 1 knows player 2 is rational, so he ignores C. In the smaller game M and D become dominated 2 Player 2 knows player 1 knows she is rational, so she expects only row U. Now R is dominated k Each round of elimination uses one more layer of \"I know that you know that…\" Lesson: Why we may eliminate again and again Table: slide 11 game · l · c · r Slide 11 game L C R U 4, 3 5, 1 6, 2 M 2, 1 8, 4 3, 6 D 3, 0 9, 6 2, 8 Lesson: Worked examples: the Marketing War and slide 11 Table: theorem · statement Theorem Statement 5 If a strict dominant strategy equilibrium exists, IESDS leaves exactly it after one round 6 Every Nash equilibrium survives IESDS 7 In a finite game, if IESDS leaves one profile, it is the unique Nash equilibrium 8 For strict dominance in finite games, the order of deletion does not change the survivors (Gilboa, Kalai and Zemel 1990) Lesson: Theorems about IESDS Table: order game · l · r Order game L R T 1, 1 0, 0 M 1, 1 2, 1 B 0, 0 2, 1 Lesson: Theorems about IESDS Table: property · iesds · why Property IESDS Why Existence Holds A finite strict partial order always has an undominated element Uniqueness Fails Marketing War leaves four profiles, RPS deletes nothing Stronger than DSE Yes Finds any DSE in one round and solves slide 11, which has none Needs Common knowledge One more layer of \"knows that knows\" per round Pareto optimal Fails Slide 11 ends at (4, 3), dominated by (9, 6) Lesson: Theorems about IESDS Tips and traps Remember Two small slip ups on slide 9: the dominator must range over the current set Sᵢᵏ, and the update is Sᵢᵏ minus DSᵢᵏ, not minus a set that contains DSᵢᵏ. Slide 6 also says \"strictly\" where it means \"weakly\" in two places. The formulas are the correct part.Lesson: Why we may eliminate again and again Common mistake IESDS does not always leave one outcome. In many games, such as the restaurant game, nothing is dominated and every strategy survives.Lesson: From intuition to research 5.3"],[50,"dominance-applications","Dominance at work: Cournot, auctions and voting","Open chapter Cournot solved by iterated elimination, truthful bidding in second price auctions, the median voter, and where weak dominance misleads. Key ideas Cournot IESDS BR = max{0, (a − c − bq)/2b}. Rounds: [0, 45], [22.5, 45], [22.5, 33.75], … halving to (a − c)/3b = 30. E1: 40, price 50, profit 1,600. Second price auction Payoff v − B if b > B. Bidding v weakly dominates every bid (never worse, sometimes better). Strategy proof. First price auction Payoff v − b if you win. Overbidding weakly dominated, but bidding v not dominant: bidders shade. Location and prices Hotelling 1 to 5: delete 1, 5 then 2, 4, both at 3 (median). Discrete Bertrand: strict deletes nothing, weak ends at (2, 2) losing (1, 1), (3, 3). Weak only Below cost Bertrand prices and the guess game: weakly but not strictly dominated, so strict IESDS cannot start. Formulas Profit with the rival fixed πi = (a − c − bqj) qi − b qi² An upside down parabola in qᵢ, with its peak at the best response. Lesson: IESDS solves the Cournot duopoly Best responseExam favourite BRi(qj) = max{{ 0, a − c − bqj2b }} With a = 100, b = 1, c = 10 this is max{0, (90 − qⱼ)/2}. Lesson: IESDS solves the Cournot duopoly Without calculus Kq − bq² = −b(q − K2b)² + K²4b Completing the square shows the peak at q = K/2b, where K = a − c − bqⱼ. Lesson: IESDS solves the Cournot duopoly Second price (Vickrey)Exam favourite payoff = v − B if b > B, otherwise 0 The winner pays the second highest bid. Bidding b = v weakly dominates every other bid. Lesson: Auctions: when truth telling is dominant First priceExam favourite payoff = v − b if b > B, otherwise 0 The winner pays her own bid. Overbidding is weakly dominated, but bidding v is not dominant. Lesson: Auctions: when truth telling is dominant Tables Table: second price, value v · rival bid b below v · b between v and the overbid · b above both Second price, value v Rival bid B below v B between v and the overbid B above both Bid v win, v − B lose, 0 lose, 0 Overbid b > v win, v − B (same) win, v − B < 0 (worse) lose, 0 Lesson: Auctions: when truth telling is dominant Table: puzzle · answer Puzzle Answer F1. Streetlamp with refunds when the lamp is not built Contributing ties with not contributing when nobody else pays, wins with one other payer, and loses with two. Still no dominance. Refunds remove the risk but not the free riding. F2. Two players guess an integer in [0, 100]. Closer to two thirds of the average wins The lower guess always wins, so 0 weakly dominates every other guess, but not strictly: if the rival guesses above you, both 0 and your guess win. Lesson: Voting, prices and puzzles Tips and traps Common mistake In a second price auction you do not pay your own bid. Students who forget this conclude that shading the bid is wise.Lesson: From intuition to research"],[50,"step-advanced-6","Step 6Advanced AI · Quiz and exam revision","6.1"],[50,"quiz1-solved","Quiz 1 solved: all four questions","Open chapter Worked answers to Quiz 1: B, A, A and B under the standard reading, and (K, 0, 0). Formulas Profit of firm i πi = a qiqi + qj + 1 − c qi Price times own quantity, minus cost. Lesson: Question 1: Cournot with a curved demand First order conditionExam favourite a(qj + 1)(qi + qj + 1)² = c The derivative of a qᵢ/(qᵢ + qⱼ + 1) is a(qⱼ + 1)/(Q + 1)². Lesson: Question 1: Cournot with a curved demand Symmetric equilibriumExam favourite q* = (a − 4c) + √a² + 8ac8c From a(q + 1) = c(2q + 1)², a quadratic 4cq² + (4c − a)q + (c − a) = 0. Lesson: Question 1: Cournot with a curved demand Joint monopolyExam favourite Q* = √a2c − 1 From a/(Q + 1)² = 2c. Positive exactly when a > 2c. Lesson: Question 1: Cournot with a curved demand Buy one more unit whenExam favourite marginal value ≥ price Because you value money, a unit is worth buying only if it is worth more to you than what it costs. Lesson: Question 2: bananas and candy Buy whenExam favourite 0.4 √400 − p + 900 + 0.6 √400 − p + 10 ≥ √400 = 20 Expected utility of final wealth after paying p and receiving the prize, against keeping ₹400. Lesson: Question 3: buying a lottery ticket Break even priceExam favourite p* ≈ 267.99 Found by bisection. Below p* she buys, above it she does not. Lesson: Question 3: buying a lottery ticket Objective v = x₁² + ½ x₂² + ¼ x₃² A convex function on the triangle x₁ + x₂ + x₃ = K. Lesson: Question 4: consumption with a convex payoff AnswerExam favourite (x₁, x₂, x₃) = (K, 0, 0), v = K² Spend everything in period 1. Lesson: Question 4: consumption with a convex payoff Tables Table: unit · marginal value · price · worth buying? Unit Marginal value Price Worth buying? Banana 1 $1.20 $0.50 Yes, +0.70 Banana 2 $0.60 $0.50 Yes, +0.10 Banana 3 $0.30 $0.50 No, −0.20 Candy 1 $0.40 $0.25 Yes, +0.15 Candy 2 $0.20 $0.25 No, −0.05 Lesson: Question 2: bananas and candy Tips and traps In the exam The trap in S1 is pattern matching. (a − c)/3 is the textbook Cournot answer, but it comes from demand a − bq. With a different demand curve you must redo the first order condition.Lesson: Question 1: Cournot with a curved demand Remember The answer depends on the numbers, not on a rule like \"always spend\" or \"never spend\". With $3 you would still stop at $1.25, because the third banana and the second candy are worth less than they cost.Lesson: Question 2: bananas and candy Common mistake This question has two true options under the standard reading (prizes added to wealth after paying p). If only one option may be marked, A is the safer choice because it covers every price below 18, and B is a special case. It is worth asking the instructor whether the question allows more than one answer.Lesson: Question 3: buying a lottery ticket 6.2"],[50,"exam-revision","Exam revision: from school to research universities","Open chapter Exam style questions from school level to research universities, with worked answers, a formula sheet and a mock quiz. Formulas Expected valueExam favourite E[X] = Σ pixi Weight each prize by its chance and add. Lesson: Tier 1: school and first year Present valueExam favourite PV = Σ δtxt A payment t periods away is worth δt of its face value today. Lesson: Tier 1: school and first year Strict orderings n! The number of ways to rank n outcomes with no ties. Lesson: Tier 1: school and first year Tables Table: tier · typical format · what earns the marks · where to practise Tier Typical format What earns the marks Where to practise School and first year Short answers, expected values, simple tables A clear definition with an example, careful arithmetic Lesson 2 University semester and IIT course exams Numericals by hand on trees, lotteries and games Formula first, every step shown, the answer stated with its meaning Lesson 3 NPTEL and GATE style papers Multiple choice, multiple select and numerical answer type Speed, spotting the trap option, exact rounding Lesson 4 MIT and Cambridge style Proofs, derivations and critique of a model Assumptions named, a complete argument, when it breaks Lesson 5 Lesson: How Advanced AI is examined Table: quick check · use it to Quick check Use it to A strictly dominant strategy equilibrium, if it exists, is unique Reject any option listing two Affine change a u + b with a > 0 keeps every expected utility ranking Reject options where a rescaled utility flips a choice Concave u means CE < EV and a positive risk premium Spot a risk averse answer at a glance VOI is never negative Reject a negative VOI at once IESDS order does not change the result, iterated weak dominance can Answer order questions in one line Linear Cournot q* = (a − c) ÷ 3b, Bertrand price = c Compute in seconds Lesson: Tier 3: multiple choice and numerical answer papers Table: topic · formula Topic Formula Rational preference complete and transitive Utility representation x ⪰ y if and only if u(x) ≥ u(y) CDF F(x) = P(X ≤ x), non decreasing, from 0 to 1 First order stochastic dominance FA(x) ≤ FB(x) for all x Expected utility E[u] = Σ pi u(xi) Certainty equivalent, risk premium u(CE) = E[u], RP = E[X] − CE Present value PV = Σ δt xt, perpetuity δ ÷ (1 − δ) per unit from next period ln consumption, two periods x₁ = K ÷ (1 + δ), x₂ = δ K ÷ (1 + δ) Value of information VOI = E[max with information] − max E[without] Linear Cournot duopoly q* = (a − c) ÷ 3b, monopoly Q = (a − c) ÷ 2b Bertrand with equal cost p* = c Pareto optimal no other profile makes someone better and nobody worse Lesson: Formula sheet and last minute checks Tips and traps Remember Try the presets stag and pennies in the game above. Stag has two equilibria and pennies has none in pure strategies.Lesson: Tier 1: school and first year In the exam Round only at the very end. A numerical answer type key often has a narrow range such as 52.6 to 52.7.Lesson: Tier 3: multiple choice and numerical answer papers In the exam In proofs, name each assumption when you use it: completeness, transitivity, the vNM axioms, common knowledge of rationality. Examiners often ask what breaks when one fails.Lesson: Tier 4: proofs, derivations and critique"],[51,"","Interview Questions","Advanced AI interview and viva questions on rational preferences, utility representation, expected utility and risk attitudes, backward induction, discounting, value of information, normal form games, Pareto optimality, dominance and IESDS, with numerical questions solved step by step. · interview · interview questions · viva · FAQ · job preparation · rational preference · utility function · ordinal vs cardinal · expected utility · certainty equivalent · St Petersburg · backward induction · discount factor · value of information · prisoners dilemma · Cournot · Bertrand · Pareto optimal · dominant strategy · IESDS Artificial Intelligence · Interview Questions 🎤"],[51,"","Advanced AI Interview and Viva Questions","The questions asked in vivas, research interviews and quant or AI roles about rational decision making and game theory: preference relations, utility functions, lotteries and expected utility, risk attitudes, decision trees and backward induction, discounting, the value of information, normal form games, Pareto optimality, dominance and IESDS, Cournot and Bertrand competition and auctions. Every numerical answer is worked out step by step. ✍️ Read the question and write your own answer in the box, as you would say it in the interview. 💡 Stuck? Open the hint. It points you in the right direction without giving the answer away. ✅ Open the answer to compare. You get the model answer, key points and a detailed explanation. 🎯 Rate yourself. Your notes and ratings stay in this browser, so you can filter to the ones to review. 🎤"],[51,"interview","Advanced AI Interview and Viva Questions","Answer out loud first, the way you would in the interview. Open the hint only if you are stuck, then compare your answer with the model answer. 0/29known ⭐ Most important topics for interviews 1 Preferences and utility Completeness, transitivity, money pumps, ordinal utility and when it exists. 2 Risk Lotteries, CDFs, stochastic dominance, expected utility, CE and risk premium. 3 Decisions over time Decision trees, backward induction, strategies, discounting and consumption. 4 Information Value of perfect and imperfect information, and why it is never negative. 5 Games Normal form, best responses, Prisoner's Dilemma, Pareto optimality. 6 Dominance Strict and weak dominance, dominant strategy equilibrium, IESDS and order. 7 Markets and auctions Cournot, Bertrand, Hotelling and second price auctions. 0 of 29 answers opened, 0 known, 0 to review Preferences and utility Q1 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptPreferences and utility What does it mean for a preference relation to be rational? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Two properties. Your answer Model answer A preference relation ⪰ over outcomes is rational when it is complete, so any two outcomes can be compared, and transitive, so x ⪰ y and y ⪰ z give x ⪰ z. Rational here means consistent, not wise or selfish. Complete Transitive Consistency, not wisdom Detailed explanation Completeness rules out \"I cannot say\". Transitivity rules out cycles. Together they let us line up all outcomes in an order with possible ties. How did you do? Q2 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptPreferences and utility How many strict rankings and how many rankings with ties are there of 3 outcomes? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first n! for strict, then count ties. Your answer Model answer Strict rankings: 3! = 6. With ties allowed (weak orders) there are 13: the 6 strict ones, 6 with exactly one tie (choose the tied pair and whether it is above or below the third), and 1 where all three are tied. 3! = 6 13 weak orders Fubini numbers Detailed explanation For 4 outcomes the counts are 24 and 75. The weak order numbers are the Fubini numbers 1, 3, 13, 75, 541. How did you do? Q3 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPreferences and utility Explain the money pump argument for transitivity. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A cycle and a small fee. Your answer Model answer Suppose someone strictly prefers A to B, B to C and C to A. Holding C, they will pay a small fee to swap to B, then pay again to swap to A, then pay again to swap to C. They are back where they started with less money, and the cycle can repeat. Cyclic preferences can be exploited without limit, which is the standard argument that rational preferences should be transitive. Strict cycle Pay to swap round the loop Exploitable without limit Detailed explanation Interviewers like a follow up: real people do show intransitive choices in experiments, often because they compare on different attributes each time. How did you do? Q4 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPreferences and utility What is the difference between ordinal and cardinal utility? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Which transformations keep the meaning. Your answer Model answer Ordinal utility only records the order: any strictly increasing transformation of u represents the same preferences, so the gaps between numbers mean nothing. Cardinal (von Neumann Morgenstern) utility is used for choices under risk: it is unique only up to a positive affine change a u + b with a > 0, so ratios of differences carry meaning. Ordinal: any increasing change Cardinal: a u + b, a > 0 Lotteries need cardinal Detailed explanation Example: u = (1, 2, 3) and v = (1, 10, 100) rank three sure outcomes the same way. Over lotteries they can rank a 50:50 mix differently, so only affine changes are safe there. How did you do? Q5 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptPreferences and utility Prove that a rational preference on a finite set has a utility representation. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Count what each outcome beats or ties. Your answer Model answer Define u(x) as the"],[51,"interview","Advanced AI Interview and Viva Questions","number of outcomes y with x ⪰ y. If x ⪰ z, transitivity puts everything below z below x as well, so u(x) ≥ u(z). If x ≻ z, then x is counted in u(x) but not in u(z), so u(x) > u(z). Completeness makes every pair comparable, so u represents ⪰. u(x) = number of outcomes x is at least as good as Uses both axioms Fails for lexicographic order on the plane Detailed explanation On infinite sets this can fail. Lexicographic preferences on the plane are complete and transitive but no real valued function represents them, because each vertical line would need its own interval of real numbers and there are uncountably many lines. How did you do? Risk Q6 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptRisk What is expected utility and how is it different from expected value? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Average of what. Your answer Model answer Expected value averages the prizes: Σ pi xi. Expected utility averages the utility of the prizes: Σ pi u(xi). They rank lotteries the same way only when u is linear, that is for a risk neutral person. EV averages prizes EU averages utilities Same only for linear u Detailed explanation For u(w) = √w, a sure 49 has utility 7 while a 50:50 lottery between 0 and 100 has expected value 50 but expected utility only 5, so the sure 49 is preferred. How did you do? Q7 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptRisk Compute the certainty equivalent and risk premium of a 50:50 lottery between 0 and 100 for u(w) = √w. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Find EU, then invert u. Your answer Model answer EU = 0.5 × 0 + 0.5 × 10 = 5. The certainty equivalent solves √CE = 5, so CE = 25. The expected value is 50, so the risk premium is 50 − 25 = 25. EU = 5 CE = 25 Risk premium = 25 Detailed explanation The person would accept any sure amount above 25 instead of the lottery. A positive premium marks risk aversion. How did you do? Q8 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputRisk What does this program print? import math x = [20, 80] p = [0.5, 0.5] ev = sum(a * b for a, b in zip(p, x)) ce = math.exp(sum(a * math.log(b) for a, b in zip(p, x))) print(round(ev, 1), round(ce, 1)) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Compute EU with log utility, then exponentiate. Your answer Model answer It prints 50.0 40.0. The expected value is 0.5 × 20 + 0.5 × 80 = 50. With u = ln w, EU = 0.5 ln 20 + 0.5 ln 80 = ln √1600 = ln 40, so CE = 40. Detailed explanation For log utility the certainty equivalent of a 50:50 lottery is the geometric mean of the two prizes. 50.040.0 How did you do? Q9 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptRisk Why can you rescale a vNM utility by a u + b but not by u²? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Linearity of expectation. Your answer Model answer E[a u + b] = a E[u] + b, which is an increasing function of E[u] when a > 0, so every ranking of lotteries is kept. Squaring is not linear, so E[u²] can rank two lotteries differently from E[u]. That is why vNM utility is cardinal. Expectation is linear a > 0 keeps order Non affine changes can flip Detailed explanation Example with u = (0, 6, 10) on three prizes: the sure middle prize beats a 50:50 mix of the others under u (6 against 5) but loses under u² (36 against 50). How did you do? Q10 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptRisk Why does a concave utility function imply risk aversion? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Jensen's inequality. Your answer Model answer For concave u, Jensen gives E[u(X)] ≤ u(E[X]). The certainty equivalent satisfies u(CE) = E[u(X)], so u(CE) ≤ u(E[X]), and since u is increasing, CE ≤ E[X]. The risk premium E[X] − CE is therefore at least 0, so the person would give up some expected value to remove risk. Jensen CE ≤ EV Arrow Pratt −u″/u′ Detailed explanation The Arrow Pratt coefficient −u″ ÷ u′ measures how strong the aversion is. For u = ln w it is 1 ÷ w, so richer people are less averse to a fixed sized bet. How did you do? Q11 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptRisk What is the St Petersburg paradox and how does expected utility resolve it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Infinite expected value. Your answer Model answer A fair coin is tossed until the"],[51,"interview","Advanced AI Interview and Viva Questions","first head. If it comes on toss k you win 2k. Each term of the expected value is 2−k × 2k = 1, so the expected value is infinite, yet people pay only a few units to play. With log utility, the expected utility Σ 2−k k ln 2 = 2 ln 2 is finite, giving a certainty equivalent of 4. EV diverges Log utility gives CE = 4 Finite banks cap it Detailed explanation A second resolution is that no bank can pay huge prizes. Capping the game at 10 rounds gives an expected value of only 10. How did you do? Q12 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptRisk Define first order stochastic dominance and say why it matters. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Compare CDFs. Your answer Model answer Lottery A first order stochastically dominates B when FA(x) ≤ FB(x) for every x, with strict inequality somewhere. A always has at least as much chance of exceeding any level. Every person with an increasing utility prefers A, so the choice does not depend on risk attitude. FA ≤ FB everywhere All increasing u agree Crossing CDFs need u Detailed explanation If the CDFs cross, neither dominates at first order and the choice depends on u. Second order dominance then compares areas under the CDFs and applies to all risk averse people. How did you do? Decisions over time Q13 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptDecisions over time How do you solve a decision tree with chance nodes? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Start at the leaves. Your answer Model answer Work backwards from the leaves. At a chance node, replace it with the expected value (or expected utility) of its branches. At a decision node, keep the best branch and record the choice. Repeat until you reach the root. The value at the root is the best achievable expected payoff, and the recorded choices are the optimal plan. Average at chance nodes Maximise at decision nodes Record the plan Detailed explanation This is backward induction. It is dynamic programming on a tree, and the same idea finds subgame perfect equilibria in sequential games. How did you do? Q14 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptDecisions over time What is a discount factor and how do you find a present value? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first δ between 0 and 1. Your answer Model answer A discount factor δ in (0, 1) says how much a unit received one period later is worth today. A payment xt at time t is worth δt xt today, so PV = Σ δt xt. With an interest rate r, δ = 1 ÷ (1 + r). δ = 1/(1 + r) PV = Σ δt xt Perpetuity x ÷ (1 − δ) Detailed explanation A constant stream of 10 forever, starting today, with δ = 0.95 is worth 10 ÷ (1 − 0.95) = 200. How did you do? Q15 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptDecisions over time Why does a strategy have to name an action at nodes that will never be reached? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Plans against outcomes. Your answer Model answer A strategy is a complete contingent plan, one action at every decision node. Nodes off the optimal path still matter because what you would do there affects other players' choices in games, and it lets us count and compare all plans. A player with 3 decision nodes and 2 actions at each has 2³ = 8 strategies. Complete contingent plan Count by multiplying Needed for threats in games Detailed explanation In the R&D tree of the course, two decision nodes with Market or Drop at each give the four strategies MM, MD, DM and DD. How did you do? Q16 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptDecisions over time Split a budget K over two periods to maximise ln x₁ + δ ln x₂. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Equate marginal utilities. Your answer Model answer The first order condition with the budget x₁ + x₂ = K gives 1 ÷ x₁ = δ ÷ x₂, so x₂ = δ x₁. Substituting, x₁ = K ÷ (1 + δ) and x₂ = δ K ÷ (1 + δ). With K = 100 and δ = 0.9, x₁ = 52.63 and x₂ = 47.37. x₂ = δ x₁ x₁ = K/(1 + δ) Convex u gives a corner Detailed explanation With a convex utility such as x², the first order point is a minimum and the best plan puts everything in the first period. Quiz 1 of the course tested exactly that trap. How did you do? Information Q17 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptInformation Define the"],[51,"interview","Advanced AI Interview and Viva Questions","value of perfect information and compute it for a simple case. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first With information minus without. Your answer Model answer VOI = E[best payoff when you learn the state first] − best expected payoff without it. Example: A pays 10 if good and 0 if bad, B pays 4 for sure, P(good) = 0.6. Without information A gives 6. With it you pick A when good and B when bad: 0.6 × 10 + 0.4 × 4 = 7.6. VOI = 1.6. With minus without Example gives 1.6 Imperfect ≤ perfect Detailed explanation The value of an imperfect forecast is found the same way using the posterior after each signal, and it is never more than the value of perfect information. How did you do? Q18 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptInformation Why is the value of information never negative for a single decision maker? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first You can always ignore it. Your answer Model answer With the information you can still choose the action that was best without it, so the expected payoff with information is at least the old best. Formally E[maxa u(a, s) given the signal] ≥ maxa E[u(a, s)], since the max of an average is at most the average of maxima. VOI is zero exactly when no signal changes the best action. Can ignore the signal Max of average ≤ average of max Can fail in games Detailed explanation In games this can fail: information that others know you have can change their behaviour and hurt you. How did you do? Games Q19 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptGames What are the parts of a game in normal form? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Players, strategies, payoffs. Your answer Model answer A normal form game lists the players, a set of strategies for each player, and a payoff function for each player that gives a number for every strategy profile. Two player games are drawn as a bimatrix with the row player's payoff first. Players Strategies Payoffs for every profile Detailed explanation The usual assumptions are that players are rational, choose at the same time without seeing each other, and that the game and rationality are common knowledge. How did you do? Q20 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptGames Why do rational players confess in the Prisoner's Dilemma even though both would be better off staying quiet? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Look at each player alone. Your answer Model answer Confess strictly dominates Quiet: whatever the other does, confessing gives a better payoff. So each confesses, and (Confess, Confess) is the outcome. (Quiet, Quiet) is better for both, so it is Pareto better, but it is not stable because each player gains by switching to Confess. Confess dominates Outcome is Pareto dominated Individual against group Detailed explanation The game shows that individual rationality and group efficiency can conflict. Repetition, contracts or caring about the other player (altruism) can change the result. How did you do? Q21 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptGames What is a Pareto optimal outcome and is it the same as an equilibrium? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Better for someone, worse for none. Your answer Model answer A profile is Pareto optimal when no other profile makes at least one player better off without making anyone worse off. It is a statement about efficiency, not about what rational players choose, so an equilibrium need not be Pareto optimal (the Prisoner's Dilemma) and a Pareto optimal profile need not be an equilibrium. No free improvement Efficiency, not prediction Zero sum: all profiles optimal Detailed explanation In a zero sum game every profile is Pareto optimal, since any gain for one player is a loss for the other. How did you do? Dominance Q22 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptDominance What is the difference between strict and weak dominance? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Greater against greater or equal. Your answer Model answer Strategy s strictly dominates t if s gives a strictly higher payoff than t against every strategy of the others. Weak dominance needs at least as high against every strategy and strictly higher against at least one."],[51,"interview","Advanced AI Interview and Viva Questions","Strict: > everywhere Weak: ≥ everywhere, > somewhere Never play strictly dominated Detailed explanation A rational player never plays a strictly dominated strategy. Weakly dominated strategies can still be part of an equilibrium. How did you do? Q23 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptDominance Can a player have two strictly dominant strategies? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Suppose two exist. Your answer Model answer No. If s and t were both strictly dominant with s ≠ t, then s strictly beats t everywhere and t strictly beats s everywhere, which is impossible. So a strictly dominant strategy is unique, and a strictly dominant strategy equilibrium, when it exists, is unique. Contradiction argument Unique if it exists Weak can tie Detailed explanation With weak dominance two strategies can each weakly dominate the rest if they tie with each other everywhere. How did you do? Q24 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptDominance Describe IESDS and say why the order of deletion does not matter. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Delete, then look again. Your answer Model answer Iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies repeatedly deletes any strategy that is strictly dominated given the strategies still left, until nothing more can be deleted. A strategy dominated at one stage stays dominated after other deletions, since removing opponents' strategies only removes constraints on the comparison. So any order of deletion reaches the same final set. Repeat until stable Strict: order independent Weak: order can matter Detailed explanation Iterated weak dominance does not have this property. Deleting in different orders can leave different profiles, which is a common exam trap. How did you do? Q25 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputDominance What does this program print? It runs IESDS on a 3 by 3 game. R = {\"U\": [4, 5, 0], \"C\": [2, 8, 9], \"D\": [3, 4, 9]} C = {\"U\": [3, 1, 0], \"C\": [1, 0, -1], \"D\": [0, 2, 1]} rows, cols = [\"U\", \"C\", \"D\"], [\"L\", \"M\", \"R\"] idx = {\"L\": 0, \"M\": 1, \"R\": 2} changed = Truewhile changed: changed = Falsefor r in rows[:]: if any(all(R[s][idx[c]] > R[r][idx[c]] for c in cols) for s in rows if s != r): rows.remove(r); changed = Truefor c in cols[:]: if any(all(C[r][idx[d]] > C[r][idx[c]] for r in rows) for d in cols if d != c): cols.remove(c); changed = Trueprint(rows, cols) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Find a dominated row or column, delete it, repeat. Your answer Model answer It prints ['U'] ['L']. Column R is strictly dominated by M for the column player. With R gone, row D is dominated by U. Then column M is dominated by L, and finally row C is dominated by U, leaving (U, L). Detailed explanation The program checks rows and columns in a loop until no deletion happens, which is exactly the definition of IESDS. ['U'] ['L'] How did you do? Markets and auctions Q26 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptMarkets and auctions Find the Cournot equilibrium for p = a − b(q₁ + q₂) with unit cost c. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Best responses and symmetry. Your answer Model answer Firm i maximises (a − b qi − b qj − c) qi. The first order condition gives qi = (a − c − b qj) ÷ 2b. By symmetry q* = (a − c) ÷ 3b. For a = 100, b = 1, c = 10, each firm makes 30 and the price is 40. q* = (a − c)/3b Between monopoly and competition IESDS converges to it Detailed explanation Total Cournot output 2(a − c)/3b lies between monopoly output (a − c)/2b and the competitive output (a − c)/b. IESDS on this game shrinks the strategy interval each round and converges to the same point. How did you do? Q27 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptMarkets and auctions Why does Bertrand competition with two identical firms drive price to marginal cost? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Undercutting. Your answer Model answer Buyers go to the cheaper firm. At any common price above cost, either firm can cut slightly below the other and take the whole market, almost doubling its profit. So no price above c is stable. At p = c neither firm can gain by moving, and profits are zero. Two firms are enough for the"],[51,"interview","Advanced AI Interview and Viva Questions","competitive outcome. Undercut to take the market p = c Paradox: two firms suffice Detailed explanation This is the Bertrand paradox. Product differentiation, capacity limits or a discrete price grid let prices stay above cost. How did you do? Q28 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptMarkets and auctions Why is bidding your true value weakly dominant in a second price sealed bid auction? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first You never set your own price. Your answer Model answer The winner pays the second highest bid, so your bid only decides whether you win, not what you pay. Bidding above your value adds wins only when the price is above your value, which lose money. Bidding below your value only loses auctions you would have won at a price below your value. Neither ever helps, and each sometimes hurts, so truthful bidding weakly dominates. Bid sets win or lose only Overbid can lose money Underbid can miss gains Detailed explanation In a first price auction you pay your own bid, so bidders shade below their value and truth telling is not dominant. How did you do? Scenarios Q29 🧠 Expert🌍 ScenarioScenarios A company prices an extended warranty at its expected repair cost and finds that almost nobody buys it at a premium. Explain using decision theory. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Risk attitude and information. Your answer Model answer If buyers were risk averse they would pay more than the expected cost, so the low uptake suggests other factors. Buyers may think their own risk is lower than average (private information, so only high risk buyers buy, adverse selection), they may be close to risk neutral for small amounts, or they may already be insured. The fix is to segment by risk, price with the risk premium for large losses, and bundle where the loss is large relative to wealth. Risk premium only for large losses Adverse selection Separate beliefs from attitude Detailed explanation The interviewer wants you to separate risk attitude (curvature of u) from beliefs (probabilities) and from information asymmetry. How did you do? No questions match these filters."]]};
